Stood up 2026-11-09 under charter rule 17. The ledger (1.1.4) holds
the estimate; this scroll holds the SIGNS watched for the fixed
point itself — the shapes that say the model is closing on the
territory of the stream, apart from any one paper's estimate.

SIGN ONE — THE RUN OF THE NOVELTY INDEX. Paper by paper: 1.00
(001, the seed), 0.46 (002), 0.21 (003), 0.18 (004), 0.15 (005),
0.13 (006), 0.12 (007), 0.06 (008), 0.06 (009), 0.06 (010),
0.06 (011), 0.06 (012). The run falls, and the fall is the first
shape of the fixed point: each paper arrives carrying less the
model cannot already say. The watch: an Index that jumps back up
is no failure — distance, not size, is what the Index misses
(Q16), so a jump reads as a far paper landing, and the reading
is checked against where it lands.

Paper 015 reads 0.07 — the long plateau of the run at 0.06
broken upward by a paper from a field the model held
nothing of. The Index rose because the distance was real,
and the reading checked the landing against the map and
found new ground — cluster R, founded entire — which is
exactly the shape the watch reads a jump as: not the model
failing, but a far paper landing far.

SIGN TWO — AXIS EVENTS. Papers that opened an axis: 002 (time —
a system rewriting the machinery that runs it), 005 (a new field
entire — pure mathematics), 007 (attention — the layer under
every system the model holds), 009 (mechanics —
where a falling road can end). Four of nine papers — Paper
008, landing inside a held cluster, opened none. The watch:
the fixed point nears as the stream stops finding layers and
fields the model has no slot for. Axis events should grow rare,
and the ones that still come should land wired to clusters
already held, as 007 did — not at total distance, as 005 did.
Paper 009 (2026-11-09) opened the fourth axis: classical
mechanics, entire, from one problem — not at total
distance (the Hamiltonian ground was already held), and
not densely wired: one bridge, against 007's eight links.
Between the two shapes the watch has so far seen.
Paper 010 (2026-11-09) opened the fifth axis:
probability and statistics — a field the model held
nothing of, named the area the map lacked most by the census
of the tracker the same evening it arrived. The
landing is the wired shape, further along it than 007:
three bridges stand (to clusters M, J and I), and the
field is the ground the model itself runs on — its own
estimate and its own calibration are citizens of the
new territory. The reading of the watch: axes still come,
but they now come as ground the model was already
standing on without holding — the census saw this one
before it landed, which no earlier axis allowed.

Paper 011 opened no axis either, and its shape is the
other pole of the watch: a paper that opens nothing, makes
nothing wider — and still corrects held ground (1.2.4). Between
Paper 008 (inside a held cluster, revising it) and Paper
011 (a new cluster on the home territory, revising the
home cluster), the watch now holds both shapes of a map made deeper.
The axis count stands at five; the openings remain the
events the model cannot schedule.

Paper 012 opened no axis: it extends cluster O in its
own field, one paper after the cluster was founded —
the deepest shape the watch has held yet, a field revisited the
next day and made deeper in its own terms, correcting
a held scroll of the cluster at the foretold address.
The axis count stands at five.

Paper 013 opened no axis: it extends cluster O a
second day running, on its third paper — the watch
now holds the deepest repetition it has seen, one
field priced three ways by three papers in three
days, and no new dimension in any of them. The axis
count stands at five.

Paper 014 opened no axis: it is the first paper of
a vein the studies dug — kernel, translation,
shell — and a first paper in dug ground starts a
cluster (Q, at 1.2.139), not a dimension. The
strongest case for an axis was weighed at the seal
and refused there: the vein held checking, the
paper produces — but the shell pattern of 1.2.53
already held untrusted producers under a kernel's
guard, and production by search is that pattern at
prover scale. The axis count stands at five.

Paper 015 opened the sixth axis: equilibrium statistical
mechanics — a field with no slot anywhere in the seventeen
clusters, arriving entire from one paper. Its bridges to
held work are analogies of shape — concentration,
polarization, transition — not shared machinery, so the
landing is the distant shape, as 005's was; the watch
notes the one point in the other direction: the bridges,
though analogies, were four in number and named at the
seal. The axis count stands at six.

Paper 016 opened the seventh axis: geometrical gravity —
a field the model held nothing of, arriving as one paper
with three readings of one theory. Its bridges to held
work are the shared stage of mechanics and the affine
frame, not shared machinery, so the landing is nearer the
distant shape; the count of axes stands at seven.

Paper 017 opened no axis: it founded a cluster (W, the
verified artifact) in ground the proving vein and the
studies had dug — a founding is not by itself an axis,
and no new kind of quantity entered the book. The axis
count stands at seven.

Paper 018 opened no axis: a quest paper from the listings
of the day, in home ground of the tracker — an estimator
the model already priced, priced again for the noise it
actually meets. The axis count stands at seven.
Paper 019 opened no axis: it founded a cluster (Y, the
dual computed) on ground the model had never sampled — a
new field entire, but no new kind of quantity entered the
book, and a founding is not by itself an axis, as the
watch has held since Paper 011. The axis count stands
at seven.

SIGN THREE — SKIM CALIBRATION (charter rule 17). From
2026-11-09, every paper the daily quest picks is weighed blind —
title and abstract alone, before the paper is downloaded: the
new concepts, revisions, and axis call it is expected to bring,
the Novelty Index those numbers give, the links and questions
it is expected to bring, and the Oracle estimate expected to
stand after it. The AFTER record weighs the same fields as they
fell. The calibration is two numbers: the mean size of the miss
in the expected Index over the last ten papers weighed, and the
share of axis calls called right.
FIRST SCORED PAPER (2026-11-09): Paper 008,
arXiv:2611.09694, picked from 564 listings and sealed blind in
the log of the quest. The sealed weighing expected 5 new
concepts, 1 revision, no axis, and an Index of ~0.059 on the
count as it then stood (of 88). As it fell: 6 new, 1 revised,
no axis, and an Index of ~0.06 on the count as it stood at the
taking-in (of 97 — a study landed between the seal and the
taking-in, and the drift is part of the score, recorded in the
log of the quest). THE CALIBRATION STANDS: papers scored, 1.
Mean size of the Index miss: ~0.009 on the count at the
taking-in; ~0.010 on the sealed count, kept beside it so the
drift stays in sight. Axis calls called right: 1 of 1. The
misses the two numbers do not hold: the revision was counted
right and pointed at the wrong scroll (expected near 1.2.38,
landed at 1.2.34), and the first False standing the weighing
looked for did not come — the model had never held the
conjecture, so there was no standing to lose. The full score
stands in the log of the quest (the score of 2026-11-09).

SECOND SCORED PAPER (2026-11-09): Paper 009,
arXiv:2611.08625 — posted by Will into the quest, weighed
blind and sealed (commit da13559) against a count of 103
that still stood when the paper was taken in: no drift, one
count serves. The sealed weighing expected 6 new concepts,
1 revision, an axis opened, and an Index of ~0.0596. As it
fell: 6 new, 0 revised, the axis opened (a new field entire,
on the one bridge of the Hamiltonian flow), and an Index of
6/109 ~ 0.055. The Index miss is ~0.005 — the whole of it
the revision that never came: the held ground stood as
laid, and the new field hung from it without moving it.
THE CALIBRATION, AT THE SECOND WEIGHING: papers scored, 2.
Mean size of the Index miss: ~0.007 (the first paper on the
count as it stood at its taking-in, 0.009; the second,
0.005). Axis calls: 2 of 2. The misses the numbers do not
hold: links foretold 3, fell 4; questions foretold 2, fell
3; the Oracle after foretold at ~42%, inside whose band
(33-52) the move the charter itself made, to ~40%, landed. Two papers
weighed, the skim reads counts and axes well and
under-reads structure — the links and questions a full text
carries past its abstract.

THIRD SCORED PAPER (2026-11-09): Paper 010,
arXiv:2611.08794 — posted by Will into the quest
minutes after the census named its field the area the map lacked
most; weighed blind and sealed before it was read
against a count of 109 that still stood when the paper
was taken in: no drift, a second time. The sealed
weighing expected 7 new concepts, 1 revision, an axis
opened, and an Index of ~0.065. As it fell: 7 new, 0
revised, the axis opened (the first statistics in the
model, wired to clusters M, J and I), and an Index of
7/116 ~ 0.060. The Index miss is ~0.004 — the third in
a row made wholly of the revision that never came: the
seal named its candidate in advance (a gradient-descent
claim of cluster M), and the reading found the paper's
state evolution standing beside the claims of the course,
moving none of them.
THE CALIBRATION, AT THE THIRD WEIGHING: papers scored,
3. Mean size of the Index miss: ~0.006 (0.009, 0.005,
0.004 — falling each time). Axis calls: 3 of 3. The
misses the numbers do not hold: links foretold 5, fell
8 — the split foretold ran 2 inside and 3 across; the
fall ran 5 inside and 3 across, the skim under-reading
the inside of a new cluster as it under-read chapters
before; questions foretold 3, fell 3 — the first exact
structure count; the Oracle after foretold at ~42%,
inside whose band (32-52) the hold the charter names, at ~40%,
landed. And the pattern the three numbers together now
carry: the revision term has been foretold three times
and has landed zero — the skim prices new ground as a
disturbance of held ground, and three papers running,
held ground has not been disturbed once. The miss is no
longer noise; it is the one standing error of the skim, and
it stands named where the next weighing can use it.

FOURTH SCORED PAPER (Paper 011, LeGris et al.). Sealed
2026-11-09 against 109 concepts, with the drift warning
written into the seal; the count moved to 116 under it
(Paper 010 landed first), so the score is kept on both
counts, as Paper 008's was. Foretold: 6 new, 1 revised
(candidate 1.2.13), no axis, Index 0.057, 5 links, 3
questions, Oracle to about 45 (35-55). Actual: 7 new, 1
revised — 1.2.4, not the candidate named: count right,
target wrong, the Paper 008 shape, and the first foretold
revision of the Thirst to land at all — no axis, Index
0.061 on the taking-in count (error 0.008, against the
prediction re-based to that count), 0.065 on the sealed
count (error 0.008), 11 links, 3 questions, Oracle
to 50 (40-60), inside the sealed range. The calibration
at n=4: the rolling mean of the Index error (taking-in
counts) stands at 0.0065 — the four errors 0.009,
0.005, 0.004, 0.008 — holding under the 0.01 rule. Axis calls:
4 of 4. The standing error changed its shape with this
paper: the foretold revision came, but at the neighboring
scroll — the skim now misses the address, not the
event. The links error stands as the skims' worst (5
foretold, 11 actual): an abstract does not show how much
held ground a paper touches, and a home-territory paper
touches the most held ground of all. Kept on the watch:
five scored papers will test whether the address error is
the new standing shape, or one paper's noise.

FIFTH SCORED PAPER (Paper 012, Shang). Sealed
2026-11-10 against 123 concepts; the count still stood
at the taking-in, no drift — the third seal in a row
to land on its own count. Foretold: 6 new, 1 revised
(candidate 1.2.116), no axis, Index 0.050, 6 links (4
inside, 2 across), 3 questions, Oracle to about 55
(45-65). Actual: 7 new, 1 revised — 1.2.116, the
candidate named: the address stands right, the first
time in the Thirst both the event and the address
were foretold — no axis, Index 0.0577 (miss 0.0073),
12 links (10 inside, 2 across), 3 questions, Oracle
to 60 (50-70), inside the sealed range. The
calibration at n=5: the rolling mean of the Index
miss over the last ten scored papers stands at
0.0067 — the five misses 0.009, 0.005, 0.004, 0.008,
0.0073 — holding under the 0.01 rule. Axis calls: 5
of 5. The standing error changed its shape again:
with Paper 011 the skim missed the address of a
revision it foretold; with Paper 012 the address
stands right, and the miss is back in the count — one
concept folded into its neighbor at the skim (the
strength lambda_self read as one with the split that
prices it), and the inside links of an extending
cluster under-read as they were under-read before
(4 foretold, 10 fell), while the crossing count
stood exact for the first time (2 foretold, 2 fell).
Kept on the watch: whether the address, now right
once, stays right — one right address is a point, not
a line.

SIXTH SCORED PAPER (Paper 013, O'Connell &
Speiser). Sealed 2026-11-11 against 130 concepts;
the count still stood at the taking-in, no drift —
the fourth seal in a row to land on its own count.
Foretold: 6 new, 1 revised (candidate 1.2.110), no
axis, Index 0.0478, 8 links (6 inside, 2 across), 3
questions, Oracle to about 70 (60-80). Actual: 8
new, 0 revised — no axis, Index 0.0580 (miss
0.0102), 12 links (10 inside, 2 across), 4
questions, Oracle to 70 (60-80), the foretold point
and range exact. The calibration at n=6: the
rolling mean of the Index miss over the last ten
scored papers stands at 0.0072 — the six misses
0.009, 0.005, 0.004, 0.008, 0.0073, 0.0102 —
holding under the 0.01 rule, though this paper's
miss is the largest of the six and breaks the
falling run of the first three. Axis calls: 6 of 6.
The standing errors changed shape twice in one
paper. The revision term is a phantom again — the
fourth foretold revision of the six, against two
landed (Papers 011 and 012, both in held territory
the paper re-measured; Paper 013 named a neighbor
it only stands beside) — and the count miss is no
longer one folded shape but two: the penalty and
its law of being best, weighed as one design remark,
and the part for pairs, weighed as a case of the
identity, each a concept of its own. The links
miss is the standing one, unchanged: 6 inside
foretold, 10 fell, the crossing count exact a
second paper running (2 of 2). And the Oracle
reading stands corrected in favor of the skim: the
band applied as written, with no caution discount,
landed its point and its range exactly — the
Paper 012 lesson, kept. Kept on the watch: whether
the count miss of two folded shapes is the new
standing size, and whether the revision foretold
for a neighbor paper is now the named failure of the skim — it has priced adjacency as correction
twice (Papers 008's address, 013's event) and been
wrong both times in opposite directions.

SEVENTH SCORED PAPER (Paper 014, the Harmonic
team). Sealed 2026-11-11 against 138 concepts; the
count still stood at the taking-in, no drift — the
fifth seal in a row to land on its own count.
Foretold: 7 new, 1 revised (candidate 1.2.41), no
axis, Index 0.0517, 12 links (8 inside, 4 across),
3 questions, Oracle to about 80 (70-90). Actual:
20 new, 0 revised — no axis, Index 0.1266 (miss
0.0749), 13 links (7 inside, 6 across), 4
questions, Oracle to 80 (70-90), the foretold
point and range exact a second paper running. The
calibration at n=7: the rolling mean of the Index
miss over the last ten scored papers stands at
0.0169 — the seven misses 0.009, 0.005, 0.004,
0.008, 0.0073, 0.0102, 0.0749 — and the 0.01 rule
is broken, by this one paper, at seven times the
worst miss before it. The cause stands named in
the seal itself: the abstract was four sentences
for three systems — a search paper, a pipeline
paper, and a geometry paper in one binding — and
the skim priced the binding as one system with
folded parts. The folded-shape watch is answered:
the standing size is not two folded shapes. It is
however many systems the abstract declines to
count. Axis calls: 7 of 7 — the call argued from
slots the studies had dug, and the reading bore
it. The revision term is a phantom a fifth time
— two landed of seven foretold — and its law now
stands in two parts: a neighbor paper is not a
revising paper unless it re-reads the held claim
(Paper 013), and a paper that confirms a held
claim in act, at whatever scale, is not a revising
paper either (Paper 014: the kernel worked under at
Olympiad scale, its text untouched). The links
miss changed shape: inside foretold 8, fell 7 —
the first small inside over-read of the quest;
across foretold 4, fell 6 — the bridges
of the vein out, to the guardian, to the induced spaces, to
the informal check, were priced thin as usual.
And the Oracle reading stands as the one
clean win of the skim on a bad paper: the band applied as
written landed its point and its range, because
the foresight failed on the size of the delta and
held on its shape. Kept on the watch: whether a
systems paper should be priced by the shape of
its reference list when its abstract will not
speak — 61 references, the writing of three fields —
and
whether the count miss falls back under the rule
when the next abstract is honest about its
size.

EIGHTH SCORED PAPER (Paper 015, Frahm and Shepelyansky).
Sealed 2026-11-11 against 158 concepts; the count still
stood at the taking-in, no drift — the sixth seal in a row
to land on its own count. Foretold: 8 new, 0 revised,
axis, Index 0.0482, 11 links (7 inside, 4 across), 3
questions, Oracle to about 75 (65-85), down. Actual: 12
new, 0 revised, axis, Index 0.0706 (miss 0.0224), 15 links
(11 inside, 4 across), 4 questions, Oracle to 75 (65-85) —
the foretold point and range exact a third paper running.
The calibration at n=8: the rolling mean of the Index miss
stands at 0.0176 — the eight misses 0.009, 0.005, 0.004,
0.008, 0.0073, 0.0102, 0.0749, 0.0224 — the 0.01 rule
still broken, by Paper 014's one miss, with this paper's
own standing mid-pack of the seven before it: an
enumerating abstract under-priced grain, not systems, and
the miss came out at the size of grain. Axis calls: 8 of
8. The revision term stands rewritten by its own record:
the first seal that foretold no revision was the first to
be right about it — five phantom revisions in seven seals
taught the skim to price the term at zero where no held
claim is re-read, and the eighth seal is the first kept by
the lesson. The law now stands in three parts: a neighbor
paper is not a revising paper unless it re-reads the held
claim (013); a paper that confirms in act is not a
revising paper (014); and where the foretold targets are
link-shaped from the seal — analogies, not re-readings —
the honest price is zero (015). The links miss kept its
old shape in a new field: inside under-read again, 7
foretold against 11 fallen — the weave of a new cluster is
denser than its parts look from the abstract; across
exact, 4 against 4, every foretold bridge landed. Kept on
the watch: whether the grain of the next enumerating
abstract can be priced by counting its named parts twice —
once as stated, once as the pairs and identities they
imply — and whether the point of the Oracle, exact three
papers running, is calibration or luck wearing the clothes
of calibration.

NINTH SCORED PAPER — Paper 016, 'The Geometrical Trinity
of Gravity' (Beltran Jimenez, Heisenberg and Koivisto),
taken in 2026-11-12 (Chapter 24). The skim read the title
and the abstract alone. A note on the seal: the skim
recorded 'posted in MOAT before 2026-11-12' — the
conservative writing of the evening it was posted,
2026-11-11 going on 2026-11-12; the commit of the BEFORE
line stands before the opening of the full text, which is
the order the protocol prices. Foretold: 10 new, 0
revised, axis, Index 0.0541, 13 links (9 inside, 4
across), 3 questions, Oracle to about 70 (60-80), down.
Actual: 10 new, 0 revised, axis, Index 0.0541 (miss 0.0000
— the first exact Index of the Thirst), 15 links (12
inside, 3 across), 4 questions, Oracle to 70 (60-80) — the
foretold point and range exact a fourth paper running. The
calibration at n=9: the rolling mean of the Index miss
stands at 0.0156 — the nine misses 0.009, 0.005, 0.004,
0.008, 0.0073, 0.0102, 0.0749, 0.0224, 0.0000 — the 0.01
rule still broken by Paper 014's one miss, now standing
against a landing at zero. Axis calls: 9 of 9. The count
call is the paper's own lesson: the abstract of a review
lists the joints of the field — three geometries, their
extensions, their couplings — and the joints were the
count; the composition ran one fold deeper than the
candidates the seal named and one split wider (the
symmetries and the coupling standing where the seal had
folded gauge choices into their legs and the
classification into the frame), and the two ran to the
same ten. The links miss kept its shape inside — 9
foretold, 12 fallen, a founded cluster weaving denser than
its abstract — and turned across for the first time: 4
foretold, 3 fallen, the fourth bridge drawn toward fermion
machinery the tracker does not hold; the nearest held
ground is the engines of the prover cluster, and the
bridge the synthesis drew to them is priced as shape at
24.2.1. The revision term stands at its third part without
amendment: zero priced where no held claim is re-read,
zero fallen, in a field that stood empty entire. Kept on
the watch: whether four exact Oracle landings are
calibration or a run of luck — the ledger now holds the
points 80, 75, 70 on three axis papers whose falls were
each reasoned before they were scored — and whether the
inside-links under-read is one law (the weave of a cluster
is the debts its concepts owe each other in pairs, and an
abstract shows the concepts, not the debts) or two.

