EVALUATION OF THE CALIBRATION CLAIM (2026-11-14, at
Will's direction; his verdict on the claim, stated
before the test: almost certainly false). The claim
under test: that the skim knows a paper's delta
before the paper is read, and the Oracle measures
the distance from knowing it whole. The record was
re-read whole and every number re-counted from the
log of the quest by script. What stands: the axis
calls, twelve of twelve, four of them calls of
yes — the one part of the claim no flat rule comes
near. What falls: the exact landings. A rule that
reads nothing — no axis, ever; an Index of 0.05,
flat; the band applied as written — lands the
foretold point and range exact on eight of the
twelve scored papers, and on five of the seven of
the strand, missing only the two axis papers of the
strand. The landing is a thing of the band and the
call, not of the count: the misses of the strand
are the largest of the quest, and eleven of the
twelve landings would still have landed had the
Index miss been five times its size. The Index
itself: the mean miss of the skim stands a hair
under the mean of a rule that calls each paper the
mean of those before it; on near ground the two
cannot be told apart, and on far ground the skim is
better and still coarse. The counts run one way
only: ten of twelve under, none over — the count of
the skim is a floor, not a centre. Half the
measured miss is grain: fold the shapes the scores
themselves name as folded, and the miss halves —
the hand that files holds the seal as it files, and
part of what the score measures is that the two
hands are one hand. Verdict of the evaluation: the
claim is false in its headline (the landings) and
unproven in its middle (the Index and the counts,
where the edge of the skim over flat rules is small
and the sample was chosen for standing near held
ground); the axis calls stand. The full record
of the test is kept with the quest
(evaluation-2026-11-14). A sharper test is drafted
and waits on Will's word; nothing on this list
changes until he gives it.

