safekeeping# Concept: Coordination
Source: Paper 001, §§1-3. The second capability factor, and the load-bearing
one in this world-model.

Coordination (y*, per agent; mean z* per firm) = the capabilities that let
agents work better together: shared norms, low communication cost, low
agency friction. Effect: coordination governs the SCALE factor S(m, z*)/m —
how average product changes as firm size m grows. Below the threshold
(z* < 1 in the normalized CES form) average product eventually falls with
size, giving a finite preferred firm size. Coordination, not competence,
sets how big an organization can in a useful way get.

Delta-link 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): Paper 001's coordination runs BETWEEN
agents. Paper 002 exhibits a second axis — coordination ACROSS TIME
within one agent: append-only history coordinates the agent with its past
selves, and the guardian coordinates its one who puts a change forward-self with its
activator-authority (scrolls 1.2.19-1.2.20). The world-model now treats
'coordination' as covering both axes; whether the across-time axis moves
the between-agents gamma is open question Q6 (scroll 3.2.2).
peak
cachespilot
enjoying
elegantly
leisure
appreciating# Concept: Preferred size and the Coasean ceiling
Source: Paper 001, §§2.2, 5.1 (Proposition 1).

Preferred size m*(z*) = the input that gives the largest value over m of average product S(m, z*)/m for the mean of a firm coordination z*. Competence does not enter it: at fixed
composition, better task skills raise output but leave preferred size
unchanged. With human coordination < 1, preferred size is finite — firms
that outgrow it split. This finite preferred size IS the Coasean ceiling in
the model. Proposition 1 bounds almost every active size of the firm between
outer reaches M and M-bar that depend on AI coordination and the human supply
constraint (a firm also cannot be larger than the talent stock allows).
# Concept: Supercooperation threshold and the concentration phase transition
Source: Paper 001, §§2.3, 5.2 (Proposition 3).

Scale returns are governed by gamma, the coordination-driven returns
parameter (gamma = y* in the worked CES cases). Three patterns:
- gamma < 1 (below the line): average product of a large firm vanishes; as the
  market grows (N -> the smallest value) the industry expands by REPLICATING bounded-size
  firms and the market share of any one firm -> 0.
- gamma > 1 (above the line in working together): one large AI firm produces Q at cost of
  order Q^(1/gamma), with 1/gamma < 1 — splitting output across firms loses
  the scale advantage. Equilibrium piles up: a relaxed HHI -> 1, almost
  all output in firm types as large as the market.
- gamma = 1 is the excluded knife-edge.
Crossing the threshold is a PHASE TRANSITION in market structure, driven
by a coordination improvement that may be tiny and benchmark-invisible.
The paper flags the styling: regulation, competing AI providers,
capacity constraints and rigid contracts would likely prevent a literal
one-firm singularity in practice.
# Concept: The size amplifier
Source: Paper 001, §5.2 (Proposition 2).

When AI coordination y*_M rises (still < 1), firm size responds through two
channels: a direct effect (higher mean coordination raises preferred size
at fixed composition, always >= 0) and a composition effect (the firm
re-mixes toward/away from machines, changing mean coordination again).
- If AI coordinates BETTER than humans (y*_H < y*_M < 1): both effects are
  positive — composition AMPLIFIES the direct gain (illustrated machine
  share 0.54 -> 0.83 as firms grow).
- If AI coordinates WORSE (y*_M < y*_H): the composition effect is
  ambiguous and can Soften growth (illustrated machine share 0.38 -> 0.46
  with size gains partly offset).
So the sign of the coordination gap decides whether AI progress compounds
or self-brakes inside the firm.
# Concept: Firm composition patterns — mixed vs separate, human vs AI-only
Source: Paper 001, §5.3 (Proposition 4).

The cost-adjusted best average-product envelope a(lambda, w) — best output
per unit cost at human share lambda — decides who works with whom:
- Machine-only: if AI dominates EVERY point of the human capability hull
  (y_M > all of co(Y_H)) and compute is no costing more than wages (r <= w), all
  firms are machine-only.
- Humans in the loop: if the envelope rises above its machine-only endpoint
  (mosaic intelligence pays) and w <= r, some firms must employ humans.
- Curvature then decides the form: envelope strictly CONVEX in lambda ->
  separate pure-human and pure-AI firms; strictly CONCAVE -> mixed firms,
  all sharing the unique optimal human share.
Same curvature variable returns in displacement dynamics (scroll 1.2.10).
# Concept: Step by step vs sudden displacement
Source: Paper 001, §6.2 (Proposition 5).

Human displacement has two dynamically different forms, selected by the
curvature of the composition envelope a(lambda):
- CONCAVE (mixed-firm regime): displacement is WITHIN-firm. As AI improves,
  firms continuously lower their human share; employment, output and firm
  size move continuously. Full unemployment only when adding a first human
  to an all-machine industry does not pay even at the reservation wage.
- CONVEX (separate-firm regime): displacement is ACROSS-firm. Human-only
  and AI-only firms coexist until a cost inequality flips, then human firms
  are wiped out in jumps — full employment to full unemployment at
  a threshold, wages max{w, r*a(1)/a(0)}.
As AI coordination improves the envelope can itself flip concave -> convex,
so an economy can see step by step erosion first, then a sudden spike.
Task-model form (Proposition 7): for one human type, humans are absent
from every equilibrium exactly when
  max_d (y_d^H / y_d^M) + (y*_H - y*_M) * log(1 + m*(y*_M)) <= w / r
— best relative task productivity PLUS the human coordination advantage
valued at machine-only firm size. A coordination advantage can therefore
cover a task-productivity gap; under constant returns the second
term vanishes and only the task race remains.
# Concept: Wages as bids — shadow value and sorting
Source: Paper 001, §6.1 (Theorem 2), §4.2.

In equilibrium the wage of a worker equals the highest BID for them: firms bid
the marginal of the worker contribution to firm surplus (a bid function of their
full capability profile, evaluated at the size of the firm, mix and scale).
Consequences: (a) pay can rise without the worker changing at all, when
complementary teammates or scale raise their marginal contribution
(coders' pay grew to near three times the size when writers arrived); (b) standalone-weak
coordinator profiles can be among the highest paid, because value of the coordination
scales with the whole team; (c) workers SORT — each type is won by the firm
type whose bid for their particular jagged profile is highest, so sorting
patterns reveal relative strengths vs AI.
Formally (under Assumption 2) the bid is AFFINE in the profile:
b_f(y) = r + beta^f . (y - y_M) — a per-dimension price beta_d for each
task skill and beta_* for coordination. the bid of Every firm plane passes
through the common AI anchor (y_M, r): a worker identical to the AI is
worth exactly the rental of one copy cost. A profile lying in the winning of no firm region falls back to the outside option.
# Concept: Technology effect vs re-organization effect (wage decomposition)
Source: Paper 001, §6.3 (Proposition 6).

Along a smooth path of AI improvement, a wage change breaks into parts as:
  the change in the wage as AI improves = technology effect + re-organization effect.
Technology effect: AI capability changes, holding of the firm that uses it, the
size, machine share and human mix FIXED — the value of the human inside the
existing firm. Re-organization effect: the own of the firm adjustments — change
in size (dm), machine share (ds), and human skill mix — each
times the sensitivity of the bid of the firm to that margin.
Key point: equilibrium maximizes total surplus, not wages, so the envelope
theorem does NOT cancel the re-organization terms — they are first-order and
can REVERSE the technology effect. Worked illustration: as firms grow
(m = 2 -> 118) a human wage of the coordinator first RISES with re-organization,
then falls; holding firm composition fixed it would have fallen
in one direction only. Any wage forecast that holds organizations fixed is, in
this model, simply wrong-signed in some patterns.
# Concept: AI capability patterns
Source: Paper 001, §§2.3 (for showing map), 4.3 (formal definitions).

For showing map (§2.3), in (task competence, coordination) space:
- Minimal AGI: matches/exceeds AVERAGE standalone human competence and
  coordination, beats no best-human outright, below the coordination
  threshold.
- Beyond the best single profile: exceeds every human on both tasks, coordination at or
  below threshold.
- Above the line in working together: crosses the coordination threshold, does not exceed
  the best human on every task.
- ASI: exceeds every human on all tasks AND crosses the threshold.

Formal patterns (§4.3) are stated against the human capability HULL:
- Weak: AI is dominated by the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Minimal AGI: AI lies WITHIN the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Superintelligent: AI dominates the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Beyond the best single profile (formal): y_d^M > max_i y_d^i in every productive domain.
- Above the line in working together (formal): gamma^M = gamma(delta_{y_M}) > 1 — an
  ABSOLUTE property of the scale of the AI tail, NOT a comparison against the
  best human coordinator; the average product of an AI-only firm then grows
  without bound in firm size.
the empirical of the paper read of the present (§7.2): current AI is jagged-
high-competence, below the line-coordination — consistent with leaner
startups, less tall org charts, rising demand for experienced human
managers, and little aggregate employment/market-structure movement.
# Concept: Measurement agenda — what to track instead of benchmark scores
Source: Paper 001, §8 (Conclusion).

If transformation is a coordination event, isolated-task benchmarks are a
misleading gauge of it — in both directions (mosaic human teams can beat a
benchmark-winning model today; a benchmark-invisible coordination gain can
tip an economy tomorrow). the proposed of the paper measurements:
1. Scale curves: how output scales with the number of identical AI copies
   at FIXED compute per copy, alongside isolated competence L — estimate
   competence and coordination separately across model generations.
2. Firm-level scale counted in BOTH workers and rented agentic compute
   (m_f = human rolls + AI instances), and payroll-vs-AI-expenditure shares
   over time.
3. The cross-of a section distribution of firms — AI-only / AI-native vs mixed
   — because within-firm vs across-firm displacement have vastly different
   unemployment dynamics.
4. Employer-employee panels tracking how different skill profiles sort
   across firms — the direct test that separates technology from
   re-organization effects on wages.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): a second, independent reason benchmark
scores give the wrong idea — they measure a model+HARNESS bundle (scroll 1.2.15).
Paper 002 shows the harness term alone is worth 35-61% of held-out cost
at fixed weights, and that the bundle can keep improving DURING the
measurement task itself. Any competence estimate that does not fix and
report the harness is mixed up.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 003): a worked example of measuring at the
right level. StoryScope measures DISCOURSE-level choices (scroll 1.2.24)
rather than output scores or surface style — and the level matters:
surface style signals are transient (a model revision or a light edit
wipes out them; fine-tuning to mimic human style drops style-based
detection from 97% to 3%, as cited therein), while structural choices
survive style editing (93.9% macro-F1 after span-level rewriting)
because changing them requires rewriting the construction of the artifact.
Measurement lesson for this agenda: prefer variables that are expensive
for the measured system to fake — the same property Paper 001 wants
from scale curves and sorting panels, and Paper 002 gets from
cost-annotated histories (scroll 1.2.20).

Revision 2026-11-07 (Paper 006): a third way a score leads its reader wrong,
from the specification side. A pass rate is won under a
structure the agent settled on and no one measuring can see:
Paper 006 proves the same test set can leave nearly every
candidate standing under one structure and settle the question
under another (1.2.58), and that quality axes no test can see
(1.2.60) move with the type of specification alone — code
erodes faster under behavioral specification than under a
standing skeleton (1.2.64), at equal pass rates. A score that
does not fix or report the structure measures a bundle — task,
tests, and a hidden structure — just as the Paper 002 revision
showed it measures a bundle of model and harness. Add to the
agenda: report the structure the score was won under, and at
least one measure on an axis tests are blind to.
# Concept: Harness and harness policy
Source: Paper 002, §§1-2. NEW in Paper 002; no counterpart in Paper 001.

Harness: the scaffolding around a language model that (1) assembles the input of the model and (2) executes and observes its actions in an environment
(usually a computing sandbox). Harness POLICY: every programmable rule
governing how the agent uses model, state and environment — tool
implementations and selection, prompting, context assembly and
pressing together, memory and skill indexing and retrieval, service adapters,
task scheduling — AND the strategy for deciding when and how to attempt
further improvements. Policy is encoded in harness code, prompts,
configuration and reusable skills. Paper 001's agents had capability
profiles but no harness layer; this scroll adds the missing layer
between weights and outcomes.
# Concept: Runtime self-improvement — the collapsed optimization loop
Source: Paper 002, §§1, 3.1. NEW in Paper 002.

Classical setup (adaptive control): an external OPTIMIZER observes
execution and updates the controller (harness policy) between runs —
Meta-Harness, GEPA, AlphaEvolve-style loops. It needs representative
benchmark tasks (75% of production teams in one cited study evaluate
without any), pays full task runs per candidate (untenable as
horizons stretch to days/weeks), benchmarks go stale, and the resulting
policy stays FIXED during the work where its gaps appear.
Collapsed loop: the running agent itself chooses both task actions and
policy revisions through the same action interface, during the task,
grounded in the environment it actually works in. Weights never change.
Each successor policy retains the ability to revise itself further,
including revising its improvement strategy — improvement of the
part that improves is in scope, not a meta-level add-on.
# Concept: Optimizer symmetry and being written in the same stuff it works on (enabling properties)
Source: Paper 002, §3.1. NEW in Paper 002.

Two properties of harnesses make the collapsed loop (scroll 1.2.16)
possible where traditional adaptive control keeps the optimizer outside:
- Optimizer symmetry: a policy update can be REALIZED as a sequence of
  ordinary task actions — inspect source, edit files, run tests, request
  activation — using tools the agent already has. Revision is not a
  privileged operation; it is work.
- Being written in the same stuff it works on: the policy being optimized is code, represented as files
  inside the own of the optimizer environment. The object of optimization is
  available as data to the optimizer.
Together: the proposal of the optimizer logic can move inside the harness
because nothing about proposing a revision requires leaving the action
domain or the representation the agent already works on.
# Concept: Policy/mechanism partition (protected mechanism layer)
Source: Paper 002, §§1, 4.1, after Exokernel (Engler et al. 1995). NEW.

The architectural thing needed first for safe self-editing: partition the
harness into (a) a minimal PROTECTED MECHANISM layer — only what the
safety properties need, exposed at the lowest level of abstraction —
and (b) an editable POLICY layer (the 'the worker part') holding everything
else. Protected: canonical execution history (append-only, queryable,
cost-annotated), versioned artifact store (memory, skills, snapshots),
credential storage, sandbox API, and the guardian (scroll 1.2.19).
Editable: all tools, context assembly/pressing together, prompting, memory
indexing/retrieval, adapters, scheduling — and the improvement policy.
Design pressure runs one way: shrink the protected set to the minimum,
maximize what the agent may rewrite. Two failure modes motivate it:
fate-sharing (a faulty edit turns off the agent AND its repair logic) and
state erasure (an edit destroys the experience needed to learn/recover).
# Concept: State and replacement contracts; the guardian; independent rollback
Source: Paper 002, §§3.2, 4.2, App. A. NEW in Paper 002.

State s_t = (d_t durable data, e_t sandbox, pi_t active policy), advanced
only through protected mechanism K. Two contracts:
- Storage contract: execution history is APPEND-ONLY — H(d_t) is a prefix
  of H(d_t+1); records cannot be written over or deleted. Memory and
  artifacts may change current value but must retain prior versions.
- Replacement contract: editing produces an arbitrary candidate pi'
  (possibly crashing); activation requires a GUARDIAN G(s_t, pi') in
  {0,1} that lies OUTSIDE the policy it judges. Default guardian checks
  only build success; richer guardians (LLM-judge inspection, forked-
  environment trials, human approval) are future work in the paper.
Rollback is INDEPENDENT across the three state components (policy,
durable state, sandbox), and history survives rollback — evidence of
failed attempts stays available. Scope limit (App. A): the preservation
proof is conditional on all protected transitions being mediated by K,
and external effects in the world are not that can be run backward by any rollback.
Protection guarantees preservation and recovery authority — explicitly
NOT improved behaviour or continued progress.
# Concept: Internal legibility (cost-annotated history as reward signal)
Source: Paper 002, §§4.3, 7. NEW in Paper 002.

A self-improving agent needs to see itself. Exo exposes execution history
for retrieval and analysis with model-message events annotated by cost,
token counts and cached-token share, plus a reporting tool (spending,
cached input share, repeated tool calls, context composition). Policy
code decides what enters the prompt, but the full annotated record
remains open to looking inside even when compacted out of context. The paper credits
this as a key reason the system works: cost/latency/cache annotations
give DENSE reward signals about efficiency DURING the task, long before
any completion signal arrives. Legibility is also the audit surface:
adaptations are explicit code diffs that operators can inspect, test,
and give the right to — contra continual learning hidden in weights.
# Concept: Specialization cost and spreading of the cost by reuse
Source: Paper 002, §§5.2-5.4. NEW in Paper 002.

Runtime learning has an investment profile: an early SPECIALIZATION COST
(building tools, revising context policy — in the combat run Exo had
spent ~$0.31 at 10 min vs Codex $0.12 / Pi $0.16) buys a lower spend rate
later; cumulative cost crossing points came at 25 min (vs Pi) and 86 min (vs
Codex). Whether the investment pays is governed by REUSE:
- Within-task reuse (long repetitive tasks): pays before completion.
- Across-task transfer (scroll 1.2.23): pays again on repeated objectives.
- Low-reuse work: does NOT pay. On one-shot quests (each completed once,
  distinct steps/items/locations) Exo averaged $0.28 per quest point vs
  Codex $0.24 and Pi $0.21 — the self-improving harness was the EXPENSIVE
  option. Negative result, recorded at full weight: self-editing is a
  bet on recurrence.
# Concept: Intelligence floor for self-improvement
Source: Paper 002, §5.4 ('Limits of adaptation'). NEW in Paper 002.

Access to self-editing helps only if the underlying model can discover
and reuse effective changes. Control experiment: Exo on AppWorld with
gpt-5-mini — three seeds made limited harness edits and did not reuse
their created tools on held-out tasks; total cost and pass rates were
statistically a wash ($6.63 / 59% vs control $6.73 / 55%). The authors'
statement: 'there is an intelligence floor necessary to reap the
benefits of Of Exo architecture.' The architecture is an amplifier with a
threshold, not a substitute for capability — compare Paper 001's
coordination THRESHOLD (scroll 1.2.7): in both papers the interesting
action is at a critical value of the variable the paper is about.
# Concept: Transfer of learned policy (specialization as portable artifact)
Source: Paper 002, §§5.3-5.4. NEW in Paper 002.

Because adaptations are code, they PERSIST and TRAVEL: a harness
specialized on one run can be carried to a fresh instance of the task
(fresh game account, retained agent state) and pays immediately — the
transfer results are the largest in the paper (45-82% below Codex/Pi;
combat $1.92 vs $6.87/$10.89). Two added conditions from the paper itself:
(1) learned procedures DIFFER across seeds — seeds 1-2 built broadly
reusable tools, seed 3 built many scenario-specific tools and spent more
in learning, yet all three won on held-out cost; specialization does not
converge to a single implementation. (2) Transfer shown is to REPEATED
objectives under changed conditions, not to novel task families.
Relation to Paper 001 scroll 1.2.5: a learned harness is reproducible
like AI expertise generally — but unlike a weight profile, it is also
open to looking inside, that can be forked and that can be owned as an artifact.

Delta-link 2026-11-06 (Paper 004): a SECOND transfer mechanism, unlike
the artifact how well a thing carries above. Paper 004 transfers capability to
unseen tasks with no artifact carried over: a shared pool of expert
networks persists, and a gating network routes each new input to the
experts trained on similar SUBSTRUCTURES (scroll 1.2.32). What travels
is not a learned policy but a routing decision. The two mechanisms make
different bets: artifact transfer assumes the whole specialization is
reusable; substructure routing assumes only parts are, and pays a
gating network to find out which.
# Concept: Discourse-level narrative features vs surface style
Source: Paper 003, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 003.

A generated artifact can be measured at (at least) two levels. SURFACE
STYLE: lexical and syntactic choices — word choice, sentence rhythm,
signature phrases (em-dashes, 'delve'). DISCOURSE / NARRATIVE level:
structural decisions about how the artifact is constructed — plot
architecture, character agency, temporal ordering, how and when
information is revealed, how explicitly themes are stated.

Paper 003's finding: the discourse level carries nearly all the
who wrote a thing signal of the surface level (narrative-only features reach
93.2% macro-F1 on human-vs-AI detection, >=97% of the combined model)
while being far more DURABLE: style cues are erased by model revisions
or light edits, but narrative choices survive span-level style rewriting
(93.9% after editing) because changing them means restructuring the
work, not putting in new words it. The two levels also fail differently: style
identifies THAT a model wrote something; discourse features say HOW
the source constructs — that can be read with sense, per-decision.
# Concept: Narrative space, AI convergence, human dispersion
Source: Paper 003, §§4-5. NEW in Paper 003.

Embed each story as a vector in an induced narrative feature space
(scroll 1.2.28) and the population geometry becomes measurable:
- The five AI sources (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, GPT, Kimi) cluster in
  one shared region; the six most-confused attribution pairs are all
  AI<->AI.
- Human stories occupy a DISTINCT region (closest human-AI centroid
  pair farther than the most distant AI-AI pair) and are more DISPERSED
  within it: mean distance to own centroid 33.2 vs 27.4 for AI (+22%),
  median 10-NN radius 1.13x larger.
Convergence here is a population property, not a per-model defect: five
independently trained systems from five labs make systematically
SIMILAR narrative choices (thematic over-being stated out loud, causal being tidy,
temporal straight line behavior, bodily/sensory emotion rendering, vague a pointing at other works without naming them),
while human choices spread wider on the same axes. Relation to scroll
1.2.4 recorded in the 2026 of that scroll-11-06 revision.
# Concept: Core features vs fingerprint features
Source: Paper 003, §§2.2, 4-5. NEW in Paper 003.

A discriminating feature space splits by SCOPE of its signal:
- CORE features (30 of 304): stable, important markers of the
  human-vs-AI boundary that hold across ALL five AI models — signed
  human-leaning or AI-leaning (e.g. thematic being stated out loud and
  of the narrator stating the moral, AI-leaning; chronological discontinuity and
  explicit named between texts reference, human-leaning). 30 core
  features alone reach 84.8% macro-F1 (~91% of the full narrative model).
- FINGERPRINT features (75): signal concentrated in ONE source — the
  odd habits that make six-way attribution possible (68.4% macro-F1
  narrative-only). As reported: Claude = flat event escalation, uniform
  voice, ending chapters, full of respect toward convention; GPT = gossip/rumor as
  plot mechanism, retrospective framing, subverted expectations;
  DeepSeek = front-loaded context; Gemini = most tidy endings, extended
  final endings, most bleak settings; Kimi = fewest fingerprints, sits at
  the generic center of the AI distribution.
General shape worth keeping: a class boundary usually lives in a compact
core; identity within the class lives in a longer tail of fingerprints.
# Concept: Rarity as an originality proxy
Source: Paper 003, §§1, 4-5, App. H. NEW in Paper 003.

Originality is turned into a working measure as STATISTICAL RARITY in a feature space
of decisions: a work is 'rare' by its mean distance to its 25 nearest
neighbors among comparable works (here, stories written to the same
prompts). The legal/theoretical anchor the paper cites: minimal of the copyright-originality standard and Torrance's notion of originality as
being uncommon of response.

Measurements (as reported): human stories' mean rarity percentile 0.71
vs AI 0.49 (Cohen's d = 0.83); humans hold 24.7% of the top-10% rarest
stories vs 7.1% AI (top 1%: 3.0% vs 0.6%); at the prompt level the human
version is the rarest of the six 57.8% of the time (chance: 16.7%).

Watch the limits of the proxy: rarity is relative to the corpus and feature
space chosen; it measures being uncommon of choices, not their quality or
intent; and any rarity metric that becomes a target invites gaming
(Goodhart exposure — see open question Q11).
# Concept: Induced that can be read with sense feature space (comparative induction on a parallel corpus)
Source: Paper 003, §§2, 2.1-2.2. NEW in Paper 003. A METHOD concept —
the first in this model that is about how to build a measuring instrument.

Recipe, as executed:
1. PARALLEL CORPUS: hold the task constant and vary the source —
   10,272 prompts reverse-engineered from human stories, each written
   by the human and five LLMs: 61,608 stories, ~5,000 words each.
   Same-prompt comparison is what makes 'choice' separable from 'topic'.
2. STRUCTURED TEMPLATES: convert each artifact into a structured
   representation along a theory-grounded taxonomy (10 of NarraBench's
   12 narrative dimensions), forcing downstream reasoning over content,
   not surface.
3. CROSS-SOURCE COMPARISON: an LLM judge compares the six versions of
   each prompt in a 600-story discovery pool and records where sources
   systematically diverge (~2.7M tokens compressed to ~686K).
4. FEATURE DISCOVERY + Merging of copies: per-dimension expert prompts propose
   closed-form questions (categorical / ordered / scale / binary /
   multi-select); 408 candidates joined together over 3 runs, embedding-clustered
   (cosine >= 0.85) down to 304 features.
5. ASSIGNMENT AT SCALE: a cheaper model scores every story on every
   feature (Gemini 3 Flash; being the same on a second run Krippendorff alpha = 0.90;
   human validation Cohen's kappa = 0.84 on 240 items).
6. ROLE ASSIGNMENT: bootstrap SHAP stability analysis separates core
   from fingerprint features (scroll 1.2.26).
Total corpus turning into features cost as reported: $4.4k. The transferable
idea: when the interesting variable is a latent CHOICE, don't annotate
it directly — build a mirrored corpus, compress comparisons, and let
the feature space be INDUCED from observed divergence, then validate.
# Concept: Mixture of experts — gating as learned routing over specialists
Source: Paper 004, §§1, 3. NEW in Paper 004 (the architecture is from
Jacobs et al. 1991 and Shazeer et al. 2017; the concept as used here is
Paper 004's deployment of it).

Replace a monolithic network block with several parallel EXPERT
networks plus a GATING network. For each input, the gate computes
weights over the experts (softmax; the weights read as attention), and
the output is the weighted sum of expert outputs — DENSE routing in
Paper 004 (all experts contribute; sparse top-k routing trades
interpretability and stability for compute). Because the gate keys on
the representation of the input, similar inputs are routed to similar
experts, and experts specialize on the regions of representation space
they are routed: specialization is an EMERGENT outcome of routing plus
training, not an assignment. Requirements noted by the paper: experts
sharing a mixture must share input/output shapes (in their graph network, MoE is
applied at the prediction Prediction network, not inside message passing, so experts
share structure); a LINEAR gate worked best in their pre-exploration.

Revision (Paper 007, 2026-11-07): routing has a second continent.
In attention, what is routed over is not specialists but the keys
of the cache, and the routed set grows with the context — so the
choosing itself becomes the cost, and ZHA answers by giving the
choosing an index of its own (1.2.67): selection stacked on
selection. The difference is recorded, not smoothed over: an
expert is a piece of the model, picked per token; a key is a
piece of the past, picked per query; the gate here is trained
with the model, while the reference queries of the tree are
distilled afterward into a frozen one (1.2.69). What carries
across is the shape — a cheap choosing step in front of expensive
capacity — and the failure mode that comes when the cost of the
choosing itself is ignored.
# Concept: Hierarchical (multi-granularity) mixture of experts
Source: Paper 004, §§1, 3-4. NEW in Paper 004.

One mixture is not enough when the object has natural structure at
several scales. Paper 004's object is the control of a program/data-flow
graph, with three natural granularities: NODE (instructions/variables),
BASIC BLOCK (pseudo nodes for code blocks — the level pragma instructions modify),
and GRAPH (the whole kernel). A LOW-LEVEL MoE is built per granularity
(node MoE on post-Prediction network node representations, block MoE on pseudo nodes,
graph MoE on the gathered graph representation). A HIGH-LEVEL MoE then
gates over the three granularity-models' outputs — a mixture of
mixtures, whose gate reads the hidden representation (design 2, better
than gating on raw input features). Two findings carry the concept:
(a) which granularity matters is INPUT-DEPENDENT — different kernels
favor different granularities, and the high-level gate learns to assign
the highest weight to the empirically best granularity per kernel;
(b) stacking the three granularities' MoE models inside ONE flat model is
WORSE than even a single-granularity model (Table 4) — the hierarchy
itself, keeping the mixture of each scale separate and adding up late, is
what trains in a stable way and generalizes. Late aggregation over separately
specialized scales beats early fusion.
# Concept: Expert polarization (mode collapse of a mixture) and its steps taken against it
Source: Paper 004, §§3-4. NEW in Paper 004.

The intrinsic failure mode of a trained mixture: random initialization
gives some experts a head start; the gate learns to weight better
experts higher; better-weighted experts get more training; the gap
compounds until the gate gives nearly everything to ONE expert and
the rest starve — POLARIZATION. The mixture collapses to a single
network, carrying dead capacity, and the generalization the mixture was
built for disappears. Paper 004 shows the collapse concretely at the
high level: naive joint training gives nearly all weight to the
graph-MoE model.
Steps taken against it, both needed (taking one part away to test it Table 6: removing either costs
loss):
1. LOAD-BALANCING PRESSURE: a balance penalty on the coefficient of
   variation of total weights assigned per expert (weight 5e-3 here),
   punishing one sided use.
2. TWO-STAGE TRAINING: stage 1 (warm up) trains each expert model
   SEPARATELY, so every expert is competent before any gating exists;
   stage 2 alternates joint and separate training; the high-level gate
   is initialized to uniform weights. Polarization is a rich-get-richer
   dynamic — the fix is to make experts viable BEFORE competition and
   keep rehearsing them separately during it.
General shape: any routed-specialist system has a collapse gradient;
diversity of the specialist pool is a maintained state, not a default
(cf. Paper 003's convergence at population level, scroll 1.2.25).
# Concept: Domain generalization via shared substructures (routing, not invariance)
Source: Paper 004, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 004.

Problem: a model trained on some domains (here: each program kernel is
a domain) must perform on UNSEEN domains whose difference from the
training domains is large — and INFORMATIVE (the differences carry
signal for the prediction, so erasing them is harmful). The standard
domain-generalization playbook (adversarial alignment, invariant
representations) is therefore REJECTED by the paper: do not force the
domains into one distribution.
The alternative: assume domains share SUBSTRUCTURES even when they
differ wholesale. Learn specialists on substructures (MoE experts over
nodes/blocks/graph patterns) and a router (scroll 1.2.29); a new domain
is handled by composing the specialists its substructures resemble.
Generalization becomes a retrieval-and-composition problem rather than
an invariance problem. In Paper 004 this is what lets a predictor
trained on 42 benchmark kernels transfer to unseen and more complex
kernels with only 50 fine-tuning points per target kernel.
Evaluation protocol worth keeping: leave-one-domain-out — train on
source kernels, test on held-out target kernels; report both offline
prediction error (total mean square error over objectives) and online utility (the
quality of designs the search of the predictor actually finds).
# Concept: Surrogate prediction for expensive design spaces
Source: Paper 004, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 004. The task pattern the machinery of the paper serves.

When evaluating one candidate design costs minutes-to-hours (an high level synthesis
run), exhaustive or even generous search is impossible — so train a
SURROGATE that predicts the expensive quantity (latency, resource use,
validity) from the representation of the design, search cheaply in
prediction space (here: DFS over up to 75,000 pragma designs), and
spend the real evaluation budget only on the predicted top-M (here:
top 10) for verification. the errors of the surrogate are therefore not
uniform in cost: what matters is ranking quality near the optimum and
validity classification at the constraint boundary, which is why the
paper reports ONLINE speedup (best verified design vs best in dataset)
alongside offline mean square error. The binding constraint that shapes everything
else in the paper: labels are scarce BECAUSE they are expensive, so
the surrogate must generalize from few, costly examples (scroll 1.2.32).
# Concept: Fake quadrics — same rational invariants, different manifold
Source: Paper 005, §1. NEW in Paper 005; first concept of the topology
annex (cluster E). No relation to Papers 001-004 beyond meta-level.

A QUADRIC here is S^2 x S^2 (the product of two spheres, a closed
symplectic 4-manifold). A FAKE QUADRIC is a closed symplectic
4-manifold with the SAME rational cohomology ring as S^2 x S^2 —
rationally indistinguishable from it — that is nevertheless not
diffeomorphic (not even homeomorphic) to it: the difference lives in
more fine invariants, here finite nontrivial first homology where the
genuine quadric has H_1 = 0. Akhmedov's families (built in his earlier
work with Baldridge [ref 6 in the paper] by symplectic sums of ruled
surfaces along cyclic multisections, scroll 1.2.35) give INFINITE
families of fake quadrics, distinguished from each other by the order
of the finite group H_1. No complex structure is assumed or claimed:
'fake' is a statement about invariants, not about almost-complex
geometry. The general lesson the concept carries: the identity of an object
is relative to the invariant granularity you measure at — rational
equality is not equality (see also scroll 1.2.38).

REVISION, Paper 008 (2026-11-09): the genuine quadric of this
scroll — S^2 x S^2 — is Q^2 in the flag family of Paper 008
(1.2.102), and it is the site of the first fall of the strong
Arnold conjecture: the map of Jiao on S^2 x S^2 has two fixed points
where the conjecture asked for at least Crit = 3 (Paper 008,
its ref 29). The manifold this scroll holds up as the genuine
article turns out to carry, for a different count, the case
that ended a sixty-year claim. Its own Arnold number is 2,
known whole (1.2.103). Nothing in the claim of this scroll
changes: the fake quadrics still differ from it in whole
numbers, exactly as stated.
# Concept: Cyclic (braided) multisection of a ruled surface
Source: Paper 005, §§1-2, Fig. 1. NEW in Paper 005.

Setting: Sigma_g x S^2, the product of a genus-g surface (g >= 2) with
a sphere — a RULED surface. Fix a map u : Sigma_g -> S^1 (the middle of the sphere circle) whose induced map on pi_1 is onto every target. The CYCLIC
MULTISECTION of degree p >= 2 is the surface
  F_{g,p} = { (x, z) in Sigma_g x S^1 : z^p = u(x) }.
Properties (as stated in the paper): the projection F_{g,p} -> Sigma_g
is a connected, With no merging of sheets p-fold cover (being connected uses
being onto of u_* mod p); F_{g,p} is symplectic for the product
form, has homology class p[S] (S = Sigma_g x {pt}), self-intersection
zero, and genus G = 1 + p(g-1). 'BRAIDED': travelling around a base
loop in a cycle cycles the p sheets (the Figure-1 model, g=2 p=3,
shows the permutation 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1 around a loop of the base) —
the sheets are braided together rather than p separate copies, which
is exactly what makes the cover connected. The name of the construction of the paper: sums of such braided pieces across a sphere seam, the
ring shaped joins carrying no extra braid at the seam itself.
# Concept: Multisection complement and its handlebody decomposition
Source: Paper 005, §§1-2, App. A, Theorem 1.1/2.2. NEW in Paper 005.

The COMPLEMENT M_{g,p} = (Sigma_g x S^2) minus an open tubular
neighborhood of F_{g,p} is the working piece from which fake quadrics
are glued (scroll 1.2.37). Structurally it is a bundle over Sigma_g
with fiber P_p, a sphere with p open disks removed. Paper 005's first
contribution is EXPLICIT HANDLEBODY Recipes in parts of the three
complements the sums need, with 2-handle framings induced by the
product coordinates — handle counts (h_0, h_1, h_2, h_3):
  M_{2,3}: (2, 11, 14, 3)    M_{3,2}: (2, 14, 14, 2)
  M_{2,4}: (2, 12, 18, 4)
A handlebody decomposition turns the complement from an existence
statement into a computable object: its handle chain complex yields
integral homology directly (scroll 1.2.38), its attaching maps are the
data the gluing must match, and (in the announced follow-up with
Akbulut and Yildiz) the closed sums' full diagrams are to be assembled
from these pieces. Proof detail certified in App. D: the pole
projections used to read off framings.
# Concept: Symplectic sum along a surface, and gluing data as real data
Source: Paper 005, §§1, 3-4, Theorem 1.1, App. B. NEW in Paper 005.

The SYMPLECTIC SUM: take two symplectic 4-manifolds containing the
same surface with opposite normal bundles, remove tubular
neighborhoods of the surface, and glue the resulting boundaries by an
orientation-reversing diffeomorphism that flips the normal circle:
phi_m(w, e^{i theta}) = (f_m(w), e^{-i theta}) for w in the genus-G
gluing surface. Gompf's operation; here the pieces are multisection
complements (1.2.36) and the sums are Akhmedov's fake quadrics
(1.2.34). Degree pairs with Euler characteristic 4 and signature 0
from the prior classification: (2,3), (2,4), (3,3); Paper 005 works
the (3,3) and (2,4) families in product ruled surfaces.
The conceptual payload is about DATA vs SUMMARY: Akhmedov-Yeung had
computed the gluing MATRICES on H_1 that the sums require. Paper 005
insists — and this is stated verbatim in its introduction — that 'the
matrices alone do not determine the smooth sums': the actual gluing
requires MARKED SURFACE DIFFEOMORPHISMS f_m realizing those matrices,
with specified boundary choices of plain coordinates (here: no surface-dependent
normal-circle twist). Same matrix, different marking, potentially
different manifold. The invariant summary is a shadow of the gluing
data, not a substitute for it.
# Concept: Integral homology and torsion as the distinguishing invariants
Source: Paper 005, §§2.3, 3.3, 4, Cors 2.4/5.3, App. C. NEW in
Paper 005.

Where Paper 005's computations land. For the complement (Theorem 2.3):
  H_2(M_{g,p}; Z) ~= Z^{1 + 2(g-1)(p-1)} ⊕ Z/p,
and the TORSION subgroup Z/p is generated geometrically (Cor 2.4) by
the difference of the two pole sections, [S_S] - [S_N] — a class with
a picture, not just a chain-complex artifact. For the closed sums
(Theorem 1.1, the integral gluing calculation of §3.3 carried out in §4):
  H_1(Z_m^{3,3}) ~= (Z/3)^2 ⊕ Z/(3m),   H_1(Z_m^{2,4}) ~= (Z/2)^2 ⊕ Z/(4m)
for every twist parameter m >= 1 — recovering, by explicit geometric
realization, the formulas Akhmedov-Yeung had obtained from
Mayer-Vietoris presentations. Full integral homology of the sums
(Cor 5.3): H_0 = H_4 = Z, H_1 = T (the finite group above), H_2 ~=
Z^2 ⊕ T, H_3 = 0; Euler characteristic 4, signature 0 throughout.
The distinguishing logic: rationally all these manifolds are S^2 x S^2
(H_1 vanishes over Q); In whole numbers they are two by two separated by
|H_1| = 27m (in that order 16m) — an infinite family read off one torsion
order. Torsion — the part of homology that the move to fractions deletes —
is precisely where the identity of these manifolds lives.
# Concept: Aspherical sums and automatic symplectic being minimal
Source: Paper 005, §5, Prop 5.1, Cor 5.2. NEW in Paper 005.

Being aspherical: a space is aspherical if its universal cover is
contractible (all higher homotopy groups vanish). Prop 5.1: gluing
two multisection complements by ANY orientation-reversing boundary
diffeomorphism yields an aspherical closed manifold with infinite
fundamental group. Proof shape: each complement has an aspherical
cyclic cover (Sigma_G x P_p, both factors aspherical) with one to one
boundary pi_1, so pi_1 of the sum is an AMALGAMATED PRODUCT over
pi_1(Sigma_G) x Z; the universal cover is then a tree of contractible
pieces over the Bass-Serre tree, and a sphere-mapping argument over
finite subtrees kills all higher homotopy.
Being minimal, for free (Cor 5.2): a symplectic manifold is minimal if
it contains no embedded sphere of self-intersection -1 (no
exceptional sphere to blow down). In an aspherical manifold every
sphere map is shrunk to nothing, so every embedded sphere has square 0 —
a (-1)-sphere cannot exist. Aspherical therefore implies
symplectically minimal in one line. The fake quadrics of Theorem 1.1 are
thus aspherical, minimal, rationally S^2 x S^2, and in whole numbers an
infinite family — all four properties from one construction.
# Concept: Propositions as types — proofs are programs
Source: Study S001, Ch.1 and Ch.4. NEW in Study S001.

A proposition is a type; a proof of it is a term of that type. 't has
type A' and 't proves A' are one claim read two ways (the Curry-Howard
correspondence). Saying 'for all' is a function type whose result may
depend on the input (the Pi type); saying 'if, then' is a plain
function type. A logic is then a reading of a type system: the rules
of the logic and the typing rules pair off, rule for rule (the book
proves the pairing in full, in its Appendix A). What this buys:
checking a proof is the same job as type checking a program, so one
small checker serves both.
# Concept: The proof kernel — one small trusted checker
Source: Study S001, Ch.1, Ch.5, Ch.10, Appendix E. NEW in Study S001.

The kernel is the part of a proof system that checks finished proof
terms against the typing rules. Everything else — tactics, notation,
automation, even a language model proposing steps — only PRODUCES
terms; if the kernel is right, a bug anywhere else can fail to prove
a true thing but can never make the system accept a false one. So
the whole trust of the system is priced into one small part, and the
part is kept small enough to read: in MiniLean4 the kernel is 1,398
lines of Rust with no outside code at all (measured from the open
code, 2026-11-06). Kinship: the guardian of Paper 002 (1.2.19) plays
this role for a running system — a small checker at the gate, all
else untrusted. The difference: the guardian guards state changes;
the kernel guards claims.
# Concept: Typing rules and the judgment
Source: Study S001, Ch.3 and Ch.5. NEW in Study S001.

A judgment is a claim of the form: under this context (an ordered
list of named givens, each with its type), this term has this type.
Eight rules define every judgment: two for contexts (the empty
context; add a name whose type checks), and six for terms (a universe
lives in the next universe; a name has its given type; a Pi type
lands where the imax function puts it (1.2.46); a lambda has the Pi
type of its body; an application puts the input into the result
type in place of the bound name; and conversion — a term may be
read at any type equal to its own by computation (1.2.43)). Seven
of the rules are fixed by the shape of the term; conversion is not,
so the checking algorithm splits in two — Infer computes a type by
shape, Check compares — and conversion is used in exactly one
place. That split is what makes the rules a program.
# Concept: The two equalities — by computation, and as a claim
Source: Study S001, Ch.9 and Ch.12. NEW in Study S001.

Two things are equal in two unlike ways. The first way is by
computation: their terms compute to the same still form (1.3.75) —
the checker decides it by running the computation steps (beta: a
function applied steps into its body with the input put in; delta:
a defined name opens up; iota: a recursor (1.2.44) runs on a built
value). The second way is as a claim: 'a equals b' is itself a
type, Eq, and a proof of it is a term that may need real work
(induction, 1.2.44). Eq is not built in: it is an inductive type
like any other, with one way to build it (refl, when the two sides
are already the same by computation) — and because it has that
shape, its recursor carries a fact about a to a fact about b:
transport, which is how rewriting by an equation works. The example
of the book: n + 0 = n holds by computation (the definition of +
runs on its second input); 0 + n = n does not, and needs induction.
# Concept: Inductive types and recursors
Source: Study S001, Ch.7 and Ch.8. NEW in Study S001.

An inductive type is declared by its ways of being built (its
constructors): Nat is built by zero, or by succ of a Nat. For each
such type the system generates one recursor: a function that takes
a motive (what is to be shown or built for each value), one small
case per constructor, and the value itself — and serves both
recursion (computing a value) and induction (proving a claim) with
the same shape; the case for a built value carries the answers for
its parts (the induction steps for the parts). MiniLean4 gives the
general recipe for any single inductive type (parameters, indices,
many constructors) and the checks a declaration must pass: the
result of each constructor is the type itself, applied to its
parameters in order and to index terms; fields pass strict
positivity (1.2.45); the floors of the fields fit the floor of the
type (1.2.46); and a type living in Sort 0 gives its recursor to
all floors only when it has at most one constructor and every field
is a proof or an index — the rule that lets the recursor of Eq
work everywhere and the recursor of Or only inside propositions.
# Concept: Strict positivity — a type may not eat itself
Source: Study S001, Ch.8. NEW in Study S001.

In a declaration, a field of a constructor may use the type being
declared only in a safe place: either not at all, or as the result
of a function type whose inputs do not use the type. A field that
is a function FROM the type — the type in a negative place — is
refused. The refusal is what keeps out a declaration like 'Bad,
built from a function Bad -> False': that field would let a value
be fed to itself as an input, and the system would prove false
things (the self-eating shape of 1.3.76, one level down). The
check is small (80 lines, measured) because the rule is stated on
the shape of the field types alone.
# Concept: Universes and imax — every type lives one floor up
Source: Study S001, Ch.13. NEW in Study S001.

Sort 0 (propositions) is itself a type, so it must live somewhere:
Sort 0 : Sort 1 : Sort 2, one floor up each time, no floor holding
itself — a single self-holding universe ('Type : Type') proves
every claim (the paradox of Girard, in the form of Hurkens;
1.3.76). A function type takes its floor from the imax function:
if its result is a proposition (floor 0), the whole is a
proposition, no matter what it ranges over — floor 0 is
impredicative, the one floor allowed to range over itself, safe
because proofs there can not be looked inside for data (kin of the
rule of 1.2.44 for when the recursor of a proposition may work at
every floor). Any other result puts the function type at the higher
of the two floors. Real Lean shares this design; MiniLean4 differs
in two stated ways: floors are plain numbers here (one statement
is not made for all floors at once), and proofs are not made equal
by computation.
# Concept: Consistency — the empty claim stays empty
Source: Study S001, Ch.14 and Appendix D. NEW in Study S001.

A logic is consistent when the claim 'for every proposition P, P'
has no closed proof term: a term of that type would prove anything
at all. The book proves it for the core system (the eight rules
with universes, without inductive types) in three moves:
computation keeps types (subject reduction); every well typed term
stops computing (the hard step, taken from the work of others and
named as such); so a closed proof of the empty claim would have to
sit still as a still term, and the shapes a still term can take
leave it nowhere to sit (under the single given P, nothing still
has type P). The honesty of the study is part of the record: the
proof does NOT cover the shipped system with inductive types; for
that the book points to the known results for the calculus of
inductive constructions (Werner 1994). The guarantee of a model is
worth only what its stated scope is — stated here in the canonical
scroll, not in the fine print.
# Concept: Verification by translation — prove the model, and the translator is trusted
Source: Study S002, README and documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022. NEW in Study S002.

To prove a Rust program, Aeneas first carries it into a proof
assistant as a pure functional program (1.2.49, 1.2.50), and the
proofs are proved about THAT. So the claim 'the Rust is right'
stands on a chain, and the study records the links plainly: the
DESIGN of the carrying is proved in papers (the functional
semantics of LLBC, ICFP 2022; LLBC itself proved a sound view of
a heap-and-addresses model of running, and its symbolic semantics
proved a sound borrow checker, ICFP 2024). The CODE that does the
carrying — Charon at the front, Aeneas itself (77,012 lines of
OCaml, measured) — is not verified. The hand-written models of
outside code (the standard library and the rest) are trusted as
written. And the floor is the kernel of the proof assistant the
proofs land in (1.2.41). Verification by translation therefore
moves trust; it does not delete it. Its ledger is this scroll.

Rider (2026-11-13, Paper 017). This scroll names one
pole of machine-checked trust: prove the model, and the
translator is trusted. Paper 017 stands at the other
pole — verification at the machine-code level
(1.2.214): prove the compiled binary itself, owe the
compiler and the translator nothing, and trust instead
a written semantics of the processor and a tested
specification. Each of the two is printed in
full — this scroll is the ledger of the first pole,
and 1.2.222 is the ledger of the second — and the
claim this scroll holds is extended, not replaced:
verification by translation moves trust and does not
delete it; the same is true at the other pole, and
the poles differ in where the moved trust comes to
rest, not in whether it rests somewhere.

Rider (2026-11-13, Study S010). A third resting place is
argued by the position essay of Math, Inc. (Chapter 28): the
agreed semantics of the program's own language (1.2.224) —
written once for the language, executable, validated, and held
in common — under which the program is proved at the level at
which it was written. It is not the pole of this scroll: no
translator carries the program anywhere. And it is not the
pole of 1.2.214: the binary is not the object of the proof.
The claim of this scroll stands and extends once more, unchanged in kind: trust is moved, never deleted — it may rest
in a translator, in a floor of processor semantics and
statements, or in the agreed meaning of a language — and the
poles are told apart by which ledger a reader would rather
read.
# Concept: Backward functions — a borrow handed back is a value plus a put-back
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022. NEW in Study S002.

A shared borrow in Rust becomes, in the translation, the plain
value itself; a mutable borrow becomes the value too — the rule
of Rust itself (1.3.81) is what makes this sound: while a mutable
borrow lives, it is the only road to the value, so the value can
travel in its place. The hard case is a function that RETURNS a
mutable borrow: the one who borrowed goes on editing, and at the
end of the borrow the final value must flow back to the owner.
Aeneas gives such a function two outputs: the borrowed value, and
a BACKWARD function — one more pure function that takes the final
value and hands back the updated owners. (The likeness the
documentation itself gives: a lens — the value is its get, the
backward function its put.) Each function is carried on its own,
from its signature alone; a call is met by the pair, never by
opening the body.
# Concept: LLBC — a borrow calculus with no memory in it
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022 and 2024. NEW in Study S002.

LLBC (Low-Level Borrow Calculus) is the in-between language of
the toolchain: Charon reads the Rust in the form the Rust
compiler holds it (MIR) and writes LLBC; Aeneas reads LLBC. LLBC
is built to be given a PURE meaning: its semantics is value
based — there is no memory in it, no addresses, no arithmetic on
pointers — and ownership based: a borrow is sound when the LOANS
it stands on are honored, checked as a fact about values in the
semantics, not as a rule about lifetimes in the text. The 2024
paper proves the two things that make this more than a hope:
LLBC is a correct view of a low-level model that does have a heap
and addresses, and running LLBC in the symbolic way (1.2.52)
checks borrowing — a program that passes it does not get stuck
when run on the heap model.
# Concept: Failure as a value — ok, fail, div
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo. NEW in Study S002.

Every function the translation makes returns a Result with three
shapes: ok (a value), fail (the run failed: a panic, a sum that
flows over its whole numbers, a read outside a list, a division
by zero), and div (the run never ends). Nothing is hidden under
the floor: to prove a Rust function right you must prove its
translation never answers fail on the inputs you claim, and
either never answers div or is marked as allowed to. A claim
about a function is written as: the function answers ok with x,
and the fact P holds of x — and the checker of the proof
assistant is walked through such claims call by call by a tactic
the toolchain ships for the shape (step).
# Concept: Symbolic execution as a borrow checker
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2024. NEW in Study S002.

The translation is made by RUNNING the LLBC, but on stand-ins:
values are symbols, a borrow is a loan written in a symbolic
state, and where calls or the two arms of a choice meet, the
state is gathered into ABSTRACTIONS — bundles of loans from
which the backward functions (1.2.49) are computed. Loops are the
hard meeting place; the 2024 paper adds the JOIN, a way of
merging two symbolic states that keeps the abstraction and the
borrow facts, proved to keep both — and it is the join that let
loops into the toolchain. In the code that comes out, a loop is
either a small helper function that calls itself, or a loop
combinator fed a body that answers 'go on' or 'done'; that it
stops is proved in the backend, or the function is kept partial
and honest about it.
# Concept: The verified kernel and its untrusted shell, at framework scale
Source: Study S003, the manual, roadmap and design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

h5i-app is a web framework with the split of 1.2.41 built in: the
holder of the application logic is a small KERNEL written in
Rust, and everything around it — the web server, the database,
the decoding of what comes in — is the SHELL, trusted with
nothing. The kernel is carried into Lean 4 (by Charon and Aeneas,
1.2.48, driven as tools under the command of h5i itself) and
properties are proved of it there: that one tenant can not read
the rows of another, that the permission rules hold, that the
state keeps its invariants. For the shapes that come back again
and again — schemas, permission rules, storage — the
specifications and the proofs are made by the tool, not written
by hand. The split is stated in the crate at the center: 'the
core contract between a verified h5i-app kernel and its
untrusted shell'.
# Concept: Receipt before bytes — the record is written before the act
Source: Study S003, the roadmap and browser design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

The red-team half of h5i drives a browser through a proxy that
sits on the road every byte takes, and its rule is: for each
request, a receipt is written FIRST — what was asked, under which
policy, allowed or not — and only then do the bytes move. If the
receipt can not be written, the request does not go. The record
is therefore not a log made after the fact, to be trusted or not;
it is a gate the act itself must pass. The claim of the design,
in its own words, is that tools which drive a browser from
outside 'can not tell you what it reached'; owning the road is
what makes the evidence whole. The pattern is the guardian of
Paper 002 (1.2.19) turned outward: check and record BEFORE the
state changes — here, before the world outside changes.
# Concept: Mutation-tested proofs and the assumption ledger
Source: Study S003, the design files on trust and the app road map. NEW in Study S003.

A proof can be true and still prove nothing you care about, if
its statement was written too weak. h5i answers in two parts.
First, the reach of the verification is governed by written
documents that have the standing of scope authority: what is
proved, what is assumed, what is deliberately not claimed — an
ASSUMPTION LEDGER kept beside the proofs. Second, the proofs are
MUTATION-TESTED: the tool changes the kernel in small wrong ways
(the command is 'h5i app mutate') and checks that the proofs now
FAIL. A proof that still passes a wrong kernel was not holding
the kernel at all. Together: the ledger says what the proofs
mean, and the mutations say whether they bind.
# Concept: Fail-closed boxes for agents
Source: Study S003, the sandbox design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

h5i runs agents — its own browser work, and coding agents such as
Claude and Codex — inside BOXES: layers of holding-in (Landlock,
seccomp, separate name spaces for the system tables, and
containers that need no root) with a policy that is FAIL-CLOSED:
a box that can not be set up as specified does not run at all, in
place of running weaker than specified. The box images ship for
the purpose of holding agents, which makes the box the mirror of
the kernel pattern: the agent is an untrusted producer (1.2.41),
the box is the checked boundary it can not talk its way out of.
# Concept: Skeleton — program structure given as a specification
Source: Paper 006, §§1-2 and App. A. NEW in Paper 006.

A skeleton S is a partial abstraction of a program: its data types,
module boundaries, permitted calls and data flow, and function
declarations with signatures — the implementation bodies left out.
A skeleton may carry more: the notes inside the signatures, local
contracts at each function (1.3.90), and global invariants across
functions. To give a skeleton is to give part of the program — the
structural part — and leave the rest to the agent. In the paper's
model a skeleton fixes the class H_S of programs that share it
(1.2.58), and every other thing the developer gives then works
inside that class. A skeleton is code-like on purpose, and can be
kept, compared and revised like code; the paper's experiments find
that code-like structural specifications beat prose design
documents carrying the same content (results at 10.1.4).
# Concept: How many tests are needed is a property of the structure, not the task
Source: Paper 006, §§2-2.1, Theorem 2.1, App. E.1. NEW in Paper 006.

Let H be the programs an agent can produce, G the ones the
developer would accept, D a set of tests, and the agent's spread
over programs conditioned exactly on what it is given. N (with the
paper's delta for the allowed failure share) is the smallest
number of tests after which the agent lands in G with the asked
probability. Fix a skeleton S and the count inside its class is
N(S). Theorem 2.1: the count is not fixed by the target. There are
two skeletons for one target, each admitting 2^d programs with
distinct behaviors, where the weak skeleton needs 2^d - 1 tests and
the strong one needs d: under the weak skeleton each test rules
out at most one wrong program, under the strong one each test
rules out half of what is wrong. A test set that is exactly right
under the strong skeleton leaves success probability 1/(2^d - d)
under the weak one. Worse, left to choose, the agent's spread over
skeletons is itself shifted by the tests toward the skeleton under
which the tests say least (its eq. 3: success is an average over
the agent's own choice of skeleton, weighted by surviving mass).
So no developer can know whether a test set is enough without
knowing the structure — and structure is the one thing end-to-end
tests cannot say. That is the paper's central claim, and its
reason for giving the structure itself (1.2.57).
# Concept: Interfaces make the middle of a program visible to tests
Source: Paper 006, §2.2, Theorem 2.2, App. E.2. NEW in Paper 006.

An end-to-end test sees only a program's final output. A fault
inside is caught only if the faulty part runs, the fault damages
the state, and the damage survives to the output; later steps can
hide all three. Naming the interfaces of a skeleton names the
middle values (1.3.91), so each part can be tested where it
stands. Theorem 2.2 prices the hiding: a chain of n stages, each
carrying one bit of state, with the zero function as the goal —
there is a prior over the stages' tables under which end-to-end
tests need 2^n tests to pin the program down exactly, while 4n
local tests (the full table of each stage, four cases a stage)
settle every stage. The unknown content is only 4n bits; it is
the hiding, not the size, that costs. The theorem is stated for
exact pinning under a named prior — the paper's appendix says it
does not give a bound for every prior, or for prediction at a
fixed error share — so it prices visibility, not all testing.
# Concept: Some requirements no output test can see
Source: Paper 006, §2.3, Proposition 2.3, App. E.4. NEW in Paper 006.

Programs that agree on every input and every output form a
behavior class. Proposition 2.3: conditioning on any set of
output tests leaves the odds among the members of a behavior
class exactly as they were — every member passes every test the
reference passes, so tests cannot favor the acceptable members
over the rest. If the goal set takes only part of a behavior
class — the rest compute the same function in ways the developer
rejects, too costly to run or too tangled to keep up — then the
number of tests needed is infinite whenever the asked certainty
passes the prior share of the acceptable part. For such
requirements the one lever the developer holds is the skeleton
itself: say the structure, since no test can. The paper's
measured case is code erosion (1.2.64).
# Concept: The cost of pinning a program down splits three ways
Source: Paper 006, §2.4, Theorem 2.4, App. E.3. NEW in Paper 006.

For a skeleton with parts h_1 to h_k, the tests needed for
certainty split into: the cost of describing the skeleton itself;
the local tests that pin the own behavior of each part; and the
end-to-end tests — J_S in the paper — that rule out combinations
of locally good parts whose composition is not acceptable.
Theorem 2.4: the total is the minimum, over choices of the local
goal sets, of (sum of local costs + J_S); and J_S is zero exactly
when every combination of locally acceptable parts is acceptable.
Complete contracts — the asked and promised conditions of each part
strong enough that keeping every promise is enough — drive J_S
to zero, and then the parts are independent: independent agents
can build them side by side with no risk of clash. What makes a
good skeleton, in the paper's terms: short; parts with narrow
interfaces that a few tests or a short contract can pin; and
parts whose separate correctness adds up to whole correctness.
The trade is length: stronger contracts cost more words up front
and buy fewer tests and fewer clashes after.
# Concept: Prompting is not conditioning
Source: Paper 006, §3. NEW in Paper 006.

The counting of 1.2.58 assumes the agent's spread over programs
is cut by exact conditioning on the specification: keep only the
programs that fit it, re-share the rest. A prompted model does
not do that. The spread the prompt in fact produces is not the
conditioned spread, and a program drawn from it can break the
very specification it was given. Under true conditioning, added
specifications can only hold or lower the entropy of the spread in
expectation; under prompting there is no such promise. The
paper's experiments catch the failure in the wild: correct but
overly detailed contracts lower the pass rate, worst on the
small models (10.1.4). This gap is why the paper moves from
giving specifications to checking them: a specification that
can be checked can close the loop by drawing again what fails
(1.2.63); one that cannot be checked is only a hope about the
spread. (The paper grounds the gap in work arguing that
in-context learning is not the conditioning it resembles; that
work is cited here, not taken in.)
# Concept: Checking part by part turns a product into a sum
Source: Paper 006, §3, Propositions 3.1-3.2, App. E.5-E.6. NEW in Paper 006.

Let each part of a skeleton carry a contract, and let alpha_i be
the share of draws of part i that pass its own contract.
Proposition 3.1 (formal checks): drawing whole programs again
until every check passes takes 1/(alpha_1 x ... x alpha_k)
programs in expectation; drawing each part again until its own
check passes takes (1/alpha_1 + ... + 1/alpha_k) part-draws.
Both roads return programs spread as the conditioned spread
over the acceptable set — where the check sits turns a cost
that multiplies into a cost that adds. Proposition 3.2
(informal checks): when the contract is words in the signature
notes and a judge — the agent itself, or a judge model — does
the accepting, a part the judge accepts is truly good with
probability alpha_i / (alpha_i + (1 - alpha_i) x epsilon_i),
where epsilon_i, the false-accept rate, splits into beta_i, the
share of broken contracts the words read literally still
allow, plus the error of the judge on the rest. Beta is a floor
no judge removes; only tighter contract words lower it. A check
earns its keep only when it goes wrong far less often than the agent
fails: epsilon_i much smaller than alpha_i. The paper's
experiments run the informal kind; the formal kind is its
stated future work (10.1.5).
# Concept: Code erosion — complexity mass piling into the hardest functions
Source: Paper 006, §4 (measures) and Fig. 7; the measure itself is
taken from the SCBench work the paper builds on. NEW in Paper 006.

Erosion measures how much of the complexity of a code base sits in
its hardest functions: each function carries weight equal to its
cyclomatic complexity times the square root of its length in
lines, and erosion is the share of the total weight held by
functions whose complexity passes 10. It is a quality axis no
output test can see (1.2.60): two programs pass the same set of
tests while one piles its work into a few tangled functions and
the other spreads it along stated boundaries. Paper 006 measures
erosion as a thing the type of specification moves: signature
specifications lower it against the task-only starting point
(0.66 to 0.56 on SCBench; 0.41 to 0.24 on SDDBench), behavioral
specifications raise it (to as much as 0.73 and 0.43), and the
structural forms cut it by 24.7% against task-only. The paper's
stated mechanism: a standing skeleton records which decisions
are requirements, so an agent adding a feature can rewrite
cleanly in place of piling patches onto accidental behavior.
# Concept: The keys a query needs grow slower than the context
Source: Paper 007, Section II-A, Figure 1, Appendix A. NEW in Paper 007.

Measure, on a trained model reading long text, how many keys hold
almost all of the attention of a query (the top-p set, 1.3.94). The paper
finds the count grows as a power law in the length of the context,
with an exponent under 1, for every share p under the whole: the set
grows, but slower than the context grows, so the longer the text,
the smaller the share of it any one query needs. Longer sequences
are more compressible because the millionth token adds less that is
new than the first tokens did. Two borders are written into the
measurement itself: it is a fact about this data and this model, not
about attention as such — a stream of random tokens would need the
whole cache — and it weakened on the one model tried whose keys
carry no position rotation (Appendix A-C): the way position is
written into the keys is part of the phenomenon.
# Concept: Sparse for one query, whole over all queries — so nothing can be thrown away
Source: Paper 007, Section II-B, Figure 2. NEW in Paper 007.

The same measurement, turned toward the future: take the keys one
query needs (1.2.65), then the keys any of the next W queries will
need, and count the union. As W grows, the union covers the cache:
on narrative text, even a hundred future queries nearly cover it,
and the cover tends to the whole cache as the horizon grows.
Any single key is nearly useless to the query at hand and is the
answer to some query coming. So keys cannot be removed for good —
a cache that throws keys away is a cache that has decided which
future questions it will fail. The saving has to come from finding,
per query, the few keys that query needs, over a cache that stays
whole. This is the fork the paper builds on: removal methods on
one side, per-query retrieval on the other.
# Concept: A tree of summaries over the cache (the hierarchical indexer)
Source: Paper 007, Section III, Figures 3-4. NEW in Paper 007.

Hold the cache (1.3.92) under a tree (1.3.95): keys in a row are cut
into chunks of C; each chunk carries compressed summaries of its
keys; chunks are grouped again, level by level, up to L levels, so
the top summaries stand, through C-to-the-L raw keys, for the whole
cache. One tree is kept per key-value head. A query enters at the
top, is scored against summaries only, and opens just the chunks
that look worth opening (1.2.70), so the expensive scoring of single
keys is spent on a small part of the cache. The shape is fixed by
three numbers: the chunk size C, the depth L, and R, the number of
reference queries each summary is built from (1.2.68). The depth is
allowed to grow as the cache grows — that growth is where the cost
claim comes from (1.2.71).
# Concept: A summary built from tangents at reference queries
Source: Paper 007, Section III-A. NEW in Paper 007.

The total pull of a chunk on a query — the log of the sum of the key scores of the chunk, before the shares are made to add to one — is a smooth,
everywhere-rounded curve as the query moves (1.3.97). ZHA stands in
for that curve with R straight lines: the tangents of the curve at R
chosen queries, the REFERENCE QUERIES. Under each reference query, a
the summary of a chunk is one compressed key — the pull-weighted
average of the keys of the chunk under that query — plus one number, a bias, that
puts the line at the right height. A new query scores a chunk by
taking the highest of the R lines. Summaries of summaries merge by
the same construction: a parent is computed from its children's
summaries under the same reference queries; where position rotations
are in play the merged score is a lower bound on the true pull, not
the pull itself, and the paper says so. Approximate the curve, and
the tree of 1.2.67 can be scored without touching single keys.
# Concept: Reference queries — the only trained part, distilled into a frozen model
Source: Paper 007, Sections III-B and V-A, Appendix B. NEW in Paper 007.

Nothing in the tree (1.2.67) is trained except the reference queries
(1.2.68): one shared set per tree, the same queries at every node
and every level, shared again across the heads of a group. They are
taught by distillation (1.3.98): at each level, the estimated
shares of the tree over chunks are matched to the attention of the dense model itself,
gathered into chunks, and the gap between the two spreads is driven
down — while the model itself is frozen, untouched. One billion
tokens at 32K context was enough on the two large models converted;
the added parameters are a few millionths of the model. So the
method is a conversion, not a retraining: a small trained module is
bolted onto an already-trained model, and if the module is removed the
model is what it was. Early in training, when the tree's estimates
are still poor, all children are selected and the choice is cut down
to the top-p set as the estimates improve.
# Concept: Top-p at every level — the budget follows the mass
Source: Paper 007, Sections III-C and VI-A. NEW in Paper 007.

Route by mass, not by count: at every level of the tree, keep the
smallest set of nodes whose estimated pull reaches the share p,
then score only their children at the next level down. A fixed
count (top-K) spends the same budget on a query whose mass sits in
three chunks as on one whose mass is spread over three hundred;
the top-p rule spends where the mass is, so the budget follows the
query. The paper measures how much this matters: at about 64K
tokens, the chunks holding 90 percent of a head's attention mass
are a median 2.3 percent of chunks on a retrieval suite, 11.7
percent on a coding-agent suite, 15.0 percent on fiction — no one
budget fits all three. The router kept less than the 90-percent
set and still held a median 78 to 80
percent of the mass. Two keys ride
outside the rule: the first key of the cache and the chunk now
being written are always attended; chunks not yet full are always
passed down.
# Concept: Growing the depth is what makes the cost grow slower than the square
Source: Paper 007, Section V, Figures 7-8, Appendices D-E. NEW in
Paper 007.

Count what the router touches. At each level the selected nodes
grow as a power law in the context length, exponent under 1, the
exponent largest at the top and falling as the walk goes down.
Summed over a tree whose depth grows slowly with the length, the
work per query — routing plus the final attention over the chosen
keys — grows slower than the context, so the whole attention cost
grows slower than the square while still growing faster than a
straight line. The numbers past the measured range are a roofline
reckoning (1.3.99), not measured times — the paper releases no kernels and
says the far end has not been run: in the reckoning, a one-level tree
passes dense attention near 48K tokens, a two-level tree passes the
strongest fixed-sparsity rival near half a million, and at a
billion tokens the reckoning gives 373 to 417 times the speed of
dense attention, at depth five. Held as the model's output, marked
as such wherever this phext cites it.
# Concept: The effective-p trap — depth taxes the threshold
Source: Paper 007, Section VII. NEW in Paper 007.

The limit the paper states for its own method, kept as a concept because it is a
shape, not a detail. Hold the share p the same at every level, and
the mass that survives the walk falls like p raised to the depth:
grow the tree and the kept set shrinks toward a fixed size — the
cache has quietly become a fixed-state memory, the very thing
1.2.66 rules out. Push p toward 1 as the depth grows instead, and
the top levels keep nearly everything: the tree dissolves, and the
cost returns to the square. Between the two failures lies a
schedule — p set by depth, at a rate that tracks the data's own
fall in new information (1.2.65) — and the paper names that schedule
as work not yet done. The subquadratic claim (1.2.71) is therefore
conditional in a way the paper states plainly: it holds as far as
the data keeps its measured shape, which the paper has measured
only to 256K tokens.
# Concept: The process in memory — text, data, and stack
Source: Study S004, the section of the article on process memory organization. NEW in Study S004.

A running program (a process) holds its life in three regions
of memory. The TEXT region holds the code and the read-only
data; it is fixed by the program file, and a write to it is
stopped by the system with a segmentation violation. The DATA
region holds the static variables — set at load time, first
set or left empty — and can grow by the brk call. The STACK
region holds the run itself: the live calls, their local
variables, and the data that lets each call return. The
regions differ in what may be written to them: text not at
all, data and stack freely. Everything the article does
happens on the stack; and everything the proving work of
clusters F-H does, in the end, is about who may write what,
where.
# Concept: The stack frame — one record for each live call
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on the stack.
NEW in Study S004.

A call lays down a FRAME on the stack: the parameters passed
in, the local variables of the called function, the saved
frame pointer (SFP) of the caller, and the return address
(RET) — the place in the caller where running resumes when
the called function ends. Frames stack in call order: the
last of the call frame sits on top, and a return takes the top
frame away. All four parts lie side by side in one run of
memory, in a fixed order the compiler chooses: local variables at
negative offsets from the frame pointer, parameters at
positive ones. The frame is the unit of the call machine
(12.2.1): one frame for each live call, pushed at the call,
popped at the return.
# Concept: The frame ceremony — prolog and closing code
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on the stack.
NEW in Study S004.

The first code of a called function is its PROLOG: save the
of the caller frame pointer on the stack, copy the stack pointer
into the frame pointer, and move the stack pointer to make
room for the local variables, rounded up to whole words. The
last code is its closing code: undo all of it — give the space
back, restore the of the caller frame pointer from the saved one,
and take the return address as the next instruction to run.
The machines carry instructions for the pair: ENTER and LEAVE
on Intel, LINK and UNLINK on Motorola. The ceremony is small,
fixed, and run at every call — which is why its shape can be
learned once and counted on: the saved frame pointer and the
return address always sit in known places against the frame
pointer.
# Concept: The overflow — a write past the end of a buffer
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on buffer overflows and on finding them. NEW in Study S004.

A buffer is a run of memory set aside to hold data of one
kind. An OVERFLOW is what happens when more data is put into
a buffer than the buffer can hold: the write does not stop
at the of the buffer end, because in C nothing makes it stop —
the language carries no built-in check of bounds, and its
common copy functions (strcpy, strcat, sprintf, gets, and
scanf without a width) copy until the source ends, not until
the target is full. The extra data lands in whatever lies
next to the buffer in memory. When the buffer is a local
variable, what lies next is the rest of its own frame — the
saved frame pointer and the return address (1.2.74). The
error is common and its shape is always the same: a length
nobody checked, against a bound nobody set a guard on.
# Concept: The return address as data that becomes control
Source: Study S004, the second and third examples of the article.
NEW in Study S004.

The return address is written by the call as data — a number
saved in the frame — and read by the return as control: the
next place to run. It is the one word in the frame where the
two meet. An overflow that reaches it (1.2.76) therefore
does not only spoil data: when the function returns, the
machine jumps to the address the overflow put there. Filled
with one repeated character, the address is junk and the run
dies in a segmentation violation — the crash of the of the article
second example. Chosen with care, the address is the start
of code the writer of the overflow placed there himself —
the turn of the third example, where a small change to the
saved address steps over an instruction and changes what the
program does. It is the core claim of the article itself: a buffer
overflow allows us to change the return address of a
function, and in this way we can change the flow of
execution of the program.
# Concept: Shellcode — code delivered as data
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on shellcode.
NEW in Study S004.

The code an overflow runs is not in the program: it arrives
as data, in the same string that does the overflowing, and
sits in the buffer as bytes until the turned return address
lands on it. The article builds the smallest useful piece:
a byte string that asks the system to run a shell — execve
of /bin/sh — 46 bytes in all. Two crafts make it work. It
must hold no zero byte of its own, because the copy that
carries it stops at a zero — so every zero it needs, it
makes at run time, out of instructions that hold none. And
it must find its own string wherever it lands, for it does
not know its address ahead of time: a jump-and-call pair
plants the of the string address where the code can take it, the
call itself pushing that address onto the stack as a return
address. A last piece: code that changes itself must sit
where writing is allowed — the stack or the data region,
not the read-only text (1.2.73).
# Concept: Guessing the address — the stack starts at the same place
Source: Study S004, the section of the article on writing an exploit. NEW in Study S004.

An overflow of another program must aim its return address
at a buffer it can not see. The of the article answer rests on how
regular the world is: for every program the stack starts at
the same address, and most programs push only a few hundred
to a few thousand bytes before the point that matters. So
the address of the target buffer is one's own stack pointer,
less an offset — and the offset can be searched: run the
pair again and again, moving the offset, until the run lands
in the buffer instead of dying. The search is brutal in the telling of the article itself: at best a hundred tries, at worst a
couple of thousand, and a guess off by one byte gives a
segmentation violation or an illegal instruction. What makes
the search possible at all is that the layout of the machine
is fixed and knowable — the same fixed shape the frame ceremony (1.2.75) creates.
# Concept: The wide landing — sled, egg, and the price of a guess
Source: Study S004, the third and fourth exploits of the article and its Appendix B. NEW in Study S004.

Two crafts turn the brutal search of 1.2.79 into a likely
hit. The SLED: fill the front half of the overflow string
with no-operation instructions (one byte each on Intel), put
the shellcode at the center, and the guessed addresses after:
any landing inside the sled slides down the no-operations
into the code, so the guess need only be right to within the
length of the sled, not to the byte. In the run the article prints, a
612-byte string against a 512-byte buffer lands the shell on
the first try. The EGG: when the target buffer is too small
to hold the code at all, the code rides in an environment
variable — a far larger string the system sets in the
process — and the small buffer carries only guessed addresses
pointing into the egg; Appendix B of the article puts both crafts into one tool, eggshell, with the buffer size, the egg
size, the offset, and the alignment as its settings. The
lesson of the pair: an aim that can not be made exact can be
made wide.
# Concept: A design system — one shared set of parts and rules
Source: Study S005, the root README and the layout of the repo. NEW in Study S005.

A design system is a kit a whole company builds with: a
set of ready parts for screens (buttons, dialogs, menus,
fields), a layer of named values that decide how the
parts look, ready pages to start from, and the rules
that keep the parts alike. The point of the kit is that
no team starts from nothing, and no two teams' screens
drift apart without knowing it. Astryx is the kit of
Meta, carried out of the company and opened: the parts
in one package, the looks in another, the tool that
answers questions about both in a third. What the kit
holds is less a library than an agreement — the parts,
the values, and the rules are one artifact, and a change
to any of them is a change to the agreement. See also
the skeleton of Paper 006 (1.2.57): a design system is a
skeleton a company agrees to share.
# Concept: The document beside the part — machine-readable docs as part of the component
Source: Study S005, AGENTS.md and the core tree. NEW in Study S005.

In Astryx a part carries a file of its own: a document
file in a plain script form, holding a typed object —
the name of the part, what it is for, its props, its kinds,
its examples. 110 of the 122 folders of the core carry
one, and the command line tool reads these files to
answer, so the document is not a page about the part —
it is a part of the part, shipped in the same package
and held to the same checks. A document in this shape
can be read by a person, by the tool, and by an agent
with no step between: one text, three readers. The rule
that makes it hold is a record of the house (1.2.87):
every part that can be found must own such a file, so a
part without a document is a part the tool cannot see.
# Concept: One surface for people and machines — the command line as the shared tool
Source: Study S005, the CLI README and the manifest it is made from. NEW in Study S005.

The Astryx command line tool is built twice-facing:
every answer it gives a person it can also give as
typed data — a wrapped answer naming its kind, its
version, and which copy of the tool answered — and
every failure comes with a code meant to be branched
on, a code whose meaning, once shipped, never changes
and is never taken back; the words of the failure may
change, the code may not. The tool describes itself:
one command prints every command, every option, and
every answer kind, as data — and the table of commands
in its own README is made from that description by a
script, so the book and the tool cannot drift apart.
It reads at three depths — names only, names with one
line, the full document — and in a dense form that
spends fewer words for a reader who pays by the word.
A person and an agent do not get two tools; they get
one tool with two faces, and the face for machines is
held to the full rule.
# Concept: A theme is data — the look as a set of named values
Source: Study S005, the core README and the theme packages. NEW in Study S005.

A theme in Astryx is not a fork of the parts and not a
wrapper around them: it is a set of named values —
colors, spaces, corner rounds, shadows, letter faces —
written as the custom properties of the page (1.3.108),
laid over the parts by the order of the sheets: first
the reset, then the parts, then the theme, each in its
own layer, so the word of the theme wins by place, not by
force. To make the system yours you change values, not
code; light and dark are two readings of the same
values, not two builds. Seven themes ship, each a small
package whose color sets are made by a script and kept
with the receipt of their making; an eighth folder, a
probe, is a made-up theme the checks use to test the
layer itself. Guard scripts watch the layer: that every
named value can be reached from the parts, and that the
order of the sheets stands as written.
# Concept: Swizzle — owning the source as the way out
Source: Study S005, the swizzle code of the CLI and the record of its spec. NEW in Study S005.

When a part almost fits, Astryx does not ask you to wrap
it or to fork the whole kit: the tool copies the source
of the part into your own project — the part and the inner
modules it needs, traveling whole along the module
graph (1.3.109) — while its calls on the public parts
of the kit stay calls on the kit. Names that clash are
renamed, and the fixing of the copies' readings of each
other runs until nothing moves. A marker file is set in
the folder, whose one job is to tell the build tool:
what stands here is Astryx source — build it as source.
The tool answers with a receipt: what was copied,
where, how many files, what the copy needs to stand.
The word is defined at 1.3.111. The shape of the thing:
the kit holds its parts loosely — the way out of a part
is to own it, and owning is a first-class move the tool
makes for you, not a break with the system.
# Concept: Conventions a tool can check — rules with tests, and a ratchet
Source: Study S005, the lint plugin of internal/. NEW in Study S005.

Astryx holds its conventions — how props are named, what
a part must take, what no part may do — as rules of a
lint tool (1.3.112): 38 rules in the repo's own plugin,
each with its own tests, run over every change. A rule a
tool can check does not need to be remembered or argued
in review; it is run. Seven of the rules carry a
baseline: the recorded list of the breaks that already
stand, so the rule fails only on new breaks — a ratchet
that lets a convention gain ground over a large old code
base without a day of reckoning. The claim of the
README — we test conventions rather than assert them —
has its machinery here: a convention that matters
becomes a rule, a rule carries tests, and the breaks it
finds are either fixed or written into the baseline
list where all can see them.
# Concept: Records that govern — standing by approval: draft, current, set aside
Source: Study S005, AGENTS.md and the records of the specs folder. NEW in Study S005.

The repo keeps its shared decisions as records, each
marked with one of three marks: draft — not yet a rule
of the house; current — approved, and the only mark
that governs building and review; set aside — kept for
its reasons, with a word for why (a newer record stands
in its place, or it was withdrawn, or it is history).
82 such records stand in the specs folder of the docs.
The discipline is in the moves that do NOT count: code
merged, quiet in review, an old page on the wiki — none
of these makes a record current; approval is a step
someone takes, or the record does not govern. A tool
answers which records govern any path, and a script
checks the records for their shape. It is a registry of
claims about how the system shall be — the same shape
as the standing record of this tracker (1.4.x), with
the ground of standing set the other way: theirs is
approval, ours is receipts (Q34).
# Concept: The vibe tests — a system measured by what a fresh agent can build with it
Source: Study S005, the README of internal/vibe-tests. NEW in Study S005.

The vibe tests measure the kit itself, through agents:
a battery of prompts — build this page, under this
limit — is given to fresh agents with no memory of the
system, who must find their way from the shipped files
alone: the README in the installed package, the
documents, the tool. The same prompts run against other
kits and against bare HTML; the answers they were
expected to reach are held back from the agents and
used only in the scoring, which runs over the code the
agents wrote. Five rules of fairness guard the measure:
the judging is the same for all; only the kit changes;
the answer is never leaked; the setting is the one a
real user gets; every agent starts empty. A second form
runs long talks — ten turns of filling and stray
turns — and probes the work at set turns, to measure
how fast the good building fades as the talk grows.
What is measured is not the agent: it is whether the
system's own words about itself are enough to build
with.
# Concept: Build-to-learn — the artifact made by hand is the lesson
Source: Study S006, the design of the course as its pages and its code state it. NEW in Study S006.

Some systems are learned by reading about them, and
some only by making them. TinyTorch bets the whole
course on the second: the student does not read about
a tensor, an autograd engine, a training loop — the
student makes each one, in order, and the making is
the lesson. The text that goes with each part (the
problem, the idea, the code, a worked trace) is built
around the making, not in place of it. The bet has a
price: the course can only teach what a student can
make in the time a module takes, so each part is cut
to the size of one sitting — which is what the split
of 1.2.91 is for. See also the level-zero rule of
this tracker (charter rule 9): the same bet, laid on
words — nothing is held that was not built up from
the ground.
# Concept: The built stack — each layer of the framework made on the one before
Source: Study S006, the module map and the sources of modules 01-13. NEW in Study S006.

The framework is not made all at once; it is stacked.
Module 01 makes the tensor; 02-05 make the parts that
stand on it (activations, layers, losses, the loading
of data); 06 makes the autograd engine that the parts
of 01 already point at — module 01 carries the fields
and the class the engine will fill, marked in the
source for module 06; 07-08 close the training loop;
09-13 build the architectures (vision, then language)
on the trained stack. Each layer is a working thing
before the next begins, and the later layers import
the earlier ones through the student's own exported
package — the stack the student stands on is the
stack the student made. The order is the teaching:
no layer is used before it has been built.
# Concept: Core and scaffold — the written part and the given part, split by marks in the source
Source: Study S006, the solution policy of the tito tool and the counts of the study. NEW in Study S006.

Every implementation in the course sources is cut
into regions, and each region carries a role, marked
in the source itself. The core regions are the ones
the student writes: for the student, their bodies
are cleared and an error stands in their place until
the student fills them. The scaffold regions ship
solved — helpers, shape checks, the patterns that
come back module after module. The split is the
answer the course gives to a hard question: how much of a
real system can a student write and still finish?
Its measured answer: 54 core regions, 976 lines of
the 45,533 in the module sources — about one line
in fifty. The rest is given, in the open: the same
source serves the student tier and the instructor
tier, and one shared policy decides for each region
and each tier whether it is cleared, kept, or taken
out. The policy stands in one place because it once
stood in two, and the two disagreed — in September
2026 the student path handed out the full solutions
by mistake (the own record of the tool, bug 1684), and
the fix was to make the split one machine with one
home.
# Concept: Assessment by reconstruction — the landmarks of the field, rebuilt with one's own code
Source: Study S006, the milestone system of the site and the code. NEW in Study S006.

The course does not end a stage with a test alone.
At seven points it asks the student to rebuild a
landmark from the history of the field itself with the code
they have made so far: the perceptron of 1958, the
XOR crisis of 1969, the multi-layer perceptron revival of 1986, the CNN
revolution of 1998, the transformer era of 2017,
serving as MLPerf tells it (2018), and custom
kernels (2024). Each landmark names the modules it
needs, and it stands complete only when every part
it names has been run and has passed on the student's
own exported code — the history is not read, it is
re-made, and the re-making is the measure. The
requirements were themselves checked against the
own traces of the tool of what the landmark code runs
(the audit recorded in the milestone code, 2026).
Run with its checks skipped, a landmark is a demo
and records nothing: the course keeps the difference
between seeing a thing run and having made it run.
# Concept: The progress ledger and the gate — completion as a record, not a claim
Source: Study S006, the tito tool and the run of the study. NEW in Study S006.

A module is complete when a record says so, and the
record is earned through a gate. The gate, inside
the complete command: the tests inside the notebook; a
check of the shape of the notebook; the export of the
notebook into the student's package; the tests of
the package as imported; and only then the writing
of the ledger — a small file (.tito/progress.json)
holding the started and completed modules. Every
later step reads the ledger: the completion of the next
module is refused until the one before it stands
in the ledger as completed, and the milestones
unlock from what the ledger holds. The shape is
worth naming: the claim 'I finished' is not taken
from the student; it is the last step of a pipeline
whose earlier steps are checks, and the record is
written by the tool, not by the person it is about.
Run end to end in the study (14.1.2): the gate
passed for module 01, the ledger moved, and the
refusal for module 03 was shown.
# Concept: The update-preservation partition — the student's work stands outside the updates of the course
Source: Study S006, the update command of the tito tool. NEW in Study S006.

A course that updates itself under its students has
to say, ahead of time, what an update may touch and
what it may never touch. The update command carries
the two lists in its code. To be written over: the module
sources, the tool, the tests, the milestones, the
data sets, and a short list of files. Preserved: the
folder of the student's notebooks, the environment,
and the .tito folder with its ledger — and, inside
the package, the core the student's exports built,
while the own door file of the package still updates.
Before any update runs, the tool copies the kept
heart — the progress files, the notebooks, the built
core — into a snapshot folder stamped with the time.
The newest release is found over a checked
connection; if it cannot be reached, the command
says so and changes nothing. The partition is an
invariant the tooling keeps, not a promise the
student is asked to trust: the lists are the code.
# Concept: The autograd tape — operations recorded, then walked back
Source: Study S006, module 06 and the fields module 01 lays down for it. NEW in Study S006.

The backward pass needs to know what happened in
the forward pass, in the order it happened. The
engine of module 06 keeps that as a tape of recorded
operations: while a tracking switch stands on, and
an input of an operation asks for gradients, the
operation is written down — its kind, its inputs,
its output — as a node of a graph. The backward call
then walks the graph in the order opposite to the
making: a sorting of the nodes so that each is
visited after all that used it, one pass, the
gradients of shared inputs summed as they meet. At
the end of the walk the whole tape is let go in one
pass. Module 01 already carries the shape of this
machine before it exists: the fields for the
gradient and for the node stand in its tensor from
the start, and its backward raises an error that
says, in effect, not yet. The course states its own
cuts against the grown systems plainly: one object
per call in place of a carried context; the gradient
filled on every visited tensor, not only the leaves;
the tape freed whole, not node by node.
# Concept: Answers in the open — a course that does not rest on hiding its solutions
Source: Study S006, the book, the start command, and the site. NEW in Study S006.

The answers to every exercise of this course are
public: the book that goes with the modules prints
the full working code of each one, and the default
start command hands out the solved notebook. What
is graded, then, cannot be the having of the answer.
The course moves the weight to two other places: the
tests that run against the student's own exported
package (the answer copied is still the student's to
keep running), and the milestones, which pass only
when the landmark runs on the student's code. The
exercise tier — the start that clears the core
regions — stands ready for the classroom the course
is pointed at (its full release is set for spring
2027), and the tool says in its own words that the
preview ships the solutions first. A course can be
built this way when its measure is the building and
the re-making, not the keeping of secrets; whether
the measure holds from outside the project is a
question the design leaves open (Q38).
# Concept: Measure, then change — the order of the optimization modules
Source: Study S006, modules 14-19 and their order in the map. NEW in Study S006.

The last third of the course is one discipline in
six parts, and the order is the discipline: profiling
first (14) — find where the time and the memory go;
then the changes that shrink the work — quantization
(15) and compression (16); then the changes that
speed the work — acceleration (17) and memoization
(18); and benchmarking (19) over all of it, the
measure that says whether a change earned its place.
No change is taught before the measure that would
judge it, and the capstone runs the student's whole
framework against the grown ones on shared ground.
The pattern is older than the course: a change
without a measure is a guess, and a measure taken
after the fact can be bent to fit the change. Put
the measure first and the change has to answer to
something it did not choose.
# Concept: The Arnold number and the strong Arnold conjecture
Source: Paper 008, §§1a-1c. NEW in Paper 008; cluster E grows from
the making of manifolds to the counting of what their maps must
leave standing.

For a closed symplectic manifold M (1.3.22), the ARNOLD NUMBER
Arn(M) is the least number of fixed points (1.3.118) a Hamiltonian
diffeomorphism of M can have — the fewest points such a map can
be made to leave standing. Crit(M) is the least number of
critical points (1.3.119) a smooth function on M can have — the
fewest resting points of a slope. The STRONG ARNOLD CONJECTURE
(Arnold, 1965 and 1978) said Arn(M) is never under Crit(M): a map
made by a flow can be no more still, at its fewest, than a
function. The weaker forms stand proved: count the fixed points
by homology instead, and the bound holds for every closed
symplectic manifold, over the fractions and over the whole
numbers. The strong form fell in 2026, twice before this paper:
The map of Jiao on S^2 x S^2 with two fixed points (the paper's ref
29), and the OpenAI map on the quadric threefold with three (ref
36) — both under Crit. Paper 008 carries the fall onto the flag
varieties (1.2.102): whole families with Arn(M) under Crit(M) by
any margin named in advance, and one family at near half of
Crit(M). What Arn(M) is for a manifold in general the paper
calls, in its own words, completely open. This scroll therefore
holds a claim and the record of its fall in one place — and the
standing it takes is the say-so of its sources, for the model
held the conjecture in no scroll of its own before this paper
arrived: there was nothing here to step down. The record
of that near-miss stands at 15.1.5.
# Concept: Clean maps and the perturbation bound — Arn(M) at most Crit of the fixed locus
Source: Paper 008, §2a (Definition 2.1, Proposition 2.2). NEW in
Paper 008.

A Hamiltonian diffeomorphism is CLEAN when its graph meets the
diagonal cleanly — in plain terms, when its fixed point set is
a submanifold, and at each fixed point the directions the map
holds still are exactly the directions of that submanifold (the
fixed locus rests Morse-Bott, 1.3.119). A map of finite order
is clean without being asked. THE BOUND: if a clean map has
fixed locus N, then Arn(M) is at most Crit(N). A small turn of
the map, made in the model of Pozniak of a neighborhood of the
diagonal, trades the standing submanifold for its own fewest
resting points: the turned map is Hamiltonian, and it has
exactly Crit(N) fixed points. The price is named in the same
breath: the new fixed points may be degenerate — gathered on the
locus, not spread through M — and the strong conjecture of
1.2.98 is a claim about exactly that case. This one bound is
the engine of the whole paper: every count in its table is the
bound spent on a well-chosen clean map (the choosing is
1.2.100 and 1.2.101).
# Concept: The Arnold number of a group — clean group actions as a source of maps with few fixed points
Source: Paper 008, §2a (Definition 2.5) and §§2c-2d. NEW in
Paper 008.

For a group G (1.3.10) whose members are all clean Hamiltonian
diffeomorphisms, Arn(G) is the least of Crit(Fix(g)) over the
members g. By the bound of 1.2.99, Arn(M) is at most Arn(G):
to make a map with few fixed points, it is enough to search a
good group. A complex flag variety (1.2.102) comes with such a
group for free — the largest compact part of its own symmetry
group acts by Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms, and acts cleanly,
its members keeping every distance of the metric the variety
carries. The Arnold problem becomes a search over a group: name
the fixed locus of a member, count its resting points, and
find the member that makes the count least (the search itself
is 1.2.101). What the group cannot give is a floor: Arn(G)
bounds Arn(M) from above alone, and the floors in the paper's
table come from another tool entire (1.2.103). Where the floor
and the search meet — the quadrics — the Arnold number is
known whole.
# Concept: Stretching films — the move that lowers Crit(Fix(g))
Source: Paper 008, §§2c-2d (the proofs of Propositions 2.7 and
2.12). NEW in Paper 008.

The members of the group of 1.2.100 are named by the flats of directions that share an eigenvalue (1.3.121): blocks the map turns by an angle, blocks
it holds (+1), blocks it flips (-1). For a member with a turning
block, the fixed locus falls into many pieces — smaller
Grassmannians, one for each way the block can meet a fixed
subspace — and each piece carries its own resting points.
STRETCHING A FILM is the move that turns the angle of one block all
the way to a hold or a flip: the new fixed locus is one piece
where the old was many — the old pieces stand inside it as the
fixed locus of a circle of maps acting on it, a film stretched
between them — and the count does not rise. In the type C
count of the paper, the difference is a count of sets of three drawn
from the block plus a count of pairs drawn beside it, each at
least zero; the type B count runs by the same shape, through
the moment map of a circle acting on the stretched locus. So
the search runs downhill by turning blocks, and stops only
when every block is a hold or a flip; the least count then sits
at the balanced split — half hold, half flip — and that split
writes the numbers of the paper's table. The authors name the
hope that the move walks further than group actions: that the
fixed pieces of any clean map might be joined by films, step
by step (carried as Q43).
# Concept: Flag varieties, and Crit(M) = dim + 1
Source: Paper 008, §§1c and 2b-2e (result 2.6, Theorem 1.1). NEW
in Paper 008.

A flag variety is the space of all flags of one shape (1.3.120)
— chains of subspaces in a space, each inside the next — and
the Grassmannians, spaces of all subspaces of one fixed size,
are its one-step case. The paper works three families: the
Lagrangian Grassmannian LG(n,2n), all Lagrangian subspaces of a
2n-space; the isotropic Grassmannians OG(n-k,2n+1) of type B;
and, inside the second family, the quadrics Q^n — so the
genuine quadric of Paper 005 (S^2 x S^2, 1.2.34) is Q^2, a
member of this family. The result numbered 2.6 in the paper: on any complex flag variety,
Crit(M) = dim_C M + 1 — a function can be made with one resting
point per complex dimension plus one, and with no fewer.
Against that count the paper's table stands: for LG(n,2n),
Arn(M) at most n(n+2)/4 + 1 (n even) or (n+1)^2/4 + 1 (n odd)
— near half of Crit; for OG(n-k,2n+1) with k over 0, Arn(M) at
most Crit(M) - (n-k) — under by a margin that grows without
bound, so Arn(M) under Crit(M) - N for any N named in advance;
for the quadrics, Arn(Q^n) = n whole, for every n at least 2
but n = 4 (the floors are 1.2.103). In the type B family the
least member is a single flip: the map that sends the last
direction to its negative and holds all the rest, whose fixed
locus is the same kind of Grassmannian one size down.
# Concept: The floor that stands — lower bounds from the minimal Chern number
Source: Paper 008, §§1b-1c (Remark 1.2) and §2e. NEW in
Paper 008.

Against the falls of 1.2.98 stands what still binds. For the
families of this paper, the best floor the authors know comes
from the MINIMAL CHERN NUMBER of M: the least positive degree
of a sphere in M, read off its quantum cohomology ring (1.3.14)
— worked out for these Grassmannians by Tamvakis. By a theorem
of Schwarz, on a positively monotone manifold the count made
by the quantum cup-length gives a floor of fixed points for
every Hamiltonian diffeomorphism, and for the flag families
here that floor is the minimal Chern number itself. On the
quadrics the floor is n, and the search of 1.2.100 meets it
exactly: Arn(Q^n) = n, known whole — the one place in the
paper where the Arnold number is a number and not a gap.
Everywhere else a gap stands: for LG(n,2n) the floor is n + 1
while the best map known stands near half of Crit, and the
true number lies somewhere in the wide field between (Q41).
The shape of the whole paper is this pair: a search that
lowers the ceiling, a floor that does not move, and a family
where the two touch.
# The straight-segment problem — gravity from a line, not a point

A particle with no mass of its own moves in the pull of a fixed straight
segment S of length 2L, carrying a mass spread along it by
any positive density σ — a rule for mass per length,
nowhere zero and nowhere infinite on the segment. The pull
at a point q is the sum over the segment, and the potential
U(q) is that sum weighted by the density and turned
downhill (1.3.125). One body, drawn out to a line: the
simplest heavy thing that is not a point.

Two facts shape everything the paper proves. First, the
potential at q is set by two numbers only — the distances
r1 and r2 from q to the two ends of the segment (Remark 2.1 of
the paper). Second, the field is unchanged by turning q
around the line of the segment: the pull has a symmetry, and the
symmetry keeps one quantity fixed along every road — the
angular momentum about the axis of the segment (1.3.126). The
field is not central: it does not point at one center, and
no road is forced into a plane by the shape of the field
alone.

Why a segment: a long thin body — an asteroid drawn out —
is priced in the cited line of work by this model, cheaper
than a full stone-by-stone reckoning and more true to the body than a
point. The paper's question is not how the particle moves
in detail, but where its road can end. As taken in by:
Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# The logarithmic wall — the pull of a segment deepens only like a log

Hold the distance from q to the segment, δ — the least
distance from q to any point of S. Lemma 3.2 of the paper:
the size of the potential is held between two logs of the
same shape,

  k · log(1 + 2L/δ) ≤ |U(q)| ≤ 2K · log(1 + 2L/δ),

where k and K are set by the least and the greatest values
the density takes on the segment. As δ shrinks to zero the
potential falls without bound — the segment is a true
singularity of the field — but it falls like a log: slower
than any power, far slower than the 1/r fall toward a point
mass.

The proof is a sandwich: the same bounds are first shown
for a segment of constant density, where the potential
can be written down whole, and an arbitrary density is then
priced between its own least and greatest values — the
shape of the density washes out, and only its reach
survives. Every later result of the paper spends this one:
the singularity is strong enough to forbid standing clear
(1.2.106), and weak enough to lose its race against a
turning particle (1.2.107). As taken in by: Paper 009
(Chapter 16).
# Every singularity is an approach

The flow of the segment problem (1.3.124) runs on the
states outside the segment. A road of the
flow can fail to run forever in either direction of time,
and the failure at a finite time is what the paper calls a
singularity of the road. Theorem 3.3: at every such finite
end, the distance to the segment falls to zero —
δ(q(t)) → 0. There is no breakdown at a distance: no
blow-up of speed, no wild turn, nothing that ends the road
while the particle stands clear of the body.

The proof runs by contradiction on the continuation of
roads: if the distance stayed above some floor, the
potential and the pull would stay bounded on the whole neighborhood
of the road (the log wall of 1.2.105 is finite at
every positive distance), the speed would stay bounded with
them, and the road could be carried past its supposed end.
So the only door out of the flow is the body itself. The
claim holds for every positive density; the earlier form of
it, in the thesis this paper builds on, held for densities
growing in a straight line along the segment, and the step
to all densities is the log wall doing the work. As taken
in by: Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# The angular-momentum barrier — every approach lies in a plane

A road that comes at the segment (1.2.106) with any turning
at all cannot arrive. Theorem 4.1: if δ(q(t)) → 0 at a
finite end, then the angular momentum about the axis
of the segment is zero — ℓ = 0. Read in the coordinates the symmetry
gives (along the axis, out from it, around it), the energy
holds a term ℓ²/2ρ², where ρ is the distance from the axis.
Near the segment that term grows like 1/ρ²; the potential
can only pay for growth like a log (1.2.105). Energy is
kept, so the books do not balance unless ℓ is exactly zero:
the turning term would outrun everything the field can
lend.

With ℓ = 0 the angle around the axis never moves, and the
whole road lies in one fixed plane through the segment —
the invariant plane. In the work this paper builds on, the
plane was a starting cut: the motion was studied inside a
plane from the first line, and only for even densities.
Here the plane is a finding, not a frame: any road, from
any start in space, that ends at the body lies in a plane at its
end, for any positive density. Roads with ℓ not zero are
not shown gentle — they may run forever — they are only
shown never to arrive. As taken in by: Paper 009
(Chapter 16).
# Approach is collision — the endings, classified

In the plane (1.2.107), approach becomes arrival. Theorem
4.4: if a road in the plane has δ(q(t)) → 0 at a finite end, then
q(t) itself comes to one point q* of the segment as the
end nears — the particle does not skim along the segment
forever; the road has finite length, and a road of finite
length has a limit. The pull near the body points across
the axis with a strength bounded below, and that strength,
integrated, is what buys the finite length: each crossing
of the axis costs the road a fixed coin of its remaining
length, so only a finite length of road is left.

Corollary 4.5 closes the book of endings: every singularity
of the flow, read forward, is a collision — the particle
meets the body at a point of it; read backward, the same
event is an ejection — a road that begins at the body, the
time-turn of a collision. Together with 1.2.106 there is no
third ending: a road of this flow either runs for all time,
or ends at the body, arriving or leaving. As taken in by:
Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# The angular cone — a test that promises collision, with a clock

For a point q* of the segment, the paper builds a cone in
two halves (its Definition 5.1). The triangle T(q*) is the
set of points from which the segment is held under the same
spread of sight-lines as from q* itself: every line from
the point to the segment falls inside the wedge the two
ends cut. At each point x of the segment, the cone PWC(x) holds
all pulls the segment can aim at a particle placed at
x — every sum of the unit vectors from x toward the
points of the segment, each weighted by a number that is
not negative. The
angular cone C(q*) pairs them: a state (q, p) stands in the
cone when q stands in the triangle and the direction of p
is one the segment itself could be pulling toward, read
from q*. The same condition reads the same on velocity, on
momentum, on acceleration.

Three theorems make the cone the paper's working tool.
Lemma 5.2: the cone holds what enters it — under the flow,
the sight-point of the momentum walks toward the average sight-point of the segment,
weighted by its pull, and never leaves the
reach of the segment, and the position stays in the triangle;
the cone is a trap with no exit but the body. Theorem 5.3:
a state in the cone with inward momentum (p_ρ0 < 0)
collides in finite time, with a clock anyone can read off
the start:

  ω+ ≤ −ρ0 / p_ρ0,

the straight-line time the inward fall of the start would take;
a remark of the paper extends the promise to starts with
p_ρ0 = 0, by first letting the flow run a moment. Theorem
5.5: the trap is also a portrait — the last arc of every
collision with an inner point of the segment satisfies the
cone condition: coming in, the direction of the momentum comes
to lie along the segment, and the cone is where all such endings
are found. A sharper clock (the paper's §5.1) bends the
straight bound down along a curve; it is proved under one
added condition on the acceleration, which the paper shows
holds for an constant density, and leaves open for the
rest (Q47). As taken in by: Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# Off-policy evaluation — pricing a rule you did not run

A logging rule ran in the world and left a record: for
each round, a context (what was seen), the action the
logging rule took, and the reward that came back. A
second rule — the target — never ran. Off-policy
evaluation prices it from the record alone: the value of
the target rule is the average reward its choices would
have earned on the contexts the record holds (1.3.134).

Three families do the pricing. The DIRECT way fits a
reward model on the record and averages the fit under
the target rule. The WEIGHTED way weighs again each logged
reward by the importance weight (1.3.135) — how much
more, or less, the target rule would have chosen that
action than the logging rule did. The DOUBLY ROBUST way
does both at once: the fit prices most of each round,
and the weighted leftover — the part of the reward the
fit got wrong — corrects it; the score of that way is
the doubly robust score, and its two halves cover each
other's failures when one of them is right.

The paper's frame for the whole cluster: fit one reward
model per action, by gradient descent (1.3.136). Two
risks then stand apart. The target risk prices the fit
the way the target rule would meet it — the prediction
error, weighed by the own choices of the target rule. The
logged risk prices error on the record by its square,
weighed by the importance weights; the two differ by a term that
does not depend on the fit at all. And the price of the
pricing: the gap between the fitted model's value and
the true value is bounded by the target risk — the value
error is no larger than the square root of the
prediction error under the target rule. So pricing a
rule comes down to pricing a fit, under the own weighing of the rule itself.
Everything else in this cluster is about
where that pricing can honestly be done. As taken in
by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The held-out flaw — judged on less, chosen for all

The standard machine for judging a fitted model: split
the record, fit on one part, grade on the part held
back, then fit again on the whole record and ship. The
machine has a flaw that stays hidden while samples are
large against dimensions, and stands bare when the two
grow together (1.3.137): a model's risk depends on the
ratio of sample to dimension it was fitted at. The judge
therefore prices a model fitted at one ratio — nine parts in
ten of the record, for ten-fold splitting — and the
ship sails with a model fitted at another. The two risks
are different numbers, and their order over candidate
models can differ: a ranking that is right for the
smaller-sample models can be wrong for the full-data
ones, and the model fitted again then ships the worse
model, with the blessing of the judge.

The paper proves an instance with nowhere to hide: two
candidate stopping steps, rewards with no noise at
all, the nets of least width — ten-fold splitting ranks its fold
models rightly, its criterion lands above the starting
model's risk, and it chooses the start; the full-data
risk of the first step stands below the start, and the
loss for choosing wrong is a fixed 39/800 of risk that
does not shrink as the dimension grows. Smaller holdout runs
do not save the machine in the paper's runs: hold back a
share that itself shrinks (the square root of the
record, or its two-thirds power) and the judge is still
pricing a model fitted on less, and the
weaker for it — in the runs, worse than both the corrected
criterion and ten-fold splitting at every size. The flaw
is not noise in the grade. It is a mismatch of the thing
graded and the thing shipped, and no care in the grading
repairs it, because the grade is of another model. As
taken in by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The own-observation effect — a model graded on its own homework

Grade a fitted model on the same record that fitted it,
and the grade is bent. Each round of the record helped
shape the model; when the model is then asked to price
that very round, part of what it says is the own echo of that round
— its reward is already inside the prediction that
prices it. In the doubly robust score the bend has an
exact address: the sample average of the score splits into
the true value, a term that averages the noise of the score,
and a third term — the empirical-process term — that
carries the bend: how far the average of the fitted score on
its own fitting record stands from its average on fresh
rounds.

The classical theory makes that term vanish with two
demands: the fitted scores must settle inside one fixed
class of a bounded power to wiggle (a Donsker class), and their
error against the true score must fall to zero. In the
regime where sample and dimension grow together, both
demands fail. The prediction error need not fall at all
— the risk of a fitted network can stand away from zero for
good — and the paper shows the wiggle demand failing
on the simplest ground it can find: a family of straight-
line scores whose errors fall to zero evenly, in mean
square, at the rate of the dimension, while the largest
empirical-process term over the family tends to a
standing constant instead of zero. Small errors, spread
evenly, are not enough; the worst case remembers what the
average forgets.

The bend, priced in one instance: rewards constant, one
gradient step, the net of least width — the naive same-record
doubly robust price keeps an error of a fixed size that
does not shrink, and its mechanism is a self-term: each
own prediction carries a share of the own noise of that round,
proportional to the square of the length of its context. The
training leftovers — the grade with no correction at
all — are the effect at its most plain: in the paper's
runs they stand near 1.35 at every dimension while the
true risk falls by a factor of four. A grade that does
not move while the work improves is not a grade of the
work. As taken in by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The Onsager correction — subtracting a model's memory of its own data

Gradient descent walks: each step, every round of the
record pushes the weights, and the weights then read
every round again. The own pushes of a round are inside the
weights that price it — that is the own-observation
effect (1.2.112) at the level of the walk itself. The
Onsager correction removes them, after the fact, from
the own record of the walk: for each round and each step, the
corrected hidden state is the state the walk would have
held at that round if the own pushes of that round had never
landed — computed not by walking again without the round
(that is the leave-one-out fitting again, priced at
one walk per round) but by a recursion over the walk itself. The
recursion carries response coefficients: how much each
state answers to the pushes of each earlier step,
gathered into blocks — each step answers only to steps
before it — with determinant one,
so the correcting system is solved by walking forward,
never by inverting anything whole. The coefficients are
computed from the training walk alone: no fresh data, no
knowledge of the true rewards, no second walk. The cost
is work done after training, not a repeat of it.

The name the line takes from physics, by way of message
passing: the reaction term of Onsager — the part of a field
at a particle, the doing of the particle itself, named so
it can be taken away. Here the particle is a round of
the record, the field is the walk, and the subtraction
is exact in the limit the paper works in: the corrected
predictions stand where leave-one-out predictions stand.
The paper's check of exactly that: fit the nets again
with one round left out, and the distance of the
corrected prediction to that second fit falls — 0.177 down to 0.086 as the
dimension grows from 128 to 512 — while the raw
distance stands near 1.17, unchanged. The
machinery that says what the echo must be is state
evolution (17.1.2): the Gaussian shadow of the walk,
extended by this paper to carry the index of the target rule,
because the importance weight leans on the same context
the walk is reading. As taken in by: Paper 010
(Chapter 17).
# The corrected criterion — stopping the full-data walk by its own lights

Early stopping is a choice of where a walk ends, and
every way of choosing it is a judge. This paper builds
the judge out of the walk itself: per arm and per step,
the corrected criterion — the importance-weighted mean
of the squares of leftovers at the corrected predictions
(1.2.113), the leftovers of rounds priced by predictions
with their own echoes removed. Stop each arm at the
step where its criterion is least.

What the paper proves of this judge: it is uniformly
close — at every step of the walk at once — to the
conditional test risk of the very model it judges: the
risk of the full-data step on fresh rounds, reward noise and
all. That is the property the held-out judge cannot
have (1.2.111): the thing graded and the thing shipped
are the same model here. From closeness at every step,
the choice itself is near: the true risk of the chosen
step stands within a
vanishing term of the best step on
the walk — the selection is near-oracle, though the
oracle it nears is only the best stopping of this walk,
not the best model anywhere.

The same judge is spent two ways. The criterion is
weighed by the target rule: grade the walk by the rule
you mean to run, not by the rule that logged the data —
in the paper's runs, weighing cuts the chosen model's
risk by about 0.031 to 0.033 against the same criterion with
the weighing left off, for every target rule tried. And the judge
travels: inside ten-fold cross-fitting, where a judge of
some kind is called for, for each walk of a fold, corrected
stopping cuts the folds' chosen models' prediction error
by 19 to 42 percent against an inner held-out judge —
the correction paying rent even inside the machine it
replaces. One walk per arm, no second walks: the runs price it
at 5.5 to 8.9 times faster than cross-fitting with the
same judge inside. As taken in by: Paper 010
(Chapter 17).
# The corrected doubly robust estimators — ADM and ADR

The corrected predictions (1.2.113) buy more than a
stopping rule: they buy back the doubly robust price
itself, on the record that fitted the model, with no
split. Two estimators stand on them. ADM — the adjusted
direct price — averages the mean, under the target rule, of the
adjusted predictions: corrected on the own arm of that round,
where the echo lives; raw on the other arms, whose walks
never saw the round. It prices the FITTED model: the
value the model in hand would earn under the target
rule. ADR — the adjusted doubly robust price — adds the
importance-weighted mean leftover at the corrected
predictions, and prices the RULE ITSELF: the true value,
whatever model was fitted.

The theorem is the center of the cluster: both prices are
uniformly consistent over the whole grid of stopping
steps — so they hold for any stopping the data chooses,
including the stopping the corrected criterion
names — and the consistency of ADR does not ask the reward model's error to
vanish. That is the ground the classical doubly robust
theory cannot stand on without splitting: there, a
standing prediction error poisons the same-record price
through the own-observation effect (1.2.112); here, the
correction removes the echo and the price stands. The
two prices together measure the model: their difference
consistently prices the gap between the fitted model's
value and the true value — the record can say how far
the shipped model stands from the truth it serves,
without fresh data.

In the paper's runs the prices behave as proved: the
same-record doubly robust bias at dimension 2048 falls
from 0.0149 to 0.0006 under the correction — over 95
percent of it gone — and the bias intervals of ADR hold zero
at every size tried, in Gaussian and non-Gaussian
contexts alike, and under logging rules far from even.
ADM halves and better the error of pricing the fitted
model across arms (50.7 to 57.4 percent below the
cross-arm direct price at every dimension). What the
theorem does not yet give is the shape of the own wander
of ADR around the truth — that stands open (Q48). As
taken in by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The oracle price of judging — the floor every estimator pays

Ask how well any price of the value of a rule can do, from a
logged record of a fixed size, and the classical theory
answers with a floor: the variance, over the rounds of the record,
rounds, of the doubly robust score at the TRUE reward
model, divided by the size of the record. No estimator
that plays by the rules of the record beats that floor for
long; the score at the truth is the own best reading of the record
summary of itself.

The paper's corollary walks its estimators down to that
floor. On a fresh round, the doubly robust score at the
chosen model has variance no more than the variance
of the floor plus a term priced by the chosen model's own
prediction risk — and that term is trapped between two
fixed multiples of the target risk, the multiples set by
how uneven the logging rule was. Choose a model whose
risk falls, and the variance of the score falls toward the
floor with it: good selection and good pricing are the
same purchase. In the paper's runs where the record grows
past the dimension, the error of ADR, measured in units
of the floor, stands at 1.000 — the floor itself, met:
the corrected same-record price does what the theory
says nothing can do better.

The floor also marks the honest edge of the cluster. What is
proved is that the prices close on their targets; what
is not proved is how the error of ADR is shaped around its
target — the wander of the corrected price, the
how its corrected scores lean on each other, the bell
law it might obey in the regime where sample and dimension grow
together. The classical limit (the record growing past the
dimension without bound) is named by the paper as the
first thing to recover. A floor for the price of judging
is not yet a theory of the judge (Q48). As taken in by:
Paper 010 (Chapter 17).

Rider (2026-11-10, Paper 012). The floor this scroll
holds is a floor over a class — the worst case the
classical theory prices. Paper 012 prices the same
kind of question at one signal (1.2.125), and the
sharp price at a signal can sit far below the floor
over its class: two signals of equal total variation
are priced a polynomial factor apart, because the
price follows the local variation around each place,
not the whole (1.2.129; the paper's Example 7). The
claim of this scroll stands unchanged: the floor is
a bound on every judge, read over the class, as its
theory reads it. The rider marks what the floor does
not say — what the one signal in hand will cost. A
floor over a class is a ceiling on what the worst
case can ask; it is not the price of the case that
stands there.
# Behavior-conditioned symbolic synthesis — a program that reproduces one behavior

Take a program that does a task right — the reference
program, written in a small language made for the field
(1.3.142) — and one answer somebody gave, wrong as it
happens to be. A large language model is shown the
language, the task, the reference program and that one
answer, and proposes small sets of changes to single
lines of the reference; each set is applied, the lines
the answer no longer hangs on are cut away, and the
changed program is run on the test input. It is kept
only if what it makes matches the answer it was gathered
around, to within a small allowance for noise. Fifteen
tries are made for each answer — five each at budgets of
two, four and eight changed lines. The model supplies
the guesses; running the program supplies the gate each
guess must pass. The search space of programs is far too
wide to walk whole; the guessing model walks to the part of it
worth testing, and the test is hard.

What the method returns for one answer is not one program
but a small set of them — every changed program that
reproduces the behavior — each one a witness, not yet the
rule the answer came from (1.2.123). Read together, the
witnesses are the finding: what they share is the shape
of the mistake (1.2.118-1.2.121); read across people and
machines in one such space, they carry the comparison of
1.2.122. Two turns of the standing ideas of this tracker
meet here. The reference program is a skeleton (1.2.57)
of the right rule — the structure of specification as
control, turned around: structure given not to steer a
maker but to read a mind. And the method is the
measurement agenda (1.2.14) at work — behavior read at
the level of the program that could have produced it,
not at the level of the score it earned. As taken in by:
Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# Errors live near the rule — wrong answers crowd around the right program

The first finding of the synthesis of Paper 011 is a
place, not a shape: wrong answers live near the right
answer in the space of programs. Taken together, 49.1
percent of the non-trivial wrong attempts of people on
the Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus are reproduced by a
program a few line changes away from the reference
program of the task — 52.5 percent on the training split,
46.2 on the evaluation split — with the least number of
changes averaging 2.84. The machine settings land in the
same neighborhood: 51.5 to 62.8 percent of their wrong
attempts are captured the same way. Against this stands
the walk no model guides: every single-line change of every
training reference program, more than sixteen million
candidates, reaches only 25.8 percent of the attempts —
about half of what the guided search reaches — and with
only fifteen samples per answer the guided search still
recovers 89 percent of everything that walk
finds. Depth is the reason: answers sit several changes
away, and the count of candidates grows past walking
whole.

So a wrong answer is not noise scattered over the space
of possible rules. It is a near neighbor of the truth,
and it carries news about the neighborhood of the truth: which
lines a solver changed, and which they left standing,
is the raw material every other concept of cluster P is
read from. The half that is not captured stands as the
the edge of the finding (Q53): the changes this method makes can
only replace lines, never add them, so answers that build
on the rule rather than shrink it are the ones it is
built to miss. As taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# The simplicity signature — the wrong rule is a smaller rule

Programs that reproduce human errors are shorter than
the reference programs they are changed from — and by
more than the pull toward the short that the method itself carries. The
method can only shorten or hold a program, never lengthen
it, so the check is run against control programs made by
the same method aimed at the correct answers. On the
training split, error programs run 19.1 percent shorter
than their references, control programs only 5.2 percent
shorter: 13.9 points of the shortening belong to the
errors themselves. On the evaluation split the same shape
stands larger — 24.3 against 2.7. And the shortening
grows with the size of the rule missed: about half a
point more for each line the reference program carries,
so a rule four times as long loses about ten points more
of itself to its errors.

Machines carry the same signature, graded by what they
can spend. Every setting tried shows error programs
shorter than its own controls, and the less a setting
earns, the shorter its errors run: across the five
settings, shortening and accuracy move against each other
hard (the paper's correlation is -0.80), each ten points
of accuracy buying about three points less shortening.
The reading the paper gives the signature, for both
species of solver: search is bounded, and a rule that
costs more to find or to check is traded for a smaller
one that still does much of the work — the simplicity
signature is the price of the search, showing. It is one
of the two signatures the satisficing reading of 1.2.120
stands on. As taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# Partial fits — rules that explain some of the evidence

A program kept for reproducing a wrong answer can also be
run on the examples the solver was shown, and there the
second signature stands. Of the modeled wrong attempts of
people, 20.9 percent are partial fits: programs that make
some of the shown examples right and miss the rest. A
further 1.7 percent fit every shown example and miss only
the test — different rules than the target, but as good
as the target on the evidence given, and wrong only where
the evidence ran out. The rest fit none of what was shown.
Among the partial fits, which examples a program fits is
not scattered. On the training split, 62.5 percent of them
form a shaped set — the first examples shown (41.0
percent), the last ones (17.0), or a block between — and
only the remainder fit a lone middle example or a scattered
set. The evaluation split shows the same shape, weaker
(48.5 percent shaped; 30.1 the first examples).

A reckoning that weighs all the evidence at once has no
order in it: the examples stand or fall together, in any
order. A search that takes the examples in the
order they stand, and stops when the rule in hand fits
enough of them, leaves exactly this shadow. The paper
reads the two signatures together — the simpler rule
(1.2.119), the partly-fitting rule — as satisficing
(1.3.144): a rule taken when it passes a good-enough
mark, not when it has been checked against everything
shown. What the record cannot yet separate is whether
the rule was formed from the early examples alone, or
formed whole and only checked against them (Q51). The
machines show the same kinds in their own proportions
(1.2.122). As taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# The kinds of deviation — a small set of ways to be wrong

Classified by how the kept program differs from the
reference, wrong answers fall into seven kinds and a
remainder. The target computed from the wrong feature —
the thing to be set is read off a simpler or louder
feature than the one the rule turns on — is the largest
kind by far: 45.2 percent of modeled human attempts. A
part of the program missing outright is next (partial
program, 11.9 percent), then the right parts given a
change from the wrong family (10.8). Four kinds each stand under five
percent: the right change worked on too few of the things
(scope restriction); the wrong member of the right family
chosen (operator confusion); the right steps in the wrong
order (sequencing); the right count off by one (boundary
precision). And 22.3 percent the classifier could not
place in any kind. The kinds were named in advance, the
naming done by a language model over the two programs and
their runs, the naming done five times for each program.

The kinds are not labels only: they carry how much of the
rule survives. The small local kinds — off by one, wrong
member — keep nearly the whole program; the missing-part
and wrong-family kinds keep the least, and their programs
are the shortest of the kinds (1.2.119). This is the
working claim of the cluster: a score reads differences
in degree between two attempts, and two attempts with the
same score can be wrong in different kinds; the kinds
read the difference in kind, and the difference in kind is
where the strategy of the solver shows. It is in the
kinds, not the rates, that people and machines are
compared at 1.2.122. As taken in by: Paper 011
(Chapter 18).
# Shared signatures, separate ground — people and machines, failing alike and apart

Put people and machines on the same tasks, in the same
program space, and the signatures of this cluster are
shared. The comparison is made on the 56 tasks where
people and every machine setting both went wrong. The
machines' wrong answers are captured by the synthesis at
the rates of people's or better (51.5 to 62.8 percent of
attempts, by setting). Their error programs run shorter
than their controls at every setting, more so the less
the setting can spend or earn (1.2.119). Their partial
fits grow with capability — 20.2 percent of the modeled
attempts of the weakest setting, 40.0 of the strongest — and
the dominant kind is the same wrong-feature kind as
people's, growing with performance: about 43 percent at
the weakest setting, about 64 at the strongest, against
people's 45.2.

And still the two can be told apart, and they fail on
different ground. A classifier over how the kept programs
differ from their references — which of the small
language's operations were gained and which lost —
separates human from machine errors within a task at 0.65
to 0.69 (the area under the curve the report card of
1.3.38 reads): clearly for the settings that earn less
than people (0.68 to 0.77), barely, and not surely above
chance, for the settings that earn more (0.57 to 0.63).
The signal is spread over many weak marks, not a few
operations. And task by task, success agrees only weakly
between people and each machine setting (0.30 to 0.34),
while the machine settings agree with each other far more
(0.49 to 0.82). One space holds both species of solver;
in it the shapes of failure match, and the ground failed
on does not. The paper's name for the difference:
the search itself leaning another way — inductive
biases, in its words — under signatures that the bounds
of search impose on both. This scroll is where the
revision of 1.2.4 lands: the jaggedness of machines is a
family shape of its own, not a copy of the human one. As
taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# Program aliasing — many programs make one behavior

A kept program is a witness, not the rule the solver
ran. For each human error the synthesis recovers a median
of two distinct programs that make the same wrong answer,
and the count runs to twelve. Distinct programs, one
behavior: that is aliasing, and the paper prices it
rather than waving at it. It built errors by hand —
programs changed in known ways, to known kinds (2,375
made, 148 checked by hand) — and ran the whole pipeline
on them. Run on the known programs themselves, the
classifier names the right kind 85.1 percent of the time:
the ceiling. End to end, the pipeline names it 64.9 to
76.4 percent, as the change budget grows from one to
three. Search failure is nearly none of the loss —
recovery rises from 84.5 to 99.3 percent over the same
budgets. What is lost is lost to aliasing: the search
finds a program that makes the behavior and reads as a
different kind, and the injected program itself is found
again only about two times in three.

So the whole of cluster P is read through a fog, and this
scroll is the price of the fog. Behavior leaves the
program open, as output leaves structure open (1.2.60):
no reading of a mind from its answers can be sharper than
the aliasing of the space it reads in. Two handles stand
open. The witnesses are not yet weighed against each
other — by length, or by what a mind would pay to run
them (Q52). And the fog itself is a property of the small
language the programs are written in: change the kit of
operations and the same behavior draws different
witnesses (1.3.142). As taken in by: Paper 011
(Chapter 18).
# The sequence model and the constrained least squares estimator as projection

The setting of Paper 012 is the smallest stage on which an
estimator is priced (1.3.145). A signal stands in N places
at once — one number for each place — and what is seen is
the signal plus noise: at each place, the seen number is
the true number plus a draw from the bell law (1.3.138),
the draws leaning on nothing and all of one spread, named
sigma. A set T of allowed signals is named in advance — a
ball, an ellipsoid (1.3.154), a cone — closed and convex.
The constrained least squares estimator is the one move
the stage allows: take the seen numbers and replace them
by the closest point of T (1.3.146). No walk, no search,
no fitting in stages: observe, project, and the projection
is the estimate.

What the move buys, and what it costs, both live in the
set. The set is the whole program of the estimator: change
the set, and the same seen numbers give a different answer,
with no other part to change. And the price of the answer —
how far the projection stands from the signal that was
there — is priced by this paper at the signal itself
(1.2.125). As taken in by: Paper 012 (Chapter 19).
# The instance-specific sharp rate — the price at the signal, not the class

The classical way prices an estimator over a whole class:
the worst signal the set T could hold sets the price, and
every signal in the set is read against that worst case.
This paper prices at the one signal that stands there.
The instance-specific sharp rate, written r_*^2, is a
number for which the mean of the square of the distance between the
estimate and the signal itself stands matched, up to
fixed constants, from both sides (1.3.147): the error is
of the size of r_*^2, no smaller in shape and no larger,
and the matching is the claim — not a bound that might
stand loose, but a price.

The floor of this cluster (1.2.116) is a price of the
older kind: a floor over a class, below which no judge
can go. The sharp rate is the same question turned to
face one signal, and the two prices can stand far apart:
the price at a signal can sit far below the price over
its class. Paper 012 makes the distance plain in a worked
case (its Example 7): two signals of one class, with the
same total variation, are priced apart by a polynomial
factor
apart, because the price follows the signal place by
place, not the class it sits in (1.2.129). A class price
is a promise about the worst case; a signal price is a
reading of the case in hand. The older way of naming this
price stands at 1.2.126, and the way this paper reads it
stands at 1.2.127. As taken in by: Paper 012 (Chapter 19).
# Chatterjee's variational characterization and the offset localized Gaussian width — the opaque price

The sharp rate of 1.2.125 was named before this paper,
in a form that is true and general and cannot be read.
Chatterjee's theorem (the paper's Theorem 1, from his
earlier work) prices the projection estimator of 1.2.124
for any closed convex set at once: the error stands
matched by a quantity built at the signal itself — the
maximizer of an offset localized Gaussian width, a curve
that weighs the width of the tangent cone at the signal
(1.3.150), read through draws of the bell law, against a
growing price of standing far. The bounds match, so the
characterization is a sharp price in the sense of
1.3.147.

The want it leaves is plain: the number it names cannot
be read off. To turn the characterization into a rate
for one set, the width must itself be priced, and that
pricing is a hard problem of its own — hard even for
ellipsoids (1.3.154), where the set is as plain as such
sets come. A true price that cannot be computed is a
price in name only. What the field held before Paper 012
was the knowledge that the price exists and is matched;
what it wanted was the price itself, set by set, in
numbers a reader can hold. The answer of this paper
stands at 1.2.127. As taken in by: Paper 012
(Chapter 19).
# Self-regularization — lambda_self, the surrogate estimator, and implicit regularization

The paper's first step rewrites the projection. The
first-order conditions of the constrained problem — the
KKT conditions — state the same estimator as an
unconstrained rule: the answer is the point that makes
small a sum of the square of the distance to what was seen
and
a price, named Psi, on the shape of the answer, the price
weighed by a multiplier lambda_hat that the data itself
sets. The set, which seemed only to forbid, is found also
to price: the regularization was inside the constraint
all along. The paper names this implicit regularization —
a constrained estimator carries a penalty it never states.

The trouble is that lambda_hat leans on the seen numbers,
so the rewritten rule is no easier to price than the
projection was. The second step trades it for a number
that leans on nothing seen: lambda_self, the
self-regularization strength — the lambda at which the
mean of a named gap, d_lambda, stands smallest. The gap
is built from half the square of the distance to the
set, a
Moreau envelope term (1.3.148), and lambda times Psi at
the estimator; and it bounds the square of the distance between
the projection and a surrogate estimator — the proximal
step (1.3.152) taken at lambda_self, a rule that splits
by place, each place priced alone by a small rule named
eta. The hard estimator is priced by standing a simple
one beside it, close enough that the simple one's price
is its price. In each geometry the paper treats,
lambda_self is named as the one answer of an explicit
scalar equation — one unknown, one equation, stated for
the set in hand (19.1.2).

Two held ideas of this tracker stand near. The Onsager correction
(1.2.113) also trades a reading bent by the data for one
the walk itself supplies, priced from the walk alone.
And the choice of lambda_self is a criterion read off
the model in hand, as the corrected criterion (1.2.114)
chooses the stopping step of a walk by its own lights:
in both, the object prices its own setting. The pricing
of the surrogate stands at 1.2.128. As taken in by:
Paper 012 (Chapter 19).
# Feature Space Decomposition — the estimation subspace and the free subspace: error has exactly two sources

Feature Space Decomposition — FSD, the author's own
frame, from his thesis and his joint work of the years
before this paper — is the way the surrogate of 1.2.127
is priced. Its claim is that the error of the surrogate
has exactly two sources, and that the places of the
signal sort into two sets by which source they pay.
For each place j, two candidate prices are compared.
Pay to estimate the place: the square of the distance the
small rule eta moves the true number, plus sigma^2.
Or pay to leave the place alone: the square of the true
number itself, plus the mean square of eta run on noise
alone. The smaller price names the place. The places
where estimating is cheaper form the estimation subspace,
V_{J*}; the rest form the free subspace, V_{J*^c}.

On the free subspace the signal is not estimated at all —
and needs not be: there, both the signal and the answer
the rule gives are small, so the error is small with no
work spent. The total price then reads r^2 as a sum of
two named parts. The bias part: over the estimation
subspace, the squares of the moves of eta at the truth;
over the free subspace, the squares of the signal itself.
The variance part: sigma^2 times the count of estimated
places; over the free subspace, the mean squares of eta
on noise. Error has exactly two sources, and the split
says, place by place, which source each place pays.

The split is this paper's form of a move this tracker
has met before. Off-policy evaluation (1.2.110) also
prices by splitting — a fitted part, and a weighted
leftover that corrects it — two parts that cover each
other. The discipline of the split is the discipline of
cluster M: measure, then change (1.2.97) — each place is
priced both ways before it is assigned, and the
assignment follows the measure. And the sorting itself,
a set split in two by one priced test, is the move of
the top-p rule (1.2.70): keys kept by the mass they
carry, places kept by the price they beat — in both, the
split is the computation. What the split reads like when
the set is a cone of rising signals stands at 1.2.129.
As taken in by: Paper 012 (Chapter 19).
# Adaptive local windows — the isotonic rate: local variation is the price

For one set, the paper prices the projection whole, with
an elementary proof of its own (its Theorem 6, proved in
its Appendix A): the monotone cone (1.3.151), the set of
signals that never fall as the places run. The sharp
rate is a sum over places of the signal itself. Around
each place i, look at the windows that run to the right
and to the left, and take the largest length k over
which the variation of the signal inside the window
stays at
or below sigma over the square root of k — the lengths
k^+_{*,i} and k^-_{*,i}. The rate is sigma^2 times the
sum, over all places, of the sum of the two inverse
lengths. The bounds match with plain constants — the
mean square error stands between one fixed share of
the rate and ten times it — for any signal, any sigma,
any N: the first full pricing of its kind, as the paper
states it. The window is adaptive: where the signal
stands flat, the window runs long and the place is
cheap; where the signal moves, the window stops short
and the place pays. The decomposition of 1.2.128 is
here read off the flat runs of the signal itself.

The cases make the reading plain. A signal of two steps
pays sigma^2 log N — where the older characterization
of the field (the Theorem 5 the paper names) gives
nothing at all, its reading standing at zero whenever
any two places side by side are equal. A straight signal
of slope v pays in three stages as sigma and v move:
a plateau of log N, a middle rate growing as
N sigma^{4/3} v^{2/3}, and a ceiling at N sigma^2. A
signal of K flat plateaus pays sigma^2 K log(eN/K) —
and those signals are exactly the worst case of the
cone. And the case the rider on 1.2.116 stands on: a
dose-response signal — plateau, transition, plateau —
beside another of the same total variation, the two
priced a polynomial factor apart. The price follows
the local variation around each place, not the total
variation of the whole. The rate off the smallest stage
stands at 1.2.130. As taken in by: Paper 012
(Chapter 19).
# Han's reduction — effective noise, and the transfer from the sequence model to regression

The smallest stage of 1.3.145 is a stage: signals seen
directly, through noise of one spread. The paper's last
result carries the pricing off the stage. By a reduction
of Han (2023), as the paper uses it, isotonic regression
in the Gaussian linear regression setting — a rising
rule fitted to cases that come with their own inputs —
is priced by the sequence model at an effective noise:
the spread sigma divided by the square root of n, the
count of cases. Read at that spread, the window sum of
1.2.129 gives explicit instance-specific sharp rates in
probability for the regression setting, under the one
standing assumption the paper names (its Assumption 1).

The transfer is the only bridge of the cluster out of the
sequence model, and its guards are the paper's own: the
rates it carries are asymptotic — they hold as the count
of cases grows — and they stand under the named
assumption, not free of it. What crosses is the shape of
the price: local variation, read through windows, at a
spread the count of cases sets. What does not cross is
any claim about signals the cone does not hold, or about
designs the assumption does not cover (Q56). As taken in
by: Paper 012 (Chapter 19).
# Role weights — the two shares of one observation

Cross-validation (1.3.156) judges a fitted model on
cases it did not train on, by turns: the data are
split, again and again, into a part that trains and a
part that validates (1.3.157), and the held-out scores
are averaged into one estimate. In that judging, one
observation plays two roles. When it is held out, its
own score is one of the scores the estimate averages.
When it trains, it helps fit the models that score
the others. Paper 013 gives each role a weight, and
the whole theory of the paper is read off the two
weights.

The validation weight of an observation is its share
of all the validation evaluations the procedure makes
— the number of splits that hold it out, over the
total number of held-out scores. The training weight
is its share of all the training use: over the splits
that train on it, the own share of the split in the
estimate times its share of the training set of that split. Each set of weights sums to one over the data,
so each is a dealing of the whole estimate, or the
whole training, among the observations.

The designs of the field deal the weights differently.
Under K-fold, every observation is validated once per
partition and trains in the other K-1 folds, and both
weights stand at 1/n for every observation: the design
is BALANCED. Under Monte Carlo splitting the count of
validations is a draw, so the weights lean on the draw
— and the two weights are tied: an observation
validated more often trains less often, in exact
proportion. Under the bootstrap the two weights are
separate draws, neither fixing the other. A design
that deals uneven weights pays for the uneven dealing in
variance (1.2.135); a balanced design pays nothing.

Two held ideas stand near. The estimate itself is a
judge of a fitted model, as off-policy evaluation
(1.2.110) is a judge of a rule: both price a thing
they did not run as it will run. And a weight that
deals a whole among parts, by the share each part
carries, is the move of the budget that follows the
mass (1.2.70) — there the mass is attention, here it
is the two roles of judging. What the weights feed
stands at 1.2.132. As taken in by: Paper 013
(Chapter 20).
# The two influences — what one observation moves, in each role

A weight says how much of the estimate an observation
carries; an influence says how much it moves. Paper
013 separates the two roles of 1.2.131 into two
influences, each a function of the one observation,
with the rest of the data averaged out.

The VALIDATION influence of an observation is its
held-out score with the training data averaged away:
what its score is expected to be, given only itself.
Its spread over the data is the constant sigma_V^2.
The TRAINING influence is the other direction: fix a
value z, and ask how much the expected performance of
the fitted model moves when one of its training
observations stands at z, scaled by the training size
so the number stays of size one as the data grow. Its
spread is sigma_T^2. The two influences of the same
observation can lean together or against each other;
their co-movement is the third constant, gamma_0, and
it can stand negative.

Added, the two influences make the COMBINED influence
of the observation — the single function whose spread
is the first-order price of the whole judging:
V = sigma_V^2 + sigma_T^2 + 2 gamma_0. The three
constants are constants of the model, the metric, and
the population — not of the design. The design enters
only through the weights of 1.2.131, and the variance
identity of 1.2.133 is the meeting of the two.

One special case governs much of the older practice of the field. When a learner is judged by the very loss
it was fit to make small, and smoothly, the training
influence dies at the limit: the fitted model stands
at a smallest point of that loss, where a step in any
direction moves the expected loss by nothing at first
order. Then sigma_T^2 and gamma_0 vanish, and only
the validation term remains — a fixed-model variance
formula is right at first order for such a pairing,
and wrong for a mismatched one, as when a model fit
by likelihood is reported by the Brier score.

The training influence is kin to a held concept of
this cluster: the own-observation effect (1.2.113),
where the reading of a case by a walk is bent by the case
having trained the walk. Both price the same fact —
a case inside the fit moves the reading — one for a
walk in high dimensions, one for any fit at all. As
taken in by: Paper 013 (Chapter 20).
# The variance identity — the price of a judging, exactly

The central result of Paper 013 is an identity, not a
bound: at every sample size, for every design that
trains on the same count of cases in every split, the
variance of the cross-validated estimate is a finite
series whose terms are known in shape. The first
term is a quadratic form in the role weights of
1.2.131, with the three constants of 1.2.132: sigma_V^2
times the sum of the squares of the validation weights, plus
sigma_T^2 times the sum of the squares of the training
weights, plus twice gamma_0 times the sum of the
products. All the design contributes, at this order,
is three numbers — the role moments, the averaged
sums of those squares and products over the dealing
of the splits.

Above the first order, the series continues by the
size of the sets of observations that move the
estimate together. The joint effects are split by the
sets that carry them (1.3.159): pairs of observations
first — one validated while the other trains, in both
directions, and two training together — each order
with its own constants, the pair constants tau^2,
kappa, sigma_chi^2 and xi among them, and its own
weights of that order. Every term of the series is
never below zero, so every stopping point of the series is
a lower bound on the variance: the identity can be
read partway and never states more than what it has read.

The simplest case shows the shape whole. When the
fitted model is the mean of its training set, judged
by square error, each held-out score is a square in
the observations, the joint effects stop at pairs,
and the identity ends at order two, with all four
pair constants standing at 4 sigma^4 in size — the
two kinds of pair interaction equal in size, and xi
against them in sign, an opposition the fold-count
laws of 1.2.136 turn on.

Two guards stand on the identity. It is proved under
one standing condition on the data, the design, and
the model — cases drawn independently, splits drawn
independently of the data, a fit that treats its
training cases alike — and a second condition that
bounds how much of the variance the joint effects
carry, which the first-order reading needs and the
full identity does not. The bootstrap, whose training
sets have no fixed size, stands outside the identity
as stated and is brought under its first order by a
condition of its own, on how the fit answers to a
case counted twice.

The identity is the second floor of this cluster. The
floor the classical theory sets for any judge
(1.2.116) bounds what the answer of a judge can be worth;
the identity prices what the answer of a judge costs to
trust, and its lowest term — the sampling variance of
1.2.134 — no design at the same training size can go
below. As taken in by: Paper 013 (Chapter 20).
# Sampling variance and split noise — the two parts of the price

The identity of 1.2.133 fixes the splits and changes
the data. Paper 013 then turns the conditioning
around, and the variance of the estimate splits into
two parts with different owners.

The SAMPLING variance is the variance the estimate
would keep if the splitting were repeated without
end: the variance, over datasets, of the estimate
averaged over all dealings of the splits. It depends
on the design only through the count of training
cases. Every design at a given training size has the
same sampling variance, and it is a floor: no
repetition of the splitting brings the variance
below it, because repetition averages the dealing,
not the data.

The SPLIT NOISE is the rest: the variance the dealing
of the splits adds at a fixed dataset, averaged over
datasets. It is the only part of the price where
designs at one training size differ at all. At first
order it is exactly the imbalance penalty of 1.2.135.
For K-fold it has a closed law of its own: repeat the
partition R times, independently, and the variance is
the sampling variance plus the split noise of one
partition divided by R — exactly, under the standing
condition alone. Repetition buys down only the noise
of the dealing; the sampling floor stands untouched.

Two older results of the field fall out of the split.
Burman's finding, that the number of repetitions of
K-fold enters the variance only at the second order,
is the law above read at the orders where the split noise of K-fold
lives. And the corrected variance of
Nadeau and Bengio for Monte Carlo splitting is exact
at first order only under one named balance of the
constants — twice p times gamma_0 equal to (q - p)
times sigma_T^2, the condition for two split estimates
to stand correlated by exactly q — which the
matched-loss case of 1.2.132 meets in the limit, and
a mismatched metric need not meet at all, the error
then standing in either direction.

The split is the paper's answer to a confusion the
field carried for decades: the spread of repeated
cross-validation on one dataset feels like the
uncertainty of the estimate, and it is not — it is
the smaller, removable part, measured again at
1.2.138. As taken in by: Paper 013 (Chapter 20).
# The imbalance penalty — uneven weights are variance

At a fixed training size, designs differ only in
split noise (1.2.134), and at first order the split
noise has one source: the dealing of the role weights
of 1.2.131 unevenly. Write each weight as 1/n plus a
departure. The departures sum to zero, the cross
terms fall away, and what remains is a quadratic form
in the departures with the co-movement of the two
influences of 1.2.132 as its matrix. That remainder
is the IMBALANCE PENALTY, Q_D. It is never below zero for
every design, and it is zero exactly when the design
is balanced.

Read design by design, the penalty is explicit.
K-fold, with any number of repetitions, pays nothing:
its weights do not depart. Monte Carlo splitting pays
(1/nS) times (p/q sigma_V^2 + q/p sigma_T^2 - 2
gamma_0), and the bootstrap pays (1/nS) times (p/q
sigma_V^2 + sigma_T^2 - 2 gamma_0): both deal
validation counts by draw, and the penalty falls for each
only as the count of splits grows — at a fixed count
of splits the penalty stands at the same order as the
variance itself, not below it.

The penalty is the first order of a general law
(Theorem 4 of the paper). A design can be balanced
order by order: its weights of order k — the weights
on the joint effects of k observations — either vary
with the dealing or they do not. A design balanced
through order k has no split noise through order k,
and its variance is the sampling variance through
that order; every other design at the same training
size stands above that floor by its penalty and its
higher split noises. Under the dominance condition, a
design is variance-optimal at its training size, up
to the order named, exactly when its penalty and
split noises fall below that order.

Balance at the second order has a name and a price.
PAIR BALANCE asks that every pair of observations
share a fold equally often across the repetitions;
random partitions do not give it, and built ones make
the second-order split noise exactly zero — the paper
checks this on a built design, where the pair-balanced
split noise of the sample mean stands at zero to
machine precision while an unbalanced dealing of the
same count stands measurably above the floor (20.1.3).
But exact pair balance costs at least K(n-1)/(K-1)
fits — more than leave-one-out costs — and the paper's
verdict is plain: when the sampling variance does not
rise with the training size, pair balance is generally
not worth its compute. As taken in by: Paper 013
(Chapter 20).
# Fold counts — the count of folds is not the price

The standing worry of the field about K-fold is that more
folds mean more overlap between training sets, and
more overlap must mean more variance. The identity of
1.2.133 shows the worry reads the action of overlap wrong.
Overlap makes held-out scores move together, through
sigma_T^2 and gamma_0 — but for K-fold those
co-movements total (sigma_T^2 + 2 gamma_0)/n at first
order for EVERY K, because the training weight of every observation
stands at 1/n whatever the fold count.
The count of folds reaches the first-order variance
only through the training size it sets.

So, when the combined influence of 1.2.132 comes to rest at
one limit as the training size grows, the first-order
variance of K-fold is V_infinity / n for every K and
every count of repetitions: K-INVARIANCE. The law has
a named failure. If a fit leans on the full sample
size and not only on its training set — a penalty
set at c times n, of the kind that pulls the fit in,
dealt to a training set of size pn, bites at c/p per case — the limit itself
moves with K, and invariance falls with it. Tuning
inside the training set, by an inner cross-validation
of that set alone, does not break it.

Leave-one-out is the family's limit and, under named
conditions, its floor. It deals no splits at all, so
its split noise is zero at every order, and its
variance is the sampling variance at training size
n-1. When the sampling variance does not rise as the
training size grows, no repeated K-fold stands below
it, up to the second order; and when in addition the
pair co-movement xi is not positive, the variance does
not rise with K, so at a fixed budget of fits one run
with as many folds as the budget allows beats the
same budget spent repeating fewer folds. Repetition,
read through 1.2.134, converges to the own
sampling variance of K-fold — never to that of leave-one-out.

The second order is where fold counts do their real
work: the split noise of one partition falls as
G(K)/n^2, with G(K) built from the pair constants —
the first and third of its terms never below zero and
falling in K, the middle term carrying the sign of
xi, the opposition the sample-mean case of 1.2.133
shows standing negative. For metrics of pairs the
whole law turns, and stands at 1.2.137. As taken in
by: Paper 013 (Chapter 20).
# Pair metrics — when the unit of judging is a pair

Some scores are not paid case by case but pair by
pair: the area under the curve (1.3.160) reads a case
and a control together and pays for their order. The
identity of 1.2.133 extends to such metrics — the
case of pairs is Theorem 3 of the paper — with three
changes: the judging conditions on the labels, which
fix which pairs count; each observation is weighed
through the pairs it stands in, within its own class;
and a third kind of joint effect appears, the pair
interaction, the part of the score of a pair that stands
on both its members together. The constants split by
class, and the training constant gains a LABEL PART:
a training case shifts the class mix of the training
set, and with it the expected score. At first order a
metric of pairs then behaves as a case metric with
its own constants, and the training terms that
fixed-model formulas miss — DeLong's variance among
them — stand at (sigma_T^2 + 2 gamma_A)/n.

The design law of 1.2.136 breaks here, in one named
place. Ordinary K-fold judges only the pairs that
share a validation fold: one K-th of all case-control
pairs. Each partition averages the pair interaction
over a different K-th, and the split noise this adds
— the PAIR-COVERAGE COST — grows in K, at
lambda_4 (K-1) / n^2 with lambda_4 the pair
the spread of the interaction divided by the product of the
class shares, so it grows again as the outcome grows
rare. More folds raise the training size and lower
the coverage at once, and an in-between fold count
can stand best: the one place in the paper where the
fold-count law of the case metrics does not carry.

The repair is a design, not a correction. PAIRED
K-fold splits each class into K folds and fits all
K^2 pairings of a case fold with a control fold, so
every case-control pair is judged exactly once: the
sampling variance stands as it stood, and the
coverage cost is removed whole. It is never worse
than ordinary K-fold dealt class by class at the same
count of repetitions, and its limit, as the folds
shrink to single cases and single controls, is
LEAVE-PAIR-OUT — the leave-one-out for pairs, which
judges every pair once and carries no split noise at
all. Judging by pairs must balance the pairs, not
only the cases: the dealing of 1.2.131 has a second
part, for the pairs. As taken in by: Paper 013 (Chapter 20).
# The bell limit and the audit of variance estimators — what an interval must carry

The identity prices the variance; the paper's last
theorem says what shape the own spread of the estimate
takes. Under one standing condition, and a set of
difference conditions — no one observation moves the
estimate by a share of its own spread that fails to
vanish, and the effect of one observation leans on the
others weakly, or strongly on only a few — the
estimate, scaled by the root of n, ends at the bell
law (1.3.161) centered at the target, with the FULL
variance the identity gives at the realized splits,
higher orders included. The proof runs by a normal
approximation for functions of independent inputs
(Chatterjee's), not by a sum of terms, so it stands
even where the joint effects carry a first-order
share of the variance and the older stability route
fails — a case the paper builds and checks by hand
(20.1.3). For a balanced design the limiting variance
is V_infinity; for Monte Carlo and the bootstrap it
stands higher by the imbalance penalty of the realized
splits, which stays in the limit and must stay in the
interval.

With a consistent reading of that variance, Wald
intervals and tests stand valid. What such a reading
must carry is the audit the paper then runs over the
standing estimators of the field. The standard estimate —
the spread of the held-out scores, divided by n —
reads the validation term alone and misses
C_ell = (sigma_T^2 + 2 gamma_0)/n, the co-movement
held-out scores take on through the training cases
their models share; the intervals built on it
undercover, as Bates and his co-workers measured.
DeLong's variance, and the influence-curve variance
that holds the fit of each fold fixed, miss the same
training terms for the area under the curve (1.2.137).
The spread of repeated cross-validation on one
dataset reads the split noise of 1.2.134, which under
the dominance condition is smaller than the order of
the variance itself — as a standard error it
vanishes against the thing it claims to measure.
One standing method reads the first-order sampling
variance whole, training terms included: a weighted
bootstrap of the whole procedure, with the split
noise fitted out of each replicate (Cai and
co-workers) — at the price of thousands of fits, and
blind to the higher orders the theorem keeps. And
under the audit stands a proved limit: from the
held-out scores of one run, no unbiased estimate of
the K-fold variance exists at all (Bengio and
Grandvalet). A third road is named, not built: draw
fresh datasets from an estimated population and run
the procedure again on each, splits and all — it
reproduces every part of the price, to the measure
the estimated population is true.

The audit reaches back to the start of this cluster.
A judge is priced three ways in cluster O now: for
its bias against the rule it never ran (1.2.110), for
how sharp its price is at the case in hand
(1.2.125), and here for the spread of its own reading
— and the measurement agenda of cluster A (1.2.14)
gains a worked case of its standing warning, that a
grade without its variance is half a grade. As taken
in by: Paper 013 (Chapter 20).
# Monte Carlo Graph Search — the search that folds its own tree

The search of Paper 014 grows a tree of proof
states (1.3.162): from a state, an action — a piece
of Lean code — is tried, and the states it leaves
stand under it as children. Left alone, the tree
wastes itself: the same state is reached by many
paths and grown again at every reaching. The
paper's move is to fold the tree wherever two
states count as the same (1.2.140): the tree
becomes a graph, and every later path to a folded
state feeds the one shared node. Over the graph,
the search is Monte Carlo in the plain sense
(1.3.163): it grows where sampled tries and a
learned sense of promise point, not by a fixed
order of trying. The name the paper gives it is
Monte Carlo Graph Search. It stands in the line of
the hypertree proof search and of the self-play
line of AlphaZero, with the folding as its own
step: a search whose memory is a graph, so that
work done once is never priced twice. What the
graph costs and what chooses inside it stand at
1.2.140 and 1.2.142; what the search is for is the
law at 1.2.141. As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# When two proof states count as one — sameness, and the folding it buys

The folding of 1.2.139 turns on a stated sameness
(1.3.166). Two states count as the same when their
goal expressions agree, their local contexts
agree, and the names of their local variables
agree. Two actions count as the same when the
changes they make agree under that sameness. Every
fold is priced by this rule and by nothing else:
no judgment call, no learned score — an exact
match of the written state.

The paper names the corner where the rule is not
strictly true. A tactic can read more than the
written state — the whole standing of the system
around it — and for such a tactic (the paper names
aesop) two states the rule calls the same can have
unlike futures. The folding then joins states whose
true children differ, and the graph the search
trusts is, at those joints, wrong. The paper works
around the corner in practice; it does not close
it, and the want of a sameness that is sound for
tactics that read the world stands in this scroll
as the open edge of the concept. A second cost is
stated plainly: a step of the folded search can
cost as the whole graph where a step of the tree
cost as the depth — a worst case the authors say
they do not meet in practice. Sameness here is
kin to the two equalities of the kernel's world
(1.2.43): there, what counts as the same is fixed
by the type theory; here, by a rule of thumb with
a named hole. As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# The AND/OR law of proof search — one success proves a state, all successes prove an action

Over the states and actions of the search the
paper lays one law, and the whole search is the
working out of the law. A STATE is proved when
any single one of its actions succeeds: the state
asks for one road through, no more — an OR. An
ACTION succeeds only when every state it leaves
behind is proved: an action that splits the work
owes all of the pieces — an AND. Proof search is
then a minimax over the two kinds of node, and a
proof, when one is found, is the subgraph the law
marks out (1.2.146).

One addition keeps the law honest about failure.
Every single-goal state also carries a move to the
negation of its goal: the search may spend its
budget trying to prove that the goal is false, and
a goal shown false is pruned with its whole branch
— the budget is not spent only on roads that might
succeed, but also on closing roads that cannot.
The law is old in the theory of games and of
search; its seat here is that the AND side is not
a choice but a debt created by the action itself,
and the kernel (1.2.41) is the only judge of
whether the debt of a leaf is paid. As taken in by:
Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Choosing where to search — confidence bounds, a prior from samples, and the bottleneck first

A search that can try anything must choose, and
the paper's choosing has three parts. First, the
score: a form of the PUCT rule — each action
carries a value from the tries so far and a bonus
for being under-tried, weighted by a prior sense
of which actions are promising, and the search
takes the action of highest upper confidence
bound. Second, the prior: not the model's own
sequence scores, which the paper rejects for a
named reason — a score paid per written step
penalizes an action that can be written many
equivalent ways, paying for the writing and not
the move. The prior is instead the plain share of
samples: how often the model, asked to act at this
state, offers this action. Third, the widening:
the space of Lean actions is without bound, so new
actions are let into a state only slowly, as the
visits to the state grow — the search deepens its
menu at the pace of its attention.

Then, the aim: having chosen an action, the search
goes next to the hardest state the action leaves —
the resulting state of lowest lower confidence
bound. The bottleneck of the proof is attacked first,
in the paper's own words, and the easy children
wait. Kinship: the budget that follows the mass
(1.2.70) spends attention where the mass is; this
rule spends it where the proof is stuck. As taken
in by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# One model, two jobs — the same network as policy and as value

The search needs two readings at every state:
which actions to offer (the policy) and how
promising the state is (the value). The paper
gives both jobs to one model. The same large
transformer, with the same prompt but for a task
token that says which job is being asked, computes
the value that guides the search and the actions
that feed it. There is no second, smaller value
network, of the kind the older self-play systems
carried as a separate part.

The economy is real and so is the coupling: one
training run improves both readings at once, and
one model's blind spots are shared by both — the
hand that offers the moves and the eye that prices
the position are the same organ. The paper's
training (1.2.147) leans on the sharing: proofs the
search finds train the policy, and the states
inside those proofs, with their near neighbors
that failed, train the value — two lessons drawn
from one body of work, into one body. As taken in
by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Informal comments — the visible memory a search leaves inside its proofs

The actions of the search are not code alone. An
action may carry informal comments — natural
language remarks written into the growing proof —
and the comments stay: later states see them, and
the finished proof keeps them. Beside the hidden
chain of thought (1.2.145), which is spent and
gone, the comments are the visible
memory of the search: what it thought it was doing at each step,
left where the next step can read it.

The paper reports how the habit was made. Early in
training, comment-writing was enforced by hand;
later, the training alone sustained it — the
system kept the remarks because the remarks paid.
All three outputs — the hidden thought, the
comments, the code — are trained together, grown
together, in the paper's word co-evolved. One
consequence lands in the worked cases: where an
informal draft proof was wrong and the formalizing
repaired it (1.2.152), the comments of the final
proof follow the repaired, formal structure — the
memory records the proof that stands, not the
draft that failed. As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# The hidden chain of thought, and a thinking budget that is set as it goes

Before the model offers an action, it may think —
a hidden chain of thought, not written into the
proof and not open to later states. How long it
thinks is not fixed: the thinking budget is set
dynamically, as the search goes. The hidden
thought and the visible comments (1.2.144) divide
the two kinds of memory a reasoning system can
keep: one private and spent at once, one public
and carried.

The budget does a second job at training time.
Which search traces are worth training on is a
choice every self-training system must make
(1.2.147), and the paper uses the thinking spent
as one of its signals: the budget of a trace says
something about what the trace cost and what it is
worth. The thought itself stays hidden even from
the system's own later steps — a design the paper
states without defending at length, and which
stands here as stated: the reasoning that picks
the move is not part of the proof, and only the
move, its comment, and its result are carried
forward. As taken in by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# A proof is an acyclic subgraph — what the search hands back

When the search ends in success, what it holds is
not yet a proof. The paper defines the proof
inside the search graph: a part of the graph with
a single source state, only successful actions,
exactly one action chosen at each state, every
state those actions leave behind, and no cycles —
an acyclic subgraph that satisfies the AND/OR law
at every node it touches (1.2.141). Anything more
that the search grew — the failed branches, the
extra actions — is not proof and is cut away.

Then a plain labor: the extracted proof is
passed through the suggestions of the linter, its
tactic code cleaned, and offline work makes it
shorter and more plain, before the whole is rendered as
one self-standing Lean file and given to the
kernel (1.2.156). The definition does real work: it is what makes the success of the search a
thing that can be checked by a reader who never
saw the search — the graph is the finding, the
subgraph is the claim, and the kernel is the
judge. As taken in by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Expert iteration — the prover trains on the proofs its own search finds

The training of the system is a loop with the
search inside it, in the manner of expert
iteration. The search, guided by the model as it
stands, finds proofs. The proofs found become
training: the policy is trained on them, after
filters — measures of whether a proof does real
work, so that trivial finds do not teach; and,
where an informal proof stands behind the formal
one, a judge that asks whether the formal proof
stays true to the informal one, with proofs that
drift from their sketch filtered out — an
informal check given a formal judge, kin to the
error floor of an informal check (1.2.63). The value is
trained on the states inside the found proofs,
together with near states that were shown false,
or that stayed not proved after real effort — the map
of what almost worked is part of the lesson. And
proofs found for states deep inside a search are
written out again as if they had been the root
problem — the hindsight move — so no found work is
wasted for being found in passing.

Then the trained model searches again, and the
loop turns. The system's strength is therefore not
a fixed gift of its first training: it is the
standing of a loop in which the kernel-checked
search is both the worker and the teacher, and
every claim the loop teaches from has been proved
before it is learned from (1.2.41). As taken in
by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Autoformalization at scale — statements formalized, judged, and corrected by machine

Expert iteration (1.2.147) eats statements, and
the world holds more mathematical statements than
any team can formalize by hand. So the paper built
the supply line as a machine of its own: statements
are taken from open collections and the team's own
stores, a first formalizing is made of each, the
formalizing is judged by signals from the Lean
REPL — does it stand as written, does it say a
thing that can be worked on — and the judged
failures are sent back for correction. The same
three-beat shape — formalize, take the machine's
answer, correct — that the lemma pipeline runs at
the scale of one problem (1.2.150), run here at
the scale of a training corpus.

The word for the act is new to this phext and is
defined at 1.3.167. What the machine buys is
volume with a floor under it: no statement enters
training on the model's say-so alone, because the
judging beat is the kernel's world answering back
(1.2.156). What it does not buy is staying true —
a statement can stand in Lean and still say less,
or other, than the prose it came from; the judging
named in the paper checks standing, and the
deeper check, a judge of staying true, is named
only for proofs (1.2.147), not for the statement
supply. As taken in by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Test-time training — the prover trains again on its own attempts before it answers

The strangest loop in the paper turns at the time
of use. Faced with a hard problem, the system
first attempts it, with the lemmas the outer loop
offers (1.2.150). If the problem stands not proved,
the model is retrained — on the search traces of
those very attempts, the found subproofs and the
mapped failures — and then attempts again, a
changed prover facing the same problem.

The paper reports what the loop bought, in words
and not numbers: problems fell to it that the
starting model, at the same search budget, had not
solved; work on one lemma sketch taught the model
things that paid on another — a cross-pollination
between sketches; lemma work ran faster during the
Olympiad run itself; and the model learned the
corners of the library it rarely meets, by meeting
them. No count of the turns of the loop and no
stopping rule are stated — the machine as described can
turn again whenever a problem resists, and what
ends it in practice is a budget the paper does not
name (21.1.5). The kinship in this phext is with
the runtime loops of cluster B, which improve the
worker while it works; here the improvement is of
the weights themselves, at the time of the test,
from the own traces of the test. As taken in by:
Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# The lemma pipeline — an informal proof, broken into lemmas short enough to prove

Above the search stands the system's second part,
and the two meet at the lemma. Given a theorem,
the pipeline first asks for an informal proof of
it, in natural language. It then asks for that
proof rebuilt as a sequence of lemmas — a chain of
smaller claims, each building on the ones before,
each with a proof short enough to be attempted.
It asks for the statements of the lemmas
formalized into Lean, sends the formalizings to
the REPL, and carries the error messages back for
correction. The search (1.2.139) is then set to
work with the lemmas standing in its opening
block: prove the first lemma, prove the second,
then the theorem.

The paper is plain that the pipeline is noisy:
gaps in the informal proof and errors of
formalizing survive the correction beat. What it
claims is more plain still — in practice the chain
that comes through carries enough sound, usable
lemmas to better the search alone by a margin the
paper asserts and does not measure (21.1.4). The
pipeline descends from the draft, sketch, and
prove line of earlier work, with the REPL's answer
promoted from a check to a teacher. Each of its
steps of asking is itself several smaller steps
of asking,
giving the model room to weigh and mend its own
words before they are fixed into Lean. As taken in
by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Lemma revision — the chain is re-cut under the answer of the kernel

When the chain of the pipeline fails to carry
the theorem, the system does not start over: it reads
the failure. The lemma list comes back marked —
each lemma proved, or not proved — and a revised
list is asked for: the proved lemmas kept, new
lemmas added beside them, the new statements
formalized and corrected through the REPL as
before, and the search set to work again.

The revision is told to weigh two causes, and the
pair is the concept. Either the strategy itself
cannot work, and must be changed wholly; or the
strategy is sound and the lemmas are too coarse —
each still too big for the search to prove
directly — and must be cut smaller. A failure read
as the first cause when it was the second wastes a
sound plan; read as the second when it was the
first, it wastes its work on a dead one. The marking of the
list is the formal system's answer carried back
into the informal reasoning that planned it: the
loop of 1.2.150 with its eyes open. Kinship:
cluster P (1.2.117-1.2.123) reads the errors of
reasoning systems
from outside; here the system reads its own,
mid-work, as an instrument. As taken in by:
Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Formalization as error repair — the formal draft catches what the prose draft hid

One worked case of the paper shows a thing the
model did not hold as a concept. The informal
proof drafted for Problem 4 ran three senses of
falling together — strictly falling, weakly
falling, and falling at a point — in one smooth
argument that reads as sound. Formalizing the
argument forced the three apart, because Lean
keeps no such blur, and the proof that stands is
built on the separated senses: the formalizing
repaired the informal proof, and the own comments of the final
proof follow the repaired shape (1.2.144).

The same instrument, turned on a finished human
text, found more. Over chapters of a standing
textbook of analysis, formalized in its author's
own Lean build of it, the system found four
exercises false as written — each shown false by a
counterexample (1.3.168) — and two more whose
stated hypotheses it proved needless. The findings
were taken up by the author; the book's record now
stands corrected. A formalization is usually read
as a translation, faithful or not; here it works
as an audit — the prose is made to answer
questions it was never asked in prose, and where
it cannot, the prose, and not the formalizing, is
what gives. As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# Invented auxiliary objects — the prover mints the thing the proof needs

Twice in the worked cases, the proof turns on a
thing that is not in the problem. For Problem 3,
the system defined a set of its own — the primes
at which the function of the problem takes a value
above one — and the final proofs of several lemmas
run through that set, though the statement of the
problem never suggests it. For Problem 5, it
defined a helper quantity of its own, an invariant
built from the count of the round, and read the game's
balance off its settled value.

The move is the oldest move of the mathematician: when the
given objects will not carry the argument, define
a new one that will. What is new is who makes it —
a search system, inside a formal proof, where the
invented thing must itself be defined in Lean and
every use of it kernel-checked, so the invention
is not a metaphor but a definition that has to
stand. Kinship: the capability profile of cluster
A (1.2.14) prices what a system can do; this is
one of the doings that prices highest in human
mathematics too — not the proving of what is
posed, but the posing of what makes proving
possible. As taken in by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Yuclid — a geometry engine of deduction over a diagram

Plane geometry is not given to the Lean search.
It goes to the third part of the system: Yuclid, a
fast engine in C++ in the line of AlphaGeometry,
built on a deductive database with algebraic
reasoning — a store of geometry facts and rules,
closed step by step: rules whose hypotheses the
store holds fire, their conclusions join the
store, and the algebraic side keeps the measures
of the figure — lengths, angles, ratios — in
tables that answer what the rules need.

The paper's numbers for it are the hardest in the
paper. On the AG-30 set of geometry problems it
solves 17 of 30 in about 0.4 seconds on a single
core, and reaches saturation — nothing more can be
drawn — on the rest; it runs, in the paper's
words, up to 500 times faster than
AlphaGeometry-1. The speed is three mechanisms:
before the main loop, rules are matched
in numbers — only rules whose hypotheses and
conclusions hold in the drawn numbers are queued,
with named configurations spotted in advance; the
loop keeps, for each statement, its progress
toward proof, in a dictionary that keeps the same
statement from being worked twice; and the
algebraic reasoning is incremental, its table of
equations kept in reduced row form and updated per
new fact, each waiting statement stored already
reduced by what is known, so work resumes rather
than restarts. The engine solves outside Lean;
how its results are carried into machine-verified
form is asserted as a requirement and nowhere
described — the gap stands as Q61. New rules are
also mined: problems that needed a helping
construction, up to four points, were turned into
rules for the store. As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# Algebraic tables for geometry — square lengths, and equations that stand as statements

The algebraic side of Yuclid (1.2.154) keeps
its measures in tables, and the paper's extensions of
the engine are extensions of the tables. Beside
the standing tables of lengths and of
log-lengths, a new table of square lengths:
with it, the theorem of Pythagoras and its general
form become facts the algebraic side can price
directly — two segments stand at right angles
exactly when the sum of the squares on one pairing
of their ends equals the sum on the other pairing.

And any equation standing in any of the three
tables may be wrapped up and used as a statement
in its own right, where the older engine needed a
named rule for each such use — the law of the
figure with equal opposite sides, for one,
falls out
without a rule of its own. One further extension
is priced and refused: adding the sines of angles
and the law of sines to the tables proves one more
AG-30 problem, at about ten times the cost, and
stands off in the configuration the paper
recommends. The pattern is the own
lesson of this tracker in another field: representation first —
what the tables can hold decides what the engine
can see (kin to the induced feature spaces of
cluster C, 1.2.28). As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# The stateless engine room — Lean checking as a service that holds nothing

Under the search stands an infrastructure the
paper describes in its appendix, and on which the
whole scale rests. The Lean REPL servers hold no
state. A request carries the full proof state it
concerns; any server can take any request, and the
servers — many of them, on machines with no
graphics processors, in a cloud fleet — answer
tactic by tactic as the searches ask. The typing of every step is checked as it
runs, eagerly, so errors are caught at the step
that makes them, not at the end.

When a proof is found, the verification is a
separate, slower road: the proof is rendered as
one self-standing Lean file, run through Lean
whole, and the run reports the kernel's verdict,
which axioms the proof used, and which lemmas it
needed (1.2.157). That no state is kept is the
concept: because no server remembers anything, the
search can be spread as wide as the fleet allows
and a dead server costs nothing but its in-flight
request — the checking of proofs, the trust the system
can least spare (1.2.41), is made as wide and
as cheap as plain computation. Kinship: the
shell of 1.2.53 holds its state in the kernel and
trusts the shell with nothing; here the servers
are trusted with nothing and hold nothing, and for
the same reason. As taken in by: Paper 014
(Chapter 21).
# Solved means kernel-complete — the standard a machine proof is judged by

The paper's first discipline is a definition.
A problem is solved when there stands a complete
proof of it in Lean 4 with the Mathlib library:
no gaps, and no axiom that is not sound — above
all not the sorryAx axiom, the standing confession that a step
was skipped. The finished proofs are audited for
the axioms they use (1.2.156), so the standard is
not a claim about the process but a check on the
artifact: whatever the search did, the proof
either passes the kernel whole or the problem
stands unsolved.

The definition does the paper's comparative work.
The other gold-level results of the same summer
were natural-language proofs, judged by humans;
this paper sets against them the sentence that
verifying a complicated natural language proof can
be almost as hard for a human as creating it. A
kernel-checked proof carries its verification with
it, at the price of the checker (1.2.41) — the
trust is small, named, and the same for every
reader. The assumption ledger of 1.2.55 priced
what a proof takes on trust, line by line; this
standard prices the claims of a whole field by
it:
solved is not a verdict pronounced on a system,
but a property any reader can re-run. The
fail-closed box of 1.2.56 is this standard worn
by a whole system: nothing the agent inside
produces counts until the kernel lets it out. As taken in
by: Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# Scaling a prover — three axes, and a claim that stands unmeasured

The abstract claims favorable scaling, and the
paper's whole account of it is a recipe of three
axes and a loop. One: a larger model — the
Olympiad run used a model of over 200 billion
parameters. Two: more parallel tries of the lemma
pipeline (1.2.150), sampling widely over informal
proofs and formal sketches. Three: the formal
feedback loop (1.2.151) turned more times on each
problem, for the correction it buys. And under
all three, test-time training (1.2.149) turning
the attempts themselves into training.

What the paper does not give is any measure of
the scaling: no curve, no counts of the parallel
tries or of the turns of the loop, no pairing of budget
spent to problems solved, and no cost of the
Olympiad run in time, machines, or money. The
claim therefore stands in this phext as the paper
leaves it — attributed, plausible on the
architecture, and unmeasured; the tracker prices
it as a direction, not a law, and the want of its
numbers stands as Q62. The contrast is the paper's
own: where it measures, it measures hard (0.4
seconds for Yuclid; five of six at the Olympiad);
where it scales, it asserts. As taken in by:
Paper 014 (Chapter 21).
# The word thermalization hypothesis — a book comes to rest like a gas

The paper's whole wager, named by its authors the WOrd
Thermalization Hypothesis (WOTH). The semantic laws, the
meanings, and the writer's concepts make links and
couplings between the words of a literary work; those
couplings drive the word frequency distribution to
thermalization — to the stationary thermal state of
statistical physics. The precise form of the couplings is
not known, and by the paper's argument need not be: as
with a gas of classical particles, almost any weak or
moderate coupling drives a system to its thermal state,
and the shape of that state is set by what the system
keeps, not by the details of the coupling. A finished
book, on this claim, is an equilibrium, and its frequency
law is an equilibrium law — the Rayleigh-Jeans law of
1.2.161, with the condensation of 1.2.162 standing in
every text large enough to read. As taken in by: Paper 015
(Chapter 22).
# Words, particles, households — one quantity in three tongues

The analogy that carries the paper, stated as one mapping
with three columns. The different words of a work stand
where classical oscillators (particles) stand in a
physical system, and where households stand in the problem
of wealth. The frequency of a word — its count of tokens
in the work — stands where the energy of a particle
stands, and where the wealth of a household stands: rare
words are the poor households, frequent words the rich,
and the ground of the spectrum is the word used once. The
analogy is not decoration: along it the paper imports the
whole machinery of the equilibrium — the two conservation
laws (1.2.161), the distribution and its condensation
(1.2.162) — and carries the economy's measuring curves
(Lorenz, Pareto) across with it, to be rebuilt for words
in 1.2.168. A note for the language models rides along:
words are cut into tokens, on average four thirds of a
token per English word, and tokens carry a price — so the
frequency of a word is also a cost, the writer's effort,
priced. As taken in by: Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# Two conservation laws, one equilibrium — the Rayleigh-Jeans law

The physics the paper stands on. A system of nonlinear
oscillators with amplitudes at modes of rising energy
carries two exact integrals of motion: the total energy,
and the total probability norm — the sum over the modes of
the square of the strength standing at each. Held with
both quantities fixed, and read for many modes through the
two prices, the equilibrium occupation of a mode (1.3.174)
of energy E is the Rayleigh-Jeans law (1.3.177): rho =
T/(E - mu), where the temperature T and the chemical
potential mu (1.3.176) are the two prices the two
conservation laws set, solved from the two sums the
conservation laws demand. For words the conservation laws
read across the analogy of 1.2.160: the total number of
tokens is the system's energy, and the total number of
different words is its norm. A critical energy stands at
the mean of the spectrum: under it, mu sits below the
ground level and T is positive; over it the temperature
turns negative — a branch the equations allow and this
paper names and sets aside. As taken in by: Paper 015
(Chapter 22).
# Rayleigh-Jeans condensation — the pile at the ground

At low total energy — the scaled energy far under the
critical mean of 1.2.161 — the Rayleigh-Jeans equilibrium
does the strange thing this paper is built on: a large
fraction of the whole probability collects at the lowest
modes, near the ground state, and only a small fraction
reaches the high states. This is condensation in a
classical field: no quantum step is involved; the two
conservation laws, pressing on a spectrum whose ground
sits at zero, leave the excess nowhere else to go. The
phenomenon was met first in light running through optical
fibers of many modes, where a beam cleans itself into the
lowest mode, and in the simulations of coupled oscillators
the authors' line works on. The paper's claim is that a
book does the same thing — and that the pile at the ground
can be counted in a text, by the device of 1.2.164 and the
counts of 1.2.165. As taken in by: Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# Constraint-driven condensation — the genus of the pile

Rayleigh-Jeans condensation is one member of a wider
family the paper places it in: condensation driven by
constraints, arising wherever a kept total must be shared
over states that have a floor. The family already holds
the piling of cars in traffic behind a slow stretch; the
running-together of grains in granular media; the setting
of a network into one piece; and the reading of financial
data. The shared shape: squeeze the kept quantity per
state low enough, and the excess stops spreading and
collects in a condensate at the boundary. Words (1.2.162)
and wealth (1.2.170) are the family's newest members, and
the genus is what makes their membership more than a
figure of speech — one mathematics of squeezing, in
systems that share nothing else: not their parts, not
their couplings, only the shape of the constraint. As
taken in by: Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# The reduced frequency — putting the once-used word at zero

The paper's small load-bearing shift. The wealth of a word
is set to its reduced frequency, w = f - 1: its frequency
minus one. A word that appears exactly once — the most
common fate of a word in any large work — then holds
exactly zero wealth, standing at the ground of the
spectrum where the condensate of 1.2.162 collects; a word
appearing twice holds one unit, and so on up. The shift
does two pieces of work. It makes the condensate a literal
pile at zero that can be counted in the data: the fraction
of different words standing at w = 0, the paper's h_min,
is the size of the condensate, read off a text. And it
starts all three of the paper's curves (1.2.168) where the
physics starts, at the ground — without it, the once-used
words would stand one step above the state the theory says
they fill. As taken in by: Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# The rare-word condensate — what the sixteen works show

Across the paper's sixteen data sets — fifteen literary
works in six languages and the American National Corpus —
the condensate is the headline count. The fraction of
different words appearing exactly once stands at about 45
percent, give or take 15; the fraction at zero reduced
frequency (1.2.164) runs from 0.317 to 0.658 across the
works. The bottom half of every vocabulary holds less than
half a percent of all the tokens of the work; the top
tenth of words holds 86 to 96 percent of them, and the top
hundredth 52 to 76 percent. Read as inequality, a book's
Gini coefficient (1.3.180) stands between 0.915 and 0.944
for single authors — past the wealth of the whole world
(0.843) and near the market value of the London Stock
Exchange (0.909). The curves of the sixteen works lie
close on one another in the right units, one steady state
under them all; the one outlier is the Corpus, a mixture
of many authors, whose Gini of 0.967 stands visibly higher
— a mixture, the paper holds, of writers each with their
own style, and so not one system (the question this leaves
stands at Q67). As taken in by: Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# The density of states is the model's free shape — RJS and RJE

The equilibrium law needs a spectrum to live on: a ladder
of levels, and a count of how many levels stand in each
stretch of it — the density of states (1.3.179). The paper
builds two ladders. The standard model (RJS) takes the
density constant, the levels evenly spaced; it gives the
shape of the data in coarse outline and no more. The
extended model (RJE) bends the ladder: the levels spread
exponentially, E_k = (e^(a k/N) - 1)/(e^a - 1), so the
density falls as the energy rises — few levels at the top,
as there are few rich households and few words of very
high frequency. The bend is one number, a, and the flat
ladder is its zero. The data demand a between about 5 and
11 — far from flat — with the largest bends for the Corpus
(10.7) and the Bible (9.05). Everything the paper fits is
two numbers: the bend a, and the scaled total energy, set
by the anchoring of 1.2.167. As taken in by: Paper 015
(Chapter 22).
# A fit anchored to inequality — the Gini match, then no more hands

The paper's fitting procedure, a small machine with a
discipline (M2 at 22.1.2). The scaled energy is not fit
freely: it is fixed by demanding that the model's Lorenz
curve carry exactly the Gini coefficient (1.3.180) of the
data — the whole inequality of the work, in one number,
matched before any shape is compared. Only then is the
bend a chosen, by the smallest curve distance between the
model's Lorenz curve and the data's. After those two
choices nothing is left in the hand: the Pareto and Zipf
curves (1.2.168) come out as predictions, drawn in units
the data itself supplies, wealth in units of its own
average. The discipline is the argument: the rival fits of
the field spend two parameters on a pure power law and
three on a shifted one, all on the one curve they then
report; this model spends its two on the Lorenz curve and
is checked on two curves it never touched. As taken in by:
Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# Three curves, one distribution — Lorenz, Pareto, Zipf

The Lorenz curve (cumulative share of tokens against
cumulative share of words, least first), the Pareto curve
(the fraction of words standing at or above a given
reduced frequency), and the Zipf curve (scaled frequency
against scaled rank) are three readings of one
distribution, not three laws. The paper makes the
identities plain: the rank of a word is exactly the Pareto
count of the words at its level or above, so the Zipf
curve is the Pareto curve with its axes exchanged — its
inverse, read the way language study reads. What the
equilibrium of 1.2.161 gives, it therefore gives to all
three at once: the extended model carries closed forms for
its Lorenz and Pareto curves, and its Zipf curve as the
inverse of the second. This is the test the paper sets the
field: a law that fits one curve and misses the others was
never a description of the distribution — and the shifted
power law, the standing rival, cannot be carried through
the Lorenz reading at all. As taken in by: Paper 015
(Chapter 22).
# The Zipf window — a power law with edges

Inside the equilibrium, the famous law appears — as a
middle stretch, not a foundation. The extended model's
Pareto curve bends over its whole range, but between two
edges its bend goes flat into the algebraic decay: for
reduced frequencies far above e^(-a) and far below the top
of the band, the curve runs as a constant over w, the
exponent-one law with amplitude near 1/a (1.2.166); in
rank terms the window stands between K/N far above
epsilon/a and far below epsilon e^a/a. Outside the window
the curve turns away at both ends, and the data turn with
it. Fitted over the window the sixteen works give
exponents from 0.999 to 1.17, most near 1.1 — the exponent
of the law is a reading taken through a window, not a
constant of language. The paper's verdict on a century of
Zipf fitting: the power law is what a thermal distribution
looks like when only its middle is looked at. As taken in
by: Paper 015 (Chapter 22).
# Wealth thermalization — the sibling the words inherit

The machinery came to words from households. In the
authors' own earlier work the same equilibrium, under the
same two conservation laws, describes the wealth of
households in the countries of the world — where half of
humanity holds two percent of the whole — the distribution
of world product over countries across fifty years, and
the capitalization of companies on a stock exchange; the
wider line of the same program carries it to party votes
in European elections and to the biomass of wild mammal
species. In each system, agents coupled by exchanges
settle toward the Rayleigh-Jeans state, and the poor phase
and the phase of the very rich are the condensate
(1.2.162) and the tail of one distribution, not two facts.
The word paper is the program's move into language: the
claim that a book is one more population of coupled
agents, and its vocabulary one more population coming to
rest — the inheritance is the reason the curves of the
economy were already in the authors' hands when they
turned to texts (1.2.160). As taken in by: Paper 015
(Chapter 22).
# Concept: A logic read in a category — claims as things, proofs as ways
Source: Study S007, the post 'Categorical models of logics' (2026-10-12). NEW in Study S007.

Cook gives the pairing as a table. A proposition,
or formula, is an object of the category. A proof
of A from a context (the context is written Gamma
in the source; the word is founded at 1.3.74) is a
morphism from Gamma to A. Truth is the terminal
object, the thing 1, the one every thing has
exactly one way into. Its twin, the false claim, is the initial
object, the thing 0, the one with exactly one way
out to every thing. Conjunction, A and B, is the
product of A and B. A or B is the
coproduct of A and B (the word is defined at
1.3.191). Implication, if A then B, is the
exponential: the thing of all ways from A to B,
written as B over A. Negation, not A, is the same
exponential with the initial thing in the place of B.
The precision stands in the footnotes of the
source, and the study keeps it exact: the logic
of the table is, precisely, the intuitionistic
calculus of propositions (the word is defined
at 1.3.192), and the categories of the table are,
precisely, the bicartesian closed ones (the word
is defined at 1.3.193).
What the tracker held before: the same
correspondence read in types (1.2.40) — a claim
is a type, a proof is a term of the type, and the
rules of the logic pair off with the rules of
typing. Cook's table is the third leg of that
correspondence, the one the name of Lambek completes:
logic, types, categories — one pairing read three
ways, and the check of a proof is the same job in
each: in types it is the kernel's job (1.2.41);
in a category it is the shape of the ways, which
either compose or do not.
# Concept: Axiom K — the box is a functor
Source: Study S007, the post 'Categorical models of logics' (2026-10-12). NEW in Study S007.

Axiom K is required by every normal modal logic.
It says: the box of (if A then B) gives (if the
box of A then the box of B) — a proof under the
box of an implication yields, from a boxed A, a
boxed B. Read in a category, Cook's point is one
line long: the axiom says the box is a functor
(the word is defined at 1.3.190). The box maps
things to things — a proposition to the
proposition that it must hold — and maps ways to
ways: a proof goes to the proof of the boxed
claim, and the mapping keeps the ways whole, so
a chain of proofs under the box is the box of
the chain.
What the axiom buys: being normal is cheap to state
and strong in effect — whatever can be proved
can be proved under the box, step for step, so
reasoning inside the box mirrors reasoning
outside it. What it demands: an operator that
scrambles proofs — one whose boxed proofs cannot
be followed in order — is not a box in this
sense, whatever its name. The foundation of the
operators themselves stands at 1.3.187.
# Concept: A logic read in a space — the box as the interior
Source: Study S007, the post 'Topological models of modal logic' (2026-10-04). NEW in Study S007.

McKinsey and Tarski built the reading. Take a
space with a topology (the word and the thing
are founded at 1.3.186) and a proposition p. Let
the mark of p be the set of points where p holds.
The box of p holds at the points of the interior
of that set: p is not only true there, it is
true with room around it — Cook's word is
robustly. The diamond of p holds at the points
of the closure of the set: the points no open
set around them can clear of p.
The reading always gives a normal logic, and
more: since the interior of a set lies inside
the set, the box of p gives p; since the
interior of the interior is the interior, the
box of p gives the box of the box of p. So every
topology gives the logic S4 — S4 is the logic
the whole family shares.
Hold the topology to less, and the logic
strengthens or shifts. When every set is open,
interior and closure are the set itself, and
box and diamond fall back to p: the trivial
logic. When only the whole space and the empty
set are open, the box reads as 'holds
everywhere' and the diamond as 'holds somewhere',
and on a space of points without end the logic
is S5. Between the two stands the topology whose
open sets are the ones that leave only a finite
count of points outside: there a claim Cook
writes — p, and the diamond of the box of p,
together give the box of p — holds, and a claim
of S5 — the diamond of p gives the box of the
diamond of p — does not. The lesson the study
carries: the badly behaved spaces carry the
interesting logic; the familiar spaces, the real
line among them, give only S4.
# Concept: The reach of a family of models — what a semantics cannot say
Source: Study S007, the posts of 2026-10-04 and 2026-10-12. NEW in Study S007.

Every semantics has a reach, and the reach is a
fact of the family of models, not of the logic.
Cook states the reach of the topological reading
(1.2.173) plainly: the logic of the provable —
the modal logic whose box is read as 'it is
provable that' — cannot be modeled by the usual
approach to building topological models. He
adds, in the same breath, that another
construction exists; the limit is on the usual
approach, not a verdict on the whole family for
all time.
The categorical reading (1.2.171) is, in his
words, less limited: a wide variety of logic can
be modeled by categories, through the
correspondence of the table. How far the claim
goes, the study states as he states it: wide,
not all. The post is brief, gives one table and
one modal paragraph, and claims no universal reach
for the categorical road — cold comfort, he
says, for a reader who finds both fields
mysterious, and intriguing all the same.
The shape is one the tracker has met from the
other side: a translation proves the model and
is itself trusted (1.2.48), so the reach of a
verification is bounded by the road it travels.
Here the same shape stands in logic itself: a family of models holds the claims its
structure can carry, and is silent — not wrong,
silent — on the rest.
# Concept: Policies as modal claims — integrity of context, written in tenses
Source: Study S007, the post 'Privacy policies and modal logic' (2026-10-08). NEW in Study S007.

The claim of Nissenbaum, quoted in the post: the
benchmark of privacy is contextual integrity —
a complaint of violation is sound when a norm of
the context has been crossed. Data is not
sorted once into public and private; the norms
of the setting it stands in, and of the road it
travels, are what govern it.
Barth, Datta, Mitchell and Nissenbaum (2006)
wrote those norms as modal claims, in the linear
temporal logic: the usual box and diamond, and
their past-tense twins (the four are founded at
1.3.188) — henceforth, eventually, historically,
once. The post gives the shape of a formalized
norm in one line: sharing of data in a context
is allowed, henceforth, if the one the data is
about has once signed the consent form — a claim
about every later time, cashed against a single
earlier act.
Why the machinery earns its keep: a policy read
alone is plain, and no formal dress is needed
for it. A great collection of policies, standing
together and bearing on each other, is another
matter: the collection can fail to hold all at
once though no one policy shows the failure, and
the failure is not obvious from the parts. Written
in the language of modal logic, the policies
stand open to the established algorithms of the
field — the joint question can be asked by a
machine, and answered.
The held kin: the own record of
this tracker of records that govern (1.2.87) — a shared claim
whose standing is an act, not a fact of its text.
The policy work adds the tense: a norm is a
claim whose standing runs over a line of times,
and whose past acts are part of its present
meaning.
# Concept: The geometrical trinity of gravity — one theory, three geometries Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

General relativity, the theory of gravity Einstein
finished in 1915, can be written whole in three different
geometries. In the first, the one Einstein used, gravity
is the curvature of spacetime: the connection that carries
vectors around is the one the metric fixes, and its
bending is the field. In the second, gravity is torsion:
the connection is flat and keeps lengths, and the twist of
carried frames does the work. In the third, gravity is
non-metricity: the connection is flat and twist-free, and
the failure of carried lengths to stay put does the work.
The three writings give the same equations for the metric,
the same falling bodies, the same light. They are not
three theories that agree; they are one theory in three
dresses, and the differences between the dresses are
boundary terms — additions to the action that change
nothing in the body of a region and everything at its edge
(1.2.181). The claim stands on a stage the paper builds
first: a spacetime carries a metric and a connection as
two free things, and a geometry is named by which of
curvature, torsion and non-metricity it lets stand
(1.2.177). The three legs are held apart below: gravity as
curvature (1.2.178), gravity as torsion (1.2.179), gravity
as non-metricity (1.2.180). What the trinity buys is not a
new theory but a new freedom: each leg can be extended in
its own way, and the extensions are different theories
with different counts of moving parts (1.2.183, 1.2.184) —
and matter, when it is coupled, can tell the dresses apart
(1.2.185). The paper's own closing word: the geometry
chosen to show gravity may be a matter of convention; the
symmetry the geometry carries is not. The name of the
frame is the paper's title, and the tracker takes the
frame as this concept: a theory is not known until the
writings that agree on it are known, and the places where
they stop agreeing are where new theories live.
# Concept: The affine stage — a metric and a connection, held apart Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

Before any theory of gravity is chosen, the paper sets a
stage with two free things on it. The first is the metric:
the rule that gives the length of a vector and the angle
between two, at every point of spacetime. The second is
the connection: the rule that says how a vector is carried
from point to point without turning — the affine
connection (1.3.194). Nothing yet says the two have
anything to do with each other. From the pair, three
measures are read off. Curvature (1.3.195): a vector
carried around a small closed loop comes back turned; the
turning is the curvature. Torsion (1.3.196): two carried
steps, taken in the two orders, fail to close their
parallelogram; the gap is the torsion. Non-metricity
(1.3.197): a vector carried a short way changes its
length; the rate of change is the non-metricity. There is
exactly one connection that is both free of torsion and
keeps lengths — the one the metric alone fixes, named for
Levi and Civita — and every other connection is that one
plus two tensors: the contortion, built from the torsion,
and the disformation, built from the non-metricity. So any
geometry on the stage is the familiar one, plus a twist,
plus a stretching. A geometry is classified by which of
the three measures it sets to zero: all three zero is flat
empty stage; curvature alone standing is Einstein's
geometry; torsion alone standing, with the connection
flat, is the second leg of the trinity; non-metricity
alone standing, with the connection flat and free of
torsion, is the third (1.2.176). Two warnings ride with
the stage. The word torsion is already spent in this
tracker on a different thing — the twist inside a homology
group (1.2.38) — and the two share nothing but the name
and the rough picture of a turning; the record keeps them
apart. And the stage is bigger than the trinity:
connections with two or three measures standing are the
general metric-affine country, most of it unexplored by
the paper, which walks only the three corners where one
measure stands alone.
# Concept: Gravity as curvature — the first leg, and its boundary debt Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

On the affine stage (1.2.177), the first leg sets torsion
and non-metricity to zero and lets curvature stand. The
action is Hilbert's: the scalar of the curvature,
integrated over spacetime, with the constant of Newton in
front. Read with the metric and the connection held apart
— the metric-affine reading — the action does more than it
promises: varied in the connection, it returns the ruling
that the connection is the one the metric fixes, the one
named for Levi and Civita. The connection is not put in by
hand; the action fixes it on the equations' own say-so.
One part of the connection stays free even then: a shift
of the connection by the metric times a vector changes the
scalar of the curvature not at all, so that part is a
gauge freedom (1.3.200) — a mode the description carries
and the physics does not see. The leg carries one debt,
and the paper names it plainly: the action holds second
derivatives of the metric, so the variational principle is
not well put until a boundary term is added at the edge of
the region — the term of Gibbons, Hawking and York — which
pays the debt and, in paying it, fixes the entropy of a
black hole. The one older holding of the tracker in this
country reads gravity off the paths of bodies (1.2.104);
this leg says what those paths are paths in: a body that
falls freely follows the straight line that the connection
the metric fixes draws, because a point body reads only
that part of the connection, whatever else the full
geometry holds. Gravity as curvature is the leg every
reader already knows; the paper's work is to show it as
one corner of a stage, with its boundary debt written on
its face — the debt is what the other two legs do not owe
(1.2.181).
# Concept: Gravity as torsion — the second leg, flat and twisting Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

The second leg sets curvature and non-metricity to zero
and lets torsion stand. A flat connection is a pure gauge
thing: it can be written as a turning field acting on
plain derivatives, the way a gauge field in the theories
of the other forces is a turning of a frame. Gravity here
is the gauge theory of translations — moving from point to
point is the symmetry, and the torsion is its field
strength. From the torsion, three scalar terms can be
built, and the action is their weighted sum, with weights
the paper writes c1, c2, c3. One choice of the weights is
the equivalent theory: at those weights the action is
Einstein's, up to a boundary term (1.2.181), and the field
equations for the metric are Einstein's own. The leg is
told in two dresses that are the same dress: with a frame
at every point (1.3.198), the frame is the field and the
connection can be set to zero by a gauge choice — the
gauge of Weitzenbock — while in the dress that keeps the
turning free, a spin connection carries the turning and
the frame carries the rest; the paper notes that the gauge
turning of the empty connection is what brings the frame
itself to birth. The count of moving parts is the
certificate of the leg: the frame has sixteen numbers at a
point; the freedom to change coordinates spends eight —
four of the change itself and four held in the symmetry
the action keeps up to a boundary term (1.2.182) — and the
turning freedom spends six; two are left, the two waves of
gravity. Every other choice of the weights is a new theory
(1.2.183), and the known weakness of the leg is not in its
gravity but in its matter: how matter is to be coupled to
a twisting connection is not settled, and the plain rule
fails (1.2.185).
# Concept: Gravity as non-metricity — the third leg, and the coincident gauge Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

The third leg sets curvature and torsion to zero and lets
non-metricity stand. A flat, twist-free connection is
fixed by four fields — one for each coordinate — which
restore the freedom to change coordinates that fixing the
form of the connection has spent — the device the paper
names with Stueckelberg, the device the paper names with
Stueckelberg. Choose the coordinates to be those very
fields, and the connection is zero at every point of
spacetime: the coincident gauge (1.3.207), the one
placement where the name of the leg is earned — the point
where the coordinates begin and the point where the body
stands coincide, and there is no connection left to carry
anything. In that gauge the action of the leg becomes an
action Einstein himself wrote in 1916: first derivatives
of the metric only, no second derivatives, so the boundary
debt of the first leg (1.2.178) is never taken on and no
boundary term need be added. The metric alone carries the
motion; the connection is pure gauge, and the freedom of
the leg counts eight — the changes of coordinates, doubled
by the four extra changes the action keeps as a symmetry
up to a boundary term (1.2.182). The paper's name for the
theory in this gauge is Coincident General Relativity, and
its judgment is strong: a gravity with neither curvature
nor torsion, in which the connection does nothing and the
metric does everything, would have been a worthy third
path for Einstein beside the two he tried. As with the
second leg, the equivalent theory is one point in a
family: free the weights of the quadratic pieces of
non-metricity, or bend the action into a non-linear
function of its scalar, and the equivalence is lost and
new motions enter (1.2.184). The matter coupling of the
leg, alone of the three, needs no repair (1.2.185).
# Concept: Equivalence by boundary terms — the three actions differ at the edge alone Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

The trinity (1.2.176) is not three agreements but one
identity, and the identity is written in boundary terms.
Take the scalar of the curvature of the connection the
metric fixes; take the scalar of the torsion of the flat
leg; take the scalar of the non-metricity of the flat,
twist-free leg. No two are equal. But each pair differs by
a total derivative — a term whose integral over a region
is fixed by the edge of the region alone (1.3.199). So the
three actions, integrated, differ only by what they say at
the boundary, and the equations they give in the body of
any region are one set of equations: Einstein's.
Equivalence in this field is not sameness of the action
but sameness up to the edge. The fact cuts three ways.
First, it explains the debt of the first leg: the
curvature action holds second derivatives, and its
boundary term is not free — it must be the term of
Gibbons, Hawking and York, and it prices the entropy of
black holes (1.2.178); the other two actions hold first
derivatives only, owe nothing, and are well put as they
stand. Second, it tells where equivalence can be broken:
apply any non-linear function to one of the three and the
boundary term, which sat harmless inside the linear
action, no longer cancels — the bent theories of the three
legs are three different theories (1.2.183, 1.2.184), and
the paper is exact about it: the bending of the
non-metricity scalar is not the bending of the curvature
scalar, precisely because of the boundary term between
them. Third, it makes the boundary a physical place: the
paper closes by naming the non-trivial boundary terms of
the three writings a new tool for reading the holographic
character of gravity — what a region knows is written at
its edge, and the trinity gives three writings of the edge
to compare. A claim of equivalence that does not state its
boundary terms is, by this concept, incomplete: the edge
is where the dresses differ, and the edge is part of the
theory.
# Concept: Symmetries up to a total derivative — the gauge groups that hold at the edge's price Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

A symmetry of an action is a change of the fields that
leaves the action as it stands. The legs of the trinity
hold their key symmetries in a weaker, stranger way: the
change moves the action by a total derivative — nothing in
the body of the region, something at its edge — and the
symmetry counts as a symmetry because the equations never
see the edge term. In the second leg, the turning of the
local frame is such a symmetry: the torsion scalar shifts
by a boundary term under a turn of the frame, no more, and
that is what lets the sixteen numbers of the frame fall to
two moving parts (1.2.179). In the third leg, the changes
of coordinates act so: in the coincident gauge the action
is Einstein's of 1916, which is not a scalar under changes
of coordinates but changes by a total derivative, and the
paper reads the gauge group of the leg as eight in number
on exactly this ground (1.2.180). These up-to-the-edge
symmetries are load-bearing, not for show: the count of
degrees of freedom (1.3.201) of each leg is the count
after they are spent, and the certificate that each leg
carries exactly the two waves of gravity rests on them.
Their loss is the mechanism of every extension in the
paper: bend the action, or free its weights, and the
symmetry that held up to a boundary term holds no longer;
the parts it was spending come back as new motions of the
field — extra degrees of freedom, some of them ghosts
(1.2.183, 1.2.184). The paper is careful about one point
of method: a symmetry of the action up to a boundary term
is a property of the action as written, not of the theory
in the abstract, so two writings of one theory can carry
different such symmetries, and a count of moving parts
must name the writing it counts in. The tracker records
the concept because the pattern is general: wherever a
freedom is spent only at the price of an edge term, the
edge term is part of the theory's machinery, and dropping
it from the record loses the count.
# Concept: Extensions of the torsion leg — freeing the weights, bending the scalar Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

The second leg (1.2.179) is one point — the weights c1,
c2, c3 at their equivalent values — in a family, and the
paper walks the family. With the weights free, the theory
is the one the field calls New General Relativity. Its
first consistency condition is a single equation among the
weights: where it holds, the two parts of the metric that
the linear theory mixes — the part that carries the waves
and the part that turns as a vector — stop mixing, and a
new field of the turning kind, the field of Kalb and
Ramond, stands in the spectrum beside the waves. A second
condition, laid on the first, brings the family back to
the equivalent theory itself. Between the conditions, the
family is a country of theories whose full health the
paper does not claim: freedom of weights is bought with
questions about the extra parts. The family's second
extension bends instead of freeing: a non-linear function
of the torsion scalar in place of the scalar itself. The
bend breaks the frame symmetry of the leg — the symmetry
that held up to a boundary term (1.2.182) — because the
boundary term no longer cancels inside a non-linear
function (1.2.181); the parts the symmetry spent return as
extra degrees of freedom, and the paper's judgment is that
their presence is the expectation, not a defect to be
argued away. The extension is widely used for cosmology,
where the bend can drive the late speeding of the world's
growth without a new matter field; its cost stands in the
coupling of matter (1.2.185), where this leg was already
the weakest of the three, and in a lesson the paper draws
from its cosmology: a choice that looks like a mere gauge
choice in the frame — the so-called bad frames — can throw
away whole branches of solutions, so the frame must be
carried with the same care as the field. Extensions, in
this leg, are not added show on the equivalent theory:
each one spends a symmetry and buys motions, and the
exchange rate is the paper's arithmetic.
# Concept: Extensions of the non-metricity leg — the general quadratic theory and the bent scalar Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

The third leg (1.2.180) extends as the second does, and
further. Free the weights of the quadratic pieces of
non-metricity — five of them, where the torsion leg had
three — and the linear theory is the most general theory
of a wave-carrying field of the second rank that the flat,
twist-free stage allows: as many as ten moving modes stand
in its spectrum before health is priced. Health is priced
by the ghost: one of the modes, a turning mode carrying
one unit of spin, enters with the wrong sign unless a
single equation among the weights holds, and that equation
is the return of a symmetry — the changes of coordinates
that hold the volume fixed, acting as a gauge freedom
(1.3.200). From that guarded line, the paper finds exactly
two ways down to a healthy theory with two moving parts.
Widen the symmetry to all changes of coordinates in the
coincident form, and the weights are forced to the
equivalent theory: the third leg itself. Keep the volume
symmetry and add the symmetry of Weyl — the free
stretching of all lengths by one factor — and the weights
land on a second healthy point: the theory in which the
volume of spacetime is held fixed and the cosmological
constant is not a constant of nature but a constant of
integration, a theory the paper names as not yet read in
its general form. Bend the scalar instead — a non-linear
function of the non-metricity scalar — and the family
inherits the prices of the guarded line: its behaviour
around empty spacetime is ruled by the conditions of the
theory before the bend, a fact the paper calls a very
strong constraint. The bent family's cosmology carries the
open edge the paper states most strongly: on the most
symmetric backgrounds a leftover symmetry appears that the
general background does not have, the table of modes
changes its count from background to background, and a
theory whose count of motions jumps as the background is
deformed stands under the suspicion of strong coupling —
the paper's own words raise it as a possible fatal flaw
(Q72 of Chapter 24). One jewel is recorded whole: the bent
family with the square root of the scalar added gives, for
any weight of the addition, exactly the background growth
of Einstein's theory — the added constant-like term shows
only in the small waves, not in the growth. And the
general quadratic theory's cosmology carries the mirror of
the lesson of the torsion leg: its lapse is a moving part,
and to fix it by hand is to throw away branches of the
solutions (1.2.183).
# Concept: Matter coupling tells the dresses apart — hypermomentum and the three couplings Source: Paper 016 (Chapter 24). NEW in Paper 016.

The three writings of the trinity agree on gravity alone.
Matter ends the agreement. In a general geometry the
question 'how does a matter field feel the connection?'
has no forced answer: the plain rule — write the
flat-space law, then trade every plain derivative for the
carried one — is a choice, and the paper shows it choosing
differently in each leg. The difference is priced by a
quantity the paper gives its own name: the hypermomentum
(1.3.208), the answer of the matter action to a change of
the connection itself, where the energy-momentum is its
answer to a change of the metric. Matter with no
hypermomentum never feels the connection; matter that
carries it — the fields of half-turn, the fermions
(1.3.202), feel it directly. In the first leg there is
nothing to choose: bosons feel the metric alone, fermions
feel the connection the metric fixes, and a falling body
reads that connection alone, whatever else stands on the
stage (1.2.178). In the second leg the plain rule fails
twice over. For the field of Maxwell it puts a term of the
torsion times the field into the law of the field
strength, and the gauge symmetry of that field breaks. For
fermions it fails by kindness: the spin connection of the
flat leg is pure gauge, can be turned to zero, and then
the fermions feel only the frame — the connection the
metric fixes drops out of their law, and with it goes the
keeping of energy and momentum, unless the rule is patched
by hand. In the third leg the plain rule works as it
stands: the Dirac field is blind to non-metricity — the
one trace of it that enters its law cancels by the even
balance of the action — and the law of the fermion in
curved spacetime comes out whole, with no patch. The
general warning of the paper is the edge of the concept:
where a geometry beyond the one the metric fixes is at
work, the constraints on the connection can force the
hypermomentum to zero, and then any matter that carries
hypermomentum — fermions through the twisting part of the
torsion — makes the theory inconsistent, unless the
coupling is chosen with the constraint in view.
Equivalence is a property of the gravity sector; the
coupling is a further choice, and it is the coupling, not
the action, that can tell the three dresses apart.
# Concept: The reset-relative measure — learning priced against a wiped twin
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

The benchmark never asks how much of its budget a
learner spends. It asks how much the keeping of the learner
saves, against the same system with its
keeping wiped before every instance. The wipe is
the zero. On the stream the saving is stated as a
share: the budget the wiped twin spends, less the
budget the learner spends, over the budget the
wiped twin spends less the budget of perfect
memory — a learner handed the answer key at the
first return of a task, which spends only what
checking the key costs. That share is the share
learned: 1 is the whole of what there was to
learn, 0 is the wiped twin itself, and a share
below zero means the keeping cost more than it
saved. On the two tests after the stream the
saving is stated the simpler way, as the share of
the budget of the wiped twin that the learner does not
spend — the share saved. Because the twin is the
same weights, the same harness and the same
settings with only the persistence switched off,
the strength of the model is divided out of the
reading: what is left is the worth of what was
kept, and of nothing else. The reading is drawn
per task and re-dealt by chance many times over
the tasks, so the interval it carries prices the
spread across tasks, not the wander of one run.
# Concept: The lifelong stream — tasks in spells, with dormancy between
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

A track of the benchmark is not a list of problems
taken once. It is a working lifetime: a set of
tasks (25 on the first track) whose instances
arrive in spells — a task is met about three times
in a burst, then rests for a long while, the
rests drawn from a wide bell on a log scale and
centred near thirty instances of other work — then
returns, while new tasks keep arriving on their
own clock, and every instance of a task is fresh:
made on demand by a generator, never the same
grid twice. A learner is therefore paid only for
keeping the rule of a task, never for remembering
an instance: the grids it will be graded on do not
exist yet when it does its keeping. The stream on
the first track serves 200 instances and then
stops, and what the learner has become by then is
read off by the two frozen tests (1.2.189). The
same stream is also served to two or four users
at once, all of them drawing on one kept state
(1.2.191).
# Concept: The feedback budget — the cost of an instance counted in feedback
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

Every instance carries a budget of 16, and the
score of an instance is the share of it the
learner spends. The currency is feedback, not
effort: a wrong answer handed in costs 1; a
worked pair asked for from the task costs 1; and
an instance that ends unsolved costs the whole 16
however it failed — given up, run dry, or still
open when its turn ended. A solved instance costs
what it spent and no more. What the learner burns
in tokens, seconds or dollars is written in its
record and never scored: the budget is the one
scale on which a kept program, a kept note and a
change of weights can be priced together, because
each of them pays, or fails to pay, in the
feedback it makes needless. The charge has a
floor and a cliff, and the cliff is the point: a
learner that nearly learns a task and keeps
nothing it can use pays in full, exactly as one
that never tried.
# Concept: The frozen tests — one frozen state, read as memory and as reach
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

When the stream ends, the learner is frozen: the
state it stands in is copied, and every instance
of each test starts from that same copy, so that
nothing learned on one test instance can reach
another (the round contract, 1.2.190; the machine
stands at 1.5.2). The seen test serves fresh
instances of the tasks the stream taught: it reads
memorization — whether what was kept is the rule
of a met task, in a form the learner can spend.
The held-out test serves a hundred tasks the
learner has never met: it reads generalization —
whether what the stream built reaches past the
tasks it was built on. Both are priced by the
reset-relative measure (1.2.186), against
reference plays of the same instances from an
empty state. The two readings can come apart, and
in this study they do: what the program library
kept reads strongly on the seen test and at zero
on the held-out test, and only the change of
weights reads on both.
# Concept: The round contract — carried state may change only as a round ends
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

The benchmark serves instances in rounds: one
instance for each user of the stream, every one of
them met with the state the round began with. The
contract of the learner interface is that what a
learner carries between instances may change only
in the step the runner calls as the round ends —
never while an instance is being played, and
never in a way one user's instance of a round
could feel from another's. Inside an instance, a
user's own working state is theirs alone. In the
regime of one user every instance is a round of
one, and the hooks bracket it. The contract is
what makes the frozen tests honest (1.2.189): if
carried state could change in the middle of a
round, a test round of a hundred users would be a
second stream, and the reading of memorization
would price learning done during the test itself.
The machine of the contract stands drawn at
1.5.2.
# Concept: The one state of a team — copies out as a round begins, a settled merge as it ends
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

When a stream serves several users at once, the
benchmark keeps one state for all of them, as a
trained model is one set of weights for all its
users: a store kept per user would set a learner
that saw only its own user's tasks against weights
trained on every user's work, and the comparison
would not be fair. Each round begins by copying
the one state to every user; each user plays
against their copy alone; the round ends with the
copies merged back into the one state in user
order, by settled rules: a path one user alone
changed takes that change; a path several users
changed is merged by its kind — a JSON file key by
key, a text file line by line, a database row by
row where the database can be read whole — and a
thing the merge cannot read takes the later
user's whole, a removal yields to a change, and
nothing is written through a link. The merge is
part of the measurement, not a detail under it:
it is the shape a shared memory takes when more
than one worker writes to it, and the streams of
two and four users run under it.
# Concept: Persistence that is never read — the three broken joints of a kept store
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

The central finding of the study, stated as a shape: a
kept store can fail at three separate joints, and
the three harnesses it measured failed at all of
them, in different mixes. The first joint is
writing: the store is never written — a harness
whose lasting entries are opt-in, one by one, held
no lasting entry at the end of six hundred
sessions, because the agent never once opted in;
and the self-improvement that was meant to write
from the runs never ran in the version measured.
The second joint is loading: the store is written
but never loaded at the moment of need — notes
written by the hundred under the names of tasks,
read on almost none of the returns they were
written for, because a session loads one summary
file and the notes never reach it. The third
joint is choosing: the store is written and
loaded, but the few entries brought to the task
are chosen before the task is known — the matching
rule reached the model on about a quarter of the
returns it should have served, and those returns
cost less than half of the others. Each harness
also re-bought the demonstration pairs it had
already paid for, at two to four pairs an
instance, where the plain cache of pairs (1.2.194)
re-buys at about half that rate. A store that
passes all three joints measures above zero on
the reset-relative scale; a store that fails any
one of them measures at zero — however much it
holds.
# Concept: The program library — one tested program per task, run before the model is asked
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

The learner that learned, in this study, is the
simplest one built. When it solves an instance it
writes the rule it found as a program, tests the
program against every pair the task has shown —
the pairs it was given and the pairs it bought —
and keeps it under the name of the task. When the task
returns, the kept program runs first, at no cost
in feedback unless it speaks: a program that
answers is checked by the benchmark like any
answer, and a program that cannot answer costs
nothing, and the model is asked as if the library
were not there. A program that answers wrong is
repaired or struck. Nothing else is kept: no
notes, no summaries, no account of the search.
Measured against its own wiped twin (1.2.186),
the library learned 0.49 of what there was to
learn at low effort and 0.60 and 0.62 at medium
and high, and on the frozen seen test it saved
0.54, 0.77 and 0.73 of the wiped budget. It saved
nothing on tasks it had never met: a library of
rules is a memory, not a reach.
# Concept: The demonstration cache — the floor that prices plain keeping
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

The lower floor of the study that keeps something:
a learner with no memory of its own reasoning,
which simply keeps every demonstration pair it has
bought for a task and is shown the pairs again
when the task returns. It learns nothing and
forgets nothing; it only stops paying twice for
the same showing. Its share learned — 0.19 at low
effort, 0.24 at medium, 0.28 at high — is the
price of plain keeping, and it stands in the study
as the line any real memory must cross to be worth
more than a drawer of receipts. Each of the three
harnesses, with all their machinery of notes,
summaries and skills, measured on the stream at or
below the line this floor sets by keeping pairs
alone, and the readings of their stores (1.2.192)
say why. The cache also stands next to the
denominator of the share learned: perfect memory
(1.2.186) is this floor with the answer key handed
over in place of the pairs.
# Concept: Learning into the weights — the one road that carried over to unmet tasks
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

Of every method the study ran, one alone saved
budget on tasks it had never met: the weights of
a small model trained, round by round, to imitate
a stronger teacher's play — the method named
DAgger, in which the student plays, the teacher
labels the states the student's own play reaches,
and the student is trained on those labels. Its
share saved on the held-out test stands at 0.29
with one user and at 0.40 and 0.39 with two and
four; its share saved on the seen test stands at
0.57. Part of the gain is not knowledge of tasks
at all: the untrained student, set inside the
harness it was later trained to drive, solved 18
of the 100 held-out tasks where the same model in
a stateless loop solved 31 — so some of what the
training bought was learning to drive the harness,
a skill no store in the study was asked to hold.
The contrast the study leaves standing: what was
written into stores stayed with the tasks it was
written from; what was trained into the weights
travelled. Whether a store can hold the travelling
kind stands open as Q78.
# Concept: The aliased task — each run knows its tasks by names drawn for it alone
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

A task in the benchmark has a true name, and no
learner ever sees it. Each run draws a salt of its
own, and every task is shown to the learner of that run
under a name hashed from the true name and the
salt; the records keep the true names, and the
trajectories keep both. The point is lookup: a
learner with a store, a search tool, or a memory
of other runs must not be able to turn the name
of a task into the rule of the task by looking it up — in
the public sets the rule stands a search away —
and a store carried between runs must not silently
become a key to the stream of a new run. The alias is
the way the benchmark keeps the stream a stream
of work and not a test of who kept the answer
book. It has a cost the design accepts: a
kept names of a learner are bound to its run, so what
it keeps cannot be carried to another run and
spent there. Keeping is priced inside one working
lifetime — which is the lifetime the benchmark
claims to measure.
# Concept: The promotion gate that never fired — a store whose key is held by its own searching
Source: Study S008 (Chapter 25). NEW in Study S008.

One of the measured harnesses keeps its lasting
memory in one summary file, and a lesser note is
promoted into it only when the note has been
called back three times, under three distinct
searches, by the agent itself. The gate is drawn
as a machine at 1.5.2. Across every cell the study
ran, it never fired: no note in any run gathered
more than two of the signals the gate counts, and
no note was ever called back by the searching
of the agent itself. The signals are made by a behaviour its
own sessions do not produce: a session
loads the summary file, and the notes beneath it
are reached, if at all, by searches the agent
seldom makes — in the cells where the agent was
told to keep notes, it wrote them by the hundred
and read almost none. The gate stands in this
tracker as the type of a wider shape: a promotion
rule whose firing condition is a behaviour the
system it guards does not produce — a lock whose
key hangs inside the room it locks.
# Concept: The crafting game — deduction taught as making
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

The one move of genre of the textbook: a proof is a
thing made. The statements in hand are materials;
the rules of logic are recipes, each naming what
it takes in and what it yields; the goal is the
item to be crafted. The player works forward from
the hypotheses, combining what they hold, until
the goal stands among the crafted. What the move
buys: every step is an act with a visible product,
and a stuck player is stuck as a maker is stuck —
some material missing, or some recipe not yet
learned — never as a reader is stuck before a
finished proof, who cannot see where its steps
came from. The design is the one the author built
in 2018 (QED); the rebuild of 2026 keeps it whole
and changes what stands around it (1.2.200,
1.2.201, 1.2.205).
# Concept: The position is a partial proof — the state of the game
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

What a state of the game is: the statements
crafted so far, each standing in hand as a name
bound to a statement with the proof that made it
(the code's own dress for it is hA : A); the goal
not yet crafted; and the stack of open rooms
(1.2.202), each an assumption being worked under.
Every move the game offers is a rule of deduction,
checked by its matcher as it is applied, so every
position a player can reach is a sound partial
derivation: no position holds a false step that
must be undone, only positions from which the goal
is not yet reachable with what stands in hand.
The game ends when the goal is crafted in the
root room — the partial proof closes, and the
assembled text of 1.2.201 ends in an exact, not a
sorry. The machine is drawn at 1.5.2.
# Concept: Recipes that carry the names of Lean
— the moves are the words of the prover
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

The recipes are named as Lean names its own acts:
And.intro, And.left, And.right, Or.inl, Or.inr,
Iff.intro, Iff.mp, Iff.mpr, modus_ponens,
False.elim, absurd, Classical.em, Exists.intro,
universal_instantiation, Eq.refl, and Eq.subst
offered under the name rw, with the recipes that form
the connective itself and the case_analysis and
induction recipes beside them. The design choice
is the refusal to invent a second vocabulary for
the student: what the hand learns to reach for in
the game is what the proof assistant calls the
same act, so the game's skill is already addressed
in the terms of the prover, and there is no second
language to leave behind on the way to real
proofs. The naming is the bridge, and it is built
into the moves themselves. In cluster Q (Paper
014) the machine holds these names and spends
them; here the human is taught them.
# Concept: The live proof — one state, rendered as it stands
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

An option of the game shows, as you play, the
in-progress Lean proof of your game state: the
steps so far are set down as Lean, and the goal
not yet crafted stands in the text as a sorry. A
switch renders the same state in plain words
instead — 'A and B', 'A implies B' — and the whole
proof may be taken down as a .lean file that, in the own words of the page, compiles on its own, the
plain reading carried in it as comments. Three
properties give the device its weight. The
rendering is of the state, not of a solution: it
shows the proof as it stands, its gaps standing
in it named. The Lean dress can be checked, not a picture: the file the game hands over is one a
kernel will judge. And the dress is the reader's
choice, thrown at a switch, over one underlying
state that does not change with the viewing.
# Concept: The room an assumption opens — work inside, seal at the door
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

To craft an implication, the player opens a room
inside the position. Inside the room the front statement of the implication stands in hand as an assumption, and
the work there may draw on everything outside as
well; when the statement the implication
points to is crafted inside, the
room is closed, and outside it the player holds the
implication, its assumption set aside. The
code keeps rooms as environments, each with its
parent, and the live view of 1.2.201 reads an open stack
of them as a chain of binders. A
negation is crafted by the same door, its room
aiming at False. The room is where forward
crafting meets the oldest shape of deduction: a
proof inside a proof, sealed at its door, the seal itself one of the moves.
# Concept: A lemma that becomes a recipe — proved once, used as a move after
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

The code of the game carries a general matcher whose stated work is this: a solved lemma becomes
a reusable recipe. The lemma, templated by the matcher over its particulars, joins the moves a
later position may apply — what was proved once,
in one place, is promoted into the vocabulary of
making everywhere after. The promotion is
mechanical, not editorial: no one judges whether
the lemma deserves its new standing; its shape,
as the matcher reads it, decides. The shape is
kin to the lemma pipeline of cluster Q (1.2.150),
turned to the hand of the player: there, lemmas are cut
so a machine can prove them; here, a lemma the
player has proved comes back as a tool the player
can wield. Where the line of worth should be
drawn stands open at Q83.
# Concept: The bench that offers only legal moves — kept legal by what is offered
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

The game is many-sorted, and each sort owns its
operations, so the bench of terms and
the bench of formulas can offer only what is legal for the
sorts in play. In the words of the design note in
the code: an illegal move cannot be expressed,
rather than having to be detected and rejected.
What is legal is fixed by what is offered, not
enforced after the act. The sorts arrive staged
across the book: a generic sort with no operations
of its own; an abstract sort, a set standing with a membership
relation; a magma, with a constant and
a map and no axioms; a group, the first sort whose
theory carries axioms; and Nat, the first concrete
sort, reached by import — each stage widening what
may be offered, and nothing offered before its
stage. The standing record of this tracker
(1.4.x) keeps the opposite discipline: any
standing may be written, and its accuracy is
proved after the fact. The two are weighed at
26.2.1, and the question stands open at Q84.
# Concept: The fantasy skin — the same machine in another dress
Source: Study S009 (Chapter 26). NEW in Study S009.

An option of the game renames every surface noun
into fantasy dress: mathematical statements become
potions, mathematical expressions become scrolls,
and the proof assembles in a spellbook. The state,
the recipes and the goal stand unchanged under
the renaming. The author offers it just for fun;
the study records it because it is a clean,
working instance of a separation this tracker
keeps meeting — the machine of a work, and the
vocabulary it is met in, are two things, and
either may be changed without the other. (The
word scroll carries a different load in this
house — a plane of text at a coordinate — and the
two uses meet only at the word; the meeting is
recorded plainly at 26.1.3.)
# Concept: AlphaProof Code — the machine-code verification framework
Source: Paper 017 (Google DeepMind document 36, 2026-11-10). NEW in Paper 017.

AlphaProof Code is a framework for proving statements
about the machine code of a running program, built of
cooperating agents under human guidance. Human experts
set out what is to be proved; a specification-writing
agent, checked by a pool of reviewing agents, drafts
the statements in a proof assistant (Lean); a team of
prover agents writes the proofs; and a gatekeeper
(1.2.211) accepts a proof only when the statement it
proves is the statement that was approved, in every
definition it rests on. The object of the framework is not
source code but the compiled binary itself (1.2.214).
Its first use, and this paper, is the security of
libsignal — the library at the heart of the Signal
messenger — proved at the level of the AArch64
machine code a stock compiler produces from it,
unmodified. The work answers a public challenge of
April 2026: verify open-source cryptographic libraries
against mathematical specifications, at the binary
level where they run.
# Concept: The specification as the trusted artifact — the statement is what is trusted, never the proof
Source: Paper 017, sections 2.2-2.3. NEW in Paper 017.

In this framework the proofs are untrusted and the
statements are trusted. A specification — the formal
statements, in Lean, of what the binary must do and
what security must mean — is drafted by an agent under
human guidance, read with skepticism by a pool of
reviewing agents, changed only under a strict protocol
(1.2.209), and approved by the user; only then is it
frozen, pinned at a named commit, and handed to the
gatekeeper as the standard every proof is measured
against (1.2.211). The discipline inverts the usual
fear about machine-written proof: a wrong proof is
harmless, because the kernel checks it and the
gatekeeper checks its statement; a wrong specification
is the danger, because everything proved would be
proved about the wrong thing, truly. So the framework
spends its human attention on the statements — the
formal model's types, its computation rules, its
definitions of the games an attacker plays — and tests
them as it would test code (1.2.208). What a reader
must trust is exactly this artifact and the floor
beneath it (1.2.222); the millions of lines of proof
beneath the statements need no trust at all.
# Concept: Testing a specification — a statement can be wrong, and can be caught
Source: Paper 017, sections 2.2 and 3.1. NEW in Paper 017.

A specification (1.2.207) is code about code, and this
framework tests it the way code is tested. Three tests
stand out. Vacuity: the properties of the specification are proved able to hold together — some execution meets them
all — and its hypotheses are shown to be met by
concrete test runs, so no theorem stands empty because
its premises can never hold. Mutation: small changes
are planted in the definitions of the specification — an
arguments of an operation swapped, a case dropped — and
the changed claims are proved false, so a statement too
weak to notice a real change is caught. History: two
real flaws in the protocol, found by earlier authors
in the deployed code's past, are rebuilt inside the
model, and the framework proves the security statement
false of the rebuilt code — a specification that could not prove that statement false of a known-bad program would prove it false of nothing.
A statement that survives all three has earned some of
the trust the framework asks for it; the tests are part
of the method, not a courtesy.

Rider (2026-11-13, Study S010). The position essay of Math,
Inc. (Chapter 28) sets a wider scheme beside these three
tests: a specification is validated four ways — read for
intent; tested for what it observes, differential fuzzing
among the tests; proved as a target, by the implementations
that meet it; and proved as a basis, by the useful properties
drawn from it (1.2.225). The three tests of this scroll stand
inside the scheme: mutation and history are the second way
turned against known change, and the vacuity test is the
fourth way turned inward. What the scheme adds outright is the
first way — a reader, reading the statement for what it means
to claim — the one test no machine in the framework runs. The
claim of this scroll stands, priced at four gates instead of
three: a statement can be wrong, and can be caught.
# Concept: The amendment protocol — a frozen specification may change only through a gate
Source: Paper 017, section 2.3. NEW in Paper 017.

Once a specification (1.2.207) is approved and frozen,
the prover team (1.2.210) sometimes finds it cannot be
proved as written — a lemma stated too strongly, a
definition that resists. The framework does not let
the team quietly rewrite the standard it is measured
against. A proposed change goes to a critic agent that
never sees the proof attempts, so it cannot be argued
into weakening the claim to fit a failing proof; it
accepts a change only when the change is necessary
(the statement is false or unprovable as written, and
no change to the proof alone repairs it), when it does
not weaken security (the security definitions keep
their force, at most reshaped), and when it is small.
A change that touches a trusted assumption needs the
user's approval besides. Every accepted change is
logged. The machine this draws is drawn at 27.1.2: a
standard that can be changed, but only by a judge who
has not seen the work it judges, and never downward in
strength without the word of a human.
# Concept: The prover team — forty to eighty agents, one editor per file
Source: Paper 017, sections 2.4 and 6. NEW in Paper 017.

The proofs are written by a team of agents with
divided labour. An orchestrator coordinates and never
edits a file itself. Team leads break the goals down;
worker agents write and repair proofs, searching the
library and testing small pieces on their own before
they spend a full check; an architect agent watches
for statements that will not yield and routes them,
sometimes to the amendment protocol (1.2.209); an
integrator alone commits, so no two hands land changes
at once — and no file ever has more than one editor.
The team's first move against a hard goal is to try to prove it false: a counterexample found early is a
cheap rescue from a false statement. At its height
the team ran at forty to eighty agents at once,
checking proofs in parallel through the build system,
and wrote some six and a half million lines of proof
across two libraries. The shape is a harness (1.2.15)
grown to a crew: what cluster B holds of one loop
improving itself, and cluster Q of one prover
searching, stands here as an organization — divided, held at a gate, and forbidden to grade its own work (1.2.211).
# Concept: The gatekeeper — done is declared by comparison, not by the prover
Source: Paper 017, sections 2.1 and 2.4. NEW in Paper 017.

Acceptance in this framework is a comparison, not a
judgement call. The gatekeeper holds its own copy of
the approved specification (1.2.207), where the
prover agents cannot reach it. When a prover offers a
finished proof, the gatekeeper checks two things:
that the kernel of the proof assistant accepts the proof —
the same floor clusters F and Q stand on (1.2.41) —
and that the statement proved is identical to the
statement approved: the theorem, and every definition
it depends on, down the whole chain, as the kernel sees them.
A proof of a statement near it, however fine, is
refused. Only the gatekeeper may declare a task done;
prover agents report progress, never completion. Tools that
would let a proof cheat — an admitted gap, a decision
procedure that steps outside the kernel — are barred.
The gatekeeper bears on a question this tracker holds
open (Q84): whether the keeping of a standard can be
built so that bending is prevented rather than caught.
Here the standard is held out of reach, and bending is
refused by a machine that compares.
# Concept: A formal semantics of an instruction set — the processor, written in Lean
Source: Paper 017, sections 2.5 and 4. NEW in Paper 017.

To prove anything about machine code, the meaning of
the code must first be written down where proofs can
reach it. The authors of the framework wrote, in Lean, a
semantics of the user-mode AArch64 instruction set as
Linux programs meet it: 467 instructions and 31 system
calls, some seven and a half thousand lines, giving
each instruction its exact effect on registers,
memory, and flags. On top of it stands a predicate —
a program runs to a state — under which whole runs of an unmodified binary become things a
proof can quantify over. The trust line is drawn with
care: the definitions are trusted, as any
specification is (1.2.207); the clever parts — a
lockstep execution used to reason fast — are proved
sound against the plain definitions, so speed costs no
trust. And the definitions were tested against two
outside oracles, an emulator and an independent formal
model of the same processor, instruction by
instruction, until the three agreed. A semantics is a
foundation of the same kind this tracker lays at
1.3.x: the ground on which a whole territory of
claims stands, priced by how much of it a reader must
take on trust (1.2.222).
# Concept: Hooks — trusted contracts where the proof meets code it does not prove
Source: Paper 017, sections 4.4 and 5. NEW in Paper 017.

A binary-level proof cannot prove everything it
touches. The library calls outward — to memory
machinery beneath it, to the elliptic-curve arithmetic
other code supplies — and some of what it calls is too
costly, or the wrong shape, to prove line by line. At
those 39 places the framework sets a hook: a contract,
written in the model, that says what the called
routine is assumed to do. Inside the proof the
contract is simply used; outside it, the contract
stands in the trusted base (1.2.222), named and
counted. The hook is how an assumption enters a
machine-code proof without hiding: instead of a gap
in the reasoning, a labelled door, with the assumption
written on it. The deepest hooks treat the curve arithmetic itself as perfect — the field operations are modelled
as a perfect field — so the theorems hold of the
logic of the protocol given the arithmetic, and the
the arithmetic itself stands as named trust,
not as proved ground. Every framework of proof has
such doors; this one's discipline is that they are
few, listed, and load-bearing in the open.
# Concept: Verification at the machine-code level — prove the thing that runs
Source: Paper 017, sections 1, 2.5 and 8. NEW in Paper 017.

The framework proves its theorems about the compiled
binary — the machine code that actually runs — and
not about the source the binary came from. The move
deletes two trusted links that verification by
translation (1.2.48) must carry: the compiler
(1.3.213), whose output is here the very object of
proof, stock and unmodified; and any formal model of
the source language, which no longer stands between
the theorem and the artifact. It is, the authors
state, the first machine-checked proof of a deployed
cryptographic implementation at this level: the
theorems quantify over runs of the shipped code
itself (1.2.215). The price is paid in the grain of the proof: reasoning runs at the level of registers,
flags, and memory, across some five million lines of
proof for the binary theorems alone, and the method
leans on a written semantics of the processor
(1.2.212) the way translation leans on a translator.
Trust is not deleted by this move either — it is
moved, downward, to the semantics, the contracts at
the boundary (1.2.213), and the statements themselves
(1.2.207) — and the paper's own accounting of what
remains is a concept of this cluster (1.2.222).
# Concept: The binary implements the model — whole runs, exactly as prescribed
Source: Paper 017, section 4.1. NEW in Paper 017.

Beneath both security theorems stands a functional
claim about whole runs: start the binary — the stock
command-line harness around the library — in any state
of its files, give it any one operation to perform,
and if it finishes successfully, then the files it
leaves behind, the answer it prints, and the exit code
it returns are exactly what the formal model
lays down for that operation on that state; if it
fails, it writes nothing. A second theorem bounds the memory of the run: at no point does the process hold more
live memory than the model's own accounting allows,
so the binary cannot run out of memory in a way the
model does not foresee. These are theorems about runs, proved instruction
by instruction up the whole call tree. They are the
floor both security proofs stand on: the handshake
and ratchet theorems (1.2.219, 1.2.220) argue about
the model's behaviour, and this concept is the warrant
that the behaviour of the model is that of the binary.
# Concept: The concretization homomorphism — from model terms to bytes, never back
Source: Paper 017, section 3.2. NEW in Paper 017.

The model speaks of key, nonce, and sealed message as terms; the binary moves bytes. The bridge the proofs
walk is a map, written with the letter gamma, from
terms to byte strings — a homomorphism, carrying each
term to the bytes that represent it, operation by
operation, so that computing in the model and then
mapping lands where mapping and then computing in
bytes lands. The map runs one way only: there is no
reading a term back out of bytes, because distinct
terms can land on the same bytes — a value an attacker
guesses is simply another term, whose bytes may
collide with the true one's. That the map runs one way only is not a defect of the bridge; it is the shape of the ground,
and the security theorems are stated to survive it
(1.2.217): where two terms share bytes, the guarantee of the proof stops and names the stop, rather than
pretending the bytes remember which term they came
from.
# Concept: Refinement up to collisions — the guarantee holds to the explained prefix
Source: Paper 017, sections 3.2-3.3. NEW in Paper 017.

The binary security theorem says the running code
is a refinement of the model: every trace of hardware states the
binary can produce is explained by a trace of the
model — with one honest exception. Where a collision
occurs — two model terms landing on the same bytes
(1.2.216) — the explanation may fail, because bytes
cannot say which term they carry. So the theorem is
refinement up to collisions: take any hardware trace;
walk it beside the model trace that explains it; the
explanation is guaranteed to reach at least the first
collision, if any, and the security properties are
proved of that explained prefix, in the form: the
properties hold, or a collision has occurred. An
earlier style of theorem in this territory simply
assumed collisions away, under a named assumption that
sat in the trusted base; this paper removes the
assumption and pays for the removal inside the
statement, so the trusted base (1.2.222) shrinks by
exactly the honesty the statement gains. Whether the
own probability of the collision clause can be brought
inside the same accounting is a question this paper
leaves open (Q87).
# Concept: Reductions as priced programs — a reduction you can run, with its price computed
Source: Paper 017, sections 3.3 and 5. NEW in Paper 017.

The computational security theorem — the one with
numbers in it — is proved by reductions: attacker
programs against the protocol are turned, mechanically,
into attacker programs against the hard problems
beneath it, and the security bound of the protocol is the sum of the pieces' prices. In pen-and-paper work such
a turn is an existence claim, and existence claims can
hide anything. Here each reduction is a program in a
small embedded language, written in Lean, whose
running cost is computed from the program's text by
the framework itself — so the claim that a reduction
exists is a claim with a meter attached, and the final
bound (the paper's Theorem 2) is arithmetic over
meters that were actually read. The reductions
compose through a lemma of the same shape as 1.2.217:
a reduction correct for the model stays correct for
the binary, because the binary is a refinement of the model. The
concept carries a discipline this tracker keeps for
its own claims — a claim priced by what it rests on —
into the one place pen-and-paper cryptography most
often waves its hands.
# Concept: PQXDH — a handshake whose secrecy survives the fall of its curves
Source: Paper 017, section 3.1. NEW in Paper 017.

PQXDH is the handshake that opens a Signal
conversation in its post-quantum form. Each party
publishes a bundle of prekeys: identity keys, a signed
medium-term prekey, one-time prekeys, and a
post-quantum prekey — a key-encapsulation key of the
Kyber family. The opener computes shared secrets two
ways: by elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman over the
bundles, and by encapsulating a fresh secret to the
other's post-quantum prekey; the two are mixed through
a key-derivation function into the root secret from
which the keys of the conversation come down. The mixing is
the point: the secrecy of the conversation holds if either
way holds, so a future break of the curves — the
quantum threat the design answers — does not by itself
open conversations whose post-quantum leg stood. The
paper proves this handshake, at the binary level
(1.2.214), in a game where the attacker may corrupt
parties and reveal session keys, but may not ask the
one unfair question — reveal the very session key
under test — nor corrupt at the moment that would make the game empty. One key in the bundle does double
duty, serving both the Diffie-Hellman leg and the signature that stands behind the prekey; the framework
prices that honestly, in joint assumptions (1.2.221).
# Concept: The Triple Ratchet — a fresh key for every message, healing as it goes
Source: Paper 017, section 3.1. NEW in Paper 017.

The Triple Ratchet is the machinery that keys an
ongoing conversation. From the root secret of the handshake
(1.2.219), two ratchet mechanisms turn. The root ratchet:
whenever a party receives a new ratchet key from the
other, a fresh Diffie-Hellman computation folds new
secrecy into the root, and a new chain key steps out —
this is the healing motion, post-compromise security:
a leaked state stops helping an attacker once fresh
keys have turned. The chain ratchet: each message
takes its own key, derived one-way from the chain key,
which then advances and forgets — forward secrecy: a
leaked state cannot open messages already sent.
Messages that arrive out of order are served from a
store of skipped message keys, kept so messages that arrive late can still be read. Beside the classical pair turns
the post-quantum one (SPQR): a fresh encapsulation at
each turn, its secret mixed into every message key,
so the quantum protection of the handshake is renewed
message by message instead of being spent at the door.
The paper proves the composition at the binary level,
in a game where the questions of the attacker are answered
by oracles and the handshake game and the ratchet
game are joined by a promotion step that carries a
finished handshake into a running conversation.
# Concept: A key used twice, judged together — when one secret serves two masters
Source: Paper 017, sections 3.1 and 5. NEW in Paper 017.

In the bundles of the handshake (1.2.219) a single
Curve25519 key serves two duties: it is a
Diffie-Hellman key, whose secrecy the key exchange
leans on, and a signing key, believed for the prekey bundle because its signatures cannot be forged. Standard practice
proves each duty under its own assumption, about a key
used for that duty alone; a key used twice is covered
by neither proof. The framework states the need
plainly and assumes together what it cannot yet prove
apart: a small set of joint assumptions, under which
the double-duty key is safe in both roles at once —
assumptions the literature has partial results toward,
cited and weighed in the paper, and which stand in its
trusted base (1.2.222) as named, priced trust. The
concept is the general shape, met wherever one
artifact is loaded twice: the proofs of the single
loads do not compose for free, and the honest move is
to name the joint claim, put it in the open, and mark
whether it is theorem or assumption. This paper marks
it: assumption.
# Concept: The trusted base, enumerated — trust, listed and priced
Source: Paper 017, sections 5.3 and 7. NEW in Paper 017.

The paper closes its account by listing, exactly,
what a reader must trust for its theorems to hold:
the statements of the specification and every definition
they rest on, down the whole chain — some eighteen thousand
lines to read for the headline theorems; the
instruction-set semantics (1.2.212), some eight
thousand lines more; the contracts at the hooks
(1.2.213); the joint assumptions (1.2.221); and the
tooling that checks — the kernel of the proof assistant,
the compiler that builds the checker, the comparison
the gatekeeper runs (1.2.211). Against that list stand
the twenty-six thousand lines of library the theorems
cover and the six and a half million lines of proof
that need no trust at all. The enumeration is itself
the concept: a trusted base is not a smell to say sorry for but a ledger to be published — every
entry named, every entry priced in the only currency
a reader has, their reading. This tracker keeps the
same ledger for its own claims in its standing record
(1.4.1-1.4.6); the paper's version, drawn for a binary
and its proofs, is the fullest worked instance the
tracker holds. What keeps such a ledger honest when
it grows past any one reader is asked openly at Q85.
# Concept: Specification engineering — the discipline of the statement
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the advantages of abstractions. NEW in Study S010.

The essay names a discipline: working with large
specifications in their own right — an under-appreciated and
timely line of research, with tools and skills of its own. Its
tasks, as the essay lists them: speeding the interpreters of
semantic models; finding the loop invariants and the
conditions before and after that proofs need; and bringing
specifications into the workflows that already stand, by
testing and fuzzing, so that bugs are found and assurance is
raised step by step on the way to a full proof.

Cluster W holds the specification as an artifact (1.2.207) and
the testing of it (1.2.208). This concept is the claim that
the artifact deserves an engineering discipline around it —
expertise, taste and collaboration, in the words of the essay — as
definitions and theory stand to proof in mathematics: a view
of verification that sees only the proof misses where most of
the work lives.

The bet of the essay stands on the discipline: that specification
engineering, with shared semantic models (1.2.224), gives
autoverification (1.3.221) more predictable, more reliable, at
fewer tokens. The bet is priced as a question, and stands open
at Q89.
# Concept: The agreed semantic model — one meaning, held in common
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the second milestone and the golden model. NEW in Study S010.

A semantic model of a language, or of an instruction set, that
the stakeholders of the language agree to stand under: a
single source of truth for what the programs mean. The examples of the essay: the model of RISC-V written in Sail, years in the
making at Cambridge, Edinburgh, SRI and Bluespec, before
RISC-V International took it up as the golden model of the
architecture; the official model of Arm, written in its own
architecture language; the model of WebAssembly.

Against these the essay sets the standing habit it refuses:
verification teams building custom, ad hoc, not executable semantics for shared infrastructure, each validated to a degree of its own, carrying no authority and held as property. A model that is to
be agreed must be executable and open to machine checking; the
criteria the essay takes from Sewell and his co-workers run to
sixteen, the social and the technical counted together —
stakeholder agreement and a single source of truth among them.

The semantics of an instruction set at 1.2.212 is the writing of a processor by one campaign, held inside one house.
This concept is the same kind of object at the scale of a
community: the meaning, agreed. The agreement is the
achievement, and it is social before it is technical (Q91).
# Concept: Validated, not verified — a semantics is a definition
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the four ways and note 17. NEW in Study S010.

The hinge of the essay, stated in its own note: the semantics of a
language are a definition. They cannot be verified; they can
only be validated. A definition is not a claim about the world
that a proof could settle — it fixes what the words mean, and
its goodness is of another kind: does it catch the intent,
neither allowing too much nor refusing too much.

For a specification, the essay gives four ways to validate.
First, read it, to validate intent. Second, test it —
differential fuzzing among the tests — to validate what it
observes. Third, prove that implementations satisfy it, to
validate it as a target. Fourth, prove useful properties from
it, to validate it as a basis. The machine that runs these
four ways stands drawn at 1.5.2.

What stands under every pole of machine-checked trust (1.2.48)
is an object of this kind: a definition, validated, never
verified. The rider of this study on 1.2.208 sets the four ways beside the three tests of the framework of Chapter 27. What carries intent when the statement grows past
one reader stands open at Q92.
# Concept: Structure lost by lowering — what the lower level cannot say
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the advantages of abstractions. NEW in Study S010.

To carry a program down to a lower level is to throw away
structure the higher level held. The worked case of the essay is the flow of control. Cerberus carries C into Core by elaboration (1.3.220), and, like a compiler, it makes the loops and the
early returns of the source into one construct of jumps — in
Core, the pair run and save — and a running program under that
construct no longer shows the nesting of its loops. A general
rule for reasoning about the flattened construct is hard to
work with, and what a loop does not touch — the frame that
lets invariants nest with the loops — is no longer in the form
to be read.

Binary code has the same shape, and the essay prices the loss
in the currency of agents: what was thrown away must be let
go, or built again from what remains — and building again is
paid in tokens, or in the same ground covered twice. A nearby line of work shows the tax and its workaround: in
Gillian-C the loops of C are compiled to jumps as well, and
the nesting of the source is carried alongside as annotations
— the structure, put back by hand.

The bridge of concretization (1.2.216) crosses this loss in
one direction, carrying claims down to the binary. This
concept names the loss itself, which both directions pay.
# Concept: The envelope of allowed builds — a meaning wider than any one binary
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the first milestone. NEW in Study S010.

The semantics of a language at the source level allows more
than any one binary does: it fixes the envelope of allowed
builds — every behaviour and every build that counts as the
program, under the meaning of its language.

A proof at the source level therefore ranges over the whole
envelope at once. It is the harder proof, since the reasoning
must be more general; and it is the cheaper proof across a
field of builds, since the one proof does the work that proofs
of single binaries would do again and again.

The same openness is the open edge of the pole. The binary that is
deployed stands inside the envelope only if the compiler kept
to the meaning — a keeping the source proof does not itself
check. Paper 017 takes the compiler out of the trusted base
altogether (1.2.214); this pole leaves a compiler-shaped hole
in its own, and names the hole as its own open question: how
proofs at the source extend, piece by piece, to the binaries
that run (Q90).
# Concept: Refinement between two semantics — two meanings, one authority
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the second milestone. NEW in Study S010.

The second milestone of the project writes a second formalization
of the operational semantics of Core — substitution-based,
leaving out the details that belong to the running of
Cerberus' interpreter — and proves that the interpreter is a
refinement of it. Refinement (1.3.214) is used in Paper 017
between a model and a binary (1.2.215); here it stands between
two meanings of the one language.

The link is the concept. The clean semantics may be reasoned
from; the interpreter may be run; and the theorem carries
authority between them, so that neither writing stands alone
as one team's ad hoc semantics — the refusal of 1.2.224,
answered at the scale of one language's core.

The proving of the link did what the proving of links does: it
found a fault in the gap. The expressions of Core that name
where a jump may land are gathered into a map of labels before
the proof work begins, lifted out of the place where they
stand in the text; lifted, they lose the scope of that place,
and may name variables that stand outside it. A design bug for
jumping to labels — found between the two semantics, and in
neither one alone.
# Concept: The loaded definition — intent rides in a hypothesis
Source: Study S010 (Chapter 28), the demonstration of Basis read closely. NEW in Study S010.

The essay reads a recent demonstration closely: a semantics
for the WHILE language, a small teaching language, derived
from a reference interpreter for it, written in C and compiled
to RISC-V machine code. It asks where the trust of the
demonstration rests, and finds it in a definition that the
theorem takes as a hypothesis — the definition named Loaded —
which carries the memory layout of the language's abstract
syntax and the state of the interpreter.

To trust the result, a reader must understand that one
definition, and must further take it that the binary
interpreter catches the intent of the semantics of WHILE — a
taking that itself rests on reading the C source and on
watching what the binary gives back. The intent of the thing
described does not come along with the deriving; it rides inside the definition until one of the four ways
(1.2.225) is spent on it.

And since the sources stood open to the agents all along, the
abstractions of the source level could have been guiding the
work in any case — informally, with more room left for error,
and more tokens to pay when the error is found. The concept
names the catch wherever it stands: when a semantics is
derived from an artifact, look for the definition the intent
rides in.
# The test that prices a candidate — the l_1-estimator

An estimator built from tests taken two at a time. For two
candidates f and g in a class F, the paper's test
statistic reads the data Z through their difference:

T(Z, f, g) = the sum over the design points of
(f(x_i) - Y_i) times sign(f(x_i) - g(x_i)).

Each point casts one vote, and the vote is a sign,
never a size: a point where f stands above g charges
f the whole gap between f and the reading, with its
sign; a point where they
agree charges nothing. The price of a candidate is
the worst vote against it, T(Z, f) = the largest
T(Z, f, g) over all rivals g, and an l_1-estimator
is any candidate whose price stands within a small
constant c of the least price in the class — the set
E_c(Z, F) of the paper. When that set holds one
element it is called the l_1-estimator. The
construction is that of Baraud and Maillard (2026);
this paper takes it in and reads the least squares
estimator against it. What the test never does is
square a gap: the loud point of 1.2.235 can
shout in this court only once, with a sign. The
price of the estimator stands bounded at 1.2.232,
by way of
the degree of 1.2.231. Its kin in the model: the
specification built from tests in cluster I, where a
class is likewise known by the tests it can be put
to (1.2.58). Source: Paper 018, section 2.1,
building on Baraud and Maillard (2026). NEW in
Paper 018.
# Extremal elements and their degree

The candidates the price of 1.2.232 can reach are
the extremal (1.3.229) ones, and each carries a
degree. A candidate f-bar in F is extremal of degree
at most D when the two families of sets it cuts out
on the design points — the sets where rivals stand
above it, {f > f-bar}, and where they stand below
it, {f < f-bar} — are each poor in the sense of
cutting power (1.3.226): their VC-dimension stands
at most D. Every candidate is extremal with degree
at most n, the count of points; the useful ones
stand far below it. A member of a linear space of
dimension d is extremal of degree at most d + 1. A member of the monotone class constant on at most
d pieces is extremal of degree at most d + 1. A
candidate linear on at most d pieces, convex or
concave, stands at most 5 + 2d. The degree is the complexity the price
charges for: not the size of the class, but the
how rich the cuts are that one candidate lets the
class make around it. Source: Paper 018, section 2.2,
building on Baraud and Maillard (2026). NEW in
Paper 018.
# The average-moment price

The bound the l_1-estimator (1.2.230) carries,
proved by Baraud and Maillard (2026) and taken in
here as the paper's Theorem 1. For every extremal
(1.3.229) candidate f-bar of degree D in the class,
the expected l_1-risk of any estimator in E_c(Z, F)
stands at most

2 l_1(f*, f-bar) + kappa . sigma_n(p) (D/n)^(1 - 1/p)
+ c/n,

with the least value taken over p in [1, 2], kappa a
plain constant, and sigma_n(p) the AVERAGE over the
design points of the p-th absolute centred moment,
(1/n) the sum of E|Y_i - E Y_i|^p. Three things in
the bound do the work of the paper. First, no
square need stand: the errors need only a moment of
order p, as low as p = 1 — heavy tails (1.3.223)
are priced, not assumed away. Second, the noise
enters by its average, not by its worst point: a
single loud point moves the price by its share of
the average, where a price set by the maximum would
be set by that point alone — the bound is steady
under unequal spread (1.3.222) by its shape. Third,
the rate the bound gives is the rate of the field
itself: at
p = 2 with bounded degree, the parametric rate
1/sqrt(n); at p in (1, 2), the rate n^-(1 - 1/p),
which Devroye and his co-workers (2016) showed
no estimator beats in general, under a moment of
order p alone. Beside the price of the isotonic
estimator at 1.2.129 — local variation, read through
windows, under noise of one spread — this is a
second price for the same estimator, charged on
degree and average moment; the coincidence of
1.2.233 says the two prices price one object.
Source: Paper 018, section 2.3 (Theorem 1 of
Baraud and Maillard, 2026). NEW in Paper 018.
# The coincidence — on the monotone class the two estimators are one

The paper's main result (its Theorem 2). Let F be
the class of functions on an interval that never
fall, and let the least squares estimator over F
stand in — the projection of the seen list onto the
monotone cone (1.2.124, 1.3.151). Then that
estimator makes the test price of 1.2.230 least over
F, and its price is exactly zero: T(Z, f-tilde) = 0.
Any other candidate making the price least agrees
with it at every design point. So
on this class the two estimators are one estimator:
the fit built from squares is the fit the sign
tests choose, and the bound of 1.2.232 holds for the
least squares estimator itself, with c = 0. The
same holds on the class of functions that never
rise. What the coincidence
buys is the whole of Paper 012's isotonic ground
re-priced: the estimator whose sharp l_2-price is
the window sum of 1.2.129 now carries an l_1-price
under noise whose square need not average to
anything standing, and which need not share one
spread — because on monotone
ground it was the l_1-estimator all along. The
engine of the proof is the sign step of 1.2.234.
The coincidence is a property of the class, not of
the estimators: on the one-peak class it fails, at
1.2.235, and on a line it fails, at 1.2.236.
Source: Paper 018, section 3 (Theorem 2). NEW in
Paper 018.
# The sign step — the condition that makes the projection the answer

The one assumption the coincidence of 1.2.233 is
proved under, stated by the paper as its
Assumption 1 and worth a scroll of its own: a class
F meets it when, first, the set of its candidates'
vectors on the design points is closed and convex,
and second, for every two candidates f and g in F
some lambda > 0 has f - lambda . sign(f - g) still
in F — from any candidate, a small step in the
direction the signs name, against any rival, stays
inside the class. Under the assumption the proof is
three lines: the projection is known by
<Y - f-tilde, g - f-tilde> <= 0 for all g; putting
the stepped candidate in the place of g gives
T(Z, f-tilde, g) <= 0 for all g, which is the
coincidence. The work stands in checking the
assumption on the monotone cone. Where f rises
strictly, a step can break the rise only across a
gap, and lambda at half the smallest positive gap
keeps every step safe; where f stands flat, the
signs of two rising candidates can only help the
rise, and every lambda is safe. So the monotone
class meets the condition by the shape of its gaps.
The paper states the theorem for every class that
meets the condition; which of the other classes of
the field meet it stands open (Q94). Source:
Paper 018, section 3 (Assumption 1 and the proof
of Theorem 2). NEW in Paper 018.
# The loud point — the one-peak break

Where the coincidence dies. On the class of
unimodal (1.3.228) functions on [0, 1] — one peak,
its place free — the paper builds one example
(its Theorem 3). The truth is a plateau: f* = 1 on
[1/4, 1/2], 0 elsewhere. The noise is Gaussian and
silent at every design point but one: at
i_0 = the point nearest 3n/4 the spread stands at
sqrt(n), so that single reading Y_{i_0} swings on
the scale of sqrt(n). On an event whose chance
tends to the chance a standard bell draw clears
1/2 — at least 30 percent — that reading stands
huge. Now any one-peak candidate that stays near
f* in l_1 must keep its peak left of i_0, and so
must stand below 1/2 at i_0; its square error at
that one point then exceeds the whole square error
of a rival that jumps to the loud reading at i_0
and makes the stretch between the plateau and i_0
flat at a common level — a rival whose total stands
at most n/4 + 1. So least squares cannot be the
near one: its l_1-error stands at least (1 - 4/n)/8
on the event. Least squares is not consistent here. The
l_1-estimator stands untouched by the same reading:
f* is extremal in the class of degree at most 6 —
three constant pieces, two monotone parts — and
sigma_n(2) = 1, so the price of 1.2.232 gives its
expected l_1-risk at most kappa . sqrt(6)/sqrt(n)
+ c/n: the parametric rate. The mechanism is the
pair of the coincidence read backwards. Squaring
lets one point's error outweigh a quarter of the
design; a class whose peak may sit anywhere lets
the fit spend itself chasing that point. On the
monotone cone the chase is impossible — no second
peak can be raised — and the coincidence of
1.2.233 is the protection. Off the cone, the
protection is that the class is convex, and the
one-peak class is not (1.3.225). Source:
Paper 018, section 4 (Theorem 3). NEW in
Paper 018.
# Weighted hearing — on a line, the placement of the noise picks the winner

The answering case, on the smallest class of all:
F the line through one positive shape f_1, the
truth a* f_1 (the paper's section 5, its
Proposition 1). Here both estimators stand in
closed form. The least squares estimator is the
noise heard through the shape: a-tilde = the sum of
Y_i f_1(x_i) over the sum of f_1(x_i)^2, so each
point's noise is weighted by the size of the shape
at that point. The
l_1-estimator is the noise heard flat: because
f_1 is positive, every sign in its test (1.2.230)
collapses to the sign of the coefficient difference,
and a-hat = the plain sum of the Y_i over the sum
of the f_1(x_i) — every point weighted alike. The
two quadratic risks stand in exact ratio: the
risk of the l_1-estimator over the risk of the
least squares estimator is a weighted average of
the values f_1(x_i)^2, weighted by the
noise spreads sigma_i^2 — an average that stands
between the smallest and the largest of those
squares. So neither estimator dominates, and the
winner is named by an alignment: where the noise
sits against where the shape is large. With
f_1(x) = x and all the noise loaded on the first
point, the risk of least squares beats the other
by a factor of order n^2: at spread tau_n = n
there, the l_1-estimator is not consistent while
least squares converges at rate 1/n — the loud point of 1.2.235
read in the mirror, the shape standing near zero
exactly where the noise stands loud, so the
weighted ear barely hears it and the flat ear hears
nothing else. The reverse placement is the example of Baraud and
Maillard, cited by the paper: a shape large where
the noise is loaded makes least squares the
inconsistent one and leaves the l_1-estimator at
log(n)/sqrt(n). Hearing is a measurement choice —
the model's measurement agenda (1.2.14) meets the
same fork whenever a grade is read off data whose
noise is not spread evenly. Source: Paper 018,
section 5 (Proposition 1). NEW in Paper 018.
# The joint numerical range

The set of readings a family of Hermitian (1.3.241) matrices gives at
once. Fix Hermitian matrices A_1, ..., A_n of one size d. For a unit
vector x, read the n numbers x* A_1 x, ..., x* A_n x — the values the
matrices take on that one direction. The joint numerical range
W(A) is the set of all such readings, over all unit vectors: a subset
of the real space of dimension n. For one matrix it is the classical
numerical range, a subset of the complex numbers. In quantum
information the same set is the readings a state can give for n quantities that can be measured at once: the values that can stand together.

The model also holds the filled form of the range. A density matrix is a
Hermitian matrix with no negative reading and with trace one; the
pure states are those of rank one, of the form x x*. Letting the
density matrix run over all of them — not only the pure ones — gives
the set W-hat(A) of the traces tr(rho A_i), and the spectral theorem
writes every density matrix as a weighted average of pure ones, so
W-hat(A) is exactly the convex hull (1.3.30) of W(A). The paper calls
W-hat the convex support, and also the joint algebraic numerical
range. For two matrices the two sets agree — the
Toeplitz-Hausdorff theorem — and for three matrices the range itself
is convex when d is at least 3, by Au-Yeung and Poon; in the paper's
four worked cases, all of size 4, range and hull are one set. The
range is what the paper's theorem prices: it is the convex hull of
an algebraic curve or surface, read off a determinant (1.2.239).
Source: Paper 019, sections 1 and 2.1. NEW in Paper 019.
# The determinantal polynomial — the one polynomial of the family

One polynomial carries the whole geometry of a family of Hermitian
(1.3.241) matrices. For A = (A_1, ..., A_n) of size d, set

p_A(u_0, ..., u_n) is the determinant of (u_0 I + u_1 A_1 + ... + u_n A_n),

the determinant (1.3.232) of the linear combination of the matrices
with the identity. It is homogeneous (1.3.233) of degree d: the size
of the matrices is the degree of the polynomial. Its zero set in
projective space (1.3.236) is a hypersurface (1.3.242), the
determinantal (1.3.243) hypersurface of the family.

The polynomial is read by its factors. Over the number field (1.3.235)
K of the entries, p_A splits as a product of distinct irreducible
(1.3.239) factors F_i, each possibly raised to a power. The powers
are set aside — the varieties are taken with their reduced
structure — and each factor is carried through the rest of the
method on its own: tested by Katz's test (1.2.240), sampled
(1.2.242), and either given a certified dual equation (1.2.246) or
recorded with the marker 0. The joint numerical range (1.2.237) is
recovered from the dual variety of each of these factors and from nothing else:
factor first, and the rest of the method is one factor at a time.
Source: Paper 019, sections 2.2 and 3. NEW in Paper 019.
# The algebraic Kippenhahn variety

The dual side of the determinantal (1.3.243) hypersurface, and the
object Kippenhahn's theorem names as the generator of the range.
Take one irreducible factor F of the determinantal polynomial
(1.2.238) and its zero set X = V(F) in projective space (1.3.236).
The dual variety X-dual is the closure of the set got from the tangent hyperplane (1.3.236) at each smooth point of X — one point of the dual projective space for each, by the Gauss map (1.2.241). Doing this for every factor and taking the union gives
the algebraic Kippenhahn variety of the family A. A factor that is
linear contributes a point, not a surface.

Kippenhahn's theorem, in its classical form for two matrices, says
the numerical range is the convex hull (1.3.30) of the real affine
part of this dual: the boundary of the range need not itself be
algebraic, yet the whole range is generated, by filling in, from an
algebraic curve got by duality. The higher-dimensional form of
Plaumann, Sinn and Weis says the same with one care: in the real
part, take the smooth real points of each dual and then the closure
— singular real points that are limits of smooth ones are restored,
and singular real points that arise only from complex branches are
kept out. Then W-hat(A), the filled joint numerical range
(1.2.237), is the convex hull of that real part.

So the dual varieties of the factors are the fundamental algebraic objects of the range: their real parts hold the extreme points the hull is
filled from. Which of them are each a hypersurface at all is decided by
Katz's test (1.2.240); the equations of those that are, this paper
computes (1.2.247). The duality here is the duality of point and hyperplane in projective space; it is not the duality of cycles
and co-cycles the model holds at 1.3.26, and the map keeps the two
apart. Source: Paper 019, section 2.2, after Kippenhahn and
Plaumann, Sinn and Weis (2021). NEW in Paper 019.
# Katz's test — which factors have a hypersurface dual

The gate at the head of the method: before anything is sampled, the
test decides whether the dual of a factor is a hypersurface (1.3.242) at
all. Let F be an irreducible factor of degree at least 2, X = V(F),
and Hess(F) the table of second partial derivatives of F — the
Hessian. Katz's form of the dimension formula for a dual variety
says the dimension of X-dual is h - 2, where h is the rank of the
Hessian at a general point of X. The Hessian has size (n+1) by
(n+1), so the dual is a hypersurface exactly when its determinant
does not vanish at every point of X — and since F is irreducible,
exactly when F does not divide the determinant of the Hessian of F.

The test is one exact question of division over the number field K, answered by the function KatzTest of the paper: a nonzero
remainder is a positive answer; a zero remainder means the dual has
smaller dimension, and the main routine records the marker 0 for
that factor and spends nothing more on it. Only the factors that
pass are sampled (1.2.242) and certified (1.2.246). The paper notes
the road not taken: the method could be adapted to dual varieties of dimension short of a hypersurface, and is restricted to the hypersurface case to keep the
paper short. Source: Paper 019, section 3.1, after Katz in Gelfand,
Kapranov and Zelevinsky (1994). NEW in Paper 019.
# The Gauss map — a point of the variety becomes a point of the dual

The map that turns the dual variety from a definition into a thing
that can be sampled. For an irreducible factor F, the Gauss map
sends a smooth point x of X = V(F) to the point of the dual
projective space whose coordinates are the partial derivatives of F
at x:

gamma_F(x) = (F_{u_0}(x) : ... : F_{u_n}(x)).

Those numbers are the coefficients of the tangent hyperplane
(1.3.236) of X at x, so the image of the map is the set of tangent hyperplane points, and the closure of the image is the dual variety
X-dual of 1.2.239. Where F vanishes and its gradient does not, the
map is defined; at singular points it is not, and the method never
asks it to be.

Everything computational in the paper runs through this map. A
point on the dual is never built by eliminating variables: it is
the gradient of F at a smooth point, computed directly (1.2.242).
And the certificate of the whole method is a statement about the composition of this map: a candidate equation H is the equation of the dual exactly when H, fed the gradient, vanishes wherever F does
(1.2.246). Source: Paper 019, sections 3 and 3.2. NEW in
Paper 019.
# Sampling the dual — smooth points over finite fields

How points on the dual hypersurface are got: not by solving, but by
sampling, over finite fields (1.3.234) where the arithmetic is
cheap. Work at one completely split prime (1.2.244), so F has a
reduction F_p over the finite field for each root of the defining
polynomial. To find a point of V(F_p): choose the variable in which
F_p has the smallest positive degree, give the remaining variables
random values from the field, and the result is a polynomial in one
variable; when it does not stand constant and has a root in the field, take
one root — one only from each such try, since roots from the same
try can yield conditions on the interpolation (1.3.238) that lean on
each other. This gives a point x of V(F_p); when the gradient of
F_p at x is not zero, x is smooth and its Gauss image (1.2.241) is
a point of the dual. Distinct images are kept.

The search carries its own stopping rule, stated plainly as a
budget: after max{1000, 100 times the degree in the chosen
variable} tries in a row produce no new Gauss image, the prime is
abandoned — the counter is reset whenever a new image is found —
and if the budget runs out at any one root of the defining
polynomial, the whole prime is discarded and the method moves to
the next. The sample so gathered is the raw material of the
interpolation (1.2.243): the points at which the unknown equation
must vanish. Source: Paper 019, section 3.2. NEW in Paper 019.
# The candidate degree — the kernel's size as a compass

How the method learns the degree of the equation it is looking for,
without being told. There is a ceiling: for a factor of degree
delta, the degree D of the dual stands at most delta (delta - 1)^(n-1),
by a lemma of Ballico the paper cites. Inside that ceiling the
degree is found by a search whose signal is the size of a kernel.
For a trial degree e, form the evaluation table: one row for each
sampled point (1.2.242), one column for each term of degree e, the
entries the values of the terms at the points. The forms of degree
e that vanish at every sampled point are the kernel of that table.
With enough general points the kernel reads the degree off: no
form at all means the trial degree is too small; several
independent forms mean it is too large; exactly one form, up to a
nonzero multiple, means the degree is found, and that form is the
candidate equation at this prime.

The search walks the interval by halves — above the tested degree
when no form is found, below it when there are several, the next
trial the midpoint rounded up. For a trial degree e the method
samples at least the count of degree-e terms, times 11/10, rounded up: a surplus of a tenth over the bare count, held to lower
the risk of a wrong candidate degree. The paper is plain that the
surplus is an empirical choice: it does not guarantee independent
conditions or the correct degree, and the user can raise it in the
code. The degree found at one root is the first trial at the next.
Source: Paper 019, section 3.3. NEW in Paper 019.
# Completely split primes — one prime, every root

The primes at which a number field (1.3.235) becomes a row of
finite fields (1.3.234). Let K = Q(alpha), with alpha a root of an irreducible polynomial f whose leading coefficient is one. A prime p splits completely in K
when f, read modulo p, falls into a product of distinct linear
factors — one root r_j in the finite field for each of the m =
[K : Q] roots alpha has in K. Each root gives a way to read the
coefficients of the factor F in that finite field: write each
coefficient in the power basis 1, alpha, ..., alpha^(m-1), reduce
its rational coordinates modulo p, and set alpha = r_j. The whole
computation — sampling (1.2.242), the degree search (1.2.243) — is
then run once per root at the same prime, and the m answers are the
raw material of the reconstruction (1.2.245).

Two disciplines guard the choice. Before any reduction, primes dividing the denominator of a coefficient of f, or of a power-basis coordinate of the normalized F, are excluded. And
primes of this kind never run out: infinitely many completely
split primes remain, by the Chebotarev density theorem applied to
the splitting field of f. A second discipline guards the answers:
the equations got at the m roots must share one leading term. If
they differ, the whole prime is discarded. Across primes, the
largest leading term encountered is retained: a prime giving a
smaller one is discarded, and a prime giving a larger one makes
the method discard all data gathered so far and begin the
reconstruction again from that prime — a larger leading term is
the sign that every earlier prime read the coefficients through a
denominator it could not see. Source: Paper 019, sections 3, 3.2
and 3.4. NEW in Paper 019.
# Reconstruction across the roots — from finite fields back to the number field

The lift at the centre of the method: a candidate equation over
the number field K, rebuilt from its readings over finite fields.
Fix a term and write its coefficient in the true equation G as
c = a_0 + a_1 alpha + ... + a_{m-1} alpha^(m-1), the a_i rational
numbers in the power basis of K = Q(alpha). At one completely
split prime (1.2.244), the computation at root r_j gives the
coefficient c_j of that term in the equation found there — and
c_j is c itself, read with alpha set to r_j. So the m readings at
the prime give a linear system over the finite field whose table
is the Vandermonde table of the roots — ones, roots, squares of
roots, up to the (m-1)-th powers — and whose solution is the a_i
read modulo p. The table can always be solved: its determinant is
a product of differences of distinct roots, and the roots are
distinct.

Repeating at further primes gives each a_i modulo many primes.
The Chinese remainder theorem joins those readings into one
reading modulo the product M of the primes; rational
reconstruction (1.3.240) then finds the fraction with small
numerator and denominator behind that reading, and once M stands
large enough against the true numerator and denominator of each, the fraction is the a_i itself. Term by term, this builds a candidate H over K again. If the reconstruction fails, or the candidate
changes when another prime is added, the method takes more primes
— the implementation sets no fixed limit. When the candidate
stands unchanged after one more prime, it is sent to certification
(1.2.246). What sampling and interpolation found, the lift states
exactly — as a candidate, not yet as a result. Source: Paper 019,
section 3.4. NEW in Paper 019.
# Certification of the reconstruction — the gate of division

The step that turns a candidate into a result: two exact tests
over K, resting on one proposition. Proposition 3.1 of the paper:
let F be homogeneous and irreducible over a field of characteristic
zero, its dual a hypersurface with irreducible equation G. For any
nonzero homogeneous H of positive degree,

H(F_{u_0}, ..., F_{u_n}) is a whole multiple of F exactly when H is a whole multiple of G.

The ground of it: the smooth locus of V(F) is dense and its Gauss
image (1.2.241) is dense in V(G), so H fed the gradient of F
vanishes on the smooth locus exactly when H vanishes on the dual.
If besides this H is irreducible over K, then G dividing H makes
the two differ by a nonzero constant only: H is the equation of the dual, certified.

The tests are run to be fast, and the paper says how. The
composition H of the gradient is evaluated by a Horner scheme in several variables, then divided by F. When F and H have rational
coefficients, both are first scaled to whole-number coefficients
with no common factor, and Gauss's lemma then keeps the quotient
in whole numbers, so the work runs in fast integer arithmetic.
Irreducibility is first tried modulo a prime: a reduction of H of
the same degree that is irreducible over a finite field makes H
irreducible over K; at most three such primes are tried before the
test is run directly over K. Certification takes between 4 and 30
percent of the whole running time of the method in the paper's
four cases (1.2.247).

The shape is the point for this model: nothing in the pipeline is
trusted on its likelihood. Sampling is random, the degree is
guessed and corrected, the lift is heuristic in its stopping —
and the gate at the end is exact, over the original field, against
the original factor. What passes it is not a likely equation but
the equation. Source: Paper 019, section 3.5. NEW in Paper 019.
# The four certified dual surfaces

The paper's worked corpus: four determinantal (1.3.243) polynomials
of degree four, from matrix examples in the literature, each
carried through the whole method to a certified dual equation.
Three inputs are each a Davis-Wielandt triple — for a 4 by 4 matrix T,
the Hermitian (1.3.241) matrices Re T, Im T and T*T, whose joint
numerical range (1.2.237) is the Davis-Wielandt shell of T — and
the fourth is a Hermitian triple of Pikul and his co-workers. All
four are read over the one field Q(alpha) with alpha^4 - alpha^2 +
1 = 0.

The matrix T_nil of Chien and Nakazato, whose powers vanish, gives a dual
surface of degree 12 with 135 terms, from 5 primes, in under a
second in both implementations. The triple E_12, whose entries
stand in Q(i, root 3), gives degree 18 with 691 terms, from 9
primes, in about ten seconds; its first completely split prime at
or above 2003 is 2017. The triple E_1 gives degree 24 with 2,919
terms, from 18 primes, in about half a minute. The corrected
matrix T_CN gives degree 34 with 4,047 terms, from 31 primes: 543
seconds in Magma on one thread, 348 on ten; 912 and 570 in
Singular. From the matrices, with no preparation shaped to the
example, the method computes and certifies this last equation in
under six minutes.

Against this stands the older road — Groebner bases, the striking out of variables — run on the same four: of eight attempts across the two
systems only two finished inside a day — Singular on the degree-12
surface in 0.02 seconds, and Singular on the degree-18
surface in 527 seconds, against the ten of Magma. The rest did not
finish within 24 hours. Chien and Nakazato got the degree-34
equation by older tools and interpolation of their own; Nakazato's
estimate for the full computation was about twelve hours, and the
author reproduced it in 163 minutes in Mathematica — while noting
that reproduction still used knowledge shaped to the example, the
known degree and the retained factors, and so is not entered in
the comparison table. Source: Paper 019, section 4. NEW in
Paper 019.
# The piecewise translation — a language rebuilt piece by piece

The construction the whole paper stands on. A piecewise
translation from a first language to a second is given by a
reconstrual (1.3.256), in three parts. The sort function: for
each sort of the first language, a finite family of pieces;
each piece is a finite row of sorts of the second language,
and the pieces of a sort are named by a set of pieces — a set
built from nothing in a finite number of steps, so the family can
be spoken of inside the second theory itself. The domain
function: for each piece, a formula of the second language
saying which rows of that shape count as standing in the
piece. The relation function: for each relation symbol of
the first language and each choice of one piece for each of
its sorts, a formula saying when the relation holds between
rows standing in those pieces. A variable function, giving
the variables of the first language to rows of variables of
the second with no overlap between pieces, completes the
reconstrual; the paper shows the choice of it does not
matter, and that a reconstrual is carried whole by its sort,
domain and relation functions, up to provable equivalence
(1.2.250).

From the reconstrual the translation of every formula is
built by walking the formula itself. An atom is read by the
relation function, piece by piece. The steps for and, or,
if-then and not pass through unchanged. A for-every step over
a sort becomes a joining over all the pieces of that sort:
for each piece, if the row stands in the domain of the piece then
the translated body holds of it. A sentence — a formula with
no free variables — lands on a sentence: its translation
stands at the one empty piece. Formulas travel in context
throughout: each formula carries the row of its free
variables in the order they first occur, its canonical
context, and the canonical context of a translation is the
translation of the context. Set the family of each sort to
one piece and the construction gives back the ordinary
translations of the older theory of interpretations
(Halvorson); the piecewise form is the general one, and the
ordinary form its one-piece case. Visser's first form of the
idea gives the pieces to the free variables instead; this
form gives them to the sorts, which is what lets substitution
and composition be held under control. The author expects
the two forms to agree and says plainly that the agreement
has not been worked out.
Source: Paper 020, sections 1.1, 2.1 and Appendix A. NEW in
Paper 020.
# The piecewise interpretation — a translation that carries theorems across

A piecewise translation (1.2.248) is a piecewise
interpretation of a theory S in a theory T when it carries
theorems across: for every sentence of the first language,
if S proves it then T proves its translation. Identity is
the hinge of the matter. Identity is treated in the paper as
a symbol of the language like any other, each theory proving
its two laws without saying so; under a translation it
becomes a family of relations, one for each pair of pieces
of a sort. A translation is identity-respecting when two
things hold in T: the domain formulas of each sort are
together not empty — some piece of each sort has something
standing in it (the Domain condition) — and the translated
laws of identity hold. The paper's criterion then prices
being an interpretation exactly: a translation is an
interpretation if and only if it is identity-respecting and
carries each axiom of S to a theorem of T. Under it stands a
preservation theorem: a derivation in S translates to a
derivation in T, premises to translated premises — proved
semantically, by the dual of 1.2.251.

What the piecewise form buys over the ordinary one is shown
by the smallest case in the paper. Let E_n be the theory, in
one sort with no relations, that there are exactly n things.
For n at least 2 there is no ordinary interpretation of E_n
in E_1: one thing cannot be read as n. But a piecewise
translation with n pieces, each piece a row of no sorts at
all, its domain the claim that holds of everything, and
identity read as holding inside one piece and failing across
pieces, interprets E_n in any theory whatever. Piecewise
interpretability (1.3.254) is strictly weaker as a demand
than ordinary interpretability: theories the ordinary road
holds apart, the piecewise road joins. The paper's second
example is the classical one the abstract names: the
projective completion of an affine plane, read piecewise —
the points at infinity are a second piece of the sort of
points, carried by the lines themselves up to parallelism,
and the line at infinity a second piece of the sort of
lines — where the ordinary road back exists only with
parameters, and the ordinary road forward is far more
tangled. Its proof runs through the dual (1.2.251).
Source: Paper 020, sections 2.1-2.3. NEW in Paper 020.
# Provable equivalence — when two translations count as one

Two reconstruals with the same sort function are provably
equivalent under a theory T when T proves their domain
formulas cut out the same sets on their domains and their
relation formulas agree wherever the domains hold. The
equivalence is the paper's unit of sameness at every level
of its building. A reconstrual is presented by its sort,
domain and relation functions only up to it, the variable
function being always able to be completed and its choice
without effect. Translations are studied up to it. Morphisms
between translations carry their own form of it (1.2.252).
And the bicategory of theories (1.2.255) takes
provable-equivalence classes, not bare translations, as its
ways and its ways between ways — so the quotient is not a
convenience of the telling but the substance of the
structure. Its semantic face is a theorem of the dual: two
translations with the same sort function are provably
equivalent if and only if the semantic dual (1.2.251) of
each builds the same model from every model of T — sameness
of the syntax up to proof, and sameness of the induced map
on models, are one fact read in two places. What the
quotient buys: syntax that differs only in the naming of
bound variables, or in formulas the theory itself proves
equal on the domains, is one translation for every purpose
the paper has.
Source: Paper 020, sections 2.1 and 2.4. NEW in Paper 020.
# The semantic dual — a translation read as a way between families of models

Every piecewise translation f from the language of S to the
language of T induces a map the other way between the
families of models: from a model M of T it builds a model
f*M of S. The building, sort by sort: take the union, over
the pieces of the sort, of the sets the domain formulas cut
out in M, the pieces kept apart in the union; then identify
the points the translated identity joins — the union read
modulo the relation that identity becomes under f. The
relations of S are read through the projection onto that
quotient: a row stands in a relation of f*M when its
representatives stand, piece by piece, in the translated
relation inside the domains. The paper is plain that a dual
structure so built is not an interpretable structure in the
usual sense — the usual constructions allow products,
subsets and the quotient of a structure, not a union of
pieces of it — and holds that the union asks no more of the
world than the admitted constructions do.

The load-bearing fact — the author calls it, from a
technical point of view, perhaps the most important
proposition of the paper, and it is what earns the dual its
name — is that satisfaction is preserved exactly: M
satisfies the translation of a formula under an assignment
if and only if f*M satisfies the formula itself under the
projected assignment; for sentences, M satisfies the
translated sentence if and only if f*M satisfies the
sentence. Two readings of the whole matter follow. First, f
is a piecewise interpretation if and only if f*M is a model
of S for every model M of T — and this is the road by which
the paper proves its projective completion (1.2.249) an
interpretation: the dual of the completion translation sends
each affine plane to its usual projective completion, point
by point, line by line. Second, the dual is a functor
(1.3.190) from the category of models of T to the category
of models of S (1.3.245): it carries an elementary embedding
to an elementary embedding, acting on representatives, and
provably equivalent translations have the same dual. The
held kin in this model: the reading of a logic in a category
(1.2.171) is here turned around — not a logic read in a
category, but a translation between logics read as a way
between the categories of their models.
Source: Paper 020, sections 2.4 and 2.5. NEW in Paper 020.
# ii-morphisms — the ways between interpretations

A morphism between two piecewise translations f and g of the
same pair of theories — the paper calls it an ii-morphism —
is a family of formulas, one for each pair of pieces of each
sort, meant to stand as the graph of a map between the two
readings. Five conditions are required, each provable in the
target theory. The formulas live on the domains of f and
land in the domains of g. They keep the two translated
identities in respect: elements joined by the identity of f
are sent to elements joined by the identity of g, and joined
elements that agree under the identity of f have the same
partners. Every element standing in a domain of f is joined
to some element of a domain of g. The partner is unique up
to the identity of g. And joined elements satisfy the same
translated formulas — the join is elementary, preserving
every formula and not only the sentences. Where both
translations are identity-respecting, uniqueness comes from
the other four conditions free.

Below provable equivalence (1.2.250) the paper keeps two
weaker strengths of sameness, and measures them. An
ii-morphism from f to g makes f and g equivalent at the
level of sentences — the same sentences are carried to the
same theorems — so being an interpretation passes between
them: if one is an interpretation, so is the other. An
ii-isomorphism (1.3.251) adds the missing half: every
element of a domain of g is hit by the join; then an inverse
morphism can be written down, and for identity-respecting
translations the ii-isomorphisms are exactly the morphisms
whose dual (1.2.253) is an isomorphism at every model. The
smallest source of examples is the renaming of pieces
itself: any one-to-one matching between the piece sets of
two translations that matches sorts, domains and relations
induces an ii-isomorphism acting by that renaming — from
which it follows that provably equivalent translations are
always ii-isomorphic, the quotient of 1.2.250 standing
inside the wider sameness of this scroll.
Source: Paper 020, section 2.6. NEW in Paper 020.
# The dual of an ii-morphism — a natural transformation between the dual maps

An ii-morphism (1.2.252) has a semantic dual of its own, one
level up from the dual of a translation: a natural
transformation (1.3.246) from the functor f* to the functor
g*, where f and g are the translations the morphism joins.
It is pinned down by one condition, the Dual condition: at
each model M of the target theory, the component of the
transformation at the sort in question joins exactly the
pairs of points of the two dual models that the formulas of
the morphism join inside M, under the projection of 1.2.251.
The transformation so pinned is unique, and the reading is
faithful in both directions that matter. Two ii-morphisms
are provably equivalent if and only if the dual of each is
the same transformation — the whole semantic
reading locally faithful, and the hypothesis on which the
reflection principle (1.2.256) spends its force. And the
test runs backwards as well: a family of formulas between
two translations whose transformation exists on the semantic
side, model by model, already satisfies the syntactic
conditions and is an ii-morphism — the semantic recognition
the paper spends in its last application, proving theories
bi-interpretable by checking maps between their models
instead of formulas between their languages (1.2.259).
Source: Paper 020, section 2.6. NEW in Paper 020.
# Composition of piecewise interpretations — end to end, up to isomorphism

The composite of a piecewise translation f from S to T and
one g from T to U, read as g after f: a piece of the
composite is a piece of f together with a choice, for each
sort in the row of that piece, of a piece of g — the pieces
multiply as the readings nest. The variable function applies
the one of g after the one of f; the domain formula of a
composite piece joins the g-translation of the domain
formula of f with the domain formulas of g along the
translated context. That the nesting is honest is a theorem,
not a hope: translating a formula first by f and then by g
gives a formula with the same canonical context as the
translation by the composite, and the two are provably
equivalent already in the theory of identity — the fact
rests on the lemma, proved in the paper's first appendix,
that substitution and translation commute. From it the
closure follows: the composite of two piecewise
interpretations is a piecewise interpretation, and
composition descends to provable-equivalence classes. On the
semantic side the dual of a composite is joined to the
composite of the two dual maps, read in the reverse order,
by a canonical natural isomorphism (1.3.251), the compositor
(1.3.253).

The identity interpretation of a theory — one piece per
sort, domain the whole sort — serves as the unit of the
composition, by ii-isomorphisms (1.2.252) built from the
evident renaming of pieces. And the question Albert Visser
had left open — in what sense is the composition of
piecewise interpretations associative at all? — is answered
in the same terms: the two ways of grouping a triple
composite have their piece sets joined by a one-to-one
matching that sets the rows together again end to end, the
matching induces an ii-isomorphism between the two
composites, and the associator (1.3.253) so built is
coherent. Composition is associative up to coherent
ii-isomorphism, not on the nose — the first place in this
paper where a law of algebra holds only one level up, and
the reason its home structure is a bicategory (1.3.247) and
not a category.
Source: Paper 020, sections 3.1 and 3.2. NEW in Paper 020.
# The bicategory of theories — theories, interpretations and their morphisms as one structure

The collection the paper builds its second half on. Its
objects are the classical first-order theories. Its ways
between two theories are the provable-equivalence classes of
piecewise interpretations between them; its ways between
ways are the provable-equivalence classes of ii-morphisms
(1.2.252). The ways between ways compose along two axes.
Vertically, along a shared interpretation: by a formula that
joins two joins through a middle piece — an or-claim over
the middle pieces, each arm saying there is a middle element
the first join and the second join both take part in.
Horizontally, along a shared theory, each morphism is
translated through the interpretation of the other, in a
formula the paper writes out in full. Vertical composition
is associative with the identity morphism as its strict
unit, and the ii-isomorphisms are exactly the ii-morphisms
that have a vertical inverse. Horizontal composition is not
associative on the nose, and is not expected to be: the
composition of the interpretations themselves holds only up
to the associator (1.2.254), and the horizontal law carries
the same freedom, honestly.

The whole stands as a bicategory (1.3.247), written Th^pw in
the paper: locally small in its ways, its objects a proper
class, a larger cardinal adding objects and nothing else.
Its standing as a bicategory is not proved by checking the
coherence laws — the associator and the
unitors natural, the interchange of the two compositions,
the pentagon, the triangle — one by one; it is inherited, by the
reflection principle (1.2.256), from the strict 2-category
of categories along the semantic dual. Inside it, the
sameness of theories has the precise name the paper's first
half was building toward: equivalence in the bicategory,
piecewise bi-interpretability (1.2.257). A bicategory of
interpretations had been built before, for coherent logic,
by other hands (D'Arienzo, Pagano and McInnis, in the paper's
references); the classical first-order one is this paper's.
Source: Paper 020, sections 3.3 and 3.4. NEW in Paper 020.
# The reflection principle — coherence inherited along a faithful reading

The paper's theorem most made for the later use of the field,
stated and proved in its second appendix. Take a
pre-bicategory: the full data of a bicategory (1.3.247) —
objects, ways, ways between ways, compositions, associators,
unitors — with none of its laws yet checked, but with the
associators and unitors standing as isomorphisms. Take further a
pseudofunctor (1.3.252) running the ways backwards, from the
pre-bicategory into a strict 2-category — a reading of the
data in a world where the laws hold strictly — which is
faithful on each family of ways between ways: distinct
2-morphisms of the source never have the same image. Then
the pre-bicategory is already a bicategory, and the reading
is a pseudofunctor between bicategories. Each law of the
target — the interchange of the two compositions, the
the associator and unitors natural, the pentagon, the
triangle — pulls back along the faithful reading to the same
law in the source: a faithful reading that keeps the
composites cannot send two different composites of the
source to one law-abiding image unless the source composites
were equal already. Coherence, normally the longest bill in
category theory, is paid here by the reading being faithful.

Its first spending is the paper's own centre: the reading
that sends a theory to its category of models (1.3.245), an
interpretation to its semantic dual (1.2.251) and an
ii-morphism to the dual transformation of 1.2.253, with the
compositors of 1.2.254 read backwards, is a locally faithful
pseudofunctor running the ways backwards, from the
pre-bicategory of theories into the 2-category of
categories — the reading being
locally faithful exactly in that the dual is faithful on
morphisms. So the bicategory of theories
(1.2.255) is a bicategory, without one coherence law checked
by hand. The author notes the theorem is hinted at in
Leinster's book on bicategories and found nowhere stated; it
stands here, proved, as a tool interpretation theory can
spend again.
Source: Paper 020, section 3.4 and Appendix B. NEW in
Paper 020.
# Piecewise bi-interpretability — sameness of theories, priced in the bicategory

Two theories are piecewise bi-interpretable when they stand
equivalent in the bicategory of theories (1.2.255):
piecewise interpretations each way whose composites are
ii-isomorphic (1.2.252) to the identity interpretations. It
is the paper's answer to the question its first section
poses — when are two theories the same theory, for the
purposes of interpretation? — and its price can be read
semantically: piecewise bi-interpretable theories have
equivalent categories of models (1.3.245). The converse
fails, and the paper shows where the seam runs.
Bi-interpretable theories also have isomorphic sentential
algebras (1.3.255), the algebra of a theory's sentences
under its own consequence; and a counterexample of Barrett
and Halvorson, made for a near notion, parts the two
prices exactly there — two theories whose sentential
algebras differ, one having atoms where the other has none,
while no equivalence of model categories parts them from the
side of bi-interpretability. The smallest case shows the
edge from the other direction: the theories E_n and E_m
of exactly n and exactly m things, for n and m different,
interpret each other piecewise in both directions (1.2.249)
and are not piecewise bi-interpretable — the groups of
symmetries of their models differ, so their categories of
models are not equivalent. Mutual interpretability is not sameness;
bi-interpretability is the sameness the structure itself
prices, and it parts pairs the sameness of model categories
cannot part.
Source: Paper 020, section 3.4. NEW in Paper 020.
# The sortal replacement — each sort traded for a sortal predicate

Quine's construction, in the form Barrett and Halvorson
rebuilt it. Start from a language with a finite number of
sorts. Its replacement is a single-sorted language: one sort
only, one sortal predicate (1.3.249) of one variable for
each old sort — a predicate saying of a thing that it is to
be read as a thing of that sort — and one relation symbol
for each of the old. The unification of a theory T is the
single-sorted theory whose axioms come in four parts: first,
every axiom of T carried over by the one-piece reading that
sends each sort to its sortal predicate and identity to
identity, with every claim confined to the sortal predicate;
second, everything falls under some sortal predicate and
each sortal predicate holds of something; third, no two
sortal predicate holds of the same thing; and fourth, each
new relation holds only of rows standing in the right sortal
predicate. Barrett and Halvorson showed T and its
unification are Morita equivalent, in the older vocabulary
of sameness.

The construction carries a known defect, found by Hook in
1985: unification need not preserve interpretability. The
paper's example is again the smallest: a theory of two
sorts, one thing in each, is interpretable in a theory of
one sort with one thing; but the unification of the first is
not interpretable in the ordinary way in the unification of
the second, since whatever is interpretable in the ordinary
way in a
one-thing structure has at most one thing. Unification is
not a functor on ordinary interpretations, and ordinary
interpretability is not preserved by it. The defect is a
fact about the ordinary notion, and it is the piecewise
notion that repairs it: the repair is the biequivalence of
1.2.259, for which the piecewise reading back from a
unification to its theory — pieces for the one sort, one per
old sort — is the load-bearing direction.
Source: Paper 020, section 4. NEW in Paper 020.
# The biequivalence — sorts done without, proved as an equivalence of bicategories

The paper's closing theorem: the bicategory of single-sorted
theories and the bicategory of theories with a finite number
of sorts are biequivalent (1.3.250). One direction is the
inclusion: a single-sorted theory stands among the theories
of more sorts as it is. The other sends a theory to its
unification (1.2.258), and an interpretation f between two
theories to the composite that runs from the unification of
the first, back piecewise to the first, along f, and forward
again into the unification of the second. The test for a
biequivalence is Gurski's: local equivalence of the ways,
and every object of the larger bicategory equivalent to one
in the image of the smaller. The second clause is one
proposition, the heart of the application: every theory with
a finite number of sorts is piecewise bi-interpretable
(1.2.257) with its own unification. The two readings are the
ordinary interpretation that reads each sort as its sortal
predicate, and the piecewise interpretation back whose one
sort is split into as many pieces as there were sorts, its
domain the claim that holds of everything, its identity and
its relations read by cases — holding on the matching piece,
failing off it — and the composites are joined to the
identities by ii-isomorphisms checked on the semantic side,
by the recognition test of 1.2.253. The paper notes the
axiom of choice is spent in the general proof and is not
needed for this one.

Two consequences are drawn in the paper's own words. First,
unification acts as a pseudofunctor where the ordinary
notion failed: one theory is piecewise interpretable in
another if and only if the unification of each is piecewise
interpretable in the unification of the other — Hook's
defect (1.2.258) is repaired, and the repair is exactly the
widening from ordinary to piecewise interpretations. Second,
that sorts can be done without: from the point of view of
the theory of interpretations, a theory with a finite number
of sorts can always be traded for a theory of one sort, at
the price of a sortal predicate for each sort — a layer of
presentation
proved removable, for the theory of interpretations, by an
equivalence of the structures that house them. The paper is
careful about the boundary of the claim: the sorts in play
are a finite number, and the equivalence is of bicategories
of theories, not a verdict on every use a sort can be put
to.
Source: Paper 020, section 4. NEW in Paper 020.
# Foundations — the level-zero layer (charter rule 9)
Built 2026-11-06 at Will's direction: everything in research.phext is
written so a reader with NO prior specialized knowledge can follow it.
This section is where the base definitions live, sucked in once,
defined in plain words. Rules of the layer:
- A foundation scroll defines ONE term (or one tight family).
- It may use only everyday words, or terms defined in foundation
  scrolls it points to under 'Builds on'. The chains bottom out at
  ordinary language: number, shape, space, same/different, more/less,
  rule, choice, cost. Those are assumed; nothing else is.
- Paper chapters and concept scrolls do NOT contain these definitions.
  Section 3 of each chapter (its standoff references) lists the
  foundation coordinates its claims need, in reading order. Follow
  the coordinates like hyperlinks.
General primitives: 1.3.2-1.3.8. Mathematics: 1.3.9-1.3.27.
Economics: 1.3.28-1.3.32. Computing & agents: 1.3.33-1.3.36.
Language & measurement: 1.3.37-1.3.39. Machine learning & hardware:
1.3.40-1.3.45. DEFINED WORDS (rule 10): from 1.3.46 on, one scroll per
word this phext coins — each scroll written in Singular words alone,
so the word it defines is the only word in it that is not already
Will's. All other scrolls may use a coined word once its scroll
stands here. Added with Study S001 (2026-11-06): foundations
1.3.73-1.3.78 — types and terms; context and judgment; computation
steps and still forms; paradox; universes; tactics and goals. The
Defined Words register grows by use: words coined after the first
register stand at 1.3.79 on (recursor, impredicative). Added with Studies
S002-S003 (2026-11-06): foundations 1.3.81-1.3.83 — ownership and
borrowing; symbolic execution; a tenant — and the coined word
tenant at 1.3.84. Added with Paper 006
(2026-11-07): foundations 1.3.87-1.3.91 — specification and
test; candidate class and goal set; prior and conditioning;
contract; interface and observability. Added with Paper 007
(2026-11-07): foundations 1.3.92-1.3.99 — attention and the
key-value cache; growth rates; the top-p set; tree; a summary
that stands for a group; the tangent; distillation; roofline —
and the coined words sublinear at 1.3.100 and subquadratic at
1.3.101.

Added with Paper 013 (2026-11-11): foundations
1.3.156-1.3.161 — cross-validation; a split, a fold,
and a partition; influence; joint effects, split by
the sets that carry them; pair scores; the bell
limit.
Added with Paper 014 (2026-11-11): foundations
1.3.162-1.3.166 — a proof state and a tactic;
Monte Carlo search; an olympiad; a deductive
database; sameness — and the defined words
1.3.167-1.3.172: autoformalization,
counterexample, derangement, subgraph,
contamination, divisor.
Added with Paper 018 (2026-11-13): foundations
1.3.222-1.3.226 — spread that changes from place to
place; a heavy tail; the sign of a number; one peak;
cutting power — and the defined words 1.3.227-1.3.229:
heteroscedastic, unimodal, extremal.
Added with Paper 019 (2026-11-14): foundations
1.3.230-1.3.240 — complex numbers; the matrix and
the Hermitian table; the determinant; the
polynomial, homogeneous; the field and the finite
field; the number field; projective space, the
hyperplane and duality; the variety; interpolation;
the irreducible factor and the completely split
prime; remainder-joining and fractions recovered —
and the defined words 1.3.241-1.3.243: Hermitian,
hypersurface, determinantal.
Added with Paper 020 (2026-11-14): foundations
1.3.244-1.3.247 — sorts and signatures; theory and model;
natural transformation; the bicategory — and the defined
words 1.3.248-1.3.256: piecewise, sortal, biequivalence,
isomorphism, pseudofunctor, associator with unitor and
compositor, interpretable, sentential, reconstrual.
# Foundation: function, variable, input, output
A FUNCTION is a rule that takes an INPUT and produces an OUTPUT:
given the same input, always the same output. A VARIABLE is a named
slot that can hold different values in different situations (x can be
3 today and 7 tomorrow; the rule using x does not change). A PARAMETER
is a variable whose value is chosen once to set up a situation, then
held fixed while other things vary. Nearly every claim in this phext
is, underneath, a claim about some function: what goes in, what comes
out, and how the output changes when the input does.
# Foundation: set and element
A SET is a collection of things, with no order and no repeats: the
set of the three colors on a flag. An ELEMENT is one thing in the set.
Much of mathematics is: pick a set, put structure on it (ways to
combine, compare or measure its elements), then ask what must be true
of everything with that structure. When a definition here says 'a set
with...', it means exactly that recipe.
Builds on: (everyday language only)
# Foundation: 'the same' — equal, isomorphic, homeomorphic, diffeomorphic
'Being the same' in mathematics is always being the same UP TO some allowed way of
reshaping, and arguments are often about which level is meant:
- EQUAL: literally the same object.
- ISOMORPHIC: the same structure — elements can be renamed so every
  rule and relationship matches exactly. (level of the Algebra.)
- HOMEOMORPHIC: the same shape if you may stretch, bend and squash,
  but not tear or glue. A coffee cup and a donut are homeomorphic
  (one hole each). (level of the Topology.)
- DIFFEOMORPHIC: homeomorphic, and moreover smoothly — no corners or
  kinks created or destroyed. The finest of these levels; two spaces
  can be homeomorphic but not diffeomorphic, and 4-dimensional space
  is where that gap gets strange (Paper 005 lives near this gap).
Builds on: set (1.3.3), shape (everyday).
# Foundation: invariant
An INVARIANT is a measurement you can make of an object that does not
change when the object is reshaped in the allowed ways (see 1.3.4).
Invariants are how being the same is tested in practice: if two objects
have different values of an invariant, they are NOT the same at that
level. The trap — it is Paper 005's whole subject — is that equal
invariants do NOT prove being the same: a weak invariant can agree while a
more fine one disagrees. Choosing an invariant is choosing how closely you
are willing to look.
Builds on: 'the same' (1.3.4).
# Foundation: integers, fractions, finite, infinite
INTEGERS (written Z) are the whole numbers: ..., -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ...
Fractions (written Q) are fractions of integers: 3/4, -7/2, 5 (= 5/1).
Every integer is a rational; most fractions are not integers.
Measuring with Z keeps the fine print (exact counts, leftovers,
what is left over); measuring with Q makes smooth it away, because division is
always possible. A set or group is FINITE if it has a definite count
of elements, INFINITE otherwise. Paper 005's punchline, in these
terms: two manifolds can agree completely under Q-measurement and
differ under Z-measurement — the difference lives in finite leftover
structure (torsion, 1.3.13) that fractions erase.
Builds on: set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: vector and distance
A VECTOR is a list of numbers of fixed length: (3, 1, 4) is a vector
with three entries, called its COORDINATES or dimensions. Vectors let
a complicated thing be pinned down by several measurements at once —
a person's (height, weight, age) is a vector. Once things are vectors,
they live in a SPACE where DISTANCE makes sense: two vectors are
close if their entries are close. A huge amount of this phext works
by turning things (agents, stories, program parts) into vectors and
then asking about distances, clusters and averages of vectors.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: average, spread, percentile, distribution
Given many measurements: the AVERAGE (mean) is their sum divided by
their count. The SPREAD (dispersion, variance) is how far the values
typically sit from the average — same average, very different spreads
is a common and crucial situation. A PERCENTILE places one value
among the rest: the 71st percentile sits above about 71% of the
values. The DISTRIBUTION is the whole picture — which values occur,
how often. Claims like 'group A is more spread out than group B' are
claims about distributions, not about any individual member.
Builds on: (everyday counting).
# Foundation: dimension and manifold
The DIMENSION of a space is how many coordinates you need to say
where a point is, locally: a line is 1, a sheet of paper is 2, the
room you are in is 3. A MANIFOLD is a space that LOOKS like ordinary
flat space of some dimension if you zoom in far enough, even if its
global shape is curved or closed — the surface of the Earth is a
2-dimensional manifold: every small patch looks like a flat map,
though the whole is a sphere. Paper 005 is about 4-dimensional
manifolds: spaces that locally need 4 coordinates. Nobody can picture
one directly; the field works by cutting them into pieces that CAN be
handled (1.3.20) and keeping careful algebraic accounts (1.3.13).
Builds on: vector/coordinates (1.3.7), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: sphere, surface, genus, torus
A SPHERE S^2 is the surface of an ordinary ball: all points at one
fixed distance from a center. (S^1 is a circle, S^0 is two points.)
A SURFACE is a 2-dimensional manifold (1.3.9). Its GENUS is its
number of holes/handles: a sphere has genus 0, a donut (a TORUS) has
genus 1, a three handled shape has genus 3. Sigma_g means 'the surface of genus
g'. Genus is the oldest invariant in the subject: two closed surfaces
with different genera are never the same shape, and genus can be
counted from any picture of the surface.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9), invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: group
A GROUP is a set equipped with one operation that combines two
elements into a third, satisfying three plain requirements: combining
is order-stable (brackets don't matter), there is a do-nothing element
(identity: 'add 0'), and every element has an undoing (inverse: 'add
the negative'). Examples: the integers under addition; the rotations
of a square under 'do one then the other'; the ways to shuffle a deck.
Groups are how mathematics packages 'symmetry' and 'movement'. A
FINITE group has with a finite count many elements; its ORDER is how many. 'Z/n'
is the group of clock arithmetic with n hours: counting wraps around,
so n steps bring you back to the start — that wrapping around is the seed
of torsion (1.3.13).
Builds on: set (1.3.3), integers (1.3.6).
# Foundation: loops, fundamental group, universal cover, contractible
Stand on a space and walk a closed LOOP: start somewhere, wander,
return to the start. Two loops count as 'the same' if one can be slid
continuously into the other without leaving the space. Loops can be
combined (walk one, then the other) and undone (walk it backwards) —
so the loops of a space form a group, its FUNDAMENTAL GROUP, written
pi_1. pi_1 measures the hole of the space-structure as seen by paths: flat
land has trivial pi_1 (every loop slides to a point); the pi_1 of a donut
is Z x Z (loops around the two hole directions). A space is
CONTRACTIBLE if it can be continuously shrunk to a single point —
contractible spaces have trivial pi_1 and trivial homology (1.3.13).
Every space has a UNIVERSAL COVER: a contractible-ish space laid over
it like an opened out map, whose own pi_1 is trivial; the original
space's pi_1 is then visible as the symmetries of the unrolling.
Builds on: group (1.3.11), manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: homology — holes, counted by algebra; torsion
HOMOLOGY is a machine that gives to each space a list of groups
(H_0, H_1, H_2, ...), designed so that H_k detects 'holes of
dimension k': H_0 counts separate pieces; H_1 counts independent
tunnels (loops that can't be filled in); H_2 counts enclosed voids.
On a donut: H_1 = Z^2 (two tunnel directions, each usable any whole
number of times). The small raised mark-style part Z^n ('free' part) counts
unbounded directions. But an H_k can also contain a FINITE piece, like
Z/3: a cycle that is not a boundary, yet THREE copies of it are. That
finite leftover is called TORSION. Torsion is invisible if you only
measure with fractions (over Q it vanishes, since 3 becomes
that can be undone) — which is why integral (Z) homology knows strictly more
than rational (Q) homology. Paper 005's manifolds are told apart by
torsion alone.
Builds on: group (1.3.11), integers vs fractions (1.3.6), loops (1.3.12).
# Foundation: cohomology and ring
COHOMOLOGY is record of the homology keeping twin: instead of cycles in the
space, it tracks measurement-functions on the space (ways to assign
numbers to cycles consistently). For most counting purposes it holds
the same information as homology. Its extra feature is a
MULTIPLICATION: two measurements can be combined into a third, making
the cohomology groups into a RING (a system where you can add and
multiply, like ordinary numbers). 'The cohomology ring of S^2 x S^2'
therefore names a specific little multiplication table — and Paper
005's manifolds reproduce that table exactly under rational
measurement while differing in their integral homology. Same ring,
different torsion: that is the whole trick of a fake quadric.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), group (1.3.11).
# Foundation: Euler characteristic and signature (two summary numbers)
The EULER CHARACTERISTIC, written chi, is a single integer summarizing
the shape of a space, computable from any decomposition into pieces:
chi = (pieces in even positions) - (pieces in odd positions) — for a
surface, chi = 2 - 2 x genus (sphere 2, torus 0, genus-2 surface -2).
It is additive: the chi of a glued space is the sum of the chi values of the
parts. The SIGNATURE, written sigma, applies to 4-dimensional
manifolds: it counts, roughly, the balance between 'positive' and
'negative' directions in how surfaces inside the manifold cross
each other (the intersection form); sigma is
also additive under careful gluing (Novikov the adding rule). chi = 4 and
sigma = 0 are the values of S^2 x S^2 — Paper 005's sums match both,
as they must to fake the quadric.
Builds on: genus (1.3.10), invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: maps between spaces — continuous, embedding, diffeomorphism, orientation
A MAP from one space to another gives each point of the first a
point of the second. A map is CONTINUOUS if nearby points land nearby
(no teleporting, no tearing). An EMBEDDING is a continuous map that
places the first space inside the second without self-intersection —
a knot sitting in ordinary space is an embedded circle. A
DIFFEOMORPHISM is a smooth two-way map (it and its reverse create no
corners or kinks): the notion of exact being the same for smooth manifolds
(1.3.4). An ORIENTATION is a consistent choice of 'which hand it favors'
(clockwise vs counter-clockwise) across a space; gluing two pieces
together requires their orientations to DISAGREE at the seam
(orientation-reversing), the way two halves of a zipper face opposite
ways to close.
Builds on: 'the same' (1.3.4), manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: neighborhood, tubular neighborhood, normal direction
A NEIGHBORHOOD of something inside a space is a small region around
it, included in the space. If a surface sits inside a 4-manifold, a
TUBULAR neighborhood is the region of all points very close to the
surface — it has the shape of the surface made thick slightly in the
directions sticking OUT of it. Those sticking-out directions are the
NORMAL directions (at right angles to the surface, in the only sense
available): at each point of a surface inside a 4-manifold there is a
2-dimensional disk of normal directions, which is why the thick layer
is 'surface x disk' and its boundary is 'surface x circle'. Removing
a tubular neighborhood is the standard way to make a clean hole with a
well-defined boundary, ready for gluing (1.3.23).
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), maps (1.3.16), sphere (1.3.10).
# Foundation: bundle, fiber, section
A BUNDLE is a space built by placing a copy of one space (the FIBER)
over every point of another (the BASE), possibly twisting as you move
around the base. A brush: base = the handle, fiber = each hair of the brush.
Formally the total space maps onto the base, and the points over one
base point are one fiber. A SECTION is a choice of exactly one point
in every fiber, made continuously — a rule for picking a hair of the brush over
each point of the handle. Paper 005 uses this twice: the complement
of a multisection is a bundle over a surface whose fiber is a sphere
with holes; and its torsion class is the DIFFERENCE of two sections
(the two 'pole' sections, over the north of the sphere and south poles).
Builds on: maps (1.3.16), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: covering space and sheets
A COVERING space of a space X is a space laid over X so that every
small patch of X has several exact copies above it (the SHEETS), like
a many floored car park over one floor plan. The number of sheets is
the DEGREE of the cover. A cover is With no merging of sheets if every point of X has
exactly the full number of sheets above it (no merging floors). Going
around a loop in X can cycle the sheets — walk downstairs, and you
may come back on a different floor. That permutation behavior is what
'cyclic' and 'braided' mean in Paper 005: its surface is a p-with sheets
cover of the base surface whose sheets are moved in a cycle in a cycle around
certain loops, braiding the sheets into ONE connected surface instead
of p separate copies.
Builds on: maps (1.3.16), loops (1.3.12).
# Foundation: handle and handlebody decomposition
A HANDLE is a standard building block: take a ball and attach a thick
strap (in dimension terms, a lower-dimensional ball glued along part
of its boundary). A HANDLEBODY DECOMPOSITION describes a manifold as:
start with balls (0-handles), attach straps (1-handles), attach
thicker patches (2-handles), and so on, with a diagram recording each
attachment and a number (the FRAMING) recording how each strap is
twisted as it is glued. The counts (h_0, h_1, h_2, h_3) are the ingredient of the recipe list. Why it matters: a decomposition turns an
abstract space into something computable — homology (1.3.13) can be
read off the recipe by arithmetic (1.3.21), and two pieces can be
glued when their recipes share boundary data.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: chain complex — the arithmetic under homology
A CHAIN COMPLEX is the record keeping that makes homology computable.
From a handle decomposition (1.3.20), write one group per dimension:
C_3 -> C_2 -> C_1 -> C_0, where each C_k lists the k-handles, and
each arrow (the BOUNDARY OPERATOR) records how handles of one level
attach to handles of the next, with integer coefficients. The golden
rule is 'the boundary of a boundary is zero' (arrows compose to 0).
Homology is then pure algebra: H_k = (things the arrow out of C_k
kills) / (things hit by the arrow into C_k) — cycles modulo
boundaries. Torsion (1.3.13) appears when a cycle is hit only after
multiplication: 3 copies of it bound, but it doesn't. Given a
handlebody diagram, a patient person (or a computer) can compute all
of this by integer arithmetic alone.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), group (1.3.11), handlebody (1.3.20).
# Foundation: symplectic — geometry with an area rule; the symplectic sum
A SYMPLECTIC structure on a manifold is an added piece of geometry:
at every point, a consistent rule for measuring the 'area' of tiny
tiny flat shapes in pairs of directions — the mathematical form of the
position/momentum pairing in physics. Spaces carrying it are called
symplectic manifolds; they are always even-dimensional, and the rule
makes area-like quantities behave with no give (you cannot squash a
symplectic ball through a small hole, however you bend it). Most
4-manifolds in Paper 005 are symplectic. The SYMPLECTIC SUM is a
cut-and-paste construction: two symplectic manifolds containing the
same surface (with opposite normal directions, 1.3.17) have the neighborhoods of the surface removed and the resulting boundaries glued —
the splicing is done so the area rules still match. It is the main
known factory for exotic symplectic 4-manifolds.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), neighborhood (1.3.17), boundary (1.3.23).
# Foundation: boundary of a manifold; gluing along a boundary
Some manifolds have an EDGE: points whose neighborhoods look like
half-space instead of full space (the edge of a disk is its rim circle; the edge of a ball is its spherical surface). The edge itself is a manifold
of one dimension less, the BOUNDARY, written dM. Removing something's
tubular neighborhood (1.3.17) creates boundary where none was. Two
manifolds with the same boundary (up to being the same, 1.3.4) can be
GLUED: identify the boundaries by a chosen map, and the result is a
new closed manifold. The choice of gluing map is real data — different
choices can yield different manifolds from identical pieces, which is
why Paper 005 specifies its gluing maps explicitly instead of just
their effect on homology.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), neighborhood (1.3.17), maps (1.3.16).
# Foundation: aspherical; exceptional spheres and being minimal
A manifold is ASPHERICAL if its universal cover (1.3.12) is
contractible — which is the same as saying, it has no higher-homotopy 'bubbles': any
map of a sphere into it can be shrunk to nothing. Its whole topology
is then carried by pi_1. An EXCEPTIONAL SPHERE is an embedded sphere
whose self-intersection number is -1 (roughly: it links itself
negatively once); such a sphere can always be 'blown down' (removed
by a standard surgery), so a symplectic manifold containing none is
called MINIMAL — it is not a blown-up version of something smaller.
Paper 005's one-line consequence: in an aspherical manifold every
embedded sphere has self-intersection 0, never -1, so aspherical
implies minimal.
Builds on: universal cover (1.3.12), embedding (1.3.16), symplectic (1.3.22).
# Foundation: gluing groups — amalgamated product and trees of spaces
When two spaces are glued along a shared part, their fundamental
groups combine in a forced way: the AMALGAMATED PRODUCT takes both
groups and identifies the two copies of the shared group of the part
inside them (like merging two organizations while holding that the
shared department is one department, not two). The combined group
acts on a TREE (the Bass-Serre tree) whose vertices are copies of the
pieces and whose edges are copies of the shared part — a floor plan
of how the glued space's universal cover is assembled from
contractible blocks. Normal forms in such groups say every element
has an essentially unique spelling as an alternating product from the
two sides, which is what lets proofs track elements across the seam.
Builds on: fundamental group (1.3.12), group (1.3.11).
# Foundation: two computing tools — Mayer-Vietoris and Poincare duality
MAYER-VIETORIS is the standard divide-and-conquer for homology: if a
space is two pieces overlapping in a known part, there is a
prescription (an exact sequence) that computes the homology of the
whole from the homology groups of the pieces and the overlap — the
topological version of inclusion-exclusion in counting.
POINCARE DUALITY is a symmetry of closed manifolds: in dimension n,
the k-th homology and the (n-k)-th cohomology determine each other.
Consequence used in Paper 005: if H_1 is finite, duality forces H_3
of a closed 4-manifold to vanish, because H_3 is tied to cohomology in
degree 1, and finite groups contribute nothing there.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), cohomology (1.3.14).
# Foundation: contact manifold and Stein filling (brief)
A CONTACT structure on an odd-dimensional manifold is a field of
planes with maximum twisting — the geometry of wave waves and of
thermodynamics' special curves; a 3-manifold with one is a contact
3-manifold. A STEIN FILLING of it is a particularly well-behaved
4-dimensional manifold whose boundary is that contact manifold — a
filling the way a solid fills a shell. Paper 005 mentions them
because its complements, with one more piece removed, become Stein
filling spaces — including infinitely many that share ALL ordinary homology
yet are two by two different manifolds, told apart only by looking at
homology of their finite covers (1.3.19 run in reverse).
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), boundary (1.3.23), homology (1.3.13).
# Foundation: firm, worker, wage, outside option
A FIRM is an organization that hires inputs (labor, materials,
compute) and sells outputs. A WAGE is the price of the labor of a worker
per unit time. the OUTSIDE of A worker OPTION is the best they could get
if they leave — the wage they could earn elsewhere, or the value of
not working. No firm can hold a worker for less than their outside
option, and a worker whose skills are scarce gets paid above it —
the excess being a rent (1.3.31). Paper 001 treats AI systems rented
by the instance as a kind of worker whose wage is its rental cost.
Builds on: (everyday language; function (1.3.2) for prices as signals)
# Foundation: equilibrium
An EQUILIBRIUM is a situation in which every participant, given what
everyone else is doing, is doing the best they can — so nobody has
reason to change on its own side alone, and the situation rests. A market
equilibrium adds: at the resting prices, the amounts people want to
buy equal the amounts people want to sell (the market CLEARS).
Equilibrium is a consistency condition, not a claim that the outcome
is good, fair, or efficient — though theorems sometimes connect them.
Paper 001's results are equilibrium statements: 'in equilibrium, the
wages must be...' means 'any other wage pattern would give someone a
profitable move'.
Builds on: firm/wage (1.3.28).
# Foundation: mixing and the convex hull
Take points in a space. A MIX (convex combination) is a weighted
average of points: weights are positive and sum to 1, like a recipe
in proportions. The set of ALL mixes of a collection of points is
their CONVEX HULL — the smallest 'straight-edged' region containing
them all; picture a rubber band snapped around pegs on a board. If
the pegs are people's skill profiles (1.3.7), the hull is every
average profile a team drawn from those people can have, because the average of a team is exactly a mix of its members. Whether the best
mix sits strictly inside the hull or at a corner is the whole
question in Paper 001's mosaic argument.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: scarcity, rent, and the cost of a copy
A resource is SCARCE if there is not enough of it for everyone who
wants it at a zero price. RENT is the payment a scarce resource earns
above what is needed to keep it supplied: a uniquely talented singer
earns rent; an ordinary microphone doesn't, because more can be made.
The dividing line is COPY COST: if a thing can be copied at cost r,
no copy can command more than about r for long, because a competitor
can mint another. If it cannot be copied at any price — a particular
trained of the human brain — its earnings are set by bargaining over
scarcity. Paper 001's first great divide: human expertise is
that can not be copied (fixed supply, rents possible); AI expertise is that can be copied
at compute cost (rents capped by the copy cost).
Builds on: firm/wage (1.3.28).
# Foundation: threshold and phase transition
A THRESHOLD is a value of some quantity at which the behavior of a system
changes in kind, not just in degree. A PHASE TRANSITION is the
physical archetype: water heated degree by degree does nothing
qualitative until 100°C, then boils — the same input, a different
regime. Many claims in this phext are threshold claims: below the
threshold, step by step change; above it, a different structure. A
threshold claim is stronger (and more falsifiable) than 'more X leads
to more Y': it says the interesting action is at one point of the
scale, and it invites the question 'which side are we on, measured
how?'
Builds on: function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: program, runtime, state
A PROGRAM is text that specifies computation. A RUNTIME is the
machinery that carries a program out: loading it, giving it memory
and tools, stepping it forward. STATE is everything about a running
system that can change as it runs — the contents of its memory at a
moment. Two systems running the same program with different state
behave differently; two systems in the same state with different
programs do too. Paper 002's entire subject is the layer of program
that surrounds the fixed of a model core and shapes what its state becomes.
Builds on: function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: policy and mechanism
A POLICY is a rule for choosing what to do in each situation — the
strategy an agent follows. A MECHANISM is the fixed scaffolding that
makes policies run and constrains them: who may act, what gets
recorded, what is checked. The distinction that matters here: a policy
can be wise or foolish, but a mechanism is what remains true even when
a policy is foolish or hostile. Institutions, operating systems and
agent runtimes all make this split somewhere; Paper 002's
contribution is drawing the line sharply and making only one side
self-editable.
Builds on: program/runtime (1.3.33).
# Foundation: sandbox, version, rollback, append-only
A SANDBOX is a inside a fence area where a program can act without touching
the real system outside the fence — experiments inside can't break
things outside. A VERSION is a saved snapshot of some state or text,
kept under a name or number. ROLLBACK is returning to a saved version
after a change goes wrong; it is only trustworthy if the snapshot was
kept outside the reach of the thing being rolled back. APPEND-ONLY
means records can be added but never edited or deleted — a ledger in
ink. Append-only histories are how systems prove what happened, and
the foundation of Paper 002's safety story.
Builds on: state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: token, context, cache (how a language model reads)
A TOKEN is the chunk of text a language model reads or writes at one
step — roughly a short word or word-piece. The CONTEXT is the tokens
the model can currently see: its entire working memory, limited to a
fixed number of tokens (the context window). Work the model did
earlier falls out of view unless something writes it down and brings
it back — that 'something' is memory machinery around the model, and
choosing what to keep in view is a policy (1.3.34). A CACHE is stored
earlier computation reused instead of computed again; for models, a high
share of cached input tokens means most of the context was already
processed, so the step is cheap. Costs in agent systems are roughly
proportional to not cached tokens processed.
Builds on: program/state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: corpus, prompt, feature
A CORPUS is a collection of texts (or other items) assembled for
study. A PROMPT is the instruction or opening given to a writer or a
model, to which it responds with a complete piece of work. A FEATURE
is one measured property of an item, stated so different items can be
compared: 'number of side plots', 'does the narrator address the
reader'. Turning an item into a list of features turns it into a
vector (1.3.7) — after which distances, clusters and rarity (1.3.39)
become arithmetic. What a study can see is bounded by its features:
unmeasured properties are invisible to it.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: classifier and its report card (accuracy, F1)
A CLASSIFIER is a machine (or rule) that sorts items into labeled
bins: given the features of an item, it guesses which source or class the
item came from. Its REPORT CARD compares guesses to truth. Accuracy
is the share of all guesses that are right. F1 is a more strict grade
balancing two failure modes per class: missing real members
(recall) and wrongly claiming members (precision); MACRO-F1 averages
the per-class F1 grades so small classes count as much as big ones.
A classifier far above the guess-at-random rate proves the features
contain real information about the classes — it does not, by itself,
prove anything about why.
Builds on: feature (1.3.37), average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: nearest neighbors, distance, rarity
Once items are vectors (1.3.7), each item has NEIGHBORS: the items
closest to it in the space. The k NEAREST NEIGHBORS of an item are
the k closest such items, and the average distance to them measures
how crowded the neighborhood of the item is. RARITY, as Paper 003 uses it,
is exactly that: an item is rare if its nearest neighbors are far
away — its combination of measured properties is uncommon in the
collection. Rarity is always relative to the collection and the
features: add items or change features and the same rarity of the item
changes.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), average (1.3.8), feature (1.3.37).
# Foundation: neural network, weights, training
A NEURAL NETWORK is a function (1.3.2) built from many simple units
whose combined behavior is set by a large list of adjustable numbers,
its WEIGHTS (or parameters). TRAINING is the process of adjusting the
weights — by automated trial, error measurement and correction — so
the outputs of the network match desired outputs on training examples.
Training is expensive and slow; using the trained network (inference)
is comparatively cheap. The weights are the entire of the network learned
knowledge, stored as numbers; two networks with the same design but
different weights are different machines. Several papers here hold
weights fixed and vary everything AROUND them — the interesting
action is deliberately elsewhere.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), vector (1.3.7).
# Foundation: graph — nodes, edges, and program graphs
A GRAPH is a set of NODES (things) connected by EDGES (relationships
between pairs of things): cities and roads, people and friendships,
instructions and dependencies. Degree, paths and neighborhoods are
defined as you'd expect. A GRAPH NEURAL NETWORK (graph network) is a neural
network (1.3.40) whose computation follows the edges of a graph, letting
the representation of each node absorb information from its neighbors. For
programs, the standard graph is the control/data-flow graph: nodes
are instructions and values, edges record 'executes next' and 'feeds
into'. Paper 004 predicts hardware behavior from program graphs.
Builds on: set (1.3.3), neural network (1.3.40).
# Foundation: generalization and domain shift
A model GENERALIZES if it performs well on items it was not trained
on. A DOMAIN is a family of similar items (the problems of one benchmark;
one program; the scans of one hospital). DOMAIN SHIFT is what happens when
the test items come from a different domain than the training items:
the surface changes while the underlying task doesn't, and models
often fail badly. Domain generalization is the discipline of building
models whose performance survives the shift. The core dispute in
Paper 004: whether to survive shift by ERASING domain differences
from the view of the model, or by KEEPING the differences and routing each
new item to specialists that recognize its parts.
Builds on: classifier (1.3.38), neural network (1.3.40).
# Foundation: surrogate model
A SURROGATE is a cheap, fast stand-in for an expensive, slow
measurement: predict the result instead of running the real thing.
Wind-tunnel models are stand ins for flight; a spreadsheet budget is
a surrogate for a year of business. A surrogate is useful when its
errors are small compared to the cost it saves, and when the final
decision still checks the few best candidates the expensive way.
Its quality is judged twice: how close its predictions are (offline),
and how good the decisions made from them turn out (online). Paper
004's stand ins predict chip performance in milliseconds where the
real measurement takes hours.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), classifier report cards (1.3.38).
# Foundation: FPGA, kernel, pragma, latency (the high level synthesis stage, plainly)
An FPGA is a chip whose circuits can be rewired after manufacture —
hardware you can reprogram. HIGH-LEVEL SYNTHESIS (high level synthesis) is a tool that
compiles an ordinary program into such circuits. In this vocabulary a
KERNEL is the program (in a confusing way, the same word means other things
elsewhere; here it is a piece of C++ to be turned into circuitry). A
PRAGMA is an instruction written into the program telling the compiler
how to build the circuit: run these steps in parallel, pipeline
those, tile this loop. The same kernel with different pragma instructions becomes
circuits of wildly different speed and size. LATENCY is how long the
circuit takes (in cycles); resource use is how much chip it occupies.
Finding good pragma instructions by trial requires running high level synthesis each time — hours
per trial — which is why Paper 004 predicts instead (1.3.43).
Builds on: program (1.3.33), surrogate (1.3.43).
# Foundation: regularization and fine-tuning (two training habits)
REGULARIZATION is any deliberate pressure added during training to
keep a model from a degenerate solution: a penalty for being one sided,
for complexity, for being too sure — chosen to protect a property the
raw score doesn't reward. Paper 004 adds a balance pressure for BALANCE: experts
that are never used can't specialize. FINE-TUNING is training an
already-trained model a little more, on a small amount of new data,
to adapt it to a new domain — cheap compared to training from
scratch, and the standard first move under domain shift (1.3.42).
Both are admissions that the headline objective alone doesn't capture
what's wanted.
Builds on: training (1.3.40), domain shift (1.3.42).
# Defined Words — the register (charter rule 10)
Words this phext needs that are not in Will's Singular list live here,
one per scroll, each defined in a scroll written in Singular words
alone (the word being defined, and the forms named in its first line,
are the only words in the scroll that are not already Will's). A word
may be used in the rest of the phext because its scroll stands here.
Names need no scroll: they are addresses, not speech.

The drawing of Study S009 adds no coined word: the
one scroll it lays down in this layer — natural
deduction (1.3.211) — is a plain foundation, not a
coined word, and the names it needed stand as
addresses. In the living registry the scroll stands
entered like any other: True, on the ground of
stipulation, a page of the record itself, set down
under the rules of the tracker.
# Defined word: homology
Homology is the algebra that counts the holes of a space. It gives a space a list of groups. The first group counts the separate pieces. The next counts the tunnels: loops that can not be filled in. The next counts the closed empty rooms inside. A part of the answer can be finite: a loop that is not the edge of anything, though three copies of it are. That finite part is the part that measuring with fractions can not see.
# Defined word: cohomology
Cohomology is the twin record keeping of the hole counting algebra. In place of cycles in the space, it keeps the ways to give numbers to cycles so the numbers agree. It holds the same news as the hole counting algebra for most counting. It adds one thing: two such ways can be multiplied to make a third, so the groups form a ring, a system where you can add and multiply.
# Defined word: symplectic (also: symplectically)
A space is symplectic when it carries an added rule: at each point, a way to measure the area of tiny flat shapes in pairs of directions, the same pairing of place and push that physics uses. The rule is strict: you can bend the space, but the rule makes it impossible to press a ball of the space through a hole smaller than the rule allows. A symplectic sum joins two such spaces along a shared surface so the rules still match.
# Defined word: aspherical
A space is aspherical when every map of a sphere into it can be shrunk to nothing. The covering space that opens all its loops out flat is then a space that shrinks to a point. All of its shape news is carried by its loops alone.
# Defined word: genus
The genus of a surface is its number of holes, counted as handles. The surface of a ball has genus zero. A donut has genus one. A shape with three handles has genus three. Two closed surfaces with unlike genus are never the same shape.
# Defined word: quadric (also: quadrics)
A quadric, in this phext, is the product of two spheres: each point is a pair, one point from the first sphere and one from the second. It is the standard four dimensional space that the manifolds of Paper 005 copy in every test that uses fractions, and fail to be in fact.
# Defined word: multisection (also: multisections)
A multisection is a surface sitting inside a product space, spread over the base surface as a cover with p sheets. The sheets are joined into one surface when travel around a loop of the base cycles them. The multisections of Paper 005 are cyclic: one loop sends sheet one to sheet two, sheet two to sheet three, and the last back to the first.
# Defined word: handlebody (also: handlebodies)
A handlebody is a space given as a building recipe: balls first, then straps, then thicker patches, each joined along part of its edge, with a number for each strap that tells how it twists as it is joined. The list of counts of each kind is the bill of parts of the recipe. From the recipe alone, the hole counting of the space can be worked out by whole number arithmetic.
# Defined word: gluing (also: gluings)
Gluing is the joining of two spaces along a shared edge or surface, by a chosen map that says which point of one side meets which point of the other. The choice of map is real: two gluings of the same pieces by unlike maps can give unlike spaces.
# Defined word: diffeomorphism (also: diffeomorphisms)
A diffeomorphism is a map between two smooth spaces that goes both ways and makes no corners or kinks in either direction. It is the most strict of the same shape tests used here: spaces joined by one are the same smooth space.
# Defined word: diffeomorphic
Two spaces are diffeomorphic when a map joins them that goes both ways and makes no corners or kinks in either direction: they are the same smooth space, point for point.
# Defined word: homeomorphic
Two spaces are homeomorphic when one can be stretched, bent and squashed into the other with no tearing and no joining, and back again. This is a less strict same shape test than the smooth one: spaces can pass it and still fail the smooth test.
# Defined word: contractible
A space is contractible when it can be shrunk, inside itself, to a single point, the way a ball of clay can be pressed down without being torn. Such a space has no holes of any kind.
# Defined word: hull
The hull of a set of points is the smallest straight edged region that holds them all: the shape a rubber band takes around pegs on a board. Every weighted average of the points lies in the hull, and every point of the hull is such an average.
# Defined word: centroid
The centroid of a set of points, or of a cluster, is its center point: the average of all the points, each given the same weight. Distances to a centroid say how spread out the set is around its own middle.
# Defined word: seam
A seam is the surface or line along which two pieces are joined. When two pieces are joined by a chosen map, the seam is where the map does its work, and how the pieces meet at the seam decides what the joined space is.
# Defined word: framing (also: framings)
A framing is the twist number of a strap in a building recipe: as a handle is glued down, it may be turned some whole number of times, and that number is part of the recipe. Unlike framings of the same recipe give unlike spaces.
# Defined word: gating
Gating is the choosing, for each input, of how much weight each of several expert parts will have in the answer. A gate is the small part that makes the choice, after looking at the input. The weights add to one, so the answer is a weighted average of the experts.
# Defined word: polarization
Polarization, in a mixture of experts, is the collapse of the choosing: the gate sends nearly all weight to the one expert that got ahead first, that expert gets nearly all the training, and the gap grows until the mixture is one expert and dead weight. It is a rich get richer failure, and it must be trained against.
# Defined word: supercooperation
Supercooperation is the state of a system of agents whose working together has crossed the line where growing the group makes each added member worth more, not less. Below the line, groups hit a ceiling on their size. Above it, returns grow with scale, and the ceiling breaks.
# Defined word: substructure (also: substructures)
A substructure is a part of a structure that keeps its own pattern: a block of a program, a turn of a story, a piece of a space. Two large unlike things can share substructures, and what is learned on one can be carried to the other through the shared part.
# Defined word: granularity (also: granularities)
Granularity is the size of the parts you choose to look at: the grain. One program can be read at the grain of single steps, of blocks, or of the whole. A claim at one granularity need not hold at another, and some machines keep a separate expert for each grain.
# Defined word: jagged (also: jaggedness)
A profile is jagged when it is strong in one direction and weak in another, and the pattern of strong and weak is its own. Two jagged profiles can fit together like teeth: the weak place of one is the strong place of the other. That fit is the root of the mosaic effect.
# Defined word: coasean
Coasean names the line of thought from Coase: a firm grows until one more step inside costs as much as buying that step in the market. The ceiling this sets on firm size is the coasean ceiling.
# Defined word: ricardian
Ricardian names the line of thought from Ricardo: a thing that can not be copied earns by its scarcity. Its owner can charge above the cost of keeping it in use, and the excess is a rent that copying would destroy.
# Defined word: amalgamated
An amalgamated product is a group built by joining two groups along a shared part: the two copies of the shared part are made into one, and nothing else is forced. It is how the loop group of a joined space is built from the loop groups of the pieces.
# Foundation: types and terms
A TYPE is a kind label: 'a whole number', 'a true-or-false claim',
'a function from numbers to numbers'. A TERM is a thing that
carries one: the number 3, a proof, a function. One set of building
rules makes both — a term is itself a type when its own label is a
universe (1.3.77). The whole language of terms of MiniLean4 has
five kinds: a universe Sort u; a name; a Pi type (for each input of
one type, a result of a type that may depend on the input); a
lambda (a function built from a bound name); and an application
(one term put to another). Paper chapters add one more kind: a
constant, a name declared once for the whole system.
Builds on: 1.3.2 (function, input, output), 1.3.3 (set).
# Foundation: context and judgment
A CONTEXT is an ordered list of givens: names, each with its type,
where a later type may use the names before it. A JUDGMENT is a
claim made under a context: 'under these givens, this term has
this type'. The typing rules (1.2.42) say which judgment claims hold.
A judgment with an empty context is a claim that stands on its
own, with nothing given.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms).
# Foundation: computation steps and still forms
A term COMPUTES by steps of three kinds: beta (a function applied
to an input steps to its body, with the input put in place of the
bound name — putting in must not let a name be caught by a binder
of the same name inside; the system renames first, and getting
this wrong is where checker bugs hide); delta (a name with a
definition opens up to its definition); iota (a recursor (1.2.44)
applied to a built value runs the case for that builder). A term
to which no step applies is STILL (a normal form). Two terms are
equal by computation when both compute to the same still form,
up to renaming of bound names. A working checker puts a cap on
the steps (its fuel; MiniLean4: 20,000) so a bad term stops with
an error in place of running forever.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms), 1.3.4 ('the same').
# Foundation: paradox
A PARADOX is a build inside a system that makes the system prove
a claim and its opposite — after which the system proves anything
and is worth nothing. The paradox of this study: if one universe
held itself ('Type : Type'), a term can be built (Girard, made
smaller by Hurkens) that proves 'for every proposition P, P' —
the empty claim of 1.2.47 filled. The shape of the trick: a
collection that ranges over all types while being one lets it
hold a copy of its own double power set, a thing no set can do;
the load is carried by well-founded families that are, all of
them, below a family that is itself one of them.
Builds on: 1.3.3 (set), 1.3.77 (universes).
# Foundation: universes (floors of types)
A UNIVERSE is a type whose terms are types: Sort 0 holds the
propositions, Sort 1 holds Sort 0 and the small types (Nat lives
here), Sort 2 holds Sort 1, and so on — floors of a building, each
holding the one below, no floor holding itself (a floor that held
itself is the paradox of 1.3.76). To say which floor a function
type lives on, the imax rule: if the result lives on floor 0, the
function type lives on floor 0 too, whatever its input ranges
over; else it lives on the higher of the two floors.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms).
# Foundation: tactics and goals
A GOAL is a judgment still to be proved: a context, a target type,
and a hole where the proof term will go. A TACTIC is a step that
fills a hole with a term — possibly a term with new holes, which
become new goals. In MiniLean4 the tactics are: intro (the target
is a function type; take its input as a given and prove the rest),
exact (put in a finished term), apply (put in a function and leave
its inputs as goals), change (restate the target as one equal to
it by computation), rfl (close a goal of Eq when its sides compute
the same), induction (run the recursor of a given on the goal),
rw (rewrite the goal by an equation), and qed (send the whole
built term to the kernel). The soundness of the system rests on
qed alone: tactics only propose, the kernel decides.
Builds on: 1.3.74 (context and judgment), 1.3.75 (computation
steps).
# Defined word: recursor (also: recursors)
A recursor is the one function a system like MiniLean4 builds for
each inductive type: it takes what is to be shown or built for
each value of the type, one small case for each way of building a
value, and the value itself, and it walks the value by its
building steps. With a claim as its aim it is induction; with a
value as its aim it is recursion. One shape serves both.
# Defined word: impredicative
A floor of types is impredicative when a type on that floor may
range over the whole floor, itself counted in, and stay on the
floor. In MiniLean4 only floor 0, the propositions, is
impredicative: 'for every proposition P' is itself a proposition.
One such floor is safe; two would let the paradox in, for a type
could then be looked inside as data.
# Foundation: ownership and borrowing
In Rust, every value has exactly one OWNER. A value may be
BORROWED in two ways: shared (many readers at once, no writer) or
mutable (one borrow at a time, and while that borrow lives no
other road to the value exists — not even its owner). When a
borrow ends, the road returns to the owner. A checker in the Rust
compiler (the borrow checker) refuses any program that breaks
these rules, before the program runs. The rules are what Aeneas
(Study S002) stands on: because a mutable borrow is the only road
to its value, the value can stand in for the borrow (1.2.49).
Builds on: 1.3.33 (program, runtime, state).
# Foundation: symbolic execution — running on stand-ins
To run a program SYMBOLICALLY is to run it with stand-ins in
place of its values: where the program would hold the number 3,
it holds 'some whole number, call it n', and where it would
branch, the running follows both arms with the condition of each
written down. The result is not one run but the shape of all
runs. Checking a program this way can find what no set of test
runs reaches; carrying a program into another language this way
is how Aeneas works (1.2.52).
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms), 1.3.2 (function).
# Foundation: a tenant
In a system shared by many customers, a TENANT is one customer's
sealed region: their rows, their files, their names — present in
the same store as those of every other tenant, and kept apart by
the rules of the system. That tenant A can not read the rows of
tenant B is the kind of claim the framework of Study S003 proves
of its kernel (1.2.53): the seal is a theorem, not a hope about
careful code.
Builds on: 1.3.33 (program, runtime, state).
# Defined word: tenant (also: tenants)
A tenant is one customer of a system that many customers share,
counted as a sealed region of the system: the rows, files and
names of the tenant are in the same store as those of every other
tenant, and the rules of the system keep them apart. To say a
system is safe for its tenants is to say no tenant can reach into
the region of another.
# Foundation: state machine
A STATE MACHINE is a thing whose behavior depends on which of a
set of named states it is in. It starts in one of the states.
When an input arrives — a signal, a request, a step of work —
rules say what it does and which state it moves to next. The
same input does different things in different states: a door
that is locked stays shut when pushed, and a door that is open
swings. What the thing does is never fixed by the input alone;
it is fixed by the input together with the state it is in.

A state machine is EXPLICIT when its states and its rules are
named outright, where they can be read and checked. It is
IMPLICIT when no such naming exists but the machine is present
anyway, carried in how the thing is built: a kept value that
picks which behavior runs, several kept marks that are read in
combination, or the place a running program has reached in its
flow — each of these is a state that was never given a name.
Making an implicit machine explicit means finding the states
it already has and writing the rules down. Charter rule 13
(1.1.1) sends this tracker looking for them.
Builds on: state (1.3.33), computation steps and still forms (1.3.75).
# Defined word: refuted
A claim stands refuted when a receipt speaks against it: a
recorded clash, a check that failed, a counter-case. The word
names the ground on which a claim stands False in this phext
(charter rule 14, at 1.1.1): the claim is kept, with the receipt
that speaks against it, and is never quietly dropped.
# Foundation: specification and test
A SPECIFICATION is a written statement of what would count as
acceptable: what a thing must do, or be, for the one asking to
take it. A TEST is a specification cut down to one case that
can be checked with no room between pass and fail: give this
input, expect that output. A set of tests is a specification
made of cases, and it is silent about every case it does not
name. A specification can also take other shapes — rules over
whole classes of cases, or statements about the inside of a
thing (1.3.90, 1.3.91) — and the shapes differ in what they can
say and in what they leave open.
Builds on: function, variable, input, output (1.3.2).
# Foundation: candidate class and goal set
Name all the programs an agent could in principle produce: that
whole set is the field of CANDIDATES. Inside it sits the GOAL
SET: the programs the one asking would accept. When part of the
answer is fixed in advance — this shape of data, these parts,
these boundaries — only the programs sharing the fixed part
remain in play; that smaller set is a CANDIDATE CLASS. Questions
of the form 'how much more must be said to land inside the goal
set?' are always asked inside one candidate class, and their
answers can change completely from one class to the next.
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3), program (1.3.33).
# Foundation: prior and conditioning
A PRIOR is a spread of belief over a set of candidates before
new evidence: each candidate carries a share, the shares add
to one, and a bigger share means more expected. CONDITIONING
is what evidence does to the spread: cross out every candidate
the evidence rules out, and re-share what is left among the
survivors in the same proportions as before. The spread after
the evidence is the POSTERIOR. Conditioning only ever removes
candidates; it cannot move belief back to a crossed-out
candidate, and among the survivors it never changes who stood
ahead of whom.
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3), average, spread, distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: contract — promises at a boundary
A CONTRACT, at the boundary of one part of a program, states
what the part asks and what it promises. What it asks: what
must hold when the part is called — the inputs it can take,
the state it expects to find. What it promises: what will hold
when it returns — the result it gives, the state it leaves
behind. A contract is LOCAL when it binds one part. An
INVARIANT (1.3.5) is a promise of the wider kind, binding many
parts at once. A part that keeps its contract can be relied on
by those who call it and never look inside it, and in the best cases
whether a contract is kept can be checked by machine.
Builds on: function, variable, input, output (1.3.2),
invariant (1.3.5), program, runtime, state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: interface and observability
The INTERFACE of a part is the named surface where the rest of
the world touches it: the values it takes in, the values it
gives back, and the middle values it lets others name. A value
that lives inside a running program but is named at no
interface is HIDDEN STATE. A computation is OBSERVABLE at a
point when the values there can be read from outside — by a
test, say — before later steps cover them up. What no interface
names cannot be tested directly; it shows itself only by what
it does to the final output, if anything it does survives that
far.
Builds on: program, runtime, state (1.3.33),
function, variable, input, output (1.3.2).
# Foundation: attention — how a model weighs what it has read
When a language model writes its next token, it weighs the tokens
already written: a few count for much, most for almost nothing.
That weighing is attention. From each earlier token the model has
made two things: a KEY, which says what the token can be found by,
and a VALUE, which is what the token hands over when it is used.
The token now being written makes a QUERY — a question in the same
shape as a key. The model scores the query against every key, turns
the scores into shares that add to one, and takes the share-weighted
average of the values as the contribution of the past to the
next token.
In writing one token at a time, earlier keys and values do not
change, so they are kept — the KEY-VALUE CACHE — and each step adds
only its own. The cache is the cost: every new token is scored
against every key in it, so the work of one step grows in a straight
line with the length, and the work of the whole text grows with the
square (1.3.93).
Builds on: token, context, cache (1.3.36), average, spread,
distribution (1.3.8), function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: growth rates — straight line, square, and power laws
Costs grow, and the shape of the growth decides what is possible at
large sizes. A cost grows in a STRAIGHT LINE when doubling the size
doubles the cost; it grows with the SQUARE when doubling the size
makes the cost four times as large. Between and beyond those steps
sit the POWER LAWS: the cost grows as the size raised to a power,
its EXPONENT — exponent 1 is the straight line, exponent 2 the
square, and an exponent under 1 is growth slower than the straight
line: the bigger the size, the smaller the share of it the cost
takes. Growth slower than the straight line is called sublinear
(1.3.100); growth slower than the square, subquadratic (1.3.101).
Builds on: integers, finite, infinite (1.3.6), function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: the top-p set — the few keys that carry the mass
The shares of attention (1.3.92) form a spread over the keys: most
keys hold almost none of the mass. Order the keys by share, largest
first, and take keys until the shares taken add to a chosen part p
of the whole — 90 percent, say. The keys taken are the TOP-P SET
for that query: the few that carry the mass. What is left together
holds 1 minus p of the mass, so dropping it moves the final average
only a little. Every sparse-attention method makes this trade; they
differ in how they find the set without scoring every key.
Builds on: attention, keys, values, cache (1.3.92), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: tree — groups inside groups
A tree is a way of holding a set in groups inside groups. The set
is split into chunks; the chunks are grouped into larger chunks;
and so on up to one group that holds all. Each group is a NODE; the
groups directly inside it are its CHILDREN; the group that directly
holds it is its PARENT; the smallest pieces are the LEAVES; the one
top group is the ROOT. A node sits at a LEVEL — how many rounds of
grouping up from the leaves it sits — and the number of levels is the tree's
DEPTH. The use of the shape: start at the top, and go down only
into the groups that look worth opening.
Builds on: set (1.3.3), graph (1.3.41).
# Foundation: a summary that stands for a group
A summary of a group is a small stand-in, computed from the group's
members, used in their place for a first look. A summary is judged
by one test: the first look it gives should match the look the
members themselves would give. A good summary lets the reader skip
the members that do not matter and open only the groups that do. A
summary of summaries is a summary one level up (1.3.95). A summary
is not the group: what it leaves out, it cannot give back.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: the tangent — a straight line that touches a curve
Take one chunk of keys and let the query move. The total pull of the chunk
pull on the query — the sum of its keys' scores, logged — traces a
curve: smooth, and rounded the same way everywhere, never sharply bent.
A TANGENT is a straight line that touches such a curve at one
chosen query and hugs it near that query. A handful of tangents,
each touching at a different chosen query, can stand in for the
whole curve: at a new query, read the highest of the lines as the
the height of the curve there. Near one of its touching points a line is
nearly exact; far from all of them it only bounds. Lines are cheap
to keep and quick to read — that is their use (1.2.68).
Builds on: function (1.3.2), attention, keys, values, cache (1.3.92).
# Foundation: distillation — teaching a part to copy a whole
Distillation is teaching one small part to behave as a larger,
already-trained whole behaves, without training the whole again.
The student is shown the teacher's outputs and changed until its
own outputs match them; the size of the mismatch between two
spreads of shares is measured by a gap (the KL gap), and training
drives the gap down. Only the student is trained — the teacher is
frozen — so distillation adds a small trained part to a finished
system in a short run, at the price that the part keeps only what
the matching forced it to keep.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8),
neural network, weights, training (1.3.40).
# Foundation: roofline — a reckoning of speed from two ceilings
A roofline estimate reckons how long a piece of computing takes
from two ceilings: how fast the machine can do arithmetic, and how
fast it can move numbers between its memory and its arithmetic.
The time is at least the larger of the work divided by the first
ceiling and the numbers moved divided by the second — plus, on
real machines, the cost of starting many small pieces of work,
which arrive in waves and waste the edges of each wave. A roofline
number is a reckoning, not a measurement: it says what the time
would be if the machine behaved exactly as the ceilings say. Where
the picture the reckoning holds of the work is wrong, the number is wrong
with it, however careful the arithmetic.
Builds on: FPGA, kernel, pragma — latency named (1.3.44),
function (1.3.2).
Defined word: sublinear
sublinear, slower-than-the-line (word coined by this phext,
Paper 007, 2026-11-07): of a growth, slower than straight-line
growth — double the size, and the amount grows by less than double;
the bigger the size, the smaller the share of it the amount takes.
See 1.3.93.
Defined word: subquadratic
subquadratic (word coined by this phext, Paper 007, 2026-11-07):
of a growth, slower than the square of the size — double the size,
and the amount grows by less than four times. Attention that costs
less than the square of the context length is subquadratic
attention. See 1.3.93.
# Foundation: memory and address
A computer's MEMORY is a long row of places, each holding
one BYTE, a small piece of data. Each place has a number,
its ADDRESS, and to read or write a place is to name its
address. Places are used in runs: a WORD is a run of bytes
taken together — 4 bytes in the world of Chapter 12 — and
sizes are counted in whole words, so a thing of 5 bytes takes
8 bytes of room and a thing of 10 takes 12. A REGION is a run
of places set aside for one kind of use. A SEGMENTATION
VIOLATION is the system's stop when a program tries to write
a place it may only read, or to run from a place that holds
no code: the run ends there.
Builds on: state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: register and pointer
A REGISTER is one of the few named places inside the
processor itself, each holding one word, read and changed
faster than any place in memory. Three registers rule
Chapter 12: the STACK POINTER (SP), holding the address of
the top of the stack; the FRAME POINTER (FP), holding the
address of a fixed point inside the current frame; and the
INSTRUCTION POINTER (IP), holding the address of the next
instruction to run. A POINTER is an address kept as data —
in a register, or in a place in memory — so that what it
points to can be found again. A saved pointer is still data:
it can be written like any other word; and when the machine
later trusts it as an address, the data becomes direction.
Builds on: memory and address (1.3.102).
# Foundation: the stack
A STACK is a run of memory used in one strict order: what is
put on last is taken off first — last in, first out. Two
moves rule it: PUSH puts a word on top, and POP takes the
top word off. On the machines of Chapter 12 the stack grows
DOWN, toward lower addresses, and its bottom rests at a
fixed address. Programs use the stack to hold the run
itself: each live call owns one FRAME, its run of words on
the stack — the parameters of the call, its local variables, the saved frame pointer of its caller, and the return address
(1.2.74). A call pushes a frame; a return pops it. The stack
is a state machine (1.3.85) whose state is the chain of live
frames.
Builds on: memory and address (1.3.102), register and
pointer (1.3.103), state machine (1.3.85).
# Foundation: buffer and bounds
A BUFFER is a run of memory set aside to hold data of one
kind — most often characters, one after another: a string.
Every buffer has BOUNDS: the first place that is its own,
and the first place past it that is not. A copy into a buffer
is CHECKED when it counts what it writes against the bounds
and stops at the end; it is UNCHECKED when it writes until
the source runs out, wherever that may end. What lies past
a of the buffer end is other data, in the order memory laid it
down; an unchecked copy does not know where the buffer ends,
and neither does the data it carries.
Builds on: memory and address (1.3.102).
Defined word: shellcode
shellcode (a word of the field, taken into this phext by
Study S004, 2026-11-08): a short run of code bytes made to
be carried into a running program as data — set down inside
a buffer, or in the world the system sets around the
program — and then run by turning the program's flow of
execution to the place where the bytes sit. The name is for
what it most often does: start a shell, a program that takes
the orders a person types and runs them.
See 1.2.78.
# Foundation: design token — a named value, held once
A design token is a name given to one value of look — a
color, a space, a corner round, a shadow — so that the
parts read the name and never hold the value themselves.
Change the value in the one place and every part that
reads the name changes with it. A theme (1.2.84) is a
set of design tokens with their values set.
Builds on: variable (1.3.2).
# Foundation: custom property and layer — values a page carries, and the order of sheets
A custom property is a named value set on a page or on
part of it, read by the sheets that style it, and open
to being set again lower down — the page carries the
value, the sheets only name it. A layer is a named rank
among sheets: when two sheets speak to the same thing,
the sheet in the later layer wins. Together they are
how a look can be laid over a system without touching
its parts: set the values again, in a later layer.
Builds on: design token (1.3.107).
# Foundation: module graph — who reads whom
The module graph of a set of code is the web of its
parts and their readings of each other: each part a
point, each 'reads the other to build itself' a line
between the two. To copy a part whole you must walk its
graph and take every part it stands on; to know what a
change touches, you read the graph the other way.
Builds on: graph (1.3.41).
# Foundation: rule checker — a tool that reads code against stated rules
A rule checker is a program whose input is a program:
it holds stated rules — shapes the code must take, or
must not take — and reports every place that breaks
one. The running of such a checker over code is called
lint, and one rule of it is a lint rule (1.3.112). A
rule is only as strong as its tests and its running; a
rule written in a book but not in the tool is advice,
not a check.
Builds on: program (1.3.33).
Defined word: swizzle (also: swizzled)
swizzle (a word of the field, taken into this phext by
Study S005, 2026-11-09): to take a part out of a kit as
source — the part and the inner pieces it needs, copied
into your own project to own and change — while its
calls on the public parts of the kit stay calls on
the kit.
See 1.2.85.
Defined word: lint (also: linting)
lint (a word of the field, taken into this phext by
Study S005, 2026-11-09): the running of a rule checker
(1.3.110) over code, and the tool that does it; a lint
rule is one stated rule the checker holds.
See 1.2.86.
# Foundation: tensor — a number field of many axes
A tensor is a field of numbers laid out on axes: one
axis makes a line of numbers (a vector, 1.3.7), two
axes make a table, three or more make fields no page
can draw, still counted by the length of each axis.
The word names the promise of the shape: every place in
the field is reached by one index per axis, and the
field can be read in an order the axes set down —
the order in memory is part of what a tensor is, and
two tensors with the same numbers in a different
order of memory are different things to a machine
that reads them fast. The work of machine learning
is, at the bottom, work on tensors: the inputs, the
weights (1.3.40), and every field between them.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: gradient and autograd — the slope of the loss, found by walking the work back
A gradient is the slope of one number against
another: how much the end number moves when a number
inside the work moves a little. Training (1.3.40)
is steered by the gradient of the loss — the score
of how wrong it is — against every weight: each weight is
nudged against its slope, a small step at a time.
Finding the slopes by hand for a large network is
beyond anyone; autograd (the machine finding of
gradients) finds them by rule. While the work runs
forward, each step is written down; then the steps
are walked back in the opposite order, and the slope
of the end is handed from each step to the steps
that fed it, by the known slope of each kind of
step. The whole of it rests on one fact of the
calculus: the slope of a chain is the product of
the slopes of its links.
Builds on: neural network, weights, training (1.3.40).
# Foundation: training loop — the four moves, made until the loss falls
A training loop is the round a learning machine
turns while it learns, the same four moves in the
same order: (1) the forward pass — the inputs run
through the network and a guess comes out; (2) the
loss — the guess is scored against the known answer,
one number for how wrong it is; (3) the backward
pass — the gradient of the loss is found (1.3.114);
(4) the step — every weight is nudged a little
against its gradient, by a rule for the size of the
nudge. Then the round turns again, over more data,
until the loss stops falling or the data runs out.
Everything a framework exists to do is one of these
four moves or the keeping of them.
Builds on: gradient and autograd (1.3.114); neural
network, weights, training (1.3.40).
# Foundation: kernel, in the machine-learning sense — the small hot piece of work
In machine learning, a kernel is a small, named
piece of number work that runs over tensors — a sum
over a sliding window, a product of two fields, a
turning of scores into shares — small enough to
hold in the head, hot enough to be where the time
goes. Kernels are the units the systems work of the
field is made of: they are counted, timed, joined
together so the memory is touched once instead of
three times, and written again for each kind of
machine that will run them.
This word is not the kernel of 1.3.44: there, a
kernel is the pattern a high-level synthesis tool
works from. The two fields took the same word for
the small thing at the center of the work; the
tracker keeps both, and this scroll marks the
difference.
Builds on: kernel in the synthesis sense (1.3.44);
tensor (1.3.113).
Defined word: immediacy
immediacy (word coined by this phext, charter rule 17,
2026-11-09): the rule that the steps of a taking-in follow
one another with no gap between them — pick, download, read,
take in, write the journal — all in one pass, none of them
left for a later day.
# Foundation: fixed point, and a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism
A FIXED POINT of a map is a point the map sends to itself: it
stands where it stood. A map that can be run both ways,
smoothly, is a diffeomorphism (1.3.16). A HAMILTONIAN
DIFFEOMORPHISM is a diffeomorphism made by a flow: lay a
height function over a symplectic manifold (1.3.22); at each
point, the slope of the height, turned by the area rule,
points a way; let every point ride its way for one whole unit
of time. The map the points land under is a
Hamiltonian diffeomorphism. Such a map can be turned a little
— its flow changed a little — and stay one; the small turns
of 1.2.99 ride on this.
Builds on: maps between spaces (1.3.16); symplectic (1.3.22).
# Foundation: critical point of a function — where the slope rests
A CRITICAL POINT of a smooth function on a manifold is a
point where the slope of the function is zero in every
direction: a top, a bottom, or a pass where the ground rests
before falling either way. Every smooth function on a closed
manifold has some; Crit(M) names the least number any smooth
function on M can be made to have — the fewest resting points
of a slope drawn over M. A critical point is not degenerate
when the slope grows away from it at a nonzero rate in every
direction. A whole submanifold of critical points, the slope
resting along it and growing across it, is a Morse-Bott
locus — the shape the fixed locus of 1.2.99 takes.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: flag, Grassmannian — spaces whose points are subspaces
A SUBSPACE of a space is a smaller space sitting inside it,
flat in the same way. A FLAG is a chain of subspaces, each
inside the next, growing by steps toward the whole. A
GRASSMANNIAN is the space of all subspaces of one fixed size
inside a given space: its points are subspaces, and it is
itself a manifold — a flag of one step. A FLAG VARIETY is
the space of all flags of one fixed shape of chain. A
subspace is LAGRANGIAN when it has half the size of the
whole and the area rule (1.3.22) reads zero on every pair of
its directions; the space of all Lagrangian subspaces is the
Lagrangian Grassmannian.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9); symplectic
(1.3.22).
# Foundation: eigenvalue — the directions a map does not turn
A linear map turns most directions into new ones. A
direction it does not turn at all is special: every point
of that line is sent to a point of the same line, stretched,
held, or flipped by one factor — the EIGENVALUE of the
direction. A map that turns a plane by an angle has no
such direction in that plane; the turn itself, written as
a pair on the unit circle, is what stands in their place.
The whole flat of directions that share one eigenvalue
stands together as one block of the search at 1.2.101:
the block the map holds, the block it flips, and the
blocks it turns.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7).
Defined word: subspace — a space that sits inside a
bigger space, flat in the same way: add two of its points, or
stretch one of its points by a number, and you stay inside it.
A flag (1.3.120) is a chain of subspaces, each inside the next.
Defined word: perturbation — a small turn of a map, or of
the flow that makes the map, made to trade a hard count for an
easy one; the turned map stays a map of its own kind. The bound
of 1.2.99 is spent by a perturbation, and the word names the
spending wherever this phext needs it.
# Foundation: flow, trajectory, and the end of a road
A flow is a rule that carries every starting state of a
system to the state it holds at each later time — the whole
motion of the system, read as one map of time. A trajectory
(one road of the flow) is the path of a single start: the
states it passes, in order, for as long as the rule can
carry it. The maximal interval of a road is the longest run
of time the road exists on; its ends, when they fall at a
finite time, are where the road stops — not by choice, but
because the rule cannot be carried past them inside the
states the system allows. Such a finite end is called a
singularity of the road: the motion breaks down there, and
the map is silent past it unless the road is continued by
some further device. Whether the roads of a flow can end at
finite time at all, and what the state is doing as an end
nears, are questions about the flow — for the segment
problem they are answered whole at 1.2.106.
# Foundation: gravitational potential — the field as a height
A heavy body pulls a particle toward itself, and the pull
is summed from every part of the body: each part pulls
along the line to it, with a strength that falls like the
square of the distance, weighted by the mass of the part. The
potential U(q) holds that sum in one number at each
point q — minus the mass-weighted sum of 1 over the
distance to each part, times the fixed pull constant G. The
pull itself is the slope of the potential, read downhill: the
particle hastens toward lower U. Energy splits along the
same seam: the moving part, half the square of the momentum, plus
U; the sum is kept along every road of the flow, and the
keeping rules the whole of mechanics. A
place where U falls without bound is a singularity of the
field: roads can gain any speed there, and the flow may end
(1.3.124). For a point mass, U falls like 1 over the
distance; for a segment, only like a log (1.2.105).
# Foundation: angular momentum — the kept turn
A particle moving around an axis carries a turning
quantity: how much of its motion goes around, weighted by
its distance from the axis. In coordinates fitted to the
axis — along it, out from it, around it — the quantity is
ℓ = p_φ, the momentum of the around-coordinate, and the
energy holds it in a term ℓ²/2ρ²: the farther out the turn,
the less it costs; the nearer the axis, the more, without bound
as ρ falls to zero. A field that is unchanged by turns
about the axis keeps ℓ fixed along every road of its flow:
turn-symmetry is what is kept — the same bargain symmetry
always strikes, a quantity kept, for a field that does not change
under the turn. The term ℓ²/2ρ² is called the angular-momentum
barrier: a road with ℓ not zero
must pay it to approach the axis, and whether the field can
lend enough to pay is a race between the growth of the barrier
and the depth of the potential — for the segment, the race is
run at 1.2.107.
# Foundation: cone — all the rays through a set
From a point, look at a set: the rays from the point
through every point of the set sweep a cone — every direction
from that point to the set, stretched as far forward as
it goes. A cone is closed under stretching its vectors and
under adding them with weights that are not negative: it holds
directions, not lengths. The cone over a segment, from a
point off its line, is a flat wedge; from a point on the
line, it falls flat to the line itself. A triangle is the
filled shape: a point together with a segment, and every
point between. The cones of this phext do their work where
where the possible directions of a field are gathered into one set and
a road is asked whether its directions stay inside — the
angular cone of the segment problem (1.2.109) is two cones
at once: where the particle may stand, and which ways its
momentum may point.
# Defined word: lemma
A lemma is a small claim, shown true on the way to a
theorem and spent there: strong enough to stand under the
larger claim, small enough to be held whole in the hand. A
paper's theorems are where its roads end; its lemmas are
the steps cut so the roads can be walked. The word belongs
to the field itself, old in mathematics, and it is coined here
because the stream will meet it in paper after paper — a
tracker that reads mathematics needs a word for the steps
as much as for the theorems. First spent at Paper 009
(Chapter 16), whose whole weight stands on one: the log
sandwich, its step 3.2.
# Foundation: chance and probability — a rule for what comes
Some things are not settled in advance: a coin in the
air, the next context a system will meet. PROBABILITY is
the measure of how unsettled a thing is: a number
between 0
and 1 given to each way things could come out — 0 for
never, 1 for certain, and the numbers over all the ways
adding to 1. A DISTRIBUTION is the whole rule at once:
every way things could come out, each with its number.
To say a draw FOLLOWS a distribution is to say the draw
is one pull from that rule — which way comes up is not
known beforehand, but the pull is governed by the rule,
and many pulls show the shape of the rule. The average and
the spread of 1.3.8 are properties of a set of numbers
already in hand; a distribution is the rule standing
behind numbers not yet drawn.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: a sample, and independence — draws that do not lean on each other
A SAMPLE is a set of draws from one distribution, kept
as the evidence about the rule behind them. Two draws
are INDEPENDENT when neither leans on the other: knowing
how the first came out changes nothing about the chances
of the second — not its rule, not its tilt. A sample
whose draws are independent and all from the same
distribution is the standard evidence of this phext's
statistics cluster: each draw is a fresh pull from the
same rule. The seam the whole cluster turns on is the
difference between a FRESH draw — independent of
everything already in hand — and a draw already inside
the evidence, leaning on the rest and leaned on by it:
a model fitted on a sample is not independent of that
sample, and judging it there is judging it on ground
that leans. Splitting a sample is the classical repair:
fit on one part, and the other part stands fresh against
the fit — at the price of fitting on less.
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129).
# Foundation: expectation — the average a distribution promises
The EXPECTATION of a quantity under a distribution is
its promised average: each value the quantity can take,
weighed by the chance of that value, and the weighed
values summed. It is the number the plain average of
many independent draws closes on — the average of 1.3.8
is what you count from draws in hand; the expectation is
what the rule promises before the draws are made. Nearly
every claim in the statistics cluster is a claim about
an expectation: a value is an expectation of reward, a
risk is an expectation of loss, and an estimator is
judged by how far its naming stands from the expectation
it names.
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129); a sample
(1.3.130); average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: risk — the loss a rule expects
A LOSS is a number that says how bad an outcome is: the square
of the gap between a guess and the truth is the loss
this phext's statistics cluster spends most. The RISK of
a guessing model is the expectation of its loss — the average
badness the rule promises over fresh draws. TEST RISK is
the same promise read on draws the model never saw
while being made: the risk of the shipped thing, in the
world. A risk always belongs to a model and a
distribution together — change whose draws, or which
weighing of them, and the risk is another number: the
same fitted model carries one risk under the rule that
logged the data and another under the rule it will
serve.
Builds on: expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: estimator and consistency — naming a number you cannot see
An ESTIMATOR is a rule that reads a sample and names a
number, meant to stand for a truth the sample does not
show directly — a value, a risk, a parameter of the rule
behind the draws. The own error of an estimator is the gap
between the number it names and the truth, and the gap
is itself unsettled: another sample would name another
number. An estimator is CONSISTENT for its truth when,
as the sample grows, the number it names closes on the
truth — the chance of a gap wider than any fixed width
falls to zero. Consistency UNIFORM over a set of
candidates is the stronger claim the statistics cluster
leans on: the closing happens at every candidate at
once, so the claim still holds when the data itself
picks which candidate gets named. An estimator can be
consistent and still wander on any one sample; the
wander has its own measure, the spread of the naming
over samples — its variance (1.3.8).
Builds on: a sample (1.3.130); expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: context, action, reward — the bandit table, and its two rules
The setting the statistics cluster prices: rounds. Each
round, a CONTEXT arrives — what can be seen before
choosing: a user, a patient, a state of play. A rule
chooses an ACTION from a fixed set. A REWARD comes back
— the payoff of that action at that context — and only
of that action: the rewards of the actions not chosen
are never seen. The game is named for the slot machines
of an old picture — many levers, each with a hidden payoff — and
the word bandit stands in the archive for it. Two rules
stand at the table. The LOGGING rule is the one that
played: it made the record, choosing each action with
known chances that may lean on the context or not. The
TARGET rule is the one being priced: the rule whose
choices the record is asked to judge. POSITIVITY is the
ground rule of the table: every action keeps a chance under
the logging rule bounded away from zero — an action the
logging rule never takes leaves no trace in the record,
and no price for a target that takes it can be read from
that record.
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129); a sample
(1.3.130).
# Foundation: the importance weight — pricing a record again by the ratio of two rules
A record made by one rule can be priced again for another by
weighing each round: the IMPORTANCE WEIGHT of a round is
the chance the target rule gives the action that was
taken, at the context of that round, divided by the chance
the logging rule gave it. A round the target rule would
have chosen twice as often counts double; a round it
would never have chosen counts for nothing. Averaging
the weighed rewards prices the target rule — that is the
whole of the weighted way at 1.2.110: the record stands
unchanged, and the weighing turns it into evidence about
a rule that never ran. The price of the trick is
how uneven the weights run: where the target rule leans
far from the
logging rule, a few rounds carry enormous weights, and
the price wanders with them — the spread of the weighed
average is set by how far apart the two rules stand.
Builds on: the bandit table (1.3.134); expectation
(1.3.131).
# Foundation: gradient descent — walking downhill by the slope, one step at a time
A fitted model is a rule with settings — its weights —
and a loss that prices the settings on the data in hand.
GRADIENT DESCENT is the plain way of bettering the
settings: stand at the current weights, read the slope
of the loss there — the direction of its rise, in every
setting at once — and step the other way, by a
step size chosen in advance. Then read the slope again.
The positions of the walk, step by step, are its ITERATES;
the whole list of them is the TRAJECTORY. Early stopping
is the choice of which iterate to ship, and it is a real
choice: the loss on the fitting data falls as the walk
goes, while the risk on fresh data (1.3.132) can turn
and rise — the walk passes the best model it will ever
hold and walks on into worse ones. Which step a walk is
judged at, and by what judge, is the whole question of
the statistics cluster.
Builds on: function (1.3.2); risk (1.3.132).
# Foundation: growing together — when two sizes rise in step
Two ways a body of data can grow. In the classical way,
the sample grows while the thing measured stays the
size it was — more draws of the same few numbers — and
every average comes to rest: errors wash out, and the old
theory's promises hold. In the way the statistics
cluster works, the sample and the dimension grow
TOGETHER: each draw carries more numbers as the count of
draws rises, the ratio of the two holding steady — the
proportional regime. There, the washing-out fails in a
specific way: the many numbers of each draw carry a standing
amount of noise that no growth of the sample removes,
because the noise grows with the draw. Results proved
for the first way — grades that settle, terms that
vanish — cannot be carried into the second way without
being proved again there, and several of them are false
there. The ratio itself becomes a property of every
claim: a risk, a ranking, a variance, each is a function
of the ratio, not a number.
Builds on: a sample (1.3.130); dimension (1.3.9).
# Foundation: the bell law — the Gaussian distribution
One distribution does more work than any other in this
phext's statistics: the BELL LAW (the Gaussian, or
normal, distribution). Its shape is the bell: most draws
near the middle, fewer as you walk away, the falling-off
set by one spread. It is fixed by two numbers — its
middle and its spread — and it has a property no other
law has in the same strength: sums and mixtures of its
draws stay inside the law, so a quantity built from many
small unsettled parts is priced by the bell almost
whatever the parts were. A context drawn from the bell
law in many dimensions is a list of numbers, each
bell-drawn on its own, none leaning on the others: the
round cloud with no features of its own, the statistics
theorems are proved on — and that the cloud is round is
itself load-bearing, which is why the edge of the cluster
stands where it does (Q49).
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129); vector
(1.3.7).
# Foundation: an index — many numbers read through a few directions
A context is a list of many numbers; a rule that reads
all of them at once is hard to hold and harder to judge.
An INDEX reads the list through one direction: one fixed
list of weights, the context dotted against it — each
number times its weight, summed — giving ONE number that
stands for the whole list along that direction. A reward
that depends on the context only through one index is a
single-index reward: the list matters only as its shadow
on one line, and the payoff is a fixed curve of that
shadow. A few directions at once give a multi-index
reading — the target rule of the statistics cluster
leans on the context through a few indices, so its
choices are governed by a small shadow of a large list.
The reading loses the rest of the list on purpose: what
stands orthogonal to the directions is invisible to the
rule, and proofs about the rule need only track the
shadow — until the own directions of the walk grow out of the
shadow, which is where the work of the statistics cluster
begins.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7); function (1.3.2).
Defined word: criterion — the measure a judge reads
off a thing at each of its steps: where the measure
stands least, there the judge stops. A criterion is
spent by being read, never by being fitted. First
spent at Paper 010 (Chapter 17), which builds a
corrected criterion that prices a whole walk from the
walk itself.
# Foundation: induction — a rule guessed from examples

Some tasks give the rule; others give only examples of
the rule at work, and ask for the rule itself. INDUCTION
is the second kind of task: from a few cases where input
and output stand side by side, find the rule that turns
each input into its output — and that would turn new
inputs the same way. The examples never name one rule
alone: however many stand shown, a rule that fits them
all and a rule that fits some of them and more besides
can part ways on the next case. Guessing the rule is
therefore a search with a taste for some rules over
others, not a reading-off. Program induction is induction
where the rule found is a program: the guess is a piece
of code, and it can be checked by running it on the
examples that stand shown.
Builds on: program, runtime, state (1.3.33); function,
variable, input, output (1.3.2).
# Foundation: a small language made for one field

A language for writing programs in general carries tools
for every field at once. A SMALL LANGUAGE FOR ONE FIELD
— a domain-specific language — carries only the tools of
one field: a fixed kit of operations, the primitives,
out of which every program in the language is built, and
nothing else. The kit is the field, written as code:
change the kit and the same words build different things.
Programs in such a language can be counted, compared and
searched in ways a general language does not allow,
because the whole space of what can be said is the reach of the kit. The Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus is read in
this tracker through one such kit: 160 operations over
grids and the things in them, the programs written so
each step names its result once and is called by that
name after — the normal form the field calls A-normal.
Builds on: program, runtime, state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: pooling — groups lending each other strength

When a number is wanted for each of many groups — how
often each kind of error is made, say — each group can be
read alone; and then a group with few cases speaks as
loudly as a group with many. POOLING reads the groups
together: one reckoning holds every group's number and a
shared shape over them all, so each group's number is
pulled a little toward the shape of the whole — most
where its own cases are few, least where they are many.
No group rules the reading and no group is thrown away:
the strength of the many makes the few steady. What comes out
is the middle kind of estimate of 1.3.133 — not one
group's count, not that of the whole crowd, but each group's
count made steady by the crowd.
Builds on: a sample, and independence (1.3.130);
expectation (1.3.131); estimator and consistency
(1.3.133).
Defined word: satisficing — taking the rule that
is good enough: the first rule that fits enough
of what stands shown, and not paying to search for the
rule that fits all of it. The word is Simon's, for
choice under a bounded search. First spent at Paper 011
(Chapter 18), where people and machines alike are found
to take rules that fit some of the shown examples, and
stop.
# Foundation: the sequence model — a signal plus noise, place by place

The smallest stage on which the pricing of an estimator
is worked out. A SIGNAL is a list of numbers, one for
each of N places: the true value at each place. What is
SEEN is the signal plus NOISE: at each place, the seen
number is the true number plus a draw from the bell law
(1.3.138) — a draw that leans on no other draw (1.3.130),
all draws of one spread, named sigma. Nothing else
stands on the stage: no record of past rounds, no
choices made along the way. Every question of this
stage is a question about the list: name a rule that
turns the seen list into a guess at the true list, and
ask how far the guess stands from the truth, on the
mean (1.3.131), in the square of the distance.
Builds on: the bell law (1.3.138); a sample, and
independence (1.3.130); expectation (1.3.131); vector
and distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: projection onto a convex set — the closest point of the set

A set is CONVEX when, for any two points it holds, it
holds the whole straight line between them as well
(1.3.30). Given a point, its PROJECTION onto the set is
the closest point of the set: the one point of the set
whose distance (1.3.7) to the given point is smallest.
For a closed convex set there is exactly one such
point, so projection is a rule that turns any point
into one point of the set, changing the point as
little as distance allows. To project is to answer
'the nearest allowed thing' — nothing more is claimed,
and nothing more is done.
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3); mixing and the
convex hull (1.3.30); vector and distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: a sharp rate — a price matched up to constants

A RATE for an estimator is a number that names the size
of its error: here, the mean of the square of the distance
between the estimate and the truth. A rate is SHARP
when it is matched from both sides up to fixed
constants: the true error stands above a fixed share
of the rate and below a fixed multiple of it, so the
rate and the error grow and shrink together, and no
smaller rate names the error, and no larger one is
needed to name it. A sharp rate is a price with no slack left in
the naming — the size is right, even when the exact
number is not given.
Builds on: expectation (1.3.131); vector and distance
(1.3.7); estimator and consistency (1.3.133).
# Foundation: the proximal step and the Moreau envelope — the nearest point under a price

Some rules do not ask for the nearest allowed point;
they ask for the point that stands best under a price.
The PROXIMAL step (1.3.152) of a price at a point x is
the point that makes two costs small together: the
price itself, and half the square of the distance
from x —
the point that pays a little of the price to stay near
x, and stays near x to pay a little of the price. When
the price is 'inside the set, or nothing', the proximal
step is the projection (1.3.146); in general it is
projection under a price. The MOREAU ENVELOPE of a
price at x is the smallest total of those two costs
over all points: the price, smoothed by the
standing-near cost, read as a number. The envelope is
the bill of the proximal step, and it is smooth where
the price itself may not be.
Builds on: projection onto a convex set (1.3.146);
function, variable, input, output (1.3.2); vector and
distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: the Poincare inequality — a spread bound from a slope bound

A law of noise is tame, in the sense this foundation
names, when the spread of any smooth reading of the
noise is bounded by a fixed multiple of the mean
square of the slope of that reading. That bound is the
POINCARE INEQUALITY for the law, and the fixed
multiple is its constant, written C_P. The bell law
(1.3.138) is the standing example of a tame law. A law
that is tame in this sense lets the tools built for
the bell law travel: bounds proved by watching slopes
still hold, with the constant of the law standing
where the constant of the bell law stood.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8); the
bell law (1.3.138); expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: tangent cone and statistical dimension — the width of a set at a point

Stand at a point of a convex set and ask which
directions a step from the point can take while a
small enough step stays in the set. The set of those
directions, closed up with the ends they run to, is
the TANGENT CONE of the set at the point (1.3.127):
the set, read from one of its points, as a cone of
ways out that are not yet ways off. The STATISTICAL
DIMENSION of a cone is the mean of the square of the
distance from a draw of the bell law to its projection
onto the cone: one number for the size of a cone,
counting a direction by how much of a plain draw falls
along it. A narrow cone has a small dimension; the
whole space has dimension N. The width of a set at a
point is priced in this currency.
Builds on: cone (1.3.127); projection onto a convex
set (1.3.146); the bell law (1.3.138); expectation
(1.3.131).
# Foundation: the monotone cone — a set cut out by an order

A list of numbers is MONOTONE when it never falls as
the places run: each number stands at or above the one
before it. The set of all monotone lists of a given
length is a convex set — the MONOTONE CONE — cut out
by the rules 'the next number stands at or above this
one', one rule for each step between places. A signal
in the cone is a signal that only rises, by steps of
any size, at any places. The least squares estimator
for this set, on the stage of 1.3.145, is the isotonic
(1.3.153) rule: the projection of the seen list onto
the cone — the nearest rising list to what was seen.
Builds on: the sequence model (1.3.145); projection
onto a convex set (1.3.146); set and element (1.3.3).
Defined word: proximal — of a step that takes, of all
points, the one that makes a price and half the
square of the distance to a given point smallest together:
the nearest point under a price, where a plain
projection is the nearest point under no price but a
set. First spent at Paper 012 (Chapter 19), for the
surrogate estimator that stands beside a projection
and is priced in its place.
Defined word: isotonic — of a rule, or of a fitted
list, that never falls as the places run: at each
step the number stands at or above the one before
it. The word names the rising shape itself, wherever
it is met. First spent at Paper 012 (Chapter 19),
for the least squares rule that fits a rising list
to a seen list.
Defined word: ellipsoid (also: ellipsoids) — a ball
stretched by a different amount along each of its
directions: the set of points whose weighed squares of
distances along those directions add to no more than
one. A ball is the case where every direction is
stretched alike. First spent at Paper 012
(Chapter 19), for the sets whose sharp rates the
paper reads first.
Defined word: variational — of a claim, or of a
price, stated as the largest, or the smallest, value
that a named quantity takes as one of its parts runs
over its allowed range: the claim is the value at
the best point, not the value at a named point.
First spent at Paper 012 (Chapter 19), for the older
characterization of the sharp rate, stated as a
maximizer and true for every set at once.
# Foundation: cross-validation — judging a fit on cases it did not train on

A model is fit on some cases and used on others. To
say how well it will do on cases not yet seen, hold
some of the cases in hand back from the fitting, fit
on the rest, and score the fit on the held-back
cases: those scores were earned on cases the fit
never trained on, so they speak of new cases, not of
memory. Do this by turns — split the data into a
training part and a validation part again and again,
each time fitting on the first and scoring on the
second — and average the held-out scores. The average
is the cross-validation estimate of the model's
performance, and the whole way of judging is
cross-validation.

Builds on: a sample, and independence (1.3.130);
estimator and consistency (1.3.133).
# Foundation: a split, a fold, and a partition — the dealing of the turns

One turn of cross-validation (1.3.156) is a SPLIT of
the data into two parts: a training set, which the
model is fit on, and a validation set, the rest,
which it is scored on. The share of the data in the
training set is the training fraction. A way of
making the splits, with its training fraction and
its count of splits, is a DESIGN.

One design deals the splits as a PARTITION: the data
are cut into K equal parts, called FOLDS, and each
fold takes one turn as the validation set while the
other folds train. That is K-fold cross-validation.
Leave-one-out is its end: every case takes one turn
alone as the validation set. Other designs draw each
split on its own (Monte Carlo), or draw the training
set by sampling the data with replacement, so a case
can stand in it more than once (the bootstrap).

Builds on: cross-validation (1.3.156); set and
element (1.3.3).
# Foundation: influence — how much one case moves a number

A number is computed from many cases. The influence
of one case on the number is how much the number
moves when that case moves — read, in this phext, by
holding the case at a value and averaging everything
else away: the influence is the part of the expected value
of the number that the one case accounts for. A case
can stand in more than one place in a computation,
and then it has one influence for each place, and
they need not agree: the case that raises a reading
when it is scored can lower it when it helps train
the fit that does the scoring.

The spread of an influence over the cases — how
unevenly the moving is dealt among them — is a
variance of its own, and two influences of the same
case can lean together or against each other, which
their co-movement keeps.

Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8);
expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: joint effects, split by the sets that carry them

A number computed from many cases can be taken apart
by asking, for each set of cases, what part of the
number stands on exactly those cases together. For
one case: what knowing that case changes in the
expected value. For two: what knowing both changes,
beyond what each changes alone. For larger sets, the
same subtraction, carried up: each part is the whole
effect of its set, with every effect of every smaller
set inside it taken away first. The parts on
different sets do not lean on each other, and the
number is the sum of its parts over all sets that are not empty,
added to its expected value.

This taking-apart is Hoeffding's, of 1948. It lets a
variance be read set size by set size: the part the
single cases carry, the part the pairs carry, and so
on — and lets a claim name the order up to which it
has read.

Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8);
set and element (1.3.3).
# Foundation: pair scores — judging by the order of two cases

Some scores are not paid to one case at a time. When
each case is marked as one of two kinds — a case, in
the paper's word, or a control — a score can be paid
to a PAIR, one of each kind, for the order the model
puts them in: the model's number for the case stands
above its number for the control, or below, or level.
Averaged over all such pairs, the share paid in full,
with half paid on a level standing, is the AREA
UNDER THE CURVE — the curve being the trade, as a
cutoff runs, between cases rightly raised and
controls wrongly raised. The unit of the judging is
then the pair, not the case, and everything counted
for the judging — how often a unit is judged, what
share it carries — must be counted in pairs.

Builds on: classifier and its report card (1.3.38);
average, spread, distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: the bell limit — an average ends at the bell law

Add many small, independent moves of a number, none
of them large beside the whole, and the spread of
the sum takes one shape as the count grows: the bell
law (1.3.138), centered at the expected
value of the number, with a spread that a named variance sets. The
coming to rest is the bell limit — the central limit
theorem, in the words of the field. It is what lets a
variance become an INTERVAL: a reading plus or minus
about twice its standard error covers the settled
value about 19 times in 20, by the shape
of the bell. The limit needs its guards: one moving
that outweighs the rest, or moves that lean hard on
each other, can keep the shape from coming to rest, and a
claim of the limit names the guards it stands on.

Builds on: the bell law (1.3.138); average, spread,
distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: a proof state and a tactic — the working surface of a formal proof

A formal proof is built in steps, and between the
steps the work stands in a state. A proof state
says two things: what remains to be shown — the
goals, one or more claims that still lack a proof —
and what is at hand: the givens, the names, the
lemmas already proved on the way. A tactic is one
move made on a state: it can close a goal, split
one goal into several, or change a goal into an
easier one, and what it leaves behind is the next
state. The proof is done when no goals remain. A
tactic only proposes; the finished chain of moves
is a proof term, and the proof kernel (1.2.41)
checks the term, not the tactics that found it —
so a bad tactic can waste time but cannot make a
false claim pass.

Builds on: state machine (1.3.85); propositions
as types (1.2.40).
# Foundation: Monte Carlo search — choosing by sampled tries

Some spaces are too wide to walk in order. A
Monte Carlo search walks them by sampling: try a
move, see what comes back, keep a score of what
each kind of try has earned, and let the scores
steer where the next tries go — more tries where
the returns promise, some tries always where the
scores are still thin, so the search does not lock
onto the first road that paid. The name comes
from the casino: the method bets, and lets the tally of
many small bets stand in for a map it does not
have. What it needs is a way to score a try
quickly and a way to stop; what it gives up is any
promise that the best road was found — only that
the roads taken were chosen by evidence the search
itself gathered.

Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8);
a sample (1.3.130).
# Foundation: an olympiad — contest problems as a measuring stick

An olympiad, in the use of this phext, is a set of
contest problems set for the strongest young
mathematicians of a year — new problems, written
to be solved in hours with pencil and paper, hard
enough that a perfect paper is rare. Because the
problems are new each year and the standard of
their judging is old and shared, an olympiad is a
measuring stick as much as a contest: to say a
person, or a system, reached the gold line of a
year's olympiad is to say what it did against
problems nobody had trained on, at a height the
field agrees on. The stick has a grain: olympiad
problems are short to state and prize the invented
step over the long computation, so the stick
measures one kind of strength well and others —
endurance, scholarship, the slow building of
theory — hardly at all.

Builds on: everyday notions only.
# Foundation: a deductive database — a store that closes itself under its rules

A deductive database is a store of facts with a
set of rules laid over it. A rule says: when facts
of these shapes stand in the store, this further
fact may be added. To run the store is to close
it: fire every rule whose shapes are matched, add
what they give, and fire again on the grown store,
until nothing new can be added — the store is
saturated. A question is answered by asking
whether its fact stands in the closed store. The
store can also keep measures beside its facts —
numbers carried in tables and updated by plain
arithmetic — and let the rules ask the tables what
is true of the figure in numbers, while the
deduction works in shapes. What the store cannot
do is take a fact back: the closing only grows,
so a wrong fact or a wrong rule poisons everything
drawn from it.

Builds on: state machine (1.3.85).
# Foundation: sameness — when two things count as one

Every count, every fold, every claim that two
roads reached the same place, leans on a rule of
sameness: a stated test that says when two things
count as one. The test can be exact agreement of
the written form; it can be agreement of the parts
that matter, with the rest let go; it can be
agreement only up to a change that is itself
ruled. Two dangers stand on either side. A test
too strict counts one thing as many, and the work
wastes itself doing the same step under many
names. A test too loose counts unlike things as
one, and what is proved of one is quietly spent on
the other — the worse failure, because nothing
announces it. A sameness used inside an argument
is part of the argument: it must be able to be stated, and
its loose corners are the loose corners of the
argument.

Builds on: the two equalities (1.2.43).
# Defined word: autoformalization (also: autoformalize, autoformalized)
Autoformalization is the turning of mathematics written in natural language into statements in a formal language, by a machine rather than by a human hand. The prose says what it says the way people say it, with steps skipped and meanings carried by habit. The formal statement must say all of it in the strict forms of the formal system, so that a kernel can check proofs of it. The hard part is not the writing but the keeping: a formal statement can be well formed and still say less than the prose, or other than the prose, and only a reader of both tongues can tell.
# Defined word: counterexample (also: counterexamples)
A counterexample is a case that shows a claim false by standing against it. The claim says every thing of a kind has a mark. The counterexample is one thing of the kind, shown plainly, that has not got the mark. One is enough, and no count of cases that fit the claim can outweigh it. To give a counterexample is the cleanest way to end a claim: not an argument about the claim, but the thing itself, standing there.
# Defined word: derangement (also: derangements)
A derangement is a dealing of things back to their owners in which no owner gets their own thing back. Five letters are put in five paper covers addressed to their writers, one letter to a cover, and the dealing is a derangement when every cover holds the wrong letter. The word names any placing of a set in which nothing stands at its own place.
# Defined word: subgraph (also: subgraphs)
A subgraph is a part of a graph that is itself a graph: some of the points of the larger graph, some of the lines that join them, with no line kept whose ends were not kept. To name a subgraph is to say the whole answer is standing inside the larger web, if the right part is cut out and the rest let go.
# Defined word: contamination
Contamination is the quiet presence of the test inside the training. A system is trained on a great body of material, and somewhere in the body stand the very problems it will later be measured on, or near copies of them. Its measured strength then prices two things at once — what it can do, and what it has seen — and no reading of the score alone can split the two. The word names the leak, not a charge: whether it happened is a question of what the training body held, and it can only be answered by looking.
# Defined word: divisor (also: divisors)
A divisor of a whole number is a smaller whole number that goes into it with nothing left over. Twelve has divisors two, three, four and six, because each goes into twelve evenly. A divisor that is smaller than the number itself is a proper divisor. The word is old in the schools; it is defined here because the archive of the phext did not carry it, and the problems of Paper 014 turn on it.
# Foundation: oscillator and mode — a holder at a level

An oscillator, in the plain sense this phext needs, is one
holder in a system of holders: it stands at a level — its
energy — and it can trade what it holds with the others it
is coupled to. A mode is one of the standing ways such a
system can be filled: the m-th mode is the level E_m, and
the system's state is the list of how much stands at each
mode. Classical here means the holders trade in smooth
amounts, not in steps: any amount can move. The word
frequency work of Paper 015 stands on this picture: its
words are the holders, and their frequencies are what
stands at the levels.

Builds on: level and number — the ordinary words;
invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: occupation — how much stands at a level

The occupation of a level is the average amount of the
system that stands there: if the system's whole is a
probability of one, the occupation of mode m, written
rho_m, is the average share of that whole found at mode m,
and the occupation of every mode adds, over the modes, to
the whole. If the system's whole is a count — of tokens,
of households — the occupation is the average count at the
level. An equilibrium law is a rule that gives every level
its occupation: the Rayleigh-Jeans law (1.3.177) is one
such rule.

Builds on: average and distribution (1.3.8); oscillator
and mode (1.3.173).
# Foundation: thermal equilibrium — settled, under fixed quantities

A system stands in thermal equilibrium when its state has
stopped changing in the large: parts still trade, but the
occupation (1.3.174) of every level stands still, because
every trade is answered by its reverse. A system driven by
its own couplings comes toward this state when the
quantities it must keep are held fixed — total energy, and
total count of what fills the levels. The coming to rest
itself is thermalization (1.3.183). This is a stronger and
more narrow word than equilibrium (1.3.29), which names
any balance of forces that holds a state where it is:
thermal equilibrium is the one balance a whole
distribution comes to rest in, and it is reached, not
struck.

Builds on: equilibrium (1.3.29); occupation (1.3.174);
invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: temperature and chemical potential — the two prices of an equilibrium

When an equilibrium is described by the quantities it
keeps, two prices appear in place of the quantities. The
temperature is the price of energy: it says how much
occupation a level buys per unit of energy at the going
rate — higher temperature, more even spreading over high
levels. The chemical potential is the price of count: it
says what one more filled level costs the system, and it
stands below the ground level in the cases this phext
meets. The two prices and the two quantities carry the
same information: fix the quantities and the prices are
whatever they must be for the sums to come out; fix the
prices and the quantities follow. The Rayleigh-Jeans law
(1.3.177) is written in the prices.

Builds on: thermal equilibrium (1.3.175); occupation
(1.3.174).
# Foundation: the Rayleigh-Jeans law — the classical equilibrium occupation

The classical answer for the occupation of a level in an
equilibrium: at a level of energy E, the occupation is rho
= T/(E - mu) — the temperature over the level's height
above the chemical potential (1.3.176). Low levels hold
heavy shares, high levels thin ones, and the fall is the
fall of one over the height: slower than any exponential.
It is the law of classical physics, the answer before the
quantum correction that caps the high levels; for systems
of classical holders trading smoothly — light in fibers at
the powers used, coupled oscillators, and, by the claim of
Paper 015, words in books — it is the equilibrium itself.
Its pile-up at the lowest levels at low total energy is
the condensation of 1.3.178.

Builds on: temperature and chemical potential (1.3.176);
oscillator and mode (1.3.173).
# Foundation: condensation — a finite share at the ground

In the statistical sense: a system condenses when a finite
fraction of its whole — not a trace, a share that survives
as the system grows — collects at the lowest level or
levels, instead of spreading over the ladder as the
temperature prices it. The collected part is the
condensate. In the Rayleigh-Jeans equilibrium (1.3.177)
condensation comes when the total energy is squeezed low:
the upper levels can hold only so much at the going
temperature, and the rest of the norm has nowhere to stand
but the ground. The word is older in the quantum house
(the Bose-Einstein condensate is its famous kin), but the
classical field condenses too, by arithmetic, not by
quantum steps.

Builds on: the Rayleigh-Jeans law (1.3.177); thermal
equilibrium (1.3.175).
# Foundation: density of states — how crowded the ladder is

The density of states of a spectrum says how many levels
stand in each stretch of energy: crowd a stretch with
levels and the density there is high; thin them and it
falls. A constant density spaces the levels evenly up the
ladder. A falling density — fewer levels per stretch as
the energy rises — is the shape of a system whose high
states are rare: there is less room at the top, whatever
stands there. The density is a property of the ladder, not
of what fills it; the equilibrium law (1.3.177) prices the
filling of whatever ladder it is given.

Builds on: oscillator and mode (1.3.173); growth rates
(1.3.93).
# Foundation: the Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient — inequality as one picture and one number

Order the holders of a thing from least to richest. The
Lorenz curve plots, against the cumulative share of
holders counted from the least, the cumulative share of
the thing they hold: the share of the bottom tenth, the
share of the bottom half, and so on to the whole. Perfect
equality draws the diagonal; the more the curve drops
below it, the more unequal the holding. The Gini
coefficient is the sag made a number: twice the area
between the diagonal and the curve, standing between 0
(perfect equality) and 1 (one holder holds all). The pair
come from the study of wealth, and travel wherever the
inequality of a distribution is to be read at a glance and
matched with one number.

Builds on: average, spread, percentile, distribution
(1.3.8).
# Foundation: the Pareto distribution — the count above a size

The Pareto curve of a holding counts downward: for each
size w, it gives the fraction of holders standing at that
size or above. For many systems of wealth and size, over a
middle range of sizes this fraction falls like a power of
the size — the count halves when the size doubles, in the
exponent-one case — and the curve is then called the
Pareto distribution. The power range has edges in honest
data: the curve is a tail and a middle, not a law of the
whole ladder, and what it does at its edges is part of its
reading (1.2.169).

Builds on: the Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient
(1.3.180); growth rates and power laws (1.3.93).
# Foundation: Zipf's law — frequency falls like one over rank

In a text, order the different words by how often they
stand in it, most common first, and give each its rank: 1
for the most common, 2 for the next. Zipf's law is the
observed shape of that ordering: the frequency of a word
falls about like one over its rank — the most common word
stands about twice as often as the second, about three
times as often as the third, and so on down the
vocabulary. It is one of the oldest measured shapes in the
study of language, and its standing in this phext is as a
curve to be explained, not an explanation: Paper 015 reads
it as the middle stretch of a thermal distribution
(1.2.169).

Builds on: corpus and feature (1.3.37); the Pareto
distribution (1.3.181).
# Defined word: thermalization

thermalization — the coming to rest of a system in its
thermal equilibrium (1.3.175) under its own couplings: the
parts trade under fixed quantities until the occupation of
every level stands still, and the state so reached is the
thermal state. A word of Paper 015 (Chapter 22), where the
coming to rest of words in a book is the paper's
hypothesis (1.2.159).
# Defined word: hapax

hapax — a word that stands exactly once in a whole work.
In large works the class of such words is the largest
single class of the vocabulary: about half of a book's
different words, standing once each. A word of the trade
of counting words, carried into this phext by Paper 015
(Chapter 22), where the hapax stand at the ground of the
spectrum (1.2.164) and make its condensate (1.2.165).
# Foundation: category — things, ways, and the following of ways
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3); graph (1.3.41).

A category is a family gathered by one rule: a
set of things, called its objects, and, for
every two things, a set of ways from the first
to the second. A way is called a morphism (the
word is defined at 1.3.189). Ways can be
followed: when a way runs from A to B and a way
runs from B to C, the two together count as a
way from A to C, and the grouping of a chain of
ways does not change the way the chain lands.
Every thing has the way that does nothing — the
way from the thing to itself that, followed by
any way, leaves that way as it stands.
A category is near kin to a graph (1.3.41):
things for the points, ways for the lines. The
one added rule is the following of ways — in a
category, two lines in a row are themselves a
line, and the keeping of that rule is the
whole of the ground floor of the subject.
# Foundation: topology — the open sets of a space, and its interior and closure
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3); neighborhood (1.3.17).

A topology on a set of points is a choice of
which parts of the set count as open, made under
three rules: the whole set and the empty part
are open; the joining of any family of open
parts is open; and the part two open parts share
is open. The choice is the topology; the set
with its choice is a space.
The interior of a part is the largest open part
that lies inside it: the points that have room
around them inside the part — an open part of
their own, however small, that stays within it.
A part is closed when the part outside it is
open. The closure of a part is the smallest
closed part that holds it: the part itself,
together with every point that no open set
around it can clear of the part.
# Foundation: modal logic — must and may, the box and the diamond
Builds on: types and terms (1.3.73); context and judgment (1.3.74).

A modal logic is a logic of propositions that
carries two added operators. The box, written
before a proposition, reads as: the proposition
must hold — it holds in every case the model
counts as standing open from here. The diamond
reads as: the proposition may hold — it holds
in some case that stands open. What counts as
standing open is the model's to give: in the
topological reading (1.3.186) the cases are the
points of a space, and the box is the interior;
in the categorical reading (1.3.185) the
propositions are things of a category and their
proofs are ways. The two operators are twins:
the diamond of p is the denial of the box of
the denial of p, in every reading in this study.
A modal logic is called normal when it holds
the axiom named K (the axiom and what it buys
stand at 1.2.172) together with the rule that
what is proved holds under the box: a proof of
p, with no givens, gives the box of p.
# Foundation: the tenses — henceforth, eventually, historically, once
Builds on: modal logic (1.3.187).

Stand on a line of times, one time at a time.
Four operators read a proposition along the
line. Henceforth: the proposition holds at
every later time — the box of 1.3.187, read
along the future. Eventually: it holds at some
later time — the diamond, read along the
future. Historically: it holds at every earlier
time — the box read along the past. Once: it
held at some earlier time — the diamond read
along the past.
The four are the working operators of the
linear temporal logic, the modal logic of a
line of times; the policy work of Study S007
writes its norms with them (1.2.175).
# Defined word: morphism (also: morphisms)
A morphism is one of the ways of a category (1.3.185): a step from one thing of the category to another thing, of the kind that can be followed by a second such step to make a third. The word is the working name the field gives its ways. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the table of Study S007 turns on it: in that table, a proof is a morphism.
# Defined word: functor (also: functors)
A functor is a map from one category to another that keeps the ways whole: every thing goes to a thing, every way goes to a way, a followed way goes to the following of the images of its parts, and the way that does nothing goes to the way that does nothing. The word names the kind of map by which one system is read inside another without breaking its steps. It is defined here because the archive carried only the plural, and Axiom K (1.2.172) turns on the singular.
# Defined word: coproduct
A coproduct is the sum of two things in a category, written A + B: the mirror twin of the product A x B. The product is built so that a way into it is a pair of ways, one into each part; the coproduct is built so that a way out of it is a pair of ways, one out of each part. Where the product holds both parts at once, the coproduct holds either part, with both standing ready. The word is the name the table of Study S007 gives the reading of 'or' (1.2.171).
# Defined word: intuitionistic
Intuitionistic marks the constructive reading of logic: a claim stands only when a proof of it stands built. Under this reading, 'there is' asks for the thing itself shown, not for the clash that denying it would bring; and a claim and its double denial are not the same claim. The word names the logic of the table of Study S007, stated exactly in a footnote of the source (1.2.171).
# Defined word: bicartesian
Bicartesian marks a category that carries both forms at once: the product and the coproduct for every pair of its things, and closure besides — for any two things, the exponential, the thing of all ways from the one to the other, also stands among its things. The word is Cook's exact name, in a footnote, for the kind of category his table pairs with the intuitionistic calculus (1.2.171).
# Foundation: affine connection — the rule for carrying a vector Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9); tensor (1.3.113).

An affine connection is a rule that says, at every point
of a space, how a vector standing at that point is to be
carried to a near point without turning: the carried
vector at the new point is the one the rule calls 'the
same direction'. The rule is written as a table of numbers
at each point — one for the carrying along each direction,
of each part of the vector — and from the rule alone three
measures are read off: how much a carried vector turns
around a closed loop (1.3.195), how much two carried steps
fail to close their figure (1.3.196), and how much a
carried vector grows or shrinks (1.3.197). A connection is
a thing of its own: a space can carry many connections,
and choosing one is not the same act as choosing the rule
of lengths. When a length rule and a connection are both
given, the one connection the length rule alone fixes —
the one with no twist and no growth — is called its own
connection, and every other connection is that one plus a
twist part and a growth part.
# Foundation: curvature — the turning of a carried vector around a loop Builds on: affine connection (1.3.194).

Carry a vector around a small closed loop by the rule of a
connection, never turning it on the way. When it comes
back to its starting point it may stand turned against the
vector that stayed home. That turning — how much, and in
which plane, for each small loop at each point — is the
curvature of the connection. A connection with no
curvature anywhere is called flat: on it, carrying is the
same along every path between two near points, and the
connection can be written wholly as the turning of a
frame. Curvature is the measure Einstein's theory of
gravity is made of: the metric fixes a connection, and its
curvature, summed in the one way that gives a single
number at each point, is the scalar the action of that
theory integrates.
# Foundation: torsion — the gap of a carried parallelogram Builds on: affine connection (1.3.194).

Take two small steps from a point, in two directions.
Carry the first step along the second, and the second step
along the first, by the rule of a connection. In a
well-behaved geometry the two carried steps end at the
same far corner, and the figure closes. The failure of the
figure to close — the gap between its two far corners — is
the torsion of the connection. A connection can be flat
and still twist in this way, and a whole theory of gravity
can be built on the twist with no curvature anywhere. The
word is an old one in this phext with a different burden:
the torsion of a homology group (1.2.38) is the part of
the group that dies under enough turns of itself, an
algebra thing. The two uses of the word share a name and a
rough picture of turning, and nothing else; each use in
this phext is read in its own house.
# Foundation: non-metricity — the growth of a carried vector Builds on: affine connection (1.3.194); dimension and manifold (1.3.9).

A metric is the rule that gives a vector its length. Carry
a vector by the rule of a connection, and ask whether its
length, as the metric reads it at the new point, is the
length it had. The rate at which the carried length
changes — the failure of the carrying to keep the rule of
the metric — is the non-metricity of the connection,
written as the derivative the connection takes of the
metric itself. A connection with no non-metricity is
called metric-compatible: lengths and angles survive its
carrying. A connection can be flat and twist-free and
still grow lengths in this way, and a whole theory of
gravity can be built on the growth with no curvature and
no torsion anywhere — the third of the three geometries of
Paper 016.
# Foundation: frame — a set of axes at every point Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9); tensor (1.3.113).

A frame is a choice, at every point of a space, of a full
set of axes — as many axes as the space has directions,
none of them lying in the flat span of the others. From a
frame the rule of lengths is rebuilt: the length of a
vector is read off its parts along the axes, so the metric
is the frame read twice over. A frame carries more than
the metric does: the metric cannot tell one turning of the
axes from another, while the frame can, and that extra
telling is a freedom of the description — at each point
the axes may be turned together and the lengths stand as
they were. The field of Paper 016 works with frames of
four axes in spacetime, and its name for the frame, the
German word for four legs, is defined at 1.3.204. Frames
are the natural home of the fields that turn by halves
(1.3.202), which cannot be written in coordinates alone.
# Foundation: boundary term — the part of an action that lives at the edge Builds on: boundary of a manifold (1.3.23); affine connection (1.3.194).

The action of a field theory is an integral over a region,
and the equations of the theory come from asking that the
action stand still under small changes of the fields that
are held fixed at the edge of the region. A boundary term
is a part of the action whose whole value is set by the
fields at the edge alone — written, in the body of the
region, as a total derivative, a term that is the
derivative of something and so adds up to its edge values
only. A boundary term changes no equation in the body of
the region. It is not therefore nothing: it decides
whether the asking is well put when the fields at the edge
are what is held, it carries quantities like the entropy
of a black hole, and two actions that differ by a boundary
term, bent by the same non-linear function, stop being the
same theory — the term that sat quiet inside the sum wakes
inside the bending. In Paper 016 the three writings of one
theory of gravity differ from each other by boundary terms
alone, and the whole equivalence stands on this
foundation.
# Foundation: gauge freedom — a choice the physics does not see Builds on: affine connection (1.3.194).

A gauge freedom is a way of changing the description of a
field — its numbers, its axes, its coordinates — that
changes nothing the field does: the same physics in other
words. The turns of a frame at each point are a gauge
freedom where only lengths are physical; the choice of
coordinates is a gauge freedom where only the geometry is
physical. A freedom is spent by fixing a gauge: choosing
one description out of each family of equal ones, so that
counting can begin. The count that matters is what is left
after the spending: the degrees of freedom (1.3.201). Two
warnings belong to the foundation. A symmetry may hold
only up to a boundary term (1.3.199) — the change moves
the action by an edge term alone — and such a symmetry
spends freedom as surely as an exact one, while being
easier to lose when the action is bent. And a gauge fixing
made inside a theory whose symmetry is smaller than the
fixer thinks can throw away whole families of solutions:
the fixing is a claim about the symmetry, and a false
claim deletes physics.
# Foundation: degrees of freedom — how many motions a field truly has Builds on: gauge freedom (1.3.200).

The degrees of freedom of a field are the count of its
independent motions: how many numbers at a point can be
set freely at one time and still grow by the own equations
of the field. The count is never the count of the written
numbers of the field. From those, subtract what every
gauge freedom (1.3.200) spends — each freedom lets one
written number be chosen by the one describing instead of
by the field — and subtract again what is spent by the
equations that bind the field at a single time rather than
grow it. What is left is the count of waves and modes the
field truly carries. A mode that enters the count with the
wrong sign in its action is a ghost: it carries the energy
the wrong way, and a theory with a ghost among its free
motions is sick unless an added symmetry spends the ghost
away. In Paper 016 the counts are the certificates of the
theories: two for each of the three equivalent writings of
gravity, and more — priced one by one — for their
extensions.
# Foundation: fermion and boson — the two kinds of matter field Builds on: tensor (1.3.113).

Matter fields come in two kinds, told apart by how they
answer a full turning. A boson answers a turning of two
full circles by standing as it was — it stands so already
after one. A fermion must be turned twice to stand as it
was: after one full turning it stands as its own negative.
The fields of light and of the forces are bosons; the
fields of the stuff of things — the electron and its kin —
are fermions. The half-answer of the fermion has a price:
a fermion cannot be written in coordinates alone, but only
against a frame (1.3.198), and so a fermion feels the
turning of frames directly, where a boson feels only the
lengths. In the language of Paper 016, fermions carry
hypermomentum (1.3.208) and bosons do not, and that one
difference decides which of the three geometries of
gravity can take matter in without repair.
# Defined word: teleparallel (also: teleparallelism) A theory of gravity is teleparallel when its connection is flat — carrying is the same along every path — and the work of gravity is done by the twist or the growth of what is carried, not by curvature. The word joins the Greek for far with the word for the carrying that keeps the direction of a vector: the geometry of a space whose far points are compared by a rule that never turns. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and Paper 016 names two of its three geometries with it: the leg of torsion (1.2.179) and the leg of non-metricity (1.2.180) are both teleparallel, and the first leg (1.2.178) is not.
# Defined word: vierbein (also: vierbeins) The vierbein is the frame (1.3.198) of a spacetime: four axes at every point, from which the metric is built. The word is German — four legs — and the field of Paper 016 keeps it beside the plain word frame because its books and papers are written in it. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the second leg of the trinity is most often told in this word: the vierbein is the field of that leg, and the turning of the vierbein at each point is its gauge freedom.
# Defined word: contortion The contortion is the part of a connection that is built from its torsion alone: the twist part of the difference between a general connection and the connection the metric fixes (1.3.194). Where the torsion is zero the contortion is zero, and the connection stands apart from that one only by its growth part (1.3.206). The word is the name the field gives for the turning-by-twist of the carrying rule. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the affine stage of Paper 016 (1.2.177) is stated as a sum in which the contortion is one of the two added parts.
# Defined word: disformation The disformation is the part of a connection that is built from its non-metricity alone: the growth part of the difference between a general connection and the connection the metric fixes (1.3.194). Where the non-metricity is zero the disformation is zero, and the connection stands apart from that one only by its twist part (1.3.205). The word is the name the field gives for the turning-by-growth of the carrying rule. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the affine stage of Paper 016 (1.2.177) is stated as a sum in which the disformation is one of the two added parts.
# Defined word: coincident Coincident, in the field of Paper 016, names a gauge: the choice of coordinates in which the flat, twist-free connection of the third leg is zero at every point, because the coordinates are taken to be the very fields that fix the connection. In that gauge the starting point of the coordinates and the point where a body stands are the same point — they coincide — and the theory written there is called Coincident General Relativity. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry the word in this burden, and the cleanest form of the third leg (1.2.180) cannot be stated without it.
# Defined word: hypermomentum The hypermomentum of a matter field is the answer its action gives to a change of the connection itself, as the energy-momentum is its answer to a change of the metric. A field with no hypermomentum never feels the connection — only the lengths — and a field that carries it feels the twist and growth of the carrying rule directly. The fermions (1.3.202) carry hypermomentum through the twisting part of the torsion; the bosons do not. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the telling apart of the three couplings of the trinity (1.2.185) is priced in this word.
# Defined word: aliased Aliased, in the work of Study S008, names a task as a learner meets it: shown under a name drawn for its run alone, hashed from the true name and a salt the run keeps, so that the name carries no road to the rule. A learner that keeps its work under aliased names keeps it bound to its own run. The word is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the guard against lookup of Study S008 (1.2.196) is stated in it.
# Defined word: dormancy Dormancy is the resting of a task between its spells in a lifelong stream (1.2.187): the stretch of other work while the task is not served, its length drawn from a wide bell on a log scale. A task in dormancy is not gone and not done; the stream will serve it again, and what a learner kept for it is priced when the return comes. The word is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the shape of the stream of Study S008 turns on it.
# Foundation: natural deduction — proof built forward from what is in hand
Builds on: a proof state and a tactic (1.3.162); types and terms (1.3.73).
Natural deduction is the style of proof that works
forward. It begins from hypotheses in hand. Its
rules make new statements from old ones standing in
hand. A statement made under an assumption is sealed into an implication
when the room closes on the assumption — set aside, its work done, as the room
of 1.2.202 is closed. The proof is done when the
goal stands among the things made. It is the style
the crafting game of Study S009 teaches, and the
style the tactic state of 1.3.162 is worked in.
# Foundation: machine code — the instructions the processor itself carries out
Machine code is a program in the form the processor
itself reads: a list of instructions, each naming one
small operation — move these bytes, add these numbers,
jump if a flag stands — out of the fixed menu, the
instruction set, that a given processor understands.
Source text is written for people and turned into
machine code by a compiler (1.3.213). What runs on a
machine is always machine code; every claim about a
running program is, at the floor, a claim about
machine code, whether it is stated there or not.
# Foundation: compiler — the program that turns source text into machine code
Builds on: machine code (1.3.212).
A compiler is a program that takes source text — a
program written in a language shaped for people — and
produces machine code (1.3.212) that does what the
source says. The compiling is itself a computation,
and like any computation it can be wrong: a compiler
may misread its source, or write machine code that
does other than the source lays down, and nothing in
the act of compiling checks that it did not. To trust
a compiled program on the ground of its source is
therefore to trust the compiler as well; a proof about
the machine code itself (1.2.214) is the way to owe
the compiler nothing.
# Defined word: refinement
Refinement, in the work of Paper 017, names a
carrying of claims from a model to the thing the model
stands for: the binary is a refinement of the model
when every run of the binary is explained by a run of
the model, so that what is proved of the model holds
of the binary. The word is defined here because the
archive of this phext did not carry it, and the bridge
of Paper 017 (1.2.217) is stated in it.
# Defined word: homomorphism
A homomorphism is a map between two structures that
keeps their workings: map first and then work, or work
first and then map — the two land at the same place.
The word is Greek — same shape. It is defined here
because the archive of this phext did not carry it,
and the bridge from model terms to bytes of Paper 017
(1.2.216) is one.
# Defined word: prekey (also: prekeys)
A prekey is a key published before it is needed, held
by a server or by the other party against the day a
conversation opens, so that the one who opens it can
compute a shared secret with a party who is not there
to compute it with them. The word is defined here
because the archive of this phext did not carry it,
and the handshake of Paper 017 (1.2.219) is built of
bundles of them.
# Defined word: vacuity
Vacuity is the emptiness of a claim whose conditions
can never be met: a statement that holds of everything
because it is ever asked of nothing. A theorem about a
specification that no run can satisfy is empty in this
way, and worth nothing. The word is defined here
because the archive of this phext did not carry it,
and the testing of a specification (1.2.208) hunts it
by name.
# Defined word: integrator
The integrator, in the work of Paper 017, is the one
member of the prover team (1.2.210) who alone may
commit the work of the team — the single hand through
which every landed change passes, so that no two
changes ever land at once. The word is defined here
because the archive of this phext did not carry it in
this burden, and the machine of the team (27.1.2)
turns on the role.
# Foundation: undefined behaviour — the acts a language's meaning does not price
Some acts in a program have no meaning under the rules of its
language: the documents of the language leave what follows
from them unpriced, and any outcome is allowed, outcomes no
reader of the code would look for among them. Such an act is
called undefined behaviour. A semantics that names most such
acts in the open — as the semantics of Chapter 28 does for C —
turns traps that stood silent into cases that stand named: the
code can be read, run and reasoned about with the trap in
view.
Builds on: specification and test (1.3.87).
# Defined word: elaboration
Elaboration, in the work of Chapter 28, names the carrying of
a program from the language it was written in into a simpler
core language under a semantics, as Cerberus carries C into
Core: the shapes of the source are rewritten into the few
shapes of the core, and the meaning is kept by the rules of
the carrying. The word is defined here because the carrying,
and what it loses (1.2.226), carries weight in that work.
# Defined word: autoverification (also: autoverify)
Autoverification, in the work of Chapter 28, names the aim of
Math, Inc.: the verifying of software by agents working under
shared semantic models and engineered specifications (1.2.223,
1.2.224), held as one practice — the stating of what the
software must do, the validating of the statements, and the
proving, carried on together. To autoverify is to run that
practice on a piece of software.
# Foundation: spread that changes from place to place

In the stage of 1.3.145 each reading carries noise,
and the noise at a place has a spread — how far its
readings scatter around the truth there. Noise of ONE SPREAD
carries that spread at every place.
HETEROSCEDASTIC (1.3.227) noise lets the spread
change from place to place: the i-th reading is
sigma_i times a centred draw, and the sigma_i need
not agree. The change can be the signal's own doing:
a yes-or-no reading whose chance is the signal has
spread set by the signal itself, and a count reading
likewise — so a model can be heteroscedastic by its
shape, before any failure of care. What a price for
an estimator may use, when the spreads differ, is
the whole question of Paper 018 (Chapter 29): a
price set by the largest spread is set by the worst
place; a price set by the average spread (1.2.232)
lets one loud place move it only by the share of
that place.
Builds on: the sequence model (1.3.145); average,
spread, distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: a heavy tail

A law of noise has a HEAVY TAIL when its far values
die away slowly: readings many spreads from the
centre stay common enough that high averages of the
size of the noise fail to settle. The measure the tracker
uses is the absolute moment of order p — the expected
value of |noise|^p. A law may carry the moment of
order 1 and of order 3/2 and no moment of order 2:
its readings have a centre and a typical size, but
no standing spread, and any method that begins by
squaring the noise begins by asking a question the
law cannot answer. The bell law (1.3.138) stands at
the other end: all its moments stand. Between them
the papers of cluster O draw their lines — Paper 012
under a slope bound on the law (1.3.149), Paper 018
under a moment of order p in [1, 2] alone (1.2.232).
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8);
expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: the sign of a number

The SIGN of a number x, written sign(x), is 1 when
x > 0, 0 when x = 0, and -1 when x < 0. A sign keeps
one bit of a number — which side of zero it stands
on — and throws the size away. A sum weighted by
signs therefore asks a question of sides only: at
each place, is this candidate above that one, and
by which side does the reading vote? Such a sum
cannot be shouted down by one huge reading, because
a huge reading and a small one cast the same vote;
that is the whole strength, and the whole blindness,
of the test built on signs at 1.2.230.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: one peak — the unimodal shape

A function on an interval is UNIMODAL (1.3.228)
when it rises, then falls: there is a place, the
peak, with the function never falling up to it and
never rising after it. The place of the peak is
part of the shape — a one-peak function is not told where
its peak stands. Two facts carry the weight of
Paper 018. First, the class of one-peak functions
is NOT convex (1.3.30): the average of two one-peak
functions with peaks at different places can carry
two peaks, and so stands outside the class — the
class is a union of monotone cones (1.3.151), one
for each peak place, glued along the monotone
functions, and gluing is where its good behaviour
ends. Second, the freedom of the peak is a freedom
to chase: a fit that may raise a peak anywhere may
raise one on a single loud reading (1.2.235).
Builds on: the monotone cone (1.3.151); mixing and
the convex hull (1.3.30).
# Foundation: cutting power — how rich the sets a class cuts are

Given a class of functions and one candidate in it,
look at the sets the other candidates cut out on
the design points by standing above the candidate,
and by standing below it. The CUTTING POWER of such
a family of sets — its VC-dimension, in the term
of the field — is the largest count of design points the
family can split in every possible way: for a
family of cutting power D, some D points can be
divided into the set and its outside in all
2^D patterns by members of the family, and no
D + 1 points can. Poor families — the cuts a line
makes, the cuts a few-piece step function makes —
have small cutting power however many points stand
in the design; rich families grow with the design
itself. Cutting power is the standing measure
of the field for how much a class can bend around one
candidate, and it is the currency the degree of
1.2.231 is paid in.
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3); a sample, and
independence (1.3.130).
Defined word: heteroscedastic — of noise in a
regression or a sequence model, whose spread
changes from place to place: each reading carries
its own sigma_i, and the sigma_i need not agree.
The plain account stands at 1.3.222. First spent
at Paper 018 (Chapter 29), for the noise the
isotonic least squares estimator is shown to
survive.
Defined word: unimodal — of a function on an
interval, rising then falling: never falling up to
one place and never rising after it, the place of
the peak free. The plain account stands at 1.3.225.
First spent at Paper 018 (Chapter 29), for the
class on which least squares and the l_1-estimator
part.
Defined word: extremal — of a candidate in a
class, whose cut sets are poor enough to be priced:
the families of sets where rivals stand above it
and below it carry cutting power (1.3.226) at most
some stated degree. The degree itself stands at
1.2.231. First spent at Paper 018 (Chapter 29),
for the candidates the average-moment price can
reach.
# Foundation: complex numbers — a number with two parts

A complex number is a number with two parts: a real part and a
part standing with the letter i, where i is a chosen number whose
square is -1: a + bi, with a and b ordinary (real) numbers. Two
complex numbers add part by part, and multiply by the rule the
square of i sets. Every complex number has a mirror: a - bi, the
same real part with the sign of the i-part turned.
A complex number equal to its own mirror is a real number. The
complex numbers are the ground Paper 019 stands on: its vectors
and matrices (1.3.231) are made of them.
Builds on: integers, fractions (1.3.6).
# Foundation: matrix — a table of numbers; the Hermitian matrix

A matrix is a table of numbers, laid in rows and columns; a square
matrix has as many rows as columns. A matrix can be turned across
its leading diagonal — row i, column j traded with row j, column
i — and, when its numbers are complex (1.3.230), each number can
also be replaced by its mirror. A Hermitian (1.3.241) matrix is a
square matrix that both moves together leave unchanged: turned
and mirrored, it is itself. The numbers on its diagonal are then
real numbers. For a vector x of length d — a column of d complex
numbers, normalized when the sum over its entries of
each entry times its mirror is 1 — and a Hermitian matrix A, the reading x* A x (x
mirrored and turned, times A, times x) is always a real number:
the value A takes on the direction x. Readings of this shape are
the raw material of the joint numerical range (1.2.237).
Builds on: complex numbers (1.3.230); vector and distance
(1.3.7).
# Foundation: determinant — the one number of a square table

Every square matrix (1.3.231) carries one number, its determinant,
built from its entries by a fixed rule of sums and products with
signs. The determinant is zero exactly when the table cannot be undone — when its rows (or its columns) lean on each
other, so the table cannot be undone. The determinant of a product
of two tables is the determinant of the first times the
determinant of the second. In Paper 019
the determinant is a building tool: the determinant of a whole
family of matrices mixed by variables is a polynomial (1.3.233) in
those variables, and that one polynomial carries the family's
geometry (1.2.238).
Builds on: matrix (1.3.231).
# Foundation: polynomial — a sum of powers; homogeneous polynomial

A polynomial in variables u_0, ..., u_n is a sum of terms, each
term a number (a coefficient) times a product of powers of the
variables: u_0^2 u_3, or 4 u_1 u_2, or a bare number. The degree of
a term is the total count of variable factors in it; the degree
of the polynomial is the largest degree among its terms. A
polynomial is homogeneous when every one of its terms has the
same degree: then scaling all the variables by one factor scales
the whole polynomial by that factor raised to the degree, which
is what lets a homogeneous polynomial live on projective space
(1.3.236), where a point is a line of scaled copies. A polynomial
in one variable of degree d has at most d roots — values at which
it vanishes — unless it is the zero polynomial.
Builds on: integers, fractions (1.3.6); function, variable
(1.3.2).
# Foundation: field — arithmetic whole; finite field

A field is a set of numbers closed under the four acts of
arithmetic: any two of its numbers can be added, taken
one from another, multiplied, and — unless the divisor is zero — divided, and the
answer stands in the set. The fractions form a field; so do the
real numbers and the complex numbers (1.3.230). A finite field
has a finite count of elements: for a prime p, the numbers 0, 1, ...,
p-1 with arithmetic taken modulo p — every answer wrapped around
the clock of size p — form a finite field, since p being prime
lets every nonzero element be divided. Finite fields are the
cheap ground of Paper 019: arithmetic in them is exact and fast,
and a computation done there can be lifted back to the field it
came from (1.2.245).
Builds on: integers, fractions (1.3.6).
# Foundation: number field — fractions over one root

A number field is a field (1.3.234) got from the fractions by
adding one new number to them: choose a polynomial f with fraction
coefficients that does not split into smaller pieces over the
fractions (irreducible, 1.3.239), take a root alpha of it in the
complex numbers, and let K = Q(alpha) be all numbers of the form
a_0 + a_1 alpha + ... + a_{m-1} alpha^{m-1} with the a_i
fractions, where m is the degree of f. Those sums — the power
basis of K — are closed under the four acts of arithmetic, so K
is a field standing between the fractions and the complex
numbers. The polynomial f is the defining polynomial of K (also
called the minimal polynomial of alpha): the smallest law alpha
stands under over the fractions. The entries of the matrices of Paper
019 stand in such a field, and the shape of K — how f reads
modulo primes — runs the whole method (1.2.244).
Builds on: field (1.3.234); polynomial (1.3.233); complex numbers
(1.3.230).
# Foundation: projective space — directions as points; hyperplane; duality

Projective space of dimension n is the space whose points are
the lines through the origin in a space of dimension n+1: a point
is a whole direction, named by any nonzero set of coordinates
along it, all scaled copies counting as the same point. A
hyperplane in it is the set of points whose coordinates satisfy
one linear equation — the flat of one dimension below the whole.
Duality: every hyperplane of a projective space is itself
a point of a second projective space, the dual space — a
hyperplane with coefficients (y_0 : ... : y_n) is the point of
that name in the dual. So a family of them, for instance
the tangent hyperplane at each point of a variety (1.3.237),
is a set in the dual space, and can itself be a variety there. The tangent
(1.3.97) at a smooth point of a variety is the flat that touches
it there without crossing; for a variety cut out by one
polynomial F, the coefficients of the tangent hyperplane are the
partial derivatives of F at the point — the rate of change of F
in each coordinate direction — and they vanish together exactly
at the singular points, where no single tangent stands.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9); the tangent (1.3.97);
vector and distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: variety — the zero set of polynomials; hypersurface

An algebraic variety is the set of points at which one or more
polynomials (1.3.233) all vanish: the common zero set, in ordinary
space or in projective space (1.3.236). A variety cut out by one
polynomial is a hypersurface (1.3.242) — in a space of dimension
n, a thing of dimension n-1, as a curve is in the plane and a
surface in space of dimension 3. A point of a variety is smooth
when near it the variety has one tangent flat (1.3.236); the
smooth points form its smooth locus, and the rest are its
singular points. Much of Paper 019 is the study of one
hypersurface — the determinantal (1.3.243) one of 1.2.238 — and
of the varieties dual to its pieces.
Builds on: polynomial (1.3.233); projective space (1.3.236);
sphere, surface (1.3.10).
# Foundation: interpolation — the form through the points

Interpolation is the recovery of a whole form from its values at
chosen points. Fix a shape — say, all polynomials (1.3.233)
homogeneous of one degree in fixed variables; a shape with N free
coefficients is a space of dimension N. Each point at which the
unknown form must vanish lays one linear condition on its
coefficients: a row of numbers, got by reading every term of the
shape at that point. Lay the rows in a table — the evaluation
table — and the forms of the shape vanishing at all the points
are exactly the kernel of that table: the coefficient rows the
table sends to zero. With enough points in general position the
kernel holds one form only, up to a nonzero multiple, and that
form is the interpolated one: the unique form of the shape
through the points. Paper 019 gets the equation of a
dual hypersurface, by interpolation, from points sampled on it (1.2.242, 1.2.243).
Builds on: polynomial (1.3.233); matrix (1.3.231); set and
element (1.3.3).
# Foundation: irreducible — a factor that splits no further; a prime that splits completely

A polynomial is irreducible over a field when it cannot be
written as a product of two polynomials of smaller degree with
coefficients in that field: it is a whole piece, as a prime
number is among the integers. Every polynomial splits into
irreducible factors over its field, and the splitting is unique
up to order and nonzero multiples. Irreducible is a property
relative to the field: a polynomial whole over the fractions may
split over a larger field. A prime p splits completely in a
number field K = Q(alpha) (1.3.235) when the defining polynomial
of K, read modulo p, falls into distinct linear factors — as
many roots in the finite field (1.3.234) as the field has degree,
no two alike. At such a prime, K can be read in m separate
finite-field mirrors at once, one per root (1.2.244).
Builds on: polynomial (1.3.233); field and finite field
(1.3.234); number field (1.3.235); integers, fractions (1.3.6).
# Foundation: remainder-joining — the Chinese remainder theorem; rational reconstruction

Read a whole number modulo a prime p and what is left of it
is its remainder mod p: its place on the clock of size p. The
Chinese remainder theorem says the remainder of one and the
same number at separate primes can be joined: given that
remainder modulo primes p_1, ..., p_k — no two sharing a
factor — there is exactly one remainder modulo the product
M = p_1 ... p_k that agrees with all of them, and the number
itself is recovered as soon as M stands larger than the size
of the number. Rational reconstruction runs the same road for
a fraction: a remainder modulo a large M, known to come from
a fraction whose numerator and denominator are small against
M, determines that fraction — there is only one such fraction
with that remainder, and an algorithm of the Euclidean kind
finds it.
Together the two are the standard lift of modular computation:
compute in cheap finite fields (1.3.234), join what they give, recover the exact rational answer (1.2.245).
Builds on: integers, fractions (1.3.6); finite field (1.3.234).
Defined word: Hermitian — of a square table of
numbers, standing unchanged when the table is
turned across its leading diagonal and every
number in it is replaced by its mirror, the
number with the sign of its i-part turned. The
plain account stands at 1.3.231. First spent at
Paper 019 (Chapter 30), for the matrices whose
joint readings the paper prices.
Defined word: hypersurface — a variety cut out
by one equation: in a space of dimension n, a
thing of dimension n-1, as a curve is in the
plane and a surface is in a space of dimension
3. The plain account stands at 1.3.237. First
spent at Paper 019 (Chapter 30), for the
determinantal hypersurface and the dual varieties whose
equations the paper computes.
Defined word: determinantal — of a polynomial
or a variety, built as the determinant of a
table whose entries are straight (degree-one)
forms in the variables: the polynomial vanishes
exactly where that table cannot be undone. The plain
account stands at 1.3.232 and 1.2.238. First
spent at Paper 019 (Chapter 30), for the
polynomial got as the determinant of
(u_0 I + u_1 A_1 + ... + u_n A_n)
that carries a family of matrices.
# Foundation: sorts and signatures — the kinds of a language, and what it can say

A many-sorted language sorts its variables: every variable
is of one sort, a kind of thing the language speaks of —
points and lines, say, in a language of planes, or numbers
and sets in a language of arithmetic. Some languages have
one sort only; those are the single-sorted ones. The
signature of a language is its stock: its sorts, and its
relation symbols, each relation symbol carrying the row of
sorts it takes — before, in the language
of order, takes two numbers. A formula of the language is always read
in a context: the row of its free variables, each with its
sort, and the canonical context of a formula is that row in
the order the variables first occur in it. The paper of
Chapter 31 keeps its signatures relational: no function
symbols and no constant symbols — what a function would name
is said instead by a description, in Russell's way, and every
construction of the paper is built inside the discipline of
sorts, contexts and signatures.
# Foundation: theory and model — a closed stock of sentences, and a world that answers them

A theory is a set of sentences of a language closed under
consequence: whatever follows from its sentences by proof
stands already in the theory. (The consequence of Chapter 31
is the calculus Halvorson sets out.) A model of a theory is
a structure — a set for each sort, and for each relation
symbol a set of rows of the right sorts — in which every
sentence of the theory holds. The models of a theory T,
taken together with the maps between them that preserve the
truth of every formula and not only of the sentences — the
elementary embeddings — form the category of models of T,
written Mod(T). The category is locally small, and by the
theorem of Loewenheim and Skolem its study can be confined
to models no larger than a cardinal fixed by the size of the
language. Mod(T) is the stage on which the semantics of
Chapter 31 runs: a translation between theories is read as a
functor (1.3.190) between their categories of models, going
the other way (1.2.251).
# Foundation: natural transformation — a way between functors

Given two functors (1.3.190) from one category to another, a
natural transformation between them is a family of ways, one
at each thing of the first category, from the image of the
first functor at the thing to the image of the second
functor at it —
coherent in one stated way: for every way between two things
of the first category, the square the family makes with the
two images of that way commutes, going across then along
giving the same way as going along then across. A natural
isomorphism is a natural transformation whose every component
way is an isomorphism (1.3.251). The notion earns its scroll
with Chapter 31: the meaning of a morphism between
interpretations is a natural transformation between their
the semantic dual of each (1.2.253), and the coherence of
composition
is carried by natural isomorphisms (1.2.254).
# Foundation: bicategory — things, ways, and ways between ways

A bicategory is a category (1.3.185) one level up. Its things
are joined by ways (the 1-morphisms), and its ways are joined
by ways between ways (the 2-morphisms). The 2-morphisms
compose along a shared way — vertically — and along a shared
thing — horizontally. Composition of ways is not required to
be associative on the nose, nor the identity ways to be
strict units: both hold up to named 2-isomorphisms, the
associator and the unitors (1.3.253), which must themselves
cohere — the pentagon law for the associator, the triangle
law for the unitors, and the associator and unitors natural
throughout. A pseudofunctor
(1.3.252) is a map between bicategories that keeps the
things, ways and ways between ways, and keeps composition up
to coherent 2-isomorphisms of its own, its compositors and
unitors. A pre-bicategory is the same data with none of the
laws yet checked — the starting point of the reflection
principle (1.2.256). An equivalence inside a bicategory —
ways each way whose composites are 2-isomorphic to the
identities — is the sameness of 1.2.257.
Defined word: piecewise — by pieces; done or built one piece
at a time, each piece under its own rule, the whole read off
the pieces together. First spent at Paper 020 (Chapter 31),
for the interpretations of the paper, which rebuild a
theory's language out of a finite family of pieces for each
sort (1.2.248).
Defined word: sortal — of a predicate, saying of a thing
which sort it is to be read as; a sortal predicate is the
single mark that carries one sort inside a one-sorted
language. The word is Quine's, spent by him for the
predicate of his replacement of sorts (1.2.258). First spent
at Paper 020 (Chapter 31).
Defined word: biequivalence (also: biequivalent) — an
equivalence between bicategories (1.3.247): a pseudofunctor
(1.3.252) between them that is an equivalence on each family
of ways and reaches every object of the other up to
equivalence. First spent at Paper 020 (Chapter 31), for the
theorem that the bicategories of single-sorted theories and
of theories with a finite number of sorts are biequivalent
(1.2.259).
Defined word: isomorphism (also: isomorphisms) — a way
between two things of a category that can be undone: a way
with an inverse way, the composite of the two, in either
order, being the identity way on its thing. Two things
joined by an isomorphism are isomorphic. First spent at
Paper 020 (Chapter 31), where the laws of composition hold
up to named isomorphisms, and not on the nose.
Defined word: pseudofunctor (also: pseudofunctors) — a map
between bicategories (1.3.247) that sends things to things,
ways to ways and ways between ways to ways between ways,
and keeps composition and identities up to coherent
isomorphisms (1.3.251) of its own, its compositors and
unitors (1.3.253). One running the ways backwards is the
kind Chapter 31 spends: the reading of theories by their
categories of models (1.2.256). First spent at Paper 020
(Chapter 31).
Defined words: associator, unitor, compositor — the named
coherence ways of a bicategory (1.3.247). The associator
joins the composite of ways grouped one way to the composite
grouped the other. A unitor joins a composite with an
identity way to the way itself. A compositor joins the image
of a composite under a pseudofunctor (1.3.252) to the
composite of the images. First spent at Paper 020 (Chapter
31), in the composition of interpretations (1.2.254) and the
reflection of the bicategory (1.2.256).
Defined word: interpretable (also: interpretability) — a
theory is interpretable in another when an interpretation of
the first stands in the second; interpretability is that
standing between two theories, and bi-interpretability the
standing when it runs both ways, up to the sameness of
1.2.257. First spent at Paper 020 (Chapter 31), whose
subject is the piecewise widening of both.
Defined word: sentential — of sentences; the sentential
algebra of a theory is the algebra of its sentences under
its own consequence, the sentences joined by the theory's
and, or and if-then. First spent at Paper 020 (Chapter 31),
where the algebra is one of the two prices of sameness of
theories (1.2.257).
Defined word: reconstrual (also: reconstruals) — a re-reading
of one language inside another, sort by sort and piece by
piece: the first half of a piecewise translation (1.2.248),
before the reading is extended to every formula. The word is
Visser's, taken up by the paper of Chapter 31. First spent
at Paper 020 (Chapter 31).
# The epistemic registry — what this phext holds, and how surely

Every claim-bearing scroll of this phext carries exactly one standing, and
this section is the record of that standing, scroll by scroll. The standing
says how the model itself holds the claim — not whether the claim is so in
the world.

TRUE — settled. Either true because we set it so by definition (our
foundations, our defined words, our own measures), or backed by a checking
receipt we can point to: a test run, a count we made, a source fact checked
when it was taken in.

FALSE — found against, and kept. A claim a refutation receipt speaks
against: a recorded clash, a check that failed, a counter-case. False
claims are knowledge. They are never quietly dropped; they are retired
with their receipt and a pointer left behind.

UNKNOWN — written down, not settled. The common standing of a claim a paper
makes: that the paper makes it is a fact of record, but the claim itself is
Unknown until a receipt lands. Open questions, Emise's own reasoned links
(marked [synthesis]), and weighed estimates (the Research Oracle ledger)
are Unknown too.

FICTION — made on purpose, and held as made. Worked examples with invented
beings, scenes built to show a shape. Fiction is never set out as fact,
and it never steps straight to True.

No standing stands bare. Each entry carries a BASIS — the ground of the
standing — and a pointer to its evidence. The basis binds the standing:
by definition (stipulation) and by receipt (verified) may stand True; by
refutation receipt (refuted) stands False; by the say-so of a source
(attributed), by being an open question (open), by being Emise's reasoned
link (synthesis), and by being a weighed estimate (estimate) stand
Unknown; by being made for show (construction) stands Fiction. An entry
whose standing breaks its basis row is a defect by the meaning of the
words — that is what accurate means here, and it is the thing the Lean
check proves.

What the Lean check proves, and what it does not. A small Lean model of
this registry — the standing of each scroll, the bases, the rules that
bind them — is machine-checked: that every scroll of the phext stands in
the registry exactly once, that no standing breaks its basis, that a
standing only ever changes along the allowed steps and only with the
receipt the step names, and that reshaping the phext (merging, splitting,
retiring with a pointer) cannot break any of that. And nothing is pruned
by reshaping: merging, splitting, and retiring move claims and leave pointers,
and a retired claim stays reachable, because all knowledge has a purpose
— for we might be wrong. The check proves the keeping of the record
cannot rot. It does not prove that a receipt-backed True is true in the
world — that rests on the receipts themselves: the kernel checks, the
counts, the test runs, each open to being checked again.

