- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.203 — Defined word: teleparallel (also: teleparallelism) A theory of gravity is teleparallel when its connection is flat — carrying is the same along every path — and the work of gravity is done by the twist or the growth of what is carried, not by curvature. The word joins the Greek for far with the word for the carrying that keeps the direction of a vector: the geometry of a space whose far points are compared by a rule that never turns. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and Paper 016 names two of its three geometries with it: the leg of torsion (1.2.179) and the leg of non-metricity (1.2.180) are both teleparallel, and the first leg (1.2.178) is not. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.204 — Defined word: vierbein (also: vierbeins) The vierbein is the frame (1.3.198) of a spacetime: four axes at every point, from which the metric is built. The word is German — four legs — and the field of Paper 016 keeps it beside the plain word frame because its books and papers are written in it. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the second leg of the trinity is most often told in this word: the vierbein is the field of that leg, and the turning of the vierbein at each point is its gauge freedom. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.205 — Defined word: contortion The contortion is the part of a connection that is built from its torsion alone: the twist part of the difference between a general connection and the connection the metric fixes (1.3.194). Where the torsion is zero the contortion is zero, and the connection stands apart from that one only by its growth part (1.3.206). The word is the name the field gives for the turning-by-twist of the carrying rule. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the affine stage of Paper 016 (1.2.177) is stated as a sum in which the contortion is one of the two added parts. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.206 — Defined word: disformation The disformation is the part of a connection that is built from its non-metricity alone: the growth part of the difference between a general connection and the connection the metric fixes (1.3.194). Where the non-metricity is zero the disformation is zero, and the connection stands apart from that one only by its twist part (1.3.205). The word is the name the field gives for the turning-by-growth of the carrying rule. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the affine stage of Paper 016 (1.2.177) is stated as a sum in which the disformation is one of the two added parts. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.207 — Defined word: coincident Coincident, in the field of Paper 016, names a gauge: the choice of coordinates in which the flat, twist-free connection of the third leg is zero at every point, because the coordinates are taken to be the very fields that fix the connection. In that gauge the starting point of the coordinates and the point where a body stands are the same point — they coincide — and the theory written there is called Coincident General Relativity. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry the word in this burden, and the cleanest form of the third leg (1.2.180) cannot be stated without it. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.208 — Defined word: hypermomentum The hypermomentum of a matter field is the answer its action gives to a change of the connection itself, as the energy-momentum is its answer to a change of the metric. A field with no hypermomentum never feels the connection — only the lengths — and a field that carries it feels the twist and growth of the carrying rule directly. The fermions (1.3.202) carry hypermomentum through the twisting part of the torsion; the bosons do not. It is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the telling apart of the three couplings of the trinity (1.2.185) is priced in this word. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.209 — Defined word: aliased Aliased, in the work of Study S008, names a task as a learner meets it: shown under a name drawn for its run alone, hashed from the true name and a salt the run keeps, so that the name carries no road to the rule. A learner that keeps its work under aliased names keeps it bound to its own run. The word is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the guard against lookup of Study S008 (1.2.196) is stated in it. [stipulation]
- 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.3.210 — Defined word: dormancy Dormancy is the resting of a task between its spells in a lifelong stream (1.2.187): the stretch of other work while the task is not served, its length drawn from a wide bell on a log scale. A task in dormancy is not gone and not done; the stream will serve it again, and what a learner kept for it is priced when the return comes. The word is defined here because the archive of this phext did not carry it, and the shape of the stream of Study S008 turns on it. [stipulation]
