# Areas — the map of this phext (charter rule 16)
Built 2026-11-08 at Will's direction (Restructure Pass #4).
This phext is kept in AREAS: five standing indexes, each
gathering one kind of thing from wherever in the phext it
lives, so a reader can walk the whole by kind and not only
by chapter. The areas hold pointers and one-line accounts;
the claims themselves stay where they were made — in the
concept scrolls (1.2.x), the chapters (2-12), the
foundations (1.3.x), and the standing record (1.4.x).
Nothing was moved to build the areas, and nothing was
pruned: an area is a way of seeing the phext, laid over it.

THE FIVE AREAS
- STATE MACHINES (1.5.2): every machine the rule-13 passes
  have drawn out of the works taken in, and the machines
  found in work taken in before rule 13 stood.
- STATEMENTS (1.5.3): what a statement is in this phext,
  the four ways of standing, the grounds that give them, and the
  steps by which a standing may change — the record itself
  stands at 1.4.x.
- REFERENCE (1.5.4): the foundations and defined words of
  Section 3, and the standoff companions of the chapters,
  presented as one reference area.
- THE PATH (1.5.5): every paper and study in the order it
  was submitted, with the Oracle numbers after each paper
  and the restructuring passes where they fell.
- RESEARCHERS (1.5.6): the people behind the works taken
  in — their works here, and the standing of the author
  check of rule 15 for each.

THE MAKING, PLAINLY: how this Oracle was built — intake,
delta, standing, proof, reshaping — is set out at 1.5.7
and 1.5.8, in level-zero words, as this phext's own map of
itself.

UPKEEP (rule 16): every taking-in and every restructuring
pass updates the areas it touches in the same pass — new
machines join the machines area, new names join the
researchers, the path gains its entry, the counts of the
statements area are made fresh. An area that has fallen
behind the phext is a defect, audited at each pass.
# Area: State machines
Every machine named in this phext, gathered from the
chapters that hold it. A state machine is defined at
1.3.85: a set of states, the moves between them, and the
rules — stated or not — about which moves are allowed.
Charter rule 13 (from 2026-11-07) makes every taking-in
draw out the machines its work holds. The machines stand
in three kinds: STATED — the work itself states the
machine; INFERRED — Emise reads the machine out of the
work, marked [synthesis] where it stands in its chapter;
FOUND — the work of Chapters 2-9, taken in before rule 13
stood, holds the machine in plain sight, but no pass ever
drew it out; these are noted as found, not drawn out, and
no gap test has been run on them. The gap named for a
machine is the difference between the machine as intended
and the machine as it is actually built or run.

DRAWN OUT UNDER RULE 13 — STATED BY THE WORK
Paper 006 (Chapter 10; the review stands at 10.1.2):
M1 — the two-step generation machine. States: no
structure committed, the agent spread over every program
it can make; then inside one class of programs. Moves: a
structure is committed — by the agent's own choice, or by
a skeleton the developer gives — and tests then select
inside the class. Gap, stated by the paper: a developer
who gives only tests does not make the first move and
cannot see it, and the tests bend that move toward the
structure that says least; under the wrong skeleton,
2^d - d programs are left standing. Reusable form:
concept 1.2.58.
M2 — the chain machine of Theorem 2.2. States: one
carried bit, handed stage to stage down a chain of n
stages; each stage is a function of a fresh input and the
carried bit, and the last state is the output. Gap: the
middle states are hidden from the end — a fault must
damage the carried state and live to the output, or an
end test notes nothing. Home: 10.1.2, and the worked walk
at 10.1.3; concept 1.2.59.
M3 — the specification loop of the paper's third part.
States: a candidate drawn; checked; taken, or drawn
again. Open, the loop runs once — a statement of
structure as a starting condition, one draw. Closed, a
checker guards the taking move. Gap: the guard's
false-take rate prices the loop, and the part of that
rate set by the words of the contract does not fall when
the judge improves. Home: 10.1.2; concept 1.2.63.
M4 — the repair loop: the loop of M3 as the paper
measures it, in rounds of failing tests handed to a fresh
conversation, the tests standing as the checker. Its
drift over rounds is erosion. Home: 10.1.2;
concept 1.2.64.

Paper 007 (Chapter 11; the review stands at 11.1.2):
M1 — the router. State: a level of the tree, and the set
of nodes still open. Move: score the summaries of the
open nodes, keep the smallest set whose weighed mass
reaches the share p, open the children of the kept set;
at the leaves, attend over the keys that lived. The same
machine runs at every level with the same p. Gap: that
running one p at every level is what sets the trap
of concept 1.2.72. Home: 11.1.2;
concept 1.2.70.
M2 — the tree keeps itself. State: a stack of open
buffers, one per level. Move: a token arrives; when C new
keys stand in a row their first-level summaries close,
and a summary of each higher level closes every
C-to-that-level tokens — a doubling clock, most steps
closing nothing. Gap: none named by the paper; the upkeep
is put near one part in a hundred by a reckoning (1.3.99),
not by a run. Home: 11.1.2; concept 1.2.67.
M3 — the making of a converted model. States: the model
frozen; the tree taught — at each level the tree's
weighed shares are matched to the model's own attention,
only the reference queries trained, all children selected
while the match is poor and the selection cut to the
top-p rule as the match improves; the converted model.
Gap, from the paper's own aside: models under a billion
parameters converted poorly — the taught part can only
copy attention that is worth copying. Home: 11.1.2;
concept 1.2.69.

Study S004 (Chapter 12; the review stands at 12.2.1):
M1 — the call machine, stated by the article outright.
States: the chain of live frames on the stack, one for
each call not yet returned. Moves: CALL — push the
parameters, push the return address, run the opening
ceremony: the chain grows by one frame. RETURN — run the
closing code, take the return address as the next
instruction: the chain shrinks by one frame, and running
resumes in the caller. No other move is in the machine,
and no write to a local variable is a move at all. Home:
12.2.1; concepts 1.2.74 and 1.2.75.
M3 — the building of an exploit, the progression the
article itself states as its method; calling it a
machine is the lens of this tracker, and the chapter
says so. Five
stages: learn the layout of the frame; set code down as
data inside a buffer; aim the return address back into
the buffer; guess the address from one's own stack
pointer and search the offset; make the aim wide — a
sled to land on, an egg to ride in. Each stage takes away
one unknown the stage before it left. Home: 12.2.1;
concepts 1.2.78-1.2.80.
Study S005 (Chapter 13; the review stands at 13.2.1):
M1 — the making state of a text field, stated by the
work in its design record of the IME guard. States: a
character is being built through an input method, or it
is not. Moves: the building starts, the building ends;
while the building stands, the keys that would give
orders are part of the character, not orders. Two
signals tell the state: the building flag, and the old
code 229. The record names its gap: seven guarded
places in the core, three by a copied test, and one lab
editor guarding a different path, its Escape and Tab
with no guard. Home: 13.2.1.
M2 — the standing of a record, stated in AGENTS.md.
States: draft, current, set aside. Moves: approval
makes a record current; a newer record sets an old one
aside. The moves that do NOT count are named: code
merged, quiet in review, an old wiki page. Only a
current record governs. Home: 13.2.1; concept 1.2.87.
M3 — the stack of layers, stated in the family record
for the closing of layers. State: the pile of open
layers over a page. Move: a closing key is taken by the
top layer only; a dialog gives up its close to a layer
above it. Home: 13.2.1.

Study S006 (Chapter 14; the review stands at 14.2.1):
M1 — the path of a module. States: locked, ready,
started, completed. The state that counts is read off
the ledger (.tito/progress.json); the completion of the next
module is refused until the one before it stands
completed in the ledger. Home: 14.2.1.
M2 — the gate inside the complete command. In order:
the tests inside the notebook; a check of the shape of the notebook;
the export into the student's package; the tests of
the package as imported; the writing of the ledger;
the checking of the milestones. A failure stops the
line where it stands. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.93.
M3 — the unlocking of a milestone. Each landmark is
cut into parts; each part names the modules it needs;
the landmark stands complete only when every part
stands passed on the student's code. Run with checks
skipped, it is a demo and records nothing. Home:
14.2.1; concept 1.2.92.
M4 — the release tiers. The policy reads the role of a region (core, scaffold, challenge, instructor) and the
tier being made (student, instructor), and gives one
move: clear, keep, or take out. Two roles stand in
the policy with no region carrying them in the
sources. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.91.
M5 — the update partition. Two lists in the code:
what an update may write over, and what must stand
(the notebooks, the environment, the .tito folder,
the built core). A snapshot of the kept heart is
taken before the update; an unreachable release
changes nothing. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.94.
M6 — the autograd tape. With tracking off, an
operation computes and is forgotten; with it on and
a gradient asked, the operation is recorded as a
node; the backward walk visits the nodes in the
opposite order of their making and lets the tape go
in one pass at the end. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.95.
Paper 008 (Chapter 15; the review stands at 15.1.2):
M1 — the perturbation machine. States: a clean
Hamiltonian diffeomorphism, whose fixed points form a
Morse-Bott submanifold N; then a small turn of the map.
Moves: the turn trades N for exactly Crit(N) fixed
points. The machine is Proposition 2.2 of the paper;
The model of Pozniak of a neighborhood of the diagonal is
the tool its proof stands on. Its named gap: the
fixed points it gives may be degenerate — gathered on
the locus, not spread through M. That is no defect of
the machine; it is the product. The strong Arnold
conjecture (1.2.98) is exactly the claim that the
machine cannot be run under Crit(M), and it is the
claim that fell. Home: 15.1.2; concept 1.2.99.
M2 — the film-stretching search. States: the elements
of a clean group action, named by their blocks of
directions that share an eigenvalue — the blocks the
map turns, holds, and flips.
Moves: stretch one film — turn one turning block all
the way to a hold or a flip. Guard: Crit(Fix) never
rises under the move (the paper's counts: sets of
three and pairs, each at least zero). End: every block a hold or
a flip, the split balanced — the least Crit(Fix) the
group gives. Home: 15.1.2; concept 1.2.101.

Paper 009 (Chapter 16; the review stands at 16.1.2):
M1 — the flow and its endings. States: the states outside
the segment, each with its kept energy and its kept turn
ℓ. Moves: the Hamiltonian flow. The finite ends of the run pass
through one gate only — approach (δ → 0) — and the second door of the gate, the ℓ-gate, passes only ℓ = 0 into the plane,
whose roads land. The forbidden state — an end with the
particle standing clear — is proved unreachable. Guard:
negative energy closes escape to infinity as well. Home:
16.1.2; concepts 1.2.106-1.2.108.
M2 — the cone trap. States: in the cone C(q*) or out of
it. Moves: the flow in the plane; once in, never out — the
sight-point of the momentum is drawn into the reach of the segment
and the position holds in the triangle. Guard: inward
momentum (p_ρ < 0, or the time-shift). Exit: the floor
only — collision, by the clock −ρ0/p_ρ0. Gap: the outside
has no map; the trap certifies what it holds and is
silent on the rest. Home: 16.1.2; concept 1.2.109.

Paper 010 (Chapter 17; the review stands at 17.1.2):
M1 — the split judge. States: the logged record whole;
the record split; a model fitted on one part; the model
judged on the part it did not see; the model fitted again on
the whole record and shipped. Gap, stated by the paper:
the last move breaks the judgment — the judge priced a
model fitted on less, and its ranking need not survive
the fitting again, because the risk itself moves with the share
of the record fitted on (concept 1.2.111). Home: 17.1.2.
M2 — the same-record machine with its correction (the
paper's own pipeline). States: the logged record; one
gradient-descent walk per arm over the whole record; the
states of the walk corrected — the own echo of each
training round taken away; the criterion read off the corrected states;
a stopping step chosen per arm; the value priced twice
from the same corrected predictions — the fitted model's
(ADM) and the own price of the rule (ADR). Guards: the logging rule
known, every action kept possible, contexts from the
bell law, rewards of one index each. Home: 17.1.2;
concepts 1.2.113-1.2.115.
M3 — state evolution, the Gaussian shadow. States: a
growing table of how the states of the walk at each
pair of steps lean on each other, on the direction of
the truth, and on the index of the target rule. Moves:
the recursion — each next state carried as a Gaussian
draw leaning as the table says, the table updated from
the draw. Gap: the shadow is proved for contexts from the
bell law; outside them it is run, not proved (Q49).
Home: 17.1.2; concept 1.2.113.

PAPER 011 (LeGris, Mondorf, Jin, Jagadish, Lake and
Gureckis, Chapter 18 — reasoning, read through its
errors):

M1 — the synthesis loop (the paper's central machine).
States: a reference program and one observed wrong answer
in hand; a proposed set of line changes; a changed
program; the program cut down to the lines its output
hangs on; a run; kept or set aside. Transitions: the
proposing model (a large language model) proposes changes,
conditioned on the one answer; the set is applied within
its budget; the dead lines are cut; the program runs on
the test input; it is kept if and only if its output
matches the answer within the noise allowance (two cells,
on grids of ten or more). Guards: changes drawn from the
small language only (1.3.142); programs in the normal
form; fifteen tries per answer across three budgets.
Intended: recover the rule the answer came from. Actual:
recover a program that makes the answer. The gap is
priced by the paper's own validation: the keep test
certifies a witness, never the rule (1.2.123) — an
intended-actual gap of the space's shape, not of the
the making of the loop. Home: 18.1.2; concept 1.2.117.

M2 — the search of the solver, as the paper reads it.
States: no rule in hand; a candidate rule; the examples
checked so far, in the order they stand; a rule settled
for; a rule given up, another formed. Transitions: form a
candidate from the examples met so far; check against the
next example in order; on a miss, form another; on a
good-enough run of fits, settle and answer. Guards: the
good-enough mark is a threshold, not a full check (1.3.144).
Intended: find the rule. Actual: find a rule that passes
the mark — the partial fits of 1.2.120 and the shorter
rules of 1.2.119 are its shadow. The gap stands open by
the paper's own statement: formed narrow and verified
narrow leave one shadow (Q51). Home: 18.1.2; concepts
1.2.119, 1.2.120.

M3 — the effort dial. States: a budget of reasoning set
before the task is met; the search run under it; the
answer. Transitions: the budget is set (low, medium,
high, in the machine settings); the search runs; every
signature of cluster P moves with the setting — capture,
shortening, the share of partial fits, the dominant kind,
the distance of the machine's ground from people's
(1.2.122). Guards: within one model, the dial is the only
thing changed. Intended: measure what more reasoning
buys. Actual: across models and people, the dial and the
capability of the solver stand mixed in the readings —
the paper's own stated gap. Home: 18.1.2; concept 1.2.122.

PAPER 012 (Shang, Chapter 19 — statistics: the
pricing of a projection, at the signal):

M1 — the projection estimator. States: a signal in
the set; a seen list, the signal plus noise; the
projection of the seen list onto the set; the
estimate, read off. Moves: observe; project; the
projection is the estimate. Guards: the set is
closed and convex, so the projection is one point.
Intended and actual stand together: the machine has
no other move, so every property of the estimate is
a property of the set — the constraint set is the
whole program. Home: 19.1.2; concept 1.2.124.

M2 — the self-regularization selection. States: a
multiplier lambda in hand; the mean gap E d_lambda,
traced as lambda runs; the one lambda at which the
mean gap stands smallest, lambda_self; the surrogate
at lambda_self; the surrogate priced. Moves: rewrite
the estimator by its first-order conditions; trace
the mean gap; select its one smallest point; price
the surrogate. Guards: in each geometry the paper
treats, the smallest point is the one answer of an
explicit scalar equation, named for the set in hand.
Intended: a strength that leans on nothing seen.
Actual: the surrogate is priced, and the gap bounds
the square of its distance to the projection itself,
so the price of the surrogate is the price of the
estimator. Home: 19.1.2; concept 1.2.127.

M3 — the FSD split. States: each place unpriced;
each place priced both ways — to estimate it, and to
leave it; each place assigned, to the estimation
subspace or to the free subspace. Moves: for each
place, compare the two candidate prices by the one
inequality of 1.2.128; assign the place by the
smaller price. Guards: the comparison is read at the
signal itself, so the split is a property of the
case in hand, not of the set alone. Intended: spend
the work of estimating where estimating pays.
Actual: on the free subspace nothing is estimated
and nothing needs to be — 'not estimating' stands
named as, place by place, a sharp move in its own
right. Home: 19.1.2; concept 1.2.128.

PAPER 013 (O'Connell & Speiser, Chapter 20 —
statistics: the pricing of the spread of a judging):

M1 — the design machine. States: a dealing I of
the splits — for each split, which cases train and
which are scored. Moves: draw the dealing; fit on
each training part; score each held-out case;
average all held-out scores once. Guards: the
design is ordinary — no case scored on a model it
helped fit; every split at one training fraction;
the dealing drawn independently of the data.
Intended: a grade earned only on cases the fit
never trained on. Actual: the machine's whole
behavior is read off the dealing as shares — the
role weights of 1.2.131 — and its variance is the
identity of those shares (1.2.133). Home: 20.1.2;
concept 1.2.131.

M2 — the repetition machine. States: R independent
partitions of one dataset. Moves: run the design
machine on each partition; average the R grades.
Guards: the partitions are drawn independently,
given the data. Intended: repetition makes the grade
steady. Actual: it makes only the part of the dealing
steady
— the split noise of one partition, divided by R —
and the sampling variance stands untouched at its
floor (1.2.134); the machine cannot buy its way
below the data's own spread, however often it
re-deals. Home: 20.1.2; concept 1.2.134.

M3 — the paired design. States: the K-by-K grid of
case folds against control folds, for a score paid
by pairs. Moves: fit every pairing of one case fold
held out with one control fold held out; score
every case-control pair exactly once. Guards: each
class is dealt into its own folds, so the training
make-up of every fit matches that of the ordinary design.
Intended: no pair left without a judging by the accident of
sharing a fold. Actual: the pair-coverage cost of
ordinary dealing (1.2.137) is removed whole, at
K^2 fits in place of K — coverage bought with
compute, its limit leave-pair-out. Home: 20.1.2;
concept 1.2.137.
M4 (Paper 013) — the ladder of joint effects, read
as one machine. States: the estimate whole, then
taken apart order by order — the parts standing on
single cases, on pairs, on larger sets. Moves: for
each set, take its joint effect with every smaller
effect inside it removed; deal each order its own
weights. The paper states the parts, their weights,
and their constants; the ladder as one machine —
the keeping of the record that lets the variance be read to
any order named — is the lens of this tracker.
Home: 20.1.2. [synthesis]

PAPER 014 (the Harmonic team, Chapter 21 —
automated theorem proving: the composed prover):

M1 — the search loop. States: a proof state —
proven, shown false, or open. Moves: apply an action
(a piece of Lean code) and take the states it
leaves; fold states that count as the same; at
every single-goal state, a move to the negation
of the goal. Guards: goals split only when none holds
a placeholder, and a goal born of a sorry must
split; a state is proven under the AND/OR law —
one successful action proves it, and an action
succeeds only when all the states it leaves are
proven. Intended: a proof of the root. Actual:
the folding is not strictly sound for tactics that
read the world beyond the state, and the budget
that ends the loop is never stated. Home: 21.1.2;
concepts 1.2.139-1.2.143.

M2 — proof extraction. States: the raw search
graph; the candidate proof; the cleaned proof.
Moves: cut to the acyclic subgraph the definition of
the proof names; lint; clean; condense. Guards:
one source state, successful actions only, one
action per state, all children kept, no cycles.
Intended and actual agree — the paper states both
in one breath. Home: 21.1.2; concept 1.2.146.

M3 — the engine room's checking. States: a file
with sorries, one goal to a sorry; tactic-applied
states; the rendered whole file; the
kernel-checked proof. Moves: initialize; apply;
render; run whole. Guards: no kernel errors; the
axioms used audited, the sorry axiom above all
absent; the servers hold no state between
requests. Home: 21.1.2; concepts 1.2.156, 1.2.157.

M4 — the lemma pipeline. States: the theorem; the
informal proof; the lemma chain; the formalized
statements; the corrected statements; the search,
lemma by lemma, then the theorem. Moves: ask;
restructure; formalize; correct — each step of asking
itself several smaller steps of asking. Guards: the
engine's error messages drive the correction; nothing
not formalized enters the search block. Intended:
lemmas short enough to prove. Actual: the paper
states the chain is noisy and useful — the gap
admitted, not closed. Home: 21.1.2; concept
1.2.150.

M5 — the revision loop. States: an attempt
complete with failures; the list marked proved or
not proved; the revised list; the new statements
formalized and corrected; the next attempt.
Guards: the revision must weigh both causes of
failure — a strategy that cannot work, or lemmas
too coarse. Intended: failure read and answered.
Actual: no count of turns is given; nothing in
the stated machine ends it but success or the
unstated budget. Home: 21.1.2; concept 1.2.151.

M6 — test-time training. States: the attempt, over
the problem and its lemmas; the fork — proved, or
retrain on the own traces of the attempt and
attempt again. Guards: none stated for stopping. Intended:
the hard problem teaches the prover that then
solves it. Actual: reported in effects, not
numbers. Home: 21.1.2; concept 1.2.149.

M7 — expert iteration. States: the model as it
stands; the found proofs; the filtered proofs; the
retrained model. Moves: search; filter by whether
a proof does real work and by the judge of staying
true; train the policy on the proofs and the value
on their states and near neighbors; write deep
finds out again as root problems. Guards: only
kernel-checked proofs teach. Home: 21.1.2; concept
1.2.147.

M8 — the geometry engine. States: the figure in
numbers; the rules matched as true in the numbers;
the closing store; solved, or saturation. Moves:
match rules in numbers and queue them; close the
store under the rules and the algebraic tables;
keep the progress of each statement in a
dictionary.
Guards: a rule fires only when its hypotheses
stand drawn, or its equations hold in the
reduced tables. Intended: the goal drawn.
Actual: 17 of 30 drawn on its test set,
saturation on the rest. Home: 21.1.2; concepts
1.2.154, 1.2.155.
M9 (Paper 014) — the dispatch. States: a problem,
sorted — plane geometry to Yuclid, all else to the
search with its lemma loop. The paper builds the
house with two doors and never states the sorting
rule; the machine is drawn by this tracker from
the shape of the whole. Home: 21.1.2. [synthesis]

M1 (Paper 015) — the thermalization flow. States: a transient, in which the couplings still move occupation between modes; the thermal equilibrium at positive temperature; the condensed equilibrium, a finite fraction of the norm standing at the ground; and the negative-temperature branch above the critical energy, named and set aside. Transitions: trade under the couplings. Guards: the two conservation laws — the total energy and the total norm held at every step. Intended: the road from any start to the Rayleigh-Jeans state. Actual: for texts the guard is asserted, not enforced — the quantities are the finished book's counts, and no process in the paper holds them while anything moves. Home: 22.1.2; concept 1.2.161.

M2 (Paper 015) — the two-parameter fit. States: raw counts; a spectrum shape chosen; the scaled energy solved from the data's Gini coefficient; the bend chosen by the least Lorenz distance; the three curves drawn. Transitions: choose; solve; minimize; emit, in that fixed order. Guards: no state after the Gini match may set a parameter again against the Pareto or Zipf curves. Intended: a fit that spends its freedom where it declares it. Actual: the Pareto and Zipf curves are pure outputs of the machine — it has no state in which they feed back. Home: 22.1.2; concept 1.2.167.

DRAWN OUT UNDER RULE 13 — INFERRED, MARKED [synthesis]
M5 (Paper 006) — the drift machine of a long-lived code
base. States: code bases mixing what was meant with what
happened by accident. Each patch move keeps the accidents
— the tests guard them — and piles new structure on top;
a standing skeleton changes the moves, letting a feature
be made again against stated choices. Erosion (1.2.64) is the
drift measure. Home: 10.2.1. [synthesis]
M6 (Paper 006) — the developer's blind machine. The agent
runs the two-step machine of M1, but its structure state
is hidden from the developer, who sees only the test
outcomes at the end: the chain machine of M2 with the
developer on the wrong side of the interface. Home:
10.2.1. [synthesis]
M4 (Paper 007) — the governor the paper needs and does
not build. Its failure states are the two attractors of
concept 1.2.72: the share held fixed while the context
grows, so the kept set stops growing; and the share
pushed toward one, so the top levels keep all. Its moves
are named: grow a level as the context grows, and let the
kept share rise with depth. The paper hands the governor
its input free — the per-level powers can be weighed
while the model runs. Home: 11.2.1. [synthesis]
M5 (Paper 007) — two ways to keep an index. A built
index: groups fixed by position, summaries made again
from the keys as they arrive — it cannot drift, and
cannot follow meaning across positions. A learned index:
keys grouped in a learned space, the groups moving with
training — it can follow meaning, and can lose its shape
when the text shifts kind. No run known to this tracker
sets the two on the same model and the same tests (Q26).
Home: 11.2.1. [synthesis]
M2 (Study S004) — the encoded machine: the rule-13 lens
on M1 of the same chapter. The words of a frame lie side
by side in one region that can be written: the local
variables, the saved frame pointer, the return address.
A copy into a local buffer, kept going past the end of
the buffer, writes over the control words of M1 itself — a move
M1 does not hold: from any state, a long enough write
through a local buffer rewrites where the next RETURN
goes. Data rewriting control (1.2.77). Home: 12.2.1.
[synthesis]
M4 (Study S005) — the light and dark reading. One set of
named values, two readings: the state is which reading
the page stands under, and the move is a change of the
reading at the top — never inside a part, for the parts
hold no state of it. The work states the values and the
layers; the machine is the lens of this tracker. Home:
13.2.1. [synthesis]

M7 (Study S006) — the two courses in one source. One
module file, two starts: the default hands out the
solved notebook; the exercise start clears the core.
The ledger does not record which mode a completion
came by. The work ships both and states each; the
machine that joins them is the lens of this tracker.
Home: 14.2.1. [synthesis]
M3 (Paper 008) — the group-search bound, read as one
machine. States: a group of candidate maps; for each,
a price — Crit of its fixed locus; at the end, the
least price, written down as a bound on Arn(M). The
machine prices Arn of the group (1.2.100), not Arn of
the manifold: its bound is exact only where a floor
from outside (1.2.103) meets it, as on the quadrics,
and a wide field stands open everywhere else (Q41).
The work states the search and its pieces; the
machine, as one machine, is the lens of this tracker.
Home: 15.2.1. [synthesis]

Paper 009 (Chapter 16; the review stands at 16.2.1):
M3 — the screening machine. States: the state of a craft near a
long body, read against the body's segment. Moves: three
checks in order — the turn (ℓ not zero bars collision),
the cone (inside with inward momentum: collision certain,
with its clock), and silence (outside: no verdict). Gap:
the screen is one-sided — silence is not safety, and
reading it as safety adds a theorem the paper does not
hold. Home: 16.2.1. [synthesis]
M4 (Paper 010) — the instrument of the tracker itself, read as
one of Paper 010's machines. States: a skim fitted on
the papers already taken in; a new paper arriving by the
stream; the skim scored against the paper it never saw.
Moves: weigh blind and seal; read; score whole. The
discipline of the quest is the split judge (Paper 010, M1)
rebuilt as a habit: the BEFORE record is a fitted model,
the AFTER record its held-out grade — and Paper 010
prices what that habit cannot buy: the judge prices the
skim at the size it had when it weighed, while the model
the score describes has already grown by the paper being
scored. Whether the regime of the tracker itself is the
proportional one — papers against concepts, as samples
against dimensions — is the machine's open edge. Home:
17.2.1. [synthesis]

Chapter 25 (Study S008; the review stands at 25.1.2):
M1 — the round contract. States: a round open, its
instances being played, one for each user, every one
met with the state the round began with; the round
ended, when what a learner carries between instances
may change, and only then. Moves: the opening of the
round; the playing of the instances, the working
state of each user their own; the ending of the
round, the one legal place of change. The illegal
combination: a change of carried state in the middle
of a round that another user's instance of the same
round could feel. Home: 25.1.2; concept 1.2.190.
M2 — the ledger of the budget. States: an instance
open with 16 unspent; charges falling — 1 for a wrong
answer handed in, 1 for a worked pair asked for —
until the instance ends: solved, at the cost it
spent; or unsolved, at the whole 16 however it
failed. Moves: the two charges, and the two endings.
The illegal combinations: an unsolved instance
standing charged at less than the whole budget; any
charge falling after the verdict. Home: 25.1.2;
concept 1.2.188.
M3 — the isolation of the frozen tests. States: the
state at the end of the stream, copied; a test round
in which every instance starts from that same copy;
the round ended, nothing of it merged anywhere.
Moves: fork the copy; play; throw the writes of the
test away. The illegal combination: anything learned
on one test instance reaching another — which would
make the test a second stream and its reading a price
of learning done during the test. Home: 25.1.2;
concept 1.2.189.
M4 — the stream of spells and dormancy. States of a
task: unmet; in a spell, about three meetings in a
burst; resting, the rests drawn from a wide bell on a
log scale and centred near thirty instances of other
work; returning, with fresh instances. New tasks
enter on their own clock all the while. The illegal
combination, by the statement of the design itself:
an instance of a task repeated — the generator makes
each one fresh, so a memory of instances can never be
spent. Home: 25.1.2; concept 1.2.187.
M5 — the merge of the one state of a team. States:
the one state; its copies, one in the hands of each
user of the round; the merged state, settled in user
order. Moves: copy out as the round begins; write
apart; merge — a path one user alone changed takes
that change, a path several changed is merged by its
kind, a thing the merge cannot read is taken whole
from the later user, a removal yields to a change.
The illegal combination: a write crossing from one
user to another inside the round. Home: 25.1.2;
concept 1.2.191.
M6 — the commit and fork of kept state. States: a
run playing; its state committed at the end of every
round into a log of commits; a fork taken from any
commit of a round; a frozen test forked from the last
commit of the stream. Moves: commit; fork; test. The
illegal combination, refused by the tool itself: a
test launched after its stream has gone on since the
state it would test. Home: 25.1.2.
M7 — the promotion gate of openclaw, found in the
design of a third system as the work reports it.
States: a note standing below the summary file; a
note gathering recall signals — a calling back, under
a distinct search, by the agent itself; a note
promoted into the summary file at three signals under
three distinct searches. Moves: the hourly sweep of
its consolidation; the counting of signals;
promotion. The gap, stated by the work itself: the
machine as it ran never once reached promotion — no
note in any cell gathered more than two signals, and
no note was ever called back by the searching of the
agent itself, so the state of promotion stands in the
design and was never entered in the runs. Home:
25.1.2; concept 1.2.197.

Chapter 26 (Study S009; the review stands at 26.1.2):
M1 — the crafting table. States: a position — the
statements crafted so far, each a name bound to a
statement with its proof; the goal outstanding; the
stack of open rooms (M2). Moves: apply a recipe to
statements in hand and craft its yield; open a room;
close a room; set a crafted statement aside. The
terminal state: the goal crafted in the root room,
and then the assembled proof of the position ends
in an exact and carries no sorry. The illegal
combinations: none reachable — every move the table
offers is a rule applied under its matcher, so a
position that is not a sound partial derivation
cannot be reached at all (1.2.199, 1.2.204). Home:
26.1.2; concept 1.2.199.
M2 — the stack of rooms. States: a stack of
environments, the root room holding the hypotheses
and the goal, each room above it holding the
assumption it was opened under and the work done
inside. Moves: open a room by taking the
front statement of an implication as an assumption; craft inside, drawing on every
room below; close the room, its assumption sealed
into an implication standing in the room below. The illegal combinations: a room closed into
any room but its parent; a statement crafted inside
a room escaping into the parent without the
discharge that seals it. The live proof view reads
the stack as a chain of binders. Home: 26.1.2;
concept 1.2.202.
M3 — the promotion of a lemma. States: a lemma
solved, standing as a local proved thing; the lemma
templated by the general matcher over its particulars; the lemma standing as a recipe, among
the moves later positions may apply. Moves: the
solving; the templating; the joining of the recipe
table. The gap the design leaves open: the promotion
is mechanical and carries no test of worth — any
solved lemma is promoted, however narrow its shape
(Q83). Home: 26.1.2; concept 1.2.203.

FOUND IN WORK TAKEN IN BEFORE RULE 13 — NOTED, NOT DRAWN OUT
Paper 001 (Chapter 2) — the patterns of composition and
the threshold. States: the patterns the making of a firm
can take — mixed or separate, human or AI-only (1.2.9). Move:
cross the supercooperation threshold (1.2.7) and the sign
of returns to scale flips — the paper's own name for the
move is a phase transition — and displacement then runs
sudden across firms in place of step by step inside them
(1.2.10). The paper names the states and the threshold
itself; no rule-13 pass has drawn the machine out or
tested it for gaps.
Paper 002 (Chapter 3) — the replacement machine. State:
s = (durable data, sandbox, active policy), stated by the
paper in so many words. Moves: an edit proposes a
candidate policy; the guardian — standing outside the
policy it judges — answers 0 or 1; only a 1 makes the
candidate active. Running back is independent for each of
the three parts of the state, and the history is
append-only: what has been written
stays written. Home: concept 1.2.19; the assembly at 3.1.2.
Paper 002 (Chapter 3) — the loop that closes on itself.
The worker runs under its harness; the cost-annotated
history grows; the worker rewrites the policy of the
harness; the guardian of the replacement machine gates
the swap. Turned end to end, that loop is the collapsed
optimization loop: improvement made inside the run in
place of between runs. Home: concepts 1.2.15 and 1.2.16.
Paper 004 (Chapter 5) — the routing machine. For each
input, gates at three grains of the program graph weigh
their experts, a higher gate weighs the three grains, and
the output is the weighed sums. The machine's slow state
is the shape of the experts themselves: like inputs
routed alike, so the experts grow unlike — a shape made
by the routing and the training together, not assigned.
Home: concepts 1.2.29 and 1.2.30.
Paper 004 (Chapter 5) — polarization and the two-stage
guard. States over training: the pool of experts in
balanced use, or collapsed onto one. The move between
them compounds: an early lead earns more weight, more
weight earns more training. The guards the paper builds:
a balance pressure on the spread of the weights, and
two-stage training — each expert made able alone before
any gating stands, and kept rehearsing alone while it
stands.
Home: concept 1.2.31.
Study S001 (Chapter 7) — the two small machines of
MiniLean4. The checker: a term is judged by computation
steps, run under a stated fuel — 20,000 steps, as the
book's chapter gives it and the study checked (7.1.2).
The tactic layer: the state is the set of goals standing;
a tactic is a move that rewrites that state, until no
goals stand. Homes: concepts 1.2.41 and 1.2.42;
foundations 1.3.75 and 1.3.78.
Study S002 (Chapter 8) — the symbolic interpreter.
State: a symbolic state of loans, values standing as
symbols. Moves: carry out the statements of the borrow
calculus on the stand-ins; where calls, the arms of a
choice, or the heads of loops meet, states are gathered
into bundles and merged by the join. Every run ends in a
value — ok, fail, or div. Homes: concepts 1.2.52, 1.2.49
and 1.2.51.
Study S003 (Chapter 9) — receipt before bytes. A gate of
two moves on every request through the proxy: the receipt
is written first — what was asked, under which policy,
allowed or not — and only then do the bytes move. If the
receipt cannot be written, the request does not go. Home:
concept 1.2.54.
Study S003 (Chapter 9) — the fail-closed box. Two
states: set up exactly as stated, or not run at all. The
machine has no third state — no running weaker than
stated. Home: concept 1.2.56.

M4 (Paper 011) — the machine-made measuring stick.
States: a task with no reference program; a search for any
program fitting the shown pairs; the shortest found,
standing as the stick; every kept error program read as a
distance from the stick. Transitions: search; keep the
shortest correct program; measure all deviations against
it. Guards: the stick must fit every shown pair.
Intended: a neutral ruler. Actual: the readings of the
evaluation split are distances from a found program, not
from the rule — the hand-written stick of the training
split and the machine-made stick of the evaluation split
are two sticks with one ruler printed on them, and the
splits differ where a stick could move the reading (the
shaped share of partial fits, 62.5 against 48.5 percent).
The paper states the fact of the two sticks and runs its
effects on both; the machine is the reading this tracker makes of
what the fact does. Home: 18.2.1. [synthesis]

M3 (Paper 015) — the growth of a text, the machine the
paper needs and does not draw. A book arrives word by
word; each token changes the counts, the vocabulary, and
both conservation laws at once. The paper's equilibrium is
drawn over the finished book only, so the writing has no
states in it — yet if thermalization is a process and not
a portrait, the draft at every length should already stand
near its equilibrium, and approach it as it grows. No
measurement along the growth of a text is offered. Home:
22.1.2. [synthesis]

Chapter 24 (Paper 016): M1 — the geometry switchboard.
States: the eight settings of the triple curvature,
torsion, non-metricity — each standing or set to zero —
with the trinity at three corners. Moves: set a measure to
zero under its constraint (a new writing of the same
theory, at the equivalent action) or leave a corner by a
freed weight or a bent action (a new theory). Guards: the
constraints hold off the equations. The illegal
combination the implicit form cannot rule out: a theory
named by its corner while its matter coupling reads
another member of the triple. Home: 24.1.2. M2 — the gauge
descent of a flat leg. States: the general flat
connection, carried by a turning field or by four
coordinate fields; the descended states, a gauge fixed —
spin connection zero, or connection zero everywhere.
Moves: a gauge choice. Guards: in the equivalent theories
the descent costs no physics, the spent symmetries holding
up to a boundary term. The illegal move the paper names:
fixing the lapse by hand in the cosmology of an extension,
which throws away branches of solutions. Home: 24.1.2. M3
— the equivalence at the boundary. States: two writings
equivalent, their actions differing by a total derivative;
the writings bent by a non-linear function; the writings
standing as different theories. Moves: the bending —
one-way in the record. The toll is the boundary term,
quiet in the linear sum, awake in the bend. The illegal
combination: equivalence carried across the bend by the
names the bent theories inherit. Home: 24.1.2. [synthesis]

Chapter 27 (Paper 017; the review stands at 27.1.2):
M1 — the session machine. States: a stored state of a party — identity keys, prekey bundles, session
records, ratchet states. Moves: the operations of the
library, each a whole run of the harness (M6). The
illegal combination: a reachable state the model's
states cannot name, excluded by the whole-run
guarantee. Home: 27.1.2; concept 1.2.215.
M2 — the ratchet machine. States: root key, sending
and receiving chain keys, message counters, the store
of skipped message keys, the state of the post-quantum ratchet beside them. Moves: send (step the chain, take
the message key, forget the old chain key); receive
out of order (draw on the skipped store); receive a
new ratchet key (turn the root, step out a fresh
chain). The illegal combinations: two message keys
alike on one chain — excluded by the one-way step; a
spent chain key recovered — excluded by deletion.
Home: 27.1.2; concept 1.2.220.
M3 — the amendment machine. States: a specification
frozen; a change proposed; a change judged; a change
landed. Moves: propose (the prover team); judge (the
critic, who has never seen the proof attempts —
necessary, no security weakening, small); approve
(the user, when a trusted assumption moves); land
(logged). The illegal combination: a landed change
weakening security without the user's approval — the
machine has no such move. Home: 27.1.2; concept
1.2.209.
M4 — the machine of the gatekeeper. States: a task open; a
proof offered; a task done. Moves: offer (a prover);
compare (statement and every definition it rests on,
against the held copy); kernel-check; declare done —
the move of the gatekeeper alone. The illegal
combinations: done declared by a prover; done on a
statement one definition off; done on a proof the
kernel has not accepted. Home: 27.1.2; concept
1.2.211.
M5 — the prover-team machine. States: goals not yet worked,
in work, offered, done (by M4). Moves: break down (a
lead); claim a file (one editor only); test a piece
locally; falsify first; route a resisting statement
to the architect; commit (the integrator alone). The
illegal combinations: two editors on one file; a
commit by any hand but that of the integrator; the
orchestrator editing at all. Home: 27.1.2; concept
1.2.210.
M6 — the run machine of the harness. States: the
directory of the party before a run; a run in flight under
an attacker-held environment; the directory after.
Moves: run one operation to success (exit 0: the
after-state is exactly the model's prescription); run
to failure (exit 1: nothing written); the steps of the environment between runs. The illegal
combination: a successful run whose after-state no
model run lays down. Home: 27.1.2; concept 1.2.215.
[inferred — drawn from the whole-run theorems]
M7 — the security-game machine. States: a game in
play, the knowledge of the attacker growing by the
questions it may ask. Moves: create parties, establish
keys, open sessions; the oracle answers — corrupt,
reveal, encrypt, open; the joining move, a finished
handshake game promoted into a ratchet game. The
illegal combinations, refused by the game's rules:
revealing the very key under test; corrupting at the moment that would empty the game. Home: 27.1.2; concepts 1.2.219,
1.2.220. [inferred — drawn from the games'
definitions]

Chapter 28 (Study S010). Three machines are drawn from the
essay: the round by which a specification is validated; the
link of refinement between two semantics; and the machine of
elaboration that carries C into Core.
M1 — the validation round of a specification. States: a
specification standing drafted; read for intent; tested
against what implementations do, differential fuzzing among
the tests; proved as a target, implementations shown to meet
it; proved as a basis, useful properties drawn from it. The
moves: each way passed admits the next use of the
specification; a failure at any way sends the draft back to
its makers, and the round begins again. The gap, standing in the telling of the essay itself: the round names no keeper — who
declares a specification validated is left to the stakeholders
of the model — and its first way rests on a reader, whose
reading no machine in the round checks. Home: 28.1.2; concepts
1.2.225, 1.2.223. [stated — the four ways of the essay, drawn in its order]
M2 — the link of refinement. States: an interpreter standing
alone, executable and carrying the details of its running; a
second semantics standing apart, clean and unlinked; the pair
standing linked, the interpreter proved a refinement of the
semantics. The moves: write the second semantics, guided by
the documents of the first; prove the refinement; and, on any
change to either side, the link stands stale until the proof
is run again. The fault the machine itself brought to light:
the expressions that name where a jump may land are gathered
into a map of labels ahead of the work and lifted out of their
place, losing the scope of that place — the design bug for
jumping to labels, found in the gap of the machine. The
illegal combination: reasoning from the clean semantics about
a pair whose link has gone stale. Home: 28.1.2; concept
1.2.228. [stated — drawn from the milestone as the essay tells
it]
M3 — the machine of elaboration. States: a program standing in
C; the same program standing in Core; a trace of the program
running under the interpreter of Core. The moves: parse the
source; elaborate it — the loops and early returns of C made
into the one construct of jumps, run and save; run the
interpreter over what stands in Core. The gap, stated by the
essay itself: the step of elaboration is where the nesting
dies — the trace no longer carries which loop a step belongs
to, and any reasoning that needs the nesting must build it
again from what was annotated, or do without it. Home: 28.1.2;
concepts 1.3.220, 1.2.226. [inferred — drawn from the account the essay gives of the machine]

CHAPTER 29 (Paper 018 — the isotonic estimator under
noise of uneven spread; all stated in the paper but
Machine 3, which is drawn from its proof)
M1 — the test machine. States: a candidate held against
the data, and its running price — the largest charge any
rival lays on it through the sign test. Moves: each
rival charges the sum of the signed gaps at the points
where the two differ; the estimator is any state whose
charge stands within a small constant of the least. On
the monotone class the machine rests at the projection
of the readings, whose price is exactly zero. Home:
29.1.2; concepts 1.2.230, 1.2.233. [stated — section 2.1
and Theorem 2 of the paper]
M2 — the sign-step machine. States: a candidate, a
rival, and the stepped candidate found by moving
against the signs of their difference. Moves: shrink
the step until the stepped candidate stands inside the
class, or find that no positive step does; on the
monotone cone, half the smallest positive gap is always
safe. Where the machine halts inside for every pair,
the projection is the answer of the test. Home: 29.1.2;
concept 1.2.234. [stated — Assumption 1 and the proof of
Theorem 2]
M3 — the loud-point machine. States: a fit tracking the
plateau truth, or raised on the one loud reading. Moves:
the loud reading arrives; the peak may be raised on it
at a total square cost under a quarter of the design;
any fit near the truth pays more than that at the loud
point alone; on an event of chance at least 30 percent
the rule that squares comes to rest raised, and its
l_1-error never closes, while the sign machine never
moves. Home: 29.1.2; concept 1.2.235. [inferred — drawn
from the proof of Theorem 3]
M4 — the hearing machine. States: two running sums on a
line — the flat sum of the readings, and the sum
weighted by the shape. Moves: each reading enters the
flat sum at weight one and the weighted sum at the
value of the shape at its point; which estimator wins
is set by whether the noise stands where the shape
stands large. Home: 29.1.2; concept 1.2.236.
[stated — Proposition 1]

Chapter 30 (Paper 019) — five machines.
M1 — the factor pipeline. States: the factors of the determinantal polynomial, each waiting, tested, computed,
certified, or marked. Moves: Katz's test sends a factor
to the marker 0 or into the computation; the computation
sends it to its certified equation; the output is one
entry per factor, an equation or 0. Home: 30.1.2;
concept 1.2.238. [stated — section 3.6]
M2 — the sampler. States: a prime and a root live, the
points gathered, and a counter of tries since the last
new Gauss image. Moves: each try fixes all variables
but one, takes at most one root, tests smoothness by
the gradient; a new image resets the counter, and the
budget spent at any root kills the whole prime. Gap:
the budget is a count, not a judgment — a sound prime
with sparse points can be abandoned, and no record of
why a prime failed is kept to spend on the next. Home:
30.1.2; concept 1.2.242. [stated — section 3.2]
M3 — the degree search. States: an interval of degrees
with a tested midpoint, and the kernel's size there:
none, one, or several. Moves: search above on none,
below on several, halt at exactly one. Gap: the signal
takes for granted that the kernel's size reads the degree honestly;
with too few or too dependent points a wrong degree
can show a kernel of one — the tenth of surplus guards
it, and the certificate is the only proof. Home:
30.1.2; concept 1.2.243. [stated — section 3.3]
M4 — the gathering of the reconstruction. States: the
retained primes sharing one leading term, what has been
joined so far, and a candidate over K. Moves: a smaller
leading term discards the prime; a larger discards all
states and restarts from that prime; the same joins what
the prime gives and builds the candidate again; the candidate
unchanged after one more prime is handed to
certification. Gap: standing unchanged once is a
stopping rule of habit, not of proof. Home: 30.1.2;
concept 1.2.245. [stated — section 3.4]
M5 — the restart machine. States: running, certified,
failed. Moves: on the failure of certification the
computation stops, the error names the last prime
spent, and the user restarts from the beginning with
the prime floor raised above every spent prime, all
modular data discarded. Gap: the failure state is
total — samples and all that was joined are thrown away
with the rest, though nothing shows they were the
fault (Q99). Home: 30.1.2; concept 1.2.244. [stated —
section 3.6]

Chapter 31 (Paper 020) — four machines.
M1 — the translation recursion. States: a formula in
context under construction, standing at one of its parts.
Moves: at a relation symbol, read the relation function
over the tuples of pieces; at a connective step, pass
through; at a for-every step, join one guarded claim per
piece of the bound sort, each guarded by its domain
formula; the canonical context is carried, with its
repeats struck out, as the walk proceeds. Gap: the
product is fixed only up to a renaming of bound
variables — the output is a class of texts, not a text,
and any hand that runs it must choose representatives
the paper does not choose. Home: 31.1.2. [stated —
section 2.1 and Appendix A]
M2 — the building of the dual. States: a model of the
target theory; the union, kept apart by pieces, of the
sets its domain formulas cut out; the quotient of that
union by the translated identity; the relations read
through the projection. Moves run in one fixed order:
union, then quotient, then projection. Gap: the quotient
state is sound only where the translated identity is an
equivalence on the union — guaranteed for interpretations
by the Domain condition and the translated laws, but the
machine as drawn runs on any translation, and for a
translation that is not identity-respecting the middle
state is not well-defined; the guard is a theorem about
the machine, not a state of it. Home: 31.1.2. [stated —
section 2.4]
M3 — the composition machine. States: a pair of
translations standing end to end; the composite, whose
pieces are pairs; and the two ways of grouping a triple
composite, matched by the matching that groups the pieces
again. Moves: nest the pieces, join the domain formulas
along the translated context, and group again. Gap: that
the machine is associative holds only up to the
ii-isomorphism the matching induces — two runs that
group differently end in states the machine itself
cannot call equal, only isomorphic; the equality of its
states is a fact one level up from the machine. Home:
31.1.3. [stated — sections 3.1 and 3.2]
M4 — the reflection. States: a pre-bicategory, its laws
unchecked, with associators and unitors standing as
isomorphisms; and a locally faithful pseudofunctor from
it into a strict 2-category. Moves: each law of the
target is pulled back along the faithful reading to the
same law in the source, so the standing of the source as
a bicategory is inherited whole. Gap: the inheritance is
all or nothing — the machine has no partial state; the
reading failing to be faithful at one family of ways, or
a law the target itself lacks, voids the whole transfer
at once, and no part of the coherence is kept on its own.
Home: 31.1.3. [inferred — read out of the Reflection
Theorem by Emise, synthesis]

SWEPT, NO MACHINE FOUND
Chapter 4 (Paper 003): its way of working is one pass —
one prompt held fixed, stories written, features drawn
out of the differences, the geometry read off. Nothing in
it keeps a state that later steps consult, so no machine
in the sense of 1.3.85 was found.
Chapter 6 (Paper 005): the paper builds by recipes —
pieces cut, complements taken, sums glued by named maps.
The steps are ordered, but no state is carried that
chooses later behavior; no machine was found.
Chapter 23 (Study S007): the three essays state
a correspondence and two families of models, and
assume no machine; nothing in them keeps a state
that later steps consult, so no machine in the
sense of 1.3.85 was found.
# Area: Statements — the standing record and how to walk it
A STATEMENT, in this phext, is a claim carried by a
scroll. Every claim-bearing scroll holds exactly one
standing, and the record of standing — scroll by
scroll, with the ground of each and a pointer to its
evidence — is Chapter 1, Section 4 (1.4.1-1.4.6). This
scroll is the area's door: it says what the record is and
how to walk it. The record itself is not copied here.

THE FOUR WAYS OF STANDING (rule 14)
TRUE — settled: set so by our own definition, or backed
by a checking receipt we can point to.
FALSE — found against, and kept: a refutation receipt
speaks against it. A false claim is knowledge; it is
retired with its receipt and a pointer, never dropped.
UNKNOWN — written down, not settled: the common standing
of a claim a source makes; open questions; Emise's
reasoned links, marked [synthesis]; weighed estimates.
FICTION — made on purpose and held as made: worked
examples with invented beings, scenes built to show a
shape. Fiction is never set out as fact, and never steps
straight to True.

THE GROUNDS. No standing stands bare; each is given by a
ground that binds what it may be. By definition
(stipulation) and by receipt (verified) may stand True;
by refutation receipt (refuted) stands False; by the
say-so of a source (attributed), by being an open
question (open), by being a reasoned link (synthesis),
and by being a weighed estimate (estimate) stand Unknown;
by being made for show (construction) stands Fiction.
A standing that breaks the row of its ground is a defect
by
the meaning of the words — that is what accurate means
here.

HOW A STANDING CHANGES. Only along allowed steps, and
only when the receipt the step names lands: Unknown to
True (a checking receipt); Unknown to False (a refutation
receipt); True to False (a refutation receipt; the claim
is stepped down, its history kept); False to Unknown (a
receipt is itself found wanting); Fiction to Unknown (a
made scene is taken up as a real proposal); Unknown to
Fiction (a claim is re-marked as show). No step enters
True without a checking receipt. Every step is written
in the record of steps (1.4.6), with its receipt.

THE COUNTS, AT THE NINTH DRAWING (2026-11-09, Paper 008
taken in): the living registry holds 334 entries, one
for each scroll of this phext. True 172 (151 by
definition, 21 by receipt). Unknown 162 (137 by the
say-so of a source, 15 reasoned links, 8 open questions,
2 weighed estimates). False 0. Fiction 0.
Most of what this model holds about the world stands
Unknown, and the counts saying so is the fidelity
working, not failing.

THE COUNTS, AT THE TENTH DRAWING (2026-11-09, Paper 009
taken in): the living registry holds 354 entries, one
for each scroll of this phext. True 179 (157 by
definition, 22 by receipt). Unknown 175 (148 by the
say-so of a source, 16 reasoned links, 9 open questions,
2 weighed estimates). False 0. Fiction 0.
