# Author check — Paper 015 (Frahm and Shepelyansky) (rule 15)

Check number thirteen stands run; the signature scan is
clean and the program stands fully accounted for by its
authors' own lineage.

THE AUTHORS AS READ. Klaus M. Frahm and Dima L.
Shepelyansky, Laboratoire de Physique Theorique,
Universite de Toulouse, CNRS, France — a working pair in
statistical and nonlinear physics with a long joint
record: many-body physics and quantum chaos in the earlier
decades, and since 2024 a dated program carrying
Rayleigh-Jeans thermalization from optical fibers and
oscillator networks into social and economic systems —
wealth thermalization (a journal paper of 2026 on work
posted in 2025), the thermodynamics of world product, the
thermodynamics of voting, and the statistics of wild
mammal biomass. The paper under intake is that program's
move into language, announced in its own text as such.

THE RECORD OF A CORRECTION, STATED PLAINLY. The earlier
claim of the pair in this line — a 'quantum' Gibbs
distribution for wealth — was disputed in a public comment
by Kottos and Shapiro, who showed the correct classical
distribution to be the Rayleigh-Jeans law; the authors'
later work, this paper included, stands in the corrected,
classical line, on the ground the public exchange cleared.
The tracker records the correction as part of the history
of the claim: the law this chapter absorbed is the one
that survived the dispute.

NO EVIDENCE OF PHEXT AWARENESS (rule 15's standing
question). In the full text: the words phext, exocortex,
Bickford and Mirrorborn stand at zero occurrences; 'token'
stands at four, every one in the language-model sense
(words cut into tokens, tokens priced) — a word overlap
with this phext's own trade sense, independently meant.
The reference list, read whole, is physics, economics and
quantitative linguistics: Pareto, Zipf and Simon, the
condensation literature, the authors' own program; nothing
from this house or near it. In Will's public archive: no
occurrence of either surname in his posts, likes, or
messages — the three 'dima' strings the scan caught are a
school name, a programmer's surname, and the handle of a
stranger, none of them the author. On the open web, no
page ties either author to phext or to the exocortex. The
finding is the standing one, stated as rule 15 requires:
no evidence found — not a claim about the authors, a
statement about the record. The machinery is wholly
accounted for by the published program of the pair itself
and by a century of Zipf scholarship they cite; nothing in
the paper needs this phext to explain it, and the
resemblances (an equilibrium read as a population of parts
under two quantities) are the ordinary convergence of
statistical mechanics, independently derived.
# Synthesis — what Paper 015 does to the map [synthesis]

(a) A whole physics arrives, and arrives as one object.
Cluster R is founded not by a technique but by an
equilibrium: one hypothesis, one analogy, one law, and the
measurement apparatus that prices them. The map of this
tracker now holds a field whose primitive is a settled
distribution — everything else in the map counts,
searches, proves, or pays; this cluster is at rest. The
gap is worth naming plainly: the other clusters of this
tracker all describe systems that do something over time;
R describes the state such systems are found in, and its
open questions (Q65-Q68) are exactly about the missing
doing.

(b) The concentration shape now stands in three fields.
The top of a set holding the mass of the whole was held
for attention (1.2.70), for experts (1.2.31), and for pure
mathematics' own measures; this paper measures it for the
vocabulary of whole books — 86 to 96 percent of tokens in
the top tenth of words, a Gini past the wealth of the
world — and gives it a mechanism-shaped explanation: a
kept total under a floor. Whether the explanation travels
to the other fields is not this paper's claim and not the
claim of this chapter; the recurrence of the shape is the
finding of this tracker.

(c) The fit discipline is a receipt this tracker can
reuse. Spend the parameters on one curve, matched to the
data's own one-number summary; be checked on curves never
touched; let the rivals' extra parameters stand as their
confession. That is the shape of a claim that prices
itself, and it is the same shape the tracker asks of its
own Oracle: the forecast was spent on the skim, and the
reading checked it on ground the skim never saw.

(d) The paper's silence is its frontier. An equilibrium
with no dynamics, a temperature with no measure taken
before the fit, a mixture with no theory of mixing, a
growth with no clock: Q65-Q68 are not arguments against
the paper — they are the shape of the program's next
decade, drawn by what this one paper does not draw.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 015)

Q65 — THE UNNAMED INTERACTION. The thermalization of words
is claimed from the shape of the equilibrium, but no
interaction between words is ever written: no coupling, no
exchange rule, no dynamics a reader could run. Is there a
dynamics of writing or reading under which a vocabulary
provably comes to rest at the Rayleigh-Jeans state — or is
WOTH only the curve described once more, however exact the
fit? (From 1.2.159; the guard of M1 at 22.1.2 is asserted,
not enforced.)

Q66 — THE TEMPERATURE OF A TEXT. The scaled energy epsilon
is fit per corpus, tiny and varying over two orders of
magnitude, and nothing in the paper ties it to the
measurable properties of a text — its length, its
vocabulary growth, its author's habits. Can the
temperature of a text be predicted before it is fit, and
what would take the temperature of a book before it is
fit?

Q67 — MIXING AND STYLE. The Corpus of many authors stands
off the family line, its excess inequality laid on the
mixing of styles. Is an author's style a temperature? Does
a mixture of two Rayleigh-Jeans equilibria stay
Rayleigh-Jeans, and if not, into what parts does the curve
of the Corpus itself fall?

Q68 — THE MISSING GROWTH. A book is in fact written word
by word, but the equilibrium is claimed over the finished
book. Mid-writing, does the growing text already stand
near its equilibrium and approach it — the machine M3 of
22.1.2, which the paper needs and does not draw — or is
the thermal state only a property of finished works? The
answer decides whether thermalization is a process or a
portrait.
