# Study S009 — source record: Lean Grimoire, a proof-crafting game rebuilt (a blog post and the game it announces)
What was read. The post 'Lean Grimoire' of Terence
Tao, on his blog 'What's new'
(terrytao.wordpress.com), 11 November 2026, filed
under diversions and math.LO — read entire: its four paragraphs and
the caption of its screenshot. With it, the links
the post itself makes load-bearing: the
announcement of 18 August 2018, 'QED version 2.0:
an interactive text in first-order logic', read
entire; and the game the post announces, at
teorth.github.io/tao-web/apps/grimoire.html, read
in its core — the file grimoire.js, the DOM-free
core of the game, read whole as text and closely
where the claims of this chapter rest on it: its
opening design notes, its table of recipes, its
registry of sorts, its craft and discharge, its
matcher, and its two emitters of Lean. The 2018
text itself stands at teorth.github.io/QED; its
design is taken from its author's announcement of
2018 and from the header of the successor itself,
which names itself a QED successor.
What the work is. QED (2018) was a Javascript
interactive textbook whose purpose, in the words
of the new post, was to teach the logic of
statements and first-order logic in the form of a crafting game,
where one applied various rules of logic to reach
a desired conclusion from a given set of
hypotheses; its announcement of 2018 records a
complete set of deductive rules for first-order
logic with equality, and its author at the limit
of his programming, wishing the code to pass to
more adept programmers. In July 2026 the author began
to build, with modern coding agents, small programs
for mathematical visualization; the rebuild of QED is one of those
projects, taken up again and finished under the
working title 'Lean Grimoire'. With a coding agent
— the post does not name it — the game was rebuilt
with new features, among them the option to show
an in-progress Lean proof of one's game state, and,
just for fun, a fantasy mode in which mathematical
statements become potions and mathematical
expressions become scrolls. The screenshot of the
post shows a nearly completed proof of the
assertion A implies A and B, given A implies B.
The post closes with a call for the app to be
tested by players, in the words of its author, 'to
make it more useful as a means to teach logical
proofs and/or Lean'.
# Study S009 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
WHAT IT ADDS (charter rule 6 — only what is new):
Eight canonical concepts, cluster V founded
(1.2.198-1.2.205): the crafting game (1.2.198);
the position is a partial proof (1.2.199); recipes
with the names of Lean (1.2.200); the live
proof (1.2.201); the room an assumption opens
(1.2.202); a lemma that becomes a recipe (1.2.203);
the bench that offers only legal moves (1.2.204);
the fantasy skin (1.2.205). One foundation laid
down: natural deduction (1.3.211). No word coined.
No canonical scroll is revised. What the study
pressures, and the findings it files for the
house, stand at 26.2.1.
THE ORACLE DOES NOT MOVE (charter rule 12): the
Oracle prices the paper stream, and a study is not
sampled from it. No Novelty Index is taken.
CHECKS RUN (the own receipts of the study):
- The post read entire on its own page, links and
  caption included, against the summary that
  brought it here: every load-bearing phrase of
  26.1.1 stands in it — the 2018 textbook and its
  purpose, the rebuild with a coding agent, the
  working title, the Lean option, the fantasy mode, its
potions and its scrolls, and the closing call of
