# Study S009 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-13. The check is kept where the study chapters keep theirs: this scroll, 26.1.3.
WHO THE WORK NAMES. The post and the game stand
under one name: Terence Tao, of the University of
California, Los Angeles, writing on his own blog.
The rebuild was made, in the words of the post, 'with a
coding agent'; the agent is not named, and this
check names none.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The whole text of the post
and the whole text of the core of the game
(grimoire.js) searched by machine for the
signature terms of this tracker: phext, phextura,
Bickford, Mirrorborn, Exocortex, SBOR, CYOA. No
hit. The word scrolls stands once in the post, as
fantasy dress for mathematical expressions — a
common word in an unrelated sense; in this house
the same word names a plane of text at a
coordinate, and no road runs from the one use to
the other. The meeting is at the word only, and
is recorded here so that no later reading mistakes
it for a tie. (2) The post carries no reference
list to read; its links are its own — to the game,
to the announcement of 2018, and to the author's
own pages. (3) The open web, searched for the name
of the author with phext: no tie found; the
results return the public record of a mathematician
whose published life is among the largest of his
field, and nothing in what the search returned
touches the work of this house. (4) The Twitter
archive of Will on disk, searched in full (29,539
tweets) for the author: three hits on the bare
name — a news note of 2015, that the author had
solved a famous problem, shared from a news
account; a jest of 2024 addressed to another man,
in which the name stands for strength itself;
and a like-sounding acronym of his own making
in 2025 (TAOPDD), which is not the man. No
exchange, no reply, no thread, in either
direction.
VERDICT. No evidence found — stated as a finding:
no tie of the author to phext or to the work of
Will, and no use of that work without naming it.
The distance is a reader's, both ways: Will noted
the author's fame once, a decade ago, as a reader
of news; the author gives no sign of knowing this
house at all.
# Study S009 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Four readings, stated as the readings
of Emise, not as claims of the work. The first and
third are filed as FINDINGS for the house, in the
manner of Chapter 23: they amend nothing.
(1) FINDING — THE PROOF OF THE KEEPING, KEPT LIVE.
The standing record of this tracker is proved the
way a finished proof is printed: a registry is
drawn, a Lean model is proved over it, and the
pair stands until the next drawing. The game of
this study keeps the same kind of object the other
way — a proof rendered at every state of the work,
its unfinished places standing in the text as
sorry, named as gaps rather than hidden by polish.
Finding, not amendment: a record whose in-progress
proof state could be viewed as it is written —
the Lean state of each drawing shown with its places not yet
proved standing in it — would hand a reader of the
record what the game hands the student: the state,
legible at every step, including the steps not yet
taken. The charter is not changed by this study;
the finding is filed where the keeping can use it.
(2) HELD GROUND, WEIGHED. Cluster M — learning by
building (1.2.89-1.2.97) — SUPPORTED, and given a
second instrument: the crafting game is building
whose every step is a checked deduction, so
what the learner makes and what the learner proves are one
artifact. Cluster Q — theorem proving by machine
(1.2.139-1.2.158) — SUPPORTED as the complementary
pole: Paper 014's machines prove so that no human
need hold the moves; this game exists to teach a
human to hold them, with the same Lean as the
shared object. The model keeps the pair as the two
poles of formal proof — who holds the moves, and
how the holding is learned. Cluster S — logic read
as structure (1.2.171-1.2.175) — stands neighbour
by subject and divided by stance: there, logic is
read in other structures; here, logic is done, and
its structures are positions in a game. And
cluster B is touched once, lightly: the agent that rebuilt
this textbook was a coding agent — in Study
S008 the harness stood measured as a learner and
found at zero; here a harness stands as the maker
of a teaching instrument. The contrast is noted;
no claim is carried on it.
(3) FINDING — THE BENCH AND THE RECORD. The game
never lets an illegal state be written: its
bench offers only legal moves, and detection has
nothing to detect. The standing record of this
tracker takes the opposite road: any standing may
be written, and accuracy over what was written is
proved after the fact, at each drawing. The study
files the contrast without a winner. Prevention
fits a closed world, whose moves can be listed in
advance; the world of the record is open — new kinds of
claim arrive with new work — and a bench for it
would have to be rebuilt at every arrival. But the
game prices what the road of the record costs: every
after-the-fact proof is work a bench never does,
and the price is paid at every drawing this
tracker makes. The question stands open at Q84.
(4) THE SKIN, AND THE OWN WAGER OF THIS HOUSE. The
fantasy mode is the author's own demonstration,
offered as fun, that dress is a view: rename every
noun and the machine stands. This house wagers the
same separation in earnest elsewhere — a paper's
state against its renderings — and the study notes
the resonance at the strength it carries: a shape
shared between independent works, which is all the
author check at 26.1.3 found, anywhere, between
this work and this house.

Open questions added by this study:
Q81. The game teaches two things at once: the
crafting of proofs, and the reading of a position
— every state stands legible, its gaps named.
Which of the two carries the learning? The
testing by players that the author calls for could be shaped
to tell them apart; until it is run, the design
bets on both.
Q82. The recipes carry the names of Lean, so the
game's skill is addressed in the terms of the prover from
the first move. Does a player who masters the game
then read real Lean proofs — is the transfer the
names promise real, and by what measure would it
be known?
Q83. A solved lemma becomes a recipe through a matcher, with no test of worth. Where
should the line stand between a lemma worth
promoting and one too narrow to reuse — and what
should hold that gate: the shape of the lemma, the
judgment of the player, or a count of its uses?
Q84. The bench keeps a closed world sound by
never offering an illegal move; the standing
record keeps an open one by proving accuracy after
the fact. Could the writing of a standing be benched
— an illegal standing never offered at the moment of
writing — or does an audit need the possibility of
error standing in the record, to check against?
