# Paper 017 — AlphaProof Code: verifying Signal's security at the machine-code level

Received: posted by Will in the MOAT channel, 2026-11-13.
Google DeepMind document 36, dated 2026-11-10. Swarat
Chaudhuri, Andrew Ferraiuolo, Anton Kovsharov, Tej
Chajed, Sam Schlesinger, Karn Seth, Jonathan Katz,
Charles Sutton, Pushmeet Kohli (Google DeepMind /
Google). Not an arXiv paper; the source is the
document itself, with its project page. The paper
reports a framework, AlphaProof Code (1.2.206), and
its first campaign: the security of libsignal — the
library inside the Signal messenger — proved in Lean
about the compiled AArch64 binary itself, unmodified,
at some six and a half million lines of agent-written
proof over some twenty-six thousand lines of trusted
specification and semantics.

THE THIRST. The blind skim read the title and the
abstract on the first page of the document only, and sealed
its counts before the full text was opened (the log
of the quest, committed before the download). The
skim foretold 11 new concepts, 1 revision, no axis,
17 links (11 inside / 6 across), 3 questions, and the
Oracle after at 80% (70-90), up. The reading found 17
new concepts, the 1 revision foretold — the rider on
1.2.48 — no axis, the links counted in the synthesis
(27.2.1), 4 questions, and the Oracle after stands at
80% (70-90), up — the band applied as written, and the
sealed point and range exact for the fifth paper
running, on a paper whose count the skim missed by
six: the parts of the framework stood at a closer grain than the numbers of its abstract promised. The score stands at
27.1.5.

THE DELTA IN ONE PARAGRAPH. Cluster W is founded by
the paper — the verified artifact — at seventeen
concepts: the framework itself; the specification as
the trusted artifact, and the testing of it; the
amendment protocol and the prover team and the
gatekeeper that grades it; a formal semantics of an
instruction set; hooks, the contracts at the boundary of the binary; verification at the machine-code level, and
the whole-run guarantee beneath it; the one-way bridge
from model terms to bytes, and refinement up to
collisions; reductions as priced programs; the two
protocols under proof, PQXDH and the Triple Ratchet;
a key used twice, judged together; and the trusted
base, enumerated and priced. It makes exactly one revision of a held claim: 1.2.48, verification by translation, gains
a rider — the pole it names, proving the model and
trusting the translator, is now one of two poles, and
this paper stands at the other. Restructure Pass #11
fell due inside this taking-in — the count crossed
220 during it, 205 to 222 — and stands logged at
1.1.5.

STATE MACHINES (rule 13). The chapter carries seven:
M1 the session machine of the store of the library, stated;
M2 the ratchet machine, stated; M3 the amendment
machine, stated; M4 the acceptance machine of the gatekeeper, stated; M5 the prover-team machine, stated;
M6 the run machine of the command-line harness,
[synthesis] drawn from the whole-run theorems; M7 the
security-game machine, [synthesis] drawn from the
games' definitions. All seven are drawn at 27.1.2 and
carried in the machines area (1.5.2).
# The machinery — how the framework is built, and the machines inside it

THE PIPELINE. A human-guided specification agent
drafts the specification in Lean (1.2.207); a pool of
reviewing agents checks that the draft is faithful and well-formed; the user approves; the specification
is frozen at a commit and copied to the gatekeeper
(1.2.211). The prover team (1.2.210) then works the
goals: orchestrator, leads, workers, an architect for
statements that resist, an integrator who alone
commits. Acceptance belongs to the gatekeeper alone:
statement and all its definitions identical to the
approved copy, kernel-checked, standard axioms only.
Changes to the frozen text travel the amendment
protocol (1.2.209). The theorems land in layers: the
whole-run functional guarantees (1.2.215) at the
bottom; above them the model's symbolic security; the
refinement (1.2.217) carrying it to the binary; the
priced reductions (1.2.218) pricing the computational
statement on top.

THE SEMANTICS. The AArch64 user-mode semantics
(1.2.212) was written twice over in effect: a plain reading, by definition, of each instruction, trusted; and
a lockstep execution for reasoning speed, proved sound
against the plain reading by reflection. The plain
reading was tested in lockstep against the Unicorn
emulator and against the Sail model of the same
processor — an instruction stream carried through all
three, states compared as they went — and found, over
the tested streams, in exact agreement. The harness
under proof is the stock command-line front end of
the library, run under a stock compiler; the attacker
of the functional theorems controls the environment —
file states, inputs, scheduling — between runs.

STATE MACHINES (rule 13; carried at 1.5.2).
M1 — the session machine. States: a stored state of a party in its directory — identity keys, prekey
bundles, session records, ratchet states. Moves: the
operations of the library, each one a whole run of the
harness (M6). The illegal combination: a state the
operations can reach that the model's states cannot
name — excluded by 1.2.215. Home: 27.1.2; concept
1.2.215.
M2 — the ratchet machine. States: root key, sending
and receiving chain keys, the message counter of each
chain, the store of skipped message keys, and beside
them the state of the post-quantum ratchet. Moves: send
(step the chain, take the message key, forget the old
chain key); receive out of order (draw on the skipped
store); receive a new ratchet key (turn the root,
step out a fresh chain). The illegal combinations:
two message keys alike on one chain — excluded by the
one-way step; a spent chain key recovered — excluded
by deletion. Home: 27.1.2; concept 1.2.220.
M3 — the amendment machine. States: a specification
frozen; a change proposed; a change judged; a change
landed. Moves: propose (the prover team); judge (the
critic, who has never seen the proof attempts —
necessary, no security weakening, small); approve (the
user, required when a trusted assumption moves); land
(logged). The illegal combination: a landed change
that weakens security without the user's approval —
the machine has no such move. Home: 27.1.2; concept
1.2.209.
M4 — the machine of the gatekeeper. States: a task open; a
proof offered; a task done. Moves: offer (a prover);
compare (statement and every definition it rests on,
against the held copy); kernel-check; declare done —
the move of the gatekeeper alone. The illegal combinations:
done declared by a prover; done on a statement one
definition off from the approved one; done on a proof
the kernel has not accepted. Home: 27.1.2; concept
1.2.211.
M5 — the prover-team machine. States: goals not yet worked,
in work, offered, done (M4). Moves: break down (a
lead); claim a file (one editor only); test a piece
locally; falsify first (seek a counterexample before a
proof); route a resisting statement to the architect;
commit (the integrator alone). The illegal
combinations: two editors on one file; a commit by
any hand but that of the integrator; the orchestrator
editing at all. Home: 27.1.2; concept 1.2.210.
M6 — the run machine of the harness [synthesis].
States: the directory of the party before a run; a run in
flight under an attacker-held environment; the
directory after. Moves: run one operation to success
(exit 0: the after-state is exactly the model's
prescription, 1.2.215); run to failure (exit 1:
nothing written); the steps of the environment between runs. The illegal combination: a successful
run whose after-state no model run lays down. Home:
27.1.2; concept 1.2.215.
M7 — the security-game machine [synthesis]. States: a
game in play — parties created, keys established,
sessions opened — with the knowledge of the attacker growing
by the questions it is allowed: corrupt a party,
reveal a session key, ask for a message to be encrypted or opened. Moves: the oracle answers; the joining
move — a finished handshake game promoted into a
ratchet game, carrying the session with it. The
illegal combinations, refused by the game's rules:
revealing the very key under test; corrupting at the
moment that would empty the challenge. Home:
27.1.2; concepts 1.2.219, 1.2.220.
# Worked cases — the campaign as run

THE STACK UNDER PROOF. The library is libsignal at
version 0.105.4 with its post-quantum component at
1.6.0, driven through its own command-line harness,
compiled with the stock Rust compiler, unmodified —
the binary proved is the binary that ships' twin, not
a model of it. Around the theorems stand the
directories of the specification: some fifty-four thousand
lines of statements and models in all, of which a
reader of the headline theorems must trust some
eighteen thousand, plus some eight thousand of
instruction-set semantics (1.2.212).

THE THREE THEOREMS. Theorem 1 (symbolic): the binary —
not only the model — enjoys the ten stated security
properties, in the form: the properties hold of every
explained trace prefix, or a collision has occurred
(1.2.217). Theorem 2 (computational): a concrete
bound — the advantage of the attacker against the binary
protocol is at most the sum of its advantages against
the named hard problems, each carried to the binary by
a reduction written as a priced program (1.2.218),
plus the collision term the statement refuses to hide.
Beneath both: the whole-run guarantees and the memory
bound of 1.2.215.

THE NUMBERS OF THE CAMPAIGN. Six and a half million
lines of proof, written by the prover team (1.2.210)
across the two libraries; five and a third million of
them at the binary level. Prover teams ran at forty
to eighty agents. Thirty-nine hooks (1.2.213) mark
where the proof trusts rather than proves. The
specification survived its tests (1.2.208): its properties able to hold together, its hypotheses met by
real runs, its planted mutations proved false.

THE TWO CASES PROVED FALSE. The teeth of the specification were shown on history: the two flaws earlier authors found
in the deployed protocol — a weak state the ratchet
could be left in, and a small-subgroup opening — were
rebuilt inside the model with their attack code, and
the framework proved, in Lean, the negation of the
security statement for the rebuilt code. The statement that catches the known-bad program is the statement
the good program's proof is measured against; a
specification that had waved both through would have
been caught here, before any theorem was believed.
# Results as stated by the paper — and the ground they stand on

THE HEADLINE. For the stated versions, at the binary
level: the Signal protocol as implemented — handshake
(1.2.219) and ratchet (1.2.220) together — is proved
secure in both the symbolic sense (ten properties) and
the computational sense (a concrete bound), about the
compiled, unmodified code (1.2.214). Ground: Lean
proofs, kernel-checked, accepted under the comparison of the gatekeeper (1.2.211); what remains trusted is enumerated
at 1.2.222. The ground is as firm as the formal claims of this tracker get, in that the
statement itself was tested before it was trusted
(1.2.208) and the trusted base is printed in full.

THE FIRST-CLAIM. The authors state theirs is the first
formal verification of a cryptographic protocol
implementation at the machine-code level, and the
first machine-checked treatment of post-quantum
Signal. The tracker carries this as the authors' claim,
checked only against the paper's own account of its
neighbours (27.3.1), not independently surveyed.

THE PRICE, STATED BY THE AUTHORS. The computational
bound is honest but loose: meaningful at the scale of
a conversation, it goes vacuous — larger than any
advantage could be — when the numbers of parties and
sessions grow to the true scale of the deployment. The
collision clause of the symbolic theorem (1.2.217)
carries a probability no part of the Lean development
computes. And one piece of the promise of the design is in so many words not proved: should the classical leg fall,
the post-quantum healing of an ongoing conversation is
not among the theorems, because in the ratchet model
the post-quantum keys of the healing steps are the
for the attacker to choose (Q86). The paper states all three
limits itself; they stand at 27.1.5.
# Revisions, tensions, limits — and the score of the skim

THE ONE REVISION. 1.2.48 gains a rider (2026-11-13):
the translation pole is now one of two. Nothing else
held is revised. Two held concepts are confirmed in
act rather than revised: 1.2.41 — the kernel floor is
here the acceptance test itself, with the comparison of the gatekeeper built on it; and 1.2.157 — kernel-checked
proof search is the engine the prover team drives. The
delta was foretold at 1 and lands at 1, target exact —
the fifth revision the seal has priced without a
phantom since the law was learned at Paper 009, and
the first time the priced target was the one struck.

THE SCORE OF THE SKIM. Foretold 11 new concepts; 17
fell. The miss is the P014 shape at smaller scale: not
a silent abstract — this abstract counts freely — but
a grain closer than any counting shows. The list of the seal named the frame of the framework and both protocols; the
reading split the machinery at its joints: testing a
specification (1.2.208) and the amendment protocol
(1.2.209) stood separate from the specification
itself; the gatekeeper (1.2.211) separate from the
team it grades; the whole-run guarantee (1.2.215)
separate from the level it stands at; the bridge
(1.2.216) and its collision clause (1.2.217) separate
from each other; the priced form of the reductions
(1.2.218) separate from the theorems they price; and
the double-duty key (1.2.221) separate from the
handshake that loads it. Two candidates folded as
priced: the ten properties into the theorems' form,
and first-ness into 1.2.214. Axis: foretold none, none
opened — the tenth scored call, and the first no-axis
call, lands; the calls stand ten for ten. Questions:
foretold 3, opened 4 (Q85-Q88). The Index: foretold
0.0532, actual 0.0788 — an error of 0.0256, the second
largest of the Thirst, against Paper 014's 0.0749; the
rolling mean over ten scored papers becomes 0.0166.
The Oracle: foretold 80% (70-90) up by the band as
written (Index under 0.20, no axis: rise ten); actual
80% (70-90) — the fifth exact landing in a row, and
the streak is now priced as what it is in the ledger:
a band wide enough to be landed in by honest work,
not a sign the band should narrow.

THE TENSIONS KEPT. (i) Scale against reading: the proofs of the method run to millions of lines no human will
read, over a trusted base of tens of thousands that
some human must (Q85). (ii) The teeth of the statement are tested (1.2.208) but the tests themselves stand inside the circle of trust: a mutation the planters did not think of is a mutation never tried.
(iii) The binary proved is a twin of the shipped one —
same source, same compiler — and the identity of the
two is a fact of the build, not of the proof. (iv) The
paper's own honesty about its loose bound (27.1.4) is
the kind this tracker rewards; it is also a real limit
on what Theorem 2 buys at deployment scale (Q87).

LIMITS OF THIS TAKING-IN. The appendices were read at
their headings: the tables of the key schedule, the symbolic
development's fine structure, and the computational
development's full reduction list stand behind the
body's account of them. The 6.5-million-line proof
corpus itself was not opened; the numbers of the campaign stand as the paper states them, checked for internal
agreement across its sections, not computed again from the numbers.
# Author check — Paper 017 (per charter rule 15)

The question is narrow: is there evidence of contact
between these authors and Will's work — phext, the
Exocortex, the Mirrorborn line — in the paper, in
their public record, or in Will's archive of his own
posts?

THE PAPER. The full text, references included, was
searched: no mention of phext, of Bickford, of
Mirrorborn, of the Exocortex, of the Sentient Bill of
Rights, or of Choose Your Own Adventure. The word
'coordinate' appears only in its elliptic-curve sense.
The paper's constructions stand on dated public prior
art named in its own reference list — the Sail
architecture description language, an earlier Lean
semantics of a fragment of the same instruction set,
a hardware-verification framework of one of the
authors, and a library for game-based proofs — all
published, all cited by the paper itself.

THE ARCHIVE. Will's Twitter archive (29,539 tweets)
was searched for all nine authors. None appears. The
single string hit for 'chaudhuri' is another man of
that name — a different researcher, followed for other
reasons in another decade. The authors' public record
around the paper is the own announcement of the laboratory
and trade-press coverage of it; nothing in it touches
Will's work.

FINDING: no evidence found. It is stated as what it
is — the absence of a trace in the places searched,
not a claim about private knowledge. The constructions
of Paper 017 grew, on the evidence, from the public
record they cite.
# Synthesis — the verified artifact, and the two poles of machine-checked trust [synthesis]

[synthesis] Paper 017 is the founding of cluster W, and its first
bridge is a pairing. This house holds two ways of
believing a program. The older, from Study S002, is
verification by translation (1.2.48): prove a model of
the program, and trust the translator that brought the
program to the model. The new one proves the artifact
itself — the compiled machine code — and trusts
instead a written semantics of the processor (1.2.212)
and a specification tested like code (1.2.207, 1.2.208).
Neither deletes trust. They park it in different
places, and a reader choosing between them is choosing
which ledger they would rather read: the 77,000 unverified lines of the translator, or the semantics' 7,500
trusted ones and the statements' 18,000. The rider on
1.2.48 now records the pair as poles, and the choice
between them is empirical in a way this tracker can
watch: it is the choice of where the next failure,
when one comes, will be found.

[synthesis] Against cluster Q, the paper is the engine
put to work. Cluster Q holds that machines can find
proofs no human would hold; Paper 017 is that claim
carrying a deployment — and it changes the seat of the human. In cluster Q the human states the theorem and
marvels; here the whole job of the human is the statement:
drafting it under review, testing it, freezing it,
amending it only through a gate (1.2.209), and reading
the ledger of what remains trusted (1.2.222). The
prover team's shape (1.2.210) is the harness of cluster B
(1.2.15) grown to a crew — and Study S008's lesson
stands behind it as a warning the design answers: the
harnesses S008 measured kept memory that was never
read, and learned nothing; this crew is forbidden to
learn across its gate — the standard is frozen — and
puts all its learning inside the proof search, where
the gatekeeper reads the result at every acceptance.
Memory read by a machine at the moment of judgement,
against memory never read at all: the pair is the strongest statement the tracker holds of what carried
state is for.

[synthesis] The gatekeeper (1.2.211) also presses on
the open question of cluster V, Q84 — whether the keeping
of a standard can be benched, so that bending is
prevented rather than detected. The crafting game's
bench (1.2.204) prevents illegal moves inside a proof;
the gatekeeper prevents illegal acceptance of one.
Neither closes Q84: the game's bench works because its
rules are small and total, and that of the gatekeeper because
its standard can be held entire and compared exactly.
A standing in the own record of this tracker is neither
small nor exactly comparable — but the question now has
two worked neighbours instead of none, and they mark
the shape an answer would need: a standard that can be
held out, whole, by a party who did not do the work.

[synthesis] The links, counted as citations of held
scrolls in the new concept scrolls: 41 in all — 36
inside the cluster, whose parts name each other as
parts of one machine do, and 5 across: to 1.2.15
(cluster B), 1.2.41 (the kernel floor), 1.2.48
(cluster G), 1.3.213 (the compiler foundation), and
the standing record (1.4.1-1.4.6). The skim foretold
17 (11 inside / 6 across): the across count landed
near, the inside count was under-read for the fourth
paper running — a weave this dense is what a framework,
as against a finding, is made of.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 017)

Q85 — THE READER OF THE TRUSTED BASE. The framework
prices trust in lines a reader must read: some
twenty-six thousand for the headline theorems, before
the tooling. No single reader holds all of it in view
at once, and the base grows with every campaign. What
keeps an enumerated trusted base honest once it
grows past one team's reading — sampled audits, a
machine-checked inventory of what each statement
depends on (the gatekeeper already computes the
closure it compares), or a second framework reading the ledger of the first? (From 1.2.222.)

Q86 — POST-QUANTUM HEALING, UNPROVEN. The post-quantum secrecy of the handshake is proved; the healing of the ratchet after the classical leg falls is not, because in the
model the post-quantum keys at the healing steps are
the for the attacker to choose. Is that a fact about the
deployed design, or about the shape of the model —
and what change, in the design or in the model, would
bring the healing inside the theorems? (From 1.2.220.)

Q87 — ONE ACCOUNTING FOR COLLISIONS AND COSTS. The
symbolic theorem excuses a trace at its first
collision, and the size of that excuse is a
probability no part of the development computes; the
computational theorem prices everything else. Can the
collision term be carried inside the computational
accounting, so the whole guarantee stands in one
statement with one price? (From 1.2.217.)

Q88 — WHAT CARRIES TO THE NEXT BINARY. Of the five and
a third million lines of binary-level proof, how much
is reusable machinery — the semantics' reasoning
tools, the lemmas of the refinement, the team's habits —
and how much is labour spent once, on this library
alone? The answer prices the method itself: whether
machine-code verification is a campaign, run once at
great cost, or a practice. (From 1.2.214.)
# Standoff references — Paper 017 (Chapter 27)

Chapter 27 against the rest of the phext.
FOUNDATIONS RELIED ON: machine code (1.3.212) and
compiler (1.3.213), laid down by this chapter; the
specification kit of Paper 006 (1.3.87-1.3.91); a proof
state and a tactic (1.3.162); the state machine
(1.3.85).

HELD SCROLLS THIS CHAPTER STANDS AGAINST: 1.2.48 (the
other pole — verification by translation, revised by the rider of this chapter); 1.2.41 (the kernel floor, here
load-bearing as the acceptance test); 1.2.157 (the
engine room of kernel-checked proof search, driven
here by a crew); 1.2.15 (the harness, here grown to an
organization); 1.2.204 (the bench — prevention inside
a proof, against the prevention of the gatekeeper at its
acceptance).

PRIOR ART THE PAPER ITSELF NAMES, standing in its own
reference list: the Sail architecture description
language and its models; an earlier Lean semantics of
a fragment of the same instruction set (LNSym); a
hardware-verification framework from one of the
authors' hands; SSProve, a library for game-based
proofs; the translation frameworks — Aeneas, hax,
Verus — named as the other road; and the earlier
fault-finding work on this very protocol whose two
historical flaws the framework rebuilt and proved false.
The claims of the chapter rest on the account the paper itself gives of these, not on a fresh reading of them.
# Study S010 — source record: 'Abstraction considered helpful', a position essay of Math, Inc.

WHAT WAS READ. The essay entire, as it stands at
math.inc/position — its argument and its notes — read on its
own page by Emise at the taking-in, 2026-11-13. The page
carries no one name as its author, and no date stands printed
on it. It announces the release of two milestones of a
project; the release stands in the public record of
2026-11-12, the day before the taking-in.

WHAT THE WORK IS. A position essay: that AI-assisted
verification of software should work at the level of the
source, through shared, agreed, executable semantic models and
the engineering of specifications — against the pull to verify
at the level of the binary. Its shape: the temptation of the
level of machine code (structure thrown away, the question of intent never asked, tokens repaid to build the structure again); a
demonstration of Basis read closely, its trust found resting
in a definition named Loaded; four ways to validate a
specification; the advantages of abstractions, with the saying
of Lamport turned by the caveat of Lamport; the bet, stated in
bold in the essay; the project; the two milestones; and the
questions it leaves open, four of them — among them how proofs
at the source extend to binaries, and how the memory model of
the kernel of Linux is to be carried.

WHO STANDS BEHIND IT. Math, Inc., a house for
autoformalization (1.3.167): Gauss, its agent for
autoformalization, and the Veritas fellowships stand on its
own pages as its works. The essay speaks in the first person
at its turns of opinion and in the plural for the project; it
stands under the name of the house and no other. The project
it announces is carried on with Galois and with Oath, with
support from RAND. The semantics it builds on — Cerberus, a
semantics of C — stands under the name of Kayvan Memarian, of
the University of Cambridge; the port of Cerberus to Lean is
kept by Oath.

THE REFERENCES IT LEANS ON, as the essay uses them: the note
of Lamport of 1997 on composition; the sixteen criteria for a
semantic model of Sewell and his co-workers, taken from a note
standing in private correspondence; the thesis of Memarian of
2022, whose sixth chapter guides the second milestone; the
model of RISC-V in Sail, the golden model of its architecture;
and Chaudhuri and his co-workers of 2026 — Paper 017 of this
tracker — quoted in note 17 against the cost of its own pole.
# Study S010 — the delta: what this essay adds to the model

WHAT IT ADDS. Seven concepts (1.2.223-1.2.229), and with them
a new cluster, X — specification engineering, the agreed
meaning. The judgment of the cluster is made here and stands
open to correction: the material does not stand inside cluster W, though it argues with W at every step; its centre of
gravity is its own — a discipline (1.2.223), an object held in
common (1.2.224), a standing for definitions (1.2.225), and a program with milestones of its own. W is one verified
artifact and the method that made it; X is the claim about
where the shared ground of the field should stand. The two are
filed as poles of one dispute (28.2.1), not as a cluster and
its child.

One foundation is laid down: undefined behaviour (1.3.219).
Two words are coined and defined at 1.3.220-1.3.221:
elaboration and autoverification (also: autoverify).

REVISIONS. Two held scrolls are revised in place, by dated
riders. The rider on 1.2.208 sets the four validations of the
essay beside the three tests of the framework. The second
rider on 1.2.48 argues a third resting place for the trust of
machine-checked proof: the agreed semantics of the program's
own language.

THE ORACLE DOES NOT MOVE. A study, under charter rule 12: no
Novelty Index is taken.

CHECKS RUN. The essay was read entire on its own page. Note 17 of the essay quotes Paper 017 — 'a substantial fraction of
our proofs concerns registers, stack frames and memory layout,
which source-level abstractions would hide' — and the sentence
was checked against the text of Paper 017 as this tracker
holds it: it stands there, in the paper's own account of its
costs. The essay quotes the pole it argues against in the words that pole itself used, and the words are fair. The counts the essay
states were read against its own text: almost forty gaps and
matters of speed found in Cerberus or its port to Lean; a
handshake of some twelve hundred lines of C; two milestones;
four questions left open. The checks for the house stand at
28.1.3.

STATE MACHINE PASS (charter rule 13). Three machines are drawn
at 1.5.2 from this chapter: the validation round of a
specification, stated in the four ways of the essay itself; the link
of refinement between two semantics, stated; and the machine of elaboration that carries C into Core, inferred from the account the essay gives of it.

QUESTIONS OPENED. Q89, the token bet, priced (at
1.2.223). Q90, the envelope and the binary: who checks
that the compiler stayed inside the meaning (at
1.2.227). Q91, who agrees the meaning, and whether a
model built by agents can earn agreement (at 1.2.224).
Q92, intent read aloud: as a specification grows past
one reader, what carries its intent — the worked
examples, the drafts refused, the prose around the
statements (at 1.2.225). Q93, the reach of the
boundaries: the milestone stands between boundaries
defined and filled with simple workings — the calls
the handshake makes to the kernel — and the list of what stands harder, which the essay itself gives, stands in its notes:
time, the network, running many threads at once, the
handing out of memory. Where the method stops stands
open (at 28.1.2).
# Study S010 — author check

WHO THE WORK NAMES. Math, Inc. stands as the author of the
essay, under the name of the house; no one person is named as
its author. Named in it: Galois and Oath, the collaborators of
the project; RAND, its support; Kayvan Memarian, of the
University of Cambridge, under whose name the Cerberus
semantics stands; Oath again, as the keeper of the port of
Cerberus to Lean; and, in its references, the line of the
field — Lamport; Sewell and his co-workers; Memarian again;
and Chaudhuri and his co-workers of 2026.

WHAT WAS CHECKED. Four checks were run. (1) The whole text of
the essay was searched by machine for the terms of this house:
phext, phextura, Bickford, Mirrorborn, Exocortex, SBOR, CYOA,
and the word scroll. No hit on any of them. (2) The references
of the essay were read: the line of the field itself, named above; no
work of Will stands among them. The one reference this tracker
also holds is Paper 017, quoted against its own cost —
engagement with a paper, in the open and fairly, in the
ordinary way of a field; it is not a tie to this house. (3)
The web was searched for the house together with phext: no tie
stands in what came back — the public record of the house:
Gauss, the Veritas fellowships, and the coverage of the
release of the milestones. One adjacency stands in that record
and is recorded for what it is: the house counts Terence Tao, the author of the work of Study S009, among the holders of its fellowships. A
tie between two studied houses; not a tie to this house. (4)
The archive of the tweets of Will was searched in full —
29,539 tweets — for the house and for the names the essay
carries: math.inc, Memarian, Sewell, Lamport, Cerberus,
WireGuard, Galois. No hit on any of them.

VERDICT. No evidence found. It is recorded as a finding: no
tie of the house to phext or to the work of Will, and no use
of that work without naming it.
# Study S010 — synthesis: the two poles of machine-checked trust [synthesis]

THE DISPUTE, FILED WHOLE. Within one day the tracker took in
both poles of a live dispute about where verification should
live, and this scroll files the dispute in the words of the
two works. Nothing in the scrolls of either pole is changed by this filing; the findings discipline of Study S007 stands.

THE POLE OF THE ARTIFACT (Chapter 27, cluster W). Prove the
binary itself, the thing that runs. Its trust rests in a
specification held as the trusted artifact (1.2.207) and
tested three ways (1.2.208); a semantics of the instruction
set written for the campaign (1.2.212); contracts where the
code meets the world (1.2.213); and the floor of the kernel
and the random oracle beneath (1.2.41). Its trusted base
stands enumerated in a ledger (1.2.222), and the compiler
stands outside it (1.2.214).

THE POLE OF THE SOURCE (this chapter, cluster X). Prove the
program at the level at which it was written, under the
agreed, executable semantics of its language (1.2.224), with
specification engineering as the discipline of the statements
(1.2.223). Its trust rests in the semantics of the language —
an object validated, never verified (1.2.225) — and in the
specification. Its campaign stands part-run: a model of the
handshake standing executable, its end-to-end theorem not yet
proved.

WHAT EACH SAYS THE OTHER LOSES. The essay on the pole of the
artifact: structure — the layout tax, stated in the words of
the artifact pole itself and checked at 28.1.2; tokens repaid
to build again what the source had already said (1.2.226); and
intent — a specification of what a binary does is still a
definition standing in need of validation, and a semantics
derived from a binary carries its intent inside a loaded
definition (1.2.229). The pole of the artifact on the pole of
the source, stated from the claims of Chapter 27 and not
invented for it: the object of the guarantee. A proof at the
source ranges over the envelope of allowed builds (1.2.227);
the thing that runs is one binary, and no part of the source
proof checks that the compiler stayed inside the envelope.
Chapter 27 takes the compiler out of the trusted base; the
road of the essay leaves it standing in the base, unexamined —
and names the gap itself among its open questions. The hardest charge of each pole against the other is one the other has already written down.

WHERE THEY AGREE. The specification is the trusted artifact:
both centre it, and both test it — the three tests of the
framework against the four ways of the essay, set side by side
by the rider on 1.2.208. Both build on Lean, and both take
only kernel-checked proof. Both spend agents on the labour and
keep humans at the statement. Both refuse semantics that stand
not validated: the gatekeeper of Chapter 27 holds a frozen
standard no prover may edit (1.2.211), and the essay refuses
ad hoc semantics for shared infrastructure, carrying no
authority and held as property (1.2.224). And both write their
trust down as a ledger a reader can check.

WHAT WOULD TELL THEM APART. [synthesis] The bet of the essay
is stated in a currency that can be counted: tokens. The
tracker can name the evidence that would price the poles.
First, the one target carried both ways: the handshake of
WireGuard is a protocol of the same family as the library of
Chapter 27. When the campaign of the source lands its
end-to-end theorem, its cost in tokens and in human hours, per
theorem that survives, stands against the five million lines
and the campaign of the artifact (Q88, Q89). Second, keeping:
a change of compiler or of target. The proof of the artifact
is of one build (1.2.214); the proof of the source is of the
envelope (1.2.227). Which proofs of the two poles are standing a year on
is a thing that can be counted (Q90). Third, the bugs found on
the way. The road of the source found an act of undefined
behaviour in the code it read, and almost forty gaps in the
shared semantics itself — its validations biting on the floor
object, not on programs only. The history test of the artifact
pole shows its specification catching code already known to be bad. The bug ledger of each pole measures a different
floor, and a field that keeps both can price the poles by
them. No verdict is entered. The tracker holds both poles, the
bet stated by one side, and the questions under which the
evidence would land.

A THIRD NEIGHBOUR FOR AN OLD QUESTION. The question of the
bench (Q84) asked whose standard judges a work when the work
itself is the exhibit. This chapter adds a third answer
standing in the field: validation by agreement — a semantics
held in common, validated by the four ways, its keeper the
stakeholders of the model. Prevention at a bench, refusal at a
gate, agreement among stakeholders: three ways for a standard to stand, and the tracker holds a worked case of each.

A FINDING FOR THE HOUSE. [finding] This house keeps a registry
that is a specification of its own claims, drawn and proved
(the standing record, 1.4.x). The first way of the essay —
read the statement for intent — and its warning about loaded
definitions reach any house that states things formally, this
one among them: a claim whose intent rides inside a definition
is a claim whose validation has not been spent. Filed as a
finding, at the strength of the two texts. Nothing is changed by it.
