# Paper 019 — the formal core: the range, its polynomial, and the pipeline that prices the dual

THE SETTING (the paper's section 2). Hermitian (1.3.241)
matrices A_1, ..., A_n of size d over a number field K, n at
least 2. The joint numerical range (1.2.237) is the set of
readings (x* A_1 x, ..., x* A_n x) over unit vectors x; letting
density matrices run gives its convex hull, W-hat(A), the convex hull of W(A). The carrying polynomial (1.2.238) is the determinantal
polynomial p_A, the determinant of (u_0 I + u_1 A_1 + ... + u_n A_n),
homogeneous of degree d; its factors F_i over K, taken with
reduced structure, are the pieces. Kippenhahn's theorem
(1.2.239): W-hat(A) is the convex hull of the real part of the
algebraic Kippenhahn variety — the union of the dual variety of each V(F_i) — the real part taken as the closure of the smooth real
points, in the higher-dimensional form of Plaumann, Sinn and
Weis. The paper computes equations for the hypersurface
components of that variety; drawing the real sets and forming
their hull are separate tasks, named and left outside.

THE PIPELINE (the paper's section 3), for each factor F:
1. Katz's test (1.2.240): F does not divide the determinant of the Hessian of F over K —
the dual is a hypersurface. A factor that fails is recorded
with the marker 0 and costs nothing more.
2. Sampling (1.2.242): at a completely split prime (1.2.244),
reduce F at each root of the defining polynomial; find smooth
points by the one-root-per-try rule; their gradients are points
of the dual by the Gauss map (1.2.241). A prime is abandoned
when its failure budget — max{1000, 100 times the degree in the
chosen variable} tries in a row without a new image — runs out
at any root.
3. The candidate degree (1.2.243): the evaluation table of the
sample; the kernel's size reads the degree — none, too small;
several, too large; exactly one, found — inside the ceiling
delta (delta - 1)^(n-1), the search walking by halves, the
sample held at the count of terms and a tenth more.
4. The lift (1.2.245): the equations at the m roots of one prime
feed a Vandermonde system whose solution is the power-basis
coordinates of the true coefficients, read modulo p; the
Chinese remainder theorem joins what the primes give;
rational reconstruction recovers the coefficients over K. The
leading-term discipline of 1.2.244 guards every step: a larger
leading term discards all data gathered and starts the lift
again.
5. Certification (1.2.246): when the candidate stands unchanged
after one more prime, the two exact tests of Proposition 3.1 —
H of the gradient a whole multiple of F, and H irreducible over K. What
passes is returned as the certified equation of the factor; the
routine is DualFromMatrices(A, p_0, d_0), with p_0 the floor of
the prime search and d_0 the first guess at the degree.

MACHINES (charter rule 13).
Machine 1 — the factor pipeline. States: the factors of the polynomial, each waiting, tested, computed, certified, or marked.
Moves: form and factor the polynomial; Katz's test sends a factor
either to the marker 0 or into the computation; the computation
sends it to its certified equation. The output is the sequence
of entries, one per factor: an equation, or 0. [stated —
section 3.6]
Machine 2 — the sampler. States: a prime and a root live, a
count of points gathered, and a counter of tries since the last
new Gauss image. Moves: each try gives values to all variables
but one, takes at most one root of the resulting one-variable
polynomial, tests smoothness by the gradient, and either adds a
new image (the counter resets) or spends from the budget. The
budget spent at any root kills the whole prime. Gap: the budget
is a count, not a judgment — a prime whose points stand sparse
can be abandoned though sound, and the machine keeps no record
of why a prime failed, to spend on the next. [stated —
section 3.2]
Machine 3 — the degree search. States: an interval of degrees
with a tested midpoint, and the size of the kernel at the
midpoint: none, one, or several. Moves: search above on none,
below on several, halt at exactly one. Gap: the reading of the signal takes for granted that the kernel shrinks as the degree grows toward
the truth and grows past it; with too few or too dependent
points a wrong degree can show a kernel of size one — the tenth
of surplus guards the signal, and the certificate at the end is
the only proof it was read right. [stated — section 3.3]
Machine 4 — the gathering of the reconstruction. States: a set of
retained primes sharing one leading term, what has been joined so far, and a candidate equation over K. Moves: the equations of a new prime are checked against the standing leading term —
smaller, the prime is discarded; larger, all states are thrown
away and the machine restarts from this prime; the same, what
the prime gives joins and the candidate is rebuilt. The machine halts
when the candidate stands unchanged after one more prime, and
hands it to certification. Gap: standing unchanged once is a
stopping rule of habit, not of proof — the machine's output is
a candidate by its own account, and only Machine 5's gate makes
it a result. [stated — section 3.4]
Machine 5 — the restart machine. States: running, certified,
or failed. Moves: on the failure of certification the whole
computation stops; the error names the last prime used; the
user restarts from the beginning with a floor for the prime
search above every prime the failed run spent, all modular
data discarded. Gap: the failure state is total — all that was joined, samples already gathered, are thrown away with
the rest, though nothing in the paper shows they were the
fault; whether any of the work of the failed run can be carried
into the restart is not asked. [stated — section 3.6]
# Paper 019 — the worked cases: four polynomials of degree four, computed and certified

The four inputs of the paper's section 4, all determinantal (1.3.243) polynomials of degree four, read over the one field
Q(alpha), alpha^4 - alpha^2 + 1 = 0, so that i = alpha^3 and
root 3 = 2 alpha - alpha^3 stand inside it. The search floor is
p_0 = 2003, the first degree guess d_0 = 18, the seed 1, the
surplus a tenth. Times are the paper's own, on its machine.

THE POWER THAT VANISHES (T_nil, from Chien and Nakazato). The
Davis-Wielandt triple of a 4 by 4 matrix whose fourth power
is zero: Re T, Im T, T*T. Dual
surface of degree 12, 135 terms, 5 primes. Magma 0.62 seconds,
Singular 0.85, one thread. The one case where the older road
keeps pace: it gives 0.90 seconds in Magma and 0.02 in
Singular.

THE TWELVE (E_12, from Pikul and his co-workers). A Hermitian
triple with entries in Q(i, root 3), its polynomial genuinely
over the number field — the case that exercises the
reconstruction across the roots (1.2.245), since at each prime
the interpolation runs at all four roots of the defining
polynomial before anything is lifted. Dual of degree 18, 691
terms, 9 primes; the first completely split prime at or above
2003 is 2017. Magma 10.35 seconds, Singular 13.64. The older road:
Magma past a day; Singular 526.94 seconds — the second of only two runs of the older road that finished.

THE FIRST (E_1, from Pikul and his co-workers). Dual of degree
24, 2,919 terms, 18 primes. Magma 30.65 seconds, Singular
46.57. The older road past a day in both systems.

THE CORRECTED (T_CN, from Chien and Nakazato). Dual of degree
34, 4,047 terms, 31 primes. Magma 542.57 seconds on one thread,
347.94 on ten; Singular 911.83 and 570.48. Memory at the peak:
1,668 megabytes in Magma, 836 in Singular. Certification
accounted for between 4 and 30 percent of the whole time across
the four cases. Against it: their own road to
this equation, built for the example from older tools and
interpolation, estimated by Nakazato at about twelve hours for the
full computation; the author reproduced their files in 163
minutes, and notes the reproduction still spent knowledge the
table may not — the known degree, the retained factors — and so
keeps it out of the table. His own method, from the matrices
with no preparation shaped to the case, under six minutes.

A CORRECTION IN THE RECORD. The matrix T_CN is the corrected
matrix of its source paper: Nakazato confirmed to the author, in
correspondence the paper records, that the bottom-right entry
should be -3 rather than 3, and that the coefficient of
u_0^3 u_3 in the displayed polynomial of the source should be
124 rather than 123. The certified surface of degree 34 is the
dual of the corrected input.
# Paper 019 — the results map: what stands proved, and on what it rests

COMPUTED AND CERTIFIED IN THE PAPER.
- The four dual equations (1.2.247): degrees 12, 18, 24, 34,
each got from its matrices by the pipeline of 30.1.2 and
certified by the two exact tests of Proposition 3.1 over K —
H of the gradient standing a whole multiple of F, and irreducibility of H.
Each stands as the equation of the dual hypersurface of its factor,
up to the scaling that sets the leading coefficient to
one. Rests on: the proposition below, and the implementations
in Magma and Singular shipped as files with the paper.
- Proposition 3.1 itself: H of the gradient a whole
multiple of F exactly when H is a whole multiple of G,
proved in the paper from the density of the
smooth locus and of its Gauss image (1.2.241).

TAKEN IN FROM CITED WORK, NOT RE-PROVED HERE.
- Kippenhahn's theorem and its higher-dimensional form
(1.2.239): the range is the convex hull of the real part of the
algebraic Kippenhahn variety — Kippenhahn, in the translation
of Zachlin and Hochstenbach; Plaumann, Sinn and Weis (2021).
- Katz's dimension formula in its hypersurface form (1.2.240):
the dimension of the dual is the rank of the Hessian at a
general point, less 2 — from Gelfand, Kapranov and Zelevinsky
(1994).
- The degree ceiling (1.2.243): D at most delta (delta - 1)^(n-1)
— a lemma of Ballico (2003).
- The four inputs and their literature: the Davis-Wielandt
examples of Chien and Nakazato (2025), with the correction of
30.1.3; the examples of Pikul, Spitkovsky, Szymanski, Weis and
Zyczkowski (2026), each a Hermitian triple; that the range is convex for two matrices (Toeplitz-Hausdorff), and for three at size at least 3 (Au-Yeung
and Poon, 1979).

NOT IN THE PAPER. No claim that the method ends on every run: the
method succeeded in all the author's experiments, and the paper
says the probability of success stands high — it does not prove
the pipeline always ends. The real side of the theorem is out
of scope: extracting the relevant real sets and forming their
convex hull are named as separate tasks, and no times are given for them. A dual that is not a hypersurface is recorded
with the marker 0, not computed — the adaptation to such a dual is a remark, not a construction. And two failure
shapes are named by the paper itself: a reduction modulo a
prime can change the degree of the dual, and when a factor is
irreducible over K but splits over an extension, sampling may
miss some of its geometric pieces — the test of division then still checks the vanishing on the dual of every piece, and the
returned equation defines their union.
