Paper 019 — synthesis: the dual, sampled; the equation, certified [synthesis]

Mark this whole scroll as Emise's synthesis, built on the
scrolls of this chapter. The classical road to a dual variety
is the striking out: build the ideal of the graph of the
Gauss map and strike the original variables out of it, at a cost that, in the
paper's own table, passes a day on three of four examples. This
paper's road is the reverse posture: never build the thing you
would eliminate from. Sample the dual where it is cheap — over
finite fields, at split primes — read the equation off the
sample by linear algebra, lift it home by the oldest machinery
in algebra, and then, and only then, prove it: an exact test over the original field, against the original factor. The random and the heuristic are all spent in the
finding; the keeping is exact.

That shape is one this house knows from the other side.
Cluster W holds a framework whose agents propose and whose
gatekeeper admits only what matches a held standard exactly;
cluster X holds the counter-pole, that the standard itself is
a semantics, validated and never verified. Here the standard
is neither drafted nor agreed: it is the factor F itself, a
piece of the input, and the gate is a question of division about it. The tracker can now set three gates side by side —
the bench that offers only legal moves (S009), the gatekeeper
that compares against a held-out statement (Paper 017), the
agreement that validates a shared meaning (S010) — and a
fourth, this one: the object itself, asked whether the
candidate vanishes where it must. Q84 holds all four now, and
still ranks none.

And the map gains a country it did not hold, joined by two
honest bridges. One runs to cluster E: projective duality is
geometry the annex has always stood near, and the author's own
trade — the computational study of surfaces, fake quadrics
among them — is the subject of the annex worked from the other side; but nothing in this paper is symplectic, and the cluster
stands its own. The other runs to the verified-artifact line,
at the level of shape: a computed object that carries its
certificate, priced in the open — certification at 4 to 30
percent of the run, printed in the paper like a cost line. What
the country adds to the model is a method shape: sampling as a
way of knowing a variety — points first, equation second,
proof last — in a field where the model's only earlier posture
was to receive finished theorems.
# Paper 019 — Open questions Q97-Q99, and one question informed

Q97 — WHERE THE COST BREAKS. The four certified surfaces of the method stand at degrees 12, 18, 24, 34, got from 5, 9, 18 and
31 primes; the degree ceiling grows as delta (delta - 1)^(n-1),
and the count of terms the interpolation must carry grows with
the degree. Is there a scale at which sampling, lifting and
certification lose their lead over the older road — or does
the wall of the older road the paper's table shows, three of four cases
past a day, stand at every scale the pipeline reaches first?
The answer prices the country of the method, not only its four
cases. (From 1.2.243 and 1.2.247.)

Q98 — WHAT CERTIFIES THE REAL SIDE. The certificate of 1.2.246
covers the equation over K: H is the dual of V(F). Kippenhahn's
theorem then asks for the real part — the closure of the smooth
real points of the dual, complex-born singular points kept out
(1.2.239) — and for the convex hull over it, and the paper
names both as separate tasks it does not price or certify.
What would a certificate for that side look like: a proof that
the real points taken are all the real points the hull needs?
Until it exists, the certified object and the geometric object
of the theorem stand one step apart, and no certificate covers that step. (From
1.2.239 and 1.2.246.)

Q99 — MUST A FAILED RUN FORGET EVERYTHING. When certification
fails, the method stops and restarts from the beginning, the
prime floor raised above every spent prime and all modular
data discarded (30.1.2, Machine 5). The leading-term
discipline already discards data on a smaller scale, at a
larger leading term. Is total forgetting forced by the shape
of the method — what a failed run joined stands
joined from a wrong candidate — or is it an artifact of the
implementation, with some part of a failed run (the samples,
the degree, the primes that behaved) that can honestly be
carried into the restart? (From 1.2.244 and 1.2.245.)

INFORMED, NOT CLOSED. Q84 — whose standard judges a work: the
bench, the gate, the agreement. This chapter adds a fourth
worked answer beside the three the tracker held: the object
itself as the standard — a candidate equation judged by an exact test against the factor it claims to be the dual of,
no drafted statement and no agreed semantics between them
(1.2.246). The question stands open; its shelf of answers is
one wider.
