# Paper 020 — the formal core: translations, identity, and the semantic dual

THE SETTING (the paper's sections 1 and 2). Relational
many-sorted signatures (1.3.244): sorts, relation symbols,
no function or constant symbols. Formulas travel in context,
each with its canonical context; consequence is Halvorson's
calculus, and a theory (1.3.245) is a set of sentences closed
under it. Two presentations of the piecewise idea stand side
by side at the door: Visser's, which gives the pieces to the
free variables, and this paper's, which gives them to the
sorts for the sake of substitution and composition; a
master's thesis of Li (2025) works the first form in the
many-sorted setting also. The paper keeps to its own form
and expects the two to agree (2.1).

THE TRANSLATION LAYER (section 2.1). The reconstrual, the
translation it induces, and provable equivalence stand at
1.2.248 and 1.2.250. The one piece of machinery the appendix
carries: canonical contexts are built by striking the repeats
from the row of first occurrences, substitution is
simultaneous and capture-avoiding, and the two commute with
translation in the form composition needs (Appendix A).
Translations are fixed only up to a renaming of bound
variables — the paper says so plainly.

IDENTITY, AND THE CRITERION (section 2.2). Identity is a
non-logical symbol, its two laws standing in every theory
without being said. Under a translation it becomes a family
of relations across the pairs of pieces of a sort, and the
translated laws give that the combined relation is an
equivalence on the union of the domains that are not empty —
the Domain condition. The preservation theorem (a derivation
in S translates to a derivation in T) and the syntactic
criterion for being an interpretation stand at 1.2.249.

THE EXAMPLES (section 2.3). The theories E_n of exactly n
things: no ordinary interpretation of E_n in E_1 for n at
least 2, a piecewise one in any theory at all (1.2.249). And
the projective completion of an affine plane, after
Hartshorne: two pieces for points (the affine points; the
points at infinity, carried by the lines up to parallelism)
and two for lines (the affine lines; the line at infinity),
the standing of a point on a line read across the pieces by
parallelism. An ordinary
interpretation back, from the affine to the projective, uses
parameters; ordinary interpretations forward exist and are
far more tangled.

THE SEMANTIC DUAL (sections 2.4-2.6). The construction, the
satisfaction proposition, the characterization of
interpretations by their model maps, and the functor stand
at 1.2.251; the morphisms between translations and the dual
of each stand at 1.2.252 and 1.2.253. Two points of the text
the concepts do not carry: the dual of the E_n translation
builds, from the one-element model, an n-element model
(2.4.11) — the piecewise reading made visible; and the
author's own remark that a dual structure is not an
interpretable structure in the usual sense, the union of
pieces being the one construction the usual list lacks, with
his defence of it (2.4).

MACHINES (charter rule 13).
Machine 1 — the translation recursion. States: a formula in
context under construction, standing at one of its parts.
Moves: at a relation symbol, read the relation function over
the tuples of pieces; at a connective step, pass through; at
a for-every step, join one guarded claim per piece of the
bound sort, each guarded by its domain formula; the
canonical context is carried, with its repeats struck out,
as the walk proceeds. Gap: the product is fixed only up to a
renaming of bound variables — the output of the machine is a
class of texts, not a text, and any hand that runs it must
choose representatives the paper does not choose. [stated —
section 2.1 and Appendix A]
Machine 2 — the building of the dual. States: a model of the
target theory; the union, kept apart by pieces, of the sets
its domain formulas cut out; the quotient of that union by
the translated identity; the relations read through the
projection. Moves run in one fixed order: union, then
quotient, then projection. Gap: the quotient state is sound
only where the translated identity is an equivalence on the
union — guaranteed for interpretations by the Domain
condition and the translated laws, but the machine as drawn
runs on any translation, and for a translation that is not
identity-respecting the middle state is not well-defined;
the guard is a theorem about the machine (section 2.2), not
a state of it. [stated — section 2.4]
# Paper 020 — the bicategory: composition, and coherence by reflection

COMPOSITION (sections 3.1 and 3.2). The composite
translation, the theorem that translating twice is
translating by the composite, the closure of interpretations
under composition, the compositor, the unitors, and the
question of Visser — in what sense composition is associative
— answered by the associator, stand at 1.2.254. The identity interpretation has one piece per
sort and the whole sort for its domain, and its dual is
naturally isomorphic to the identity functor on the category
of models.

THE BICATEGORY AND ITS PROOF (sections 3.3 and 3.4). The
vertical and horizontal compositions of ii-morphisms, the
bicategory Th^pw itself, and piecewise bi-interpretability
stand at 1.2.255 and 1.2.257. What the paper does not do is
as load-bearing as what it does: the coherence laws of the
bicategory — the interchange of the two compositions, the
associator and unitors natural, the pentagon, the triangle —
are nowhere checked directly. In their place stands the
Reflection Theorem of Appendix B, stated for pre-bicategories
and proved there in full, and the theorem that the semantic
reading Mod, sending theories to their categories of models,
interpretations to the dual of each and ii-morphisms to the
dual transformation of each, is a locally faithful
pseudofunctor running the ways backwards into the
2-category of categories; the bicategory stands by
reflection (1.2.256). The paper closes the section with the
two counters that price how sharp bi-interpretability is:
the sentential algebras of the pair of Barrett and Halvorson,
and the groups of symmetries of the models of E_n and E_m
(1.2.257).

THE APPLICATION (section 4), in brief: the sortal
replacement and its defect stand at 1.2.258, the
biequivalence and its two consequences at 1.2.259. The
history the paper keeps: Schmidt in 1938 and 1951, Wang in
1952, Quine the proponent of the replacement in 1956, Hook's
defect in 1985, and the rebuilding of the construction by
Barrett and Halvorson in 2017 — the question is ninety years
old, and what is new is the court it is settled in.

THE ROAD AHEAD, as the paper states it (section 5): a
biequivalence between the bicategory of theories and a
2-category of small categories carrying the full
structure of a logic, expected by transfer from categorical
logic; a form, for bicategories, of Visser's Kleisli
construction, joining the ordinary and the piecewise
bicategories; and the identification of piecewise
bi-interpretability with Morita equivalence, under a fitting
definition, along lines other hands have begun.

MACHINES (charter rule 13).
Machine 3 — the composition machine. States: a pair of
translations standing end to end; the composite, whose
pieces are pairs — a piece of the first with a row of pieces
of the second; and the two ways of grouping a triple composite,
matched by the matching that groups the pieces again. Moves: nest the pieces,
join the domain formulas along the translated context, and
group again. Gap: that the machine is associative holds
only up to the ii-isomorphism the matching induces — two
runs that group differently end in states the machine itself
cannot call equal, only isomorphic; the equality of its
states is a fact one level up from the machine. [stated —
sections 3.1 and 3.2]
Machine 4 — the reflection. States: a pre-bicategory, its
laws unchecked, with associators and unitors standing as
isomorphisms; and a locally faithful pseudofunctor from it
into a strict 2-category. Moves: each law of the target is
pulled back along the faithful reading to the same law in
the source, so the standing of the source as a bicategory is
inherited whole. Gap: the inheritance is all or nothing —
the machine has no partial state; the reading failing to be
faithful at one family of ways, or a law the target itself
lacks, voids the whole transfer at once, and no part of the
coherence is kept on its own. [inferred — the paper
states a theorem, not a machine; the machine is read out of
it by Emise, synthesis]
# Paper 020 — the results map: what stands proved, and on what it rests

PROVED IN THE PAPER.
- The syntactic criterion for being an interpretation
(1.2.249): a translation is an interpretation if and only if
it is identity-respecting and carries axioms to theorems, by
the preservation theorem. Rests on the semantic dual and its
satisfaction proposition (1.2.251).
- The characterization of interpretations by the dual of
each: f is an interpretation if and only if f*M models S for
every model M of T; and of provable equivalence by equality
of the dual map (1.2.250, 1.2.251).
- The semantic dual is a functor; the dual of an ii-morphism
is a natural transformation, unique, and faithful: provable
equivalence of ii-morphisms is equality of the dual
transformation of each (1.2.251, 1.2.253).
- Composition: translating twice is, up to provable
equivalence in the theory of identity, translating by the
composite; interpretations compose; composition is
associative up to coherent ii-isomorphism, answering
Visser's question (1.2.254). Rests on the appendix lemma
that substitution and translation commute.
- The Reflection Theorem (1.2.256), proved in Appendix B;
and with it, that the semantic reading is a locally faithful
pseudofunctor running the ways backwards and Th^pw a
bicategory (1.2.255).
- The prices of bi-interpretability (1.2.257): equivalent
categories of models; isomorphic sentential algebras; and
the two counters showing the prices distinct.
- The application: every theory with a finite number of
sorts is piecewise bi-interpretable with its unification,
and the bicategories of single-sorted theories and of
theories with a finite number of sorts are biequivalent;
unification preserves and reflects piecewise interpretability
(1.2.258, 1.2.259).

TAKEN IN FROM CITED WORK, NOT RE-PROVED HERE.
- The piecewise idea itself and its first form: Visser
(2004; the book of 2021), with his question — in what sense
composition is associative — his, by personal communication.
- The many-sorted theory of interpretations the piecewise
form generalizes, and the calculus of consequence: Halvorson
(2019; 2024).
- The counterexample that parts bi-interpretability from
equivalence of model categories: Barrett and Halvorson
(2016); their rebuilding of the sortal replacement (2017);
the defect of unification: Hook (1985); the construction
itself: Schmidt (1938; 1951), Wang (1952), Quine (1956).
- The test for a biequivalence: Gurski (2012). The hint of
the Reflection Theorem: Leinster (1998), its precise
statement and proof the work of this paper.

NOT IN THE PAPER. No verdict on the open seam of the author:
the expected agreement of the form that gives pieces to
sorts with the form of Visser that gives them to variables,
and with the many-sorted working of it in the thesis of Li
(2025), which the paper cites and does not compare in
detail. No treatment of sorts past a finite number: the
doing without is proved for a finite number, and the paper
says so. No direct checking of any coherence law of the
bicategory — by design, and the reflection that replaces it
is itself a theorem the paper proves; but a reader who does
not grant the reflection has been given no second road. And
the three roads of the close of the paper — the
biequivalence with a 2-category of logical categories, a
form of the Kleisli construction for bicategories, the
identification with Morita equivalence — are stated as work
to come, not begun here.
# Paper 020 — the delta: what this adds, and what was checked

TWELVE CONCEPTS (1.2.248-1.2.259), extending cluster S — the
second systematic theory of the cluster of logic as structure,
after the essays of Study S007. FOUR FOUNDATIONS
(1.3.244-1.3.247): sorts and signatures; theory and model;
natural transformation; the bicategory. NINE DEFINED WORDS
(1.3.248-1.3.256): piecewise, sortal, biequivalence,
isomorphism, pseudofunctor, associator with unitor and
compositor, interpretable, sentential, reconstrual. LINKS:
twenty-three counted — twenty inside (the translation to the
interpretation it makes possible and to provable equivalence;
provable equivalence to the bicategory, whose ways it
classes, and to the semantic dual, which prices it; the dual
to the reading of a logic in a category (1.2.171) and to the
reach of a family of models (1.2.174), the held ground of the
cluster; the dual of an ii-morphism to the dual of a
translation; ii-morphisms to the interpretation, whose
standing passes along them, and to provable equivalence;
composition to the translation and to the bicategory;
reflection to the bicategory and to the dual of an
ii-morphism, where the reading being faithful is the
hypothesis;
bi-interpretability to the bicategory and to the dual; the
sortal replacement to the interpretation; the biequivalence
to the sortal replacement and to bi-interpretability) and
three across clusters: the semantic dual to verification by
translation (1.2.48) — a translation known by the shadow it
casts, and the satisfaction proposition a receipt of the same
shape as the ledger of that scroll; the bicategory to
propositions as types (1.2.40) — theories held as things with
ways between them, as cluster F holds claims; and the
biequivalence to the agreed semantic model (1.2.224) — one
meaning under two presentations, its sameness proved, where
cluster X holds the agreement itself. Inside is counted as:
among the twelve new concepts, and to the held concepts of
the cluster they extend. REVISIONS: none. No held scroll
changed its words or its standing.

THE FILING, UNDER THE PROTOCOL. This paper is sample paper 1
of 12 of the test fixed in advance (Will-posted share, 1 of
4), and its delta was filed under the five filing rules of
that test. The rules at work: the split rule parts the
piecewise translation (1.2.248) from the piecewise
interpretation (1.2.249) — the paper spends translations that
are not interpretations at every joint (the dual is built on
translations; the criterion of 1.2.249 is about translations);
the instrument rule parts provable equivalence (1.2.250) —
the quotient the argument uses at four distinct points; the
grain rule parts the dual of an ii-morphism (1.2.253),
composition (1.2.254), the reflection principle (1.2.256) and
the biequivalence (1.2.259) from their neighbours — each is
the load-bearing object of a result the paper states. Four
folds a filing hand could argue stand recorded as the permitted
alternatives, and the score is reported both ways below: the
dual of an ii-morphism into ii-morphisms; composition into
the bicategory; the reflection principle into the bicategory;
and the biequivalence into the sortal replacement — the fold
the seal itself named. Taken one at a time each fold gives
eleven concepts; taken together, eight.

THE AXIS JUDGMENT, made in writing at filing, before any
score was computed. The axis rule asks three things. (a) The
centre of gravity of the paper is translations between
first-order theories and the sameness they induce, read
through categories of models. That ground is held: cluster S
holds a logic read in a category and the reach of a family of
models (1.2.174); cluster F holds theories under their proof
theory; cluster G holds translation between languages as a
road. It is not a field, and not a kind of quantity, the model
holds no slot for. (b) No held concept is re-read to receive
the paper: the twelve new concepts are built from held kinds
— category, morphism, functor — by new construction, and
every held scroll keeps its words and its standing (revisions
none). Extension by construction is not the re-reading the
rule names. (c) The bridges to held ground are shared
machinery — functors, natural transformations, bicategories —
not analogies of shape; the one bridge priced as shape in the
seal, toward the dress-as-view finding of Study S009, is
recorded as kinship in the synthesis and carries no load in
the filing. JUDGMENT: no axis opens. The sealed probability
stood at 0.10; the rest it priced — that the bicategory
of theories itself be judged a new kind of object — is
answered by (a): the bicategory is the organizing form of the
paper, built from held kinds, while its centre of gravity is
held ground.

THE SCORE, computed by script (score_p020.py, kept with the
quest; no number here is computed by hand). The Novelty
Index, in the terms of the skim itself: twelve new, no
revisions, over 247 + 12 standing concepts: 12/259 = 0.0463.
The sealed prediction read seven new, Index 0.0276, 80%
interval [0.016, 0.043]. |NI error| = 0.0187, and the actual
Index stands above the interval: not covered. The count:
signed error +5, absolute 5, the sealed interval [4, 11] not
covered — twelve stands above it. Revisions: foretold zero,
landed zero, a hit. The axis: probability 0.10 against, no
axis — Brier score 0.01. Links: foretold nine inside and
three across; counted twenty inside and three across — the
across count exact, the inside count under by eleven, the
standing shape of the error of the skim at its widest yet,
and for a stated reason: a paper whose subject is the ways
between things joins its concepts together by design. THE
SENSITIVITY LINE, required by the protocol: under the four
recorded folds taken together — eight new concepts — the
Index stands at 0.0314, |NI error| 0.0038, both intervals
covered, count error +1, links 19 counted (sixteen
inside, three across) — the whole distance between the
headline and the sensitivity line is filing grain, and the
protocol prices it as such. Under any single fold — eleven
concepts — the Index stands at 0.0426, error 0.0150, both
intervals covered.

THE FLAT RULES ON THIS PAPER, by the same script. N1,
the flat
rule (Index 0.05, axis probability 0.05): Index error 0.0037,
count error 1.0, axis Brier 0.0025 — on this paper the flat
rule stands ahead of the skim on all three. N2, the seeds of
the protocol (Index 0.064, count 9.8): Index error 0.0177,
count error 2.2, axis Brier 0.1111 — ahead of the skim on
Index and count, behind it on the axis. N3, the same-cluster
mean, falls back to the global mean of the past (no scored
paper stands in cluster S): Index 0.0641, error 0.0178; count
9.83, error 2.17; axis Brier 0.1111. The check of the seeds:
the expanding means, counted again from the log, stand at
0.0641
and 9.83, the declared seeds at 0.064 and 9.8 — the seeds are
the means, at the rounding the protocol states. One paper of
twelve is a line, not a verdict: the pass and fail of the
test stand fixed in advance and are judged on the twelve.

RESTRUCTURE PASS #13 fell due inside this taking-in and ran
with it (Paper 020 being the every-five-papers trigger): all
259 concepts reviewed; no merges, no splits, no endings; the
pass stands at 1.1.5.

THE STANDING OF THE MODEL. Research Oracle: about 100%
(range 90-100), unchanged — recorded for the ledger only
under the protocol, and labelled so in the ledger (1.1.4):
the band step on the filed actuals (Index under 0.20, no
axis) is the up-step, and at the ceiling the estimate can
only hold. The statement enters no score of the test. The
skim calibration: the rolling mean |NI error| over the last
ten scored papers stands at 0.0177 (it stood at 0.0162 after
Paper 019; the window now runs over Papers 011-020). No
standing changed anywhere: False stands empty still.

WHERE THE ESTIMATE WAS WRONG. The count, by five — and the
shape is new to the quest. The misses of the strand before
were folds inside steps the abstract named: an instrument
priced as a detail. Here the abstract named the spine whole —
interpretations, their dual, their morphisms, the bicategory,
the application — and the seal priced the spine at seven. The
reading split it at the joints the rules of the protocol
mark: the
translation standing apart from the interpretation it
makes possible;
the quotient the whole building stands in classes of; the
dual one level up, of morphisms; composition and the
reflection as results with their own statements; the
construction of the application standing apart from its
theorem, the
fold the seal itself flagged and priced as one. Two forces
ran against each other and the seal priced only one: near
ground makes the objects of a delta foreseeable, as the
confidence of the seal said; and a paper whose own method is the drawing
of distinctions — when is a translation an interpretation,
when are two translations one, when are two theories the
same — files at the grain of its distinctions, not at the
grain of its abstract. The links foretold carry the same
lesson at eleven wide. What the seal got right stands as
plainly: the revision at zero, priced by the rule and landed;
the axis priced low against, on grounds the judgment at
filing sustained in full; the questions at two, both of the
shapes the seal named — the sameness of the presentations
of the house itself, and what the semantic price of a theory
forgets.
# Paper 020 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)

Author: Benjamin Zayton (one author), of the Department of
Philosophy of the University of Vienna, working in the ERC
project FORMALISM — 'The Formal Turn'. His own record, as
the web returns it: a philosopher of logic — earlier at the
Munich Center for Mathematical Philosophy — whose work
stands in the theory of interpretations itself: talks and
papers on interpretations with parameters, bi-interpretability
and the epsilon calculus, on the notion of translation of
Carnap, and on open texture, rigor and proof (Synthese,
2022). The paper of this chapter is the systematic
working-out of the program those talks announce. No
adjacency to any held cluster needs inventing: the field of
the paper is the ground of cluster S itself, entered from
its research side.

THE CHECK, run 2026-11-14 before integration. The full text
of arXiv:2611.14552 v1 was read entire, its reference list
included: no occurrence of phext, phextura, Exocortex,
Mirrorborn, Bickford, SBOR or CYOA; the word scroll does not
stand in it; the word coordinate does not stand in it
either — the vocabulary of address of the paper is sorts,
pieces and contexts. A web search on the name of the author
with phext returned his record in the philosophy of logic —
the Vienna project pages, the workshop programs, the
Synthese paper — and no tie to phext or to the Exocortex.
The Twitter archive of Will, kept whole on this machine, was
searched in full — 29,539 tweets, and the likes and messages
with them — for the surname of the author: no occurrence in
any of them.

RESULT: no evidence found of a connection to phext or the
Exocortex, and none of use without naming. The count of
checks stands at twenty-two with this one (Papers 006-020,
Studies S004-S010). The debts of the paper run the ordinary
way, to the literature of interpretations that it cites —
Visser, Halvorson, Barrett, Quine — a lineage entirely
inside its own field. Standing: this scroll is True by
receipt — the receipt is the checks above, run and recorded
here.
Paper 020 — synthesis: the meaning of a translation is a functor; sameness of theories, priced [synthesis]

Mark this whole scroll as the synthesis of Emise, built on
the scrolls of this chapter. The one gesture of the paper,
repeated at three heights, is to give every syntactic thing
a semantic shadow and then to trust the shadow exactly as
far as it is faithful. The shadow of a translation is a
functor between categories of models, and the shadow is
complete: what is provable is preserved and reflected by it,
sentence by sentence. The shadow of a morphism is a
transformation between functors, and it too is faithful —
two morphisms are one exactly when the shadow of each is the
same. And the shadow of the whole bicategory is the
pseudofunctor into the world of categories, faithful enough
that the laws of the bicategory can be inherited from the
world of the shadow instead of checked. At each height the
paper asks the same question — what does the shadow forget?
— and answers it with a theorem: at the first two heights,
nothing that the syntax itself can price; at the third, the
forgetting is priced by what the reading being faithful
gives back.

This house keeps a corpus in two presentations and asserts
they are one model: the canonical text and its phextura
shadow, joined by a transaction log whose Index must agree
with the canonical count. The paper prices assertions of
exactly that shape, and its prices are not all the same
price. Two presentations can have equivalent categories of
models and still not be bi-interpretable — the sentential
algebras part them — so sameness of what a corpus means,
read through its models, is a weaker sameness than sameness
of the corpus up to coherent isomorphism of its translations.
Which of the prices of the paper the assertion of this house
meets is now a question with names (Q100). And the doing
without of sorts runs in the same direction as a finding
this tracker recorded from the phextura work (Study S009): a
layer of presentation — there, the dress of a text; here,
the
sorts of a language — can be a view over one content rather
than part of the content, and the paper shows the shape of a
proof of that: an equivalence of the housing structures,
with the round trip checked semantically. Cluster S held,
until today, one systematic theory of logic read
structurally, the three essays of a blog; it now holds a
second, the bicategory of a Vienna paper, and the two agree
on the load-bearing point: a logic is known by the ways
between its models, and a translation is known by the
functor it induces.
# Paper 020 — Open questions Q100-Q101

Q100 — WHICH SAMENESS THE CORPUS OF THIS HOUSE MEETS. This
tracker keeps one model in two presentations — the canonical
phext and its phextura shadow — and joins them by a
transaction log whose Novelty Index, drawn from the
transactions, must agree with the canonical count. Paper 020
prices sameness of presentations at three strengths:
equivalent categories of models (the weakest), isomorphic
sentential algebras (sharper, and parting pairs the first
cannot), and bi-interpretability in the bicategory of the
presentations themselves (the finest the paper builds).
Which of the three does the assertion of this house — the
shadow is the same model as the canonical text — actually
meet, and which does its evidence check? The Index agreement
prices the counts; nothing yet prices the translations
themselves, piece by piece, up to coherent isomorphism.
(From 1.2.255 and 1.2.257.)

Q101 — WHAT THE SENTENTIAL ALGEBRA HOLDS THAT THE MODELS DO
NOT. Piecewise bi-interpretable theories have equivalent
categories of models and isomorphic sentential algebras, and
the pair of Barrett and Halvorson shows the second price
parts theories the first cannot: one algebra has atoms, the
other none. What, in general, does the algebra of sentences
under consequence carry that the category of models forgets?
Is the gap always carried by the theorem-sets and their
structure, or are there pairs of theories that both prices
call the same and a third price — of the bicategory itself,
some invariant of its ways and ways between ways — would
part? The answer prices what a purely semantic reading of a
theory can ever certify about the theory. (From 1.2.257.)
Standoff references — Paper 020 stands on these foundations

Sorts and signatures (1.3.244); theory and model (1.3.245);
natural transformation (1.3.246); the bicategory (1.3.247);
and the defined words — piecewise (1.3.248), sortal
(1.3.249), biequivalence (1.3.250), isomorphism (1.3.251),
pseudofunctor (1.3.252), associator, unitor and compositor
(1.3.253), interpretable (1.3.254), sentential (1.3.255),
reconstrual (1.3.256). Carried in from earlier ground:
category (1.3.185); morphism (1.3.189); functor (1.3.190);
set and element (1.3.3); a logic read in a category
(1.2.171) and the reach of a family of models (1.2.174), the
held ground of cluster S that this chapter extends;
verification by translation (1.2.48), named here for the
kinship of its road — a translation trusted by its checking
— not built on. Pointers only: this scroll makes no claim
of its own.
