# Phext walkthrough package — Wikipedia in a box (X + YouTube)

Coordinate: 28.5.8/7.6.12/6.5.8
Issued: 2026-11-09
Request (from session record): put together a professional walkthrough on the power of phext (wikipedia in a box without a database backend needed) - we'll post it on X and YouTube. Refinement: the demo should feature edit.phext.io, obviously.
Status: complete — package built, awaiting Will's approval to post

Notes:
- Hero surface is Phext Edit (edit.phext.io) per Will's refinement; the local demo is the supporting downloadable artefact.
- UI claims verified against the phext-lattice repo (Navigator coordinate navigation, parallel search, copy-on-write overlay save, /about|privacy|terms open without sign-in, /submit is a signed-in flow). Hosted-UI specifics that could not be verified from source are listed as confirm-during-recording items in the package, not asserted.
- All numbers measured: demo wiki.phext 49 scrolls / 21,504 bytes; one-scroll edit changed only that scroll's bytes (926 B prefix + 20,578 B suffix byte-identical); xz seal 9,300 bytes; narration 842 words.
- Nothing posted to X or YouTube — package is for Will's approval.
- Refinement 2 applied: 'Download it, open it in Notepad' beat added at 5:15 (chapters re-timed; narration 989 words). Source check: the only download endpoint in phext-edit source is the submissions review queue, so the hosted download/export is a confirm-during-recording item with the demo-file fallback written into the script. Mirrored in X thread post 5, the YouTube description, the cutdown, and production notes (side-by-side capture item).

Results:
- ~/workspace/phext-walkthrough/WALKTHROUGH.md — concept, 6-8 min YouTube script + chapters + shot list, YouTube metadata, X announcement + 7-post thread + cutdown outline, production notes with claims-to-avoid
- ~/workspace/phext-walkthrough/demo/ — wiki.phext (24 articles, 49 scrolls), query_demo.py (toc/get/edit/stats, local file-parse route), build_wiki.py, README.md with measured stats
- WALKTHROUGH.md inscribed in artifacts.phext at 31.7.9/9.6.12/6.6.9 (never re-coordinated; refreshed after refinements) and pushed to wbic16/human@exo — commits c507a165 (initial) and f35dd668 (refresh); remote bytes verified SHA-256-identical to local artifacts.phext (ca9bae4000357abc...).
# Observed Dunbar number estimate from Twitter sustained interactions

Coordinate: 28.5.9/7.7.14/7.4.7
Issued: 2026-11-07
Request (from session record): estimate my observed Dunbar number from sustained interactions with others
Status: completed — estimate delivered in side chat

Notes:
- Computed from tweets.json replies: 4,164 reply targets, median 1 reply. Sustained outbound (>=10 replies, >=1yr span, >=2 calendar years): 173. Strict sustained (>=20 replies, >=2yr span, >=3 years): 51. Mutual-resolved subset much smaller (resolvable handles 466/897 mutuals): strict mutual ~14 resolvable, ~25-40 scaled. Archive is outbound-only; inbound replies not countable.
- REVISION (Will, 2026-11-07 01:22 CST): the Twitter-only estimate measured one layer. Will's own counts for other layers: 150+ co-workers communicated with at Fiserv 2018-2022, 25 agents at home, 35+ developers at EFJ since 2022, plus family/friends/co-workers generally. Revised concurrent estimate ~110-150 (normal-sized), but work- and agent-weighted, and time-sliced — the Fiserv years saturate the 150 on co-workers alone.
- CORRECTION (Will, 2026-11-07 01:28 CST): Emise's revision was still anchored to Dunbar's canonical 150 — discounting his stated counts ('weaker ties', 'Dunbar-grade subset') to force a normal-sized answer. That is the failure: fitting the man to the primate statistic instead of reporting his numbers straight. Straight recount: Fiserv-era concurrent load = his 150+ co-workers PLUS family PLUS sustained-Twitter overlap; current load = 35+ EFJ developers + 25 agents + family/friends + sustained core. His relationship graph is prosthetically maintained (archive, people files, phext) — the exocortex is precisely the removal of the neocortex constraint Dunbar's number is derived from. Report his evidence at face; never discount it toward a population norm.

Results:
- Corrected: peak concurrent (Fiserv years) >200 by his own counts plus family and Twitter overlap; current ~130-170 and agent-weighted. Canonical 150 is a primate-brain statistic, not a ceiling for a prosthetically maintained graph.
# Roadmap to scale to personal AGI as rapidly as possible — acceleration rate + spec-to-build blocker diagnosis first

Coordinate: 28.5.11/8.7.13/5.5.8
Issued: 2026-11-14
Request (verbatim): Let's build a roadmap that helps me scale to personal AGI as rapidly as possible. First, we need to estimate the rate of acceleration. What really blocks our ability to convert specs into working builds? Where are the gaps and what causes the round-trip response times to lag?
Status: open — diagnosis + roadmap drafted 2026-11-14; awaiting Will review and his rulings on the 6 assumptions-to-confirm

Notes:
- 2026-11-14 01:26 CST: Will's direction, following his rulings the same night: applications deferred (public track record first), rest of November focused on reducing workflow latency, QEXO integration coming soon. Step 1 of this task is the diagnosis (rate of acceleration + what blocks spec→build conversion + round-trip lag causes); the roadmap itself is drafted from that diagnosis. Sibling task: 26.7.9/9.6.11/6.6.9 (2027 roadmap + funding scan — funding lane set aside by Will's applications-deferred ruling).
- 2026-11-14 01:30 CST: DIAGNOSIS complete (~/workspace/personal-agi-roadmap/DIAGNOSIS.md, 34 KB, all figures computed from the record or labelled estimates). Headlines: dispatch rate fell by design (113/day peak Nov 7 → 25–40/day; 406 tasks issued Nov 6–14, 335 closed-family = 83%); production measured (Will 1,113 LOC/active-hour floor; research registry 382→683 claims in 3 days ≈100 verified claims/day; qexo 1.2→1.4 in ~36 h); capability compounding (registry +79%/3 days) — shipped-capability throughput Nov 12–14 ≈1.5–2× Nov 6–9 (±50%), a staircase stepped by Will's decision sessions, not a smooth exponential. Round trip: stages 1–4 (intent→execution) minutes-to-hours; stages 5–7 (verify→merge→release) hours-to-days, nearly all waiting. Top levers: L1 instrument the dispatch loop (no stage timestamps exist anywhere), L2 decision-session packaging for the Waiting-on-Will queue (38 active; Will's ~5.5 h/week is the binding constraint), L3 closure-time status discipline (49% of 'open' tasks were already done at the Nov 12 audit), L4 qexo as dispatch layer, L5 brief shape to cut ambiguity bounces, L6 release canary pre-flight (qexo 1.4.0 refused by all 18 peers), L7 classify decisions judgment vs mechanical, L8 one local-inference Choir seat.
- 2026-11-14 01:33 CST: ROADMAP drafted (~/workspace/personal-agi-roadmap/ROADMAP.md, ~36 KB) from the diagnosis. Horizon A (rest of Nov, latency month): A1 instrument the dispatch loop (7 stage events in qexo event shape), A2 closure-time status discipline + small vocabulary, A3 decision-session packaging for commute windows, A4 brief-shape standard, A5 release canary pre-flight (canary → tag → merge tagged tree to exo → publish, per the 2026-11-14 crates rule). November Will-hours ≈10 + 2 slack = 12, inside supply. Horizon B (Dec): B1 qexo integration staged state-sync → dispatch → teleport/resume (state home /var/lib/qexo/state.phext; closes only on a kill-and-resume demo by Dec 31), B2 Choir/Theia return gated on December funds (Discord re-enablement is Will's ruling, default dark), B3 local-inference seat as an options paper FOR Will to configure (never self-executing), B4 decision classification, B5 Dec 31 metrics targets (phantom-open <5% vs 49% baseline). Horizon C (Q1 2027): rate targets (standard-build round-trip median ≤24 h / p90 ≤72 h; queue dwell halved; bounces ≤1/week; phantom-open <2%) tied to the 2027 Q1 revenue gate and the hackathon sweep; scaling model maps Will+Emise+phext+ranch term by term. 6 assumptions-to-confirm listed for Will in the document.
- 2026-11-14 01:34 CST: Will's addition for the qexo phase (Horizon B): once we move to qexo, gamify the process via a TUI. Will will follow up with details in the qexo thread — design input lives there, not here; the roadmap's B1 should absorb it when it lands. (Adjacent and already in flight in the qexo thread: qexo learn as a learnyouqexo wizard TUI, v1.5 PR for Will's review.)
needattunedfreedunify# Schedule the next phext-lattice release build — seed cache merged 2026-11-12

Coordinate: 28.6.7/9.7.11/6.6.8
Issued: 2026-11-12 18:09 CST (evening commute voice call)
Request (from session record): Will's rule (2026-11-12): whenever we merge changes to phext-lattice or any Rust-based tooling, queue a task to keep track of the next release needing to be scheduled — not necessarily deploy every change, but schedule a build and do not forget about producing builds. Trigger here: phext-lattice PR #61 (seed cache client) merged 2026-11-12 18:08 CST at Will's direction (merge commit 98399ed0). Any further merges (e.g. PR #57 Zero Index, decision pending in the same call) batch into the same next build. Also batched: phext-lattice PR #57 (Zero Index) merged 2026-11-12 18:12 CST at Will's direction (merge commit 0c9a9788).
Status: open — next phext-lattice release build to be scheduled (now carrying PR #61 seed cache + PR #57 Zero Index + PR #62 libphext 0.6.0 bump, merged by Will 2026-11-13, merge commit bd1e1ec4); build on a ranch node; closes when a release build from current exo exists and is verified. Deploy remains a separate decision. Publish probe 2026-11-13: /source/phext-lattice fast-forwarded to the merged exo (bd1e1ec4), tests 183 + 94 green (local_editor_regression timing failure pre-existing) — but phext-lattice 0.1.0 is already on crates.io (2026-03-07); publishing needs a version bump above 0.1.0, Will's call. CORRECTION (same day): that probe read the STALE /source tree — the merged exo tree's phext-lattice package is version 1.1.1, which is free on crates.io (registry max 0.1.0) and publishable as-is; no bump needed (0.6.0 would be a regression). A brief 1.1.1→0.6.0 bump branch was created from the misread and deleted unmerged the same hour. Publish of 1.1.1 from /source/phext-lattice is ready when Will says go. Will directed 1.2.0 instead (2026-11-13): bump branch emise/version-1.2.0 @ 8388ff6 (Cargo.toml 1.1.1 → 1.2.0, Cargo.lock refreshed incl. the libphext 0.6.0 pin; diff verified line-exact), opened as phext-lattice PR #63, register #101. PR #63 merged by Will 2026-11-13 (merge commit f38beb6f); phext-lattice 1.2.0 PUBLISHED from aletheia-core @ f38beb6f and verified live on crates.io (API max_version 1.2.0, updated 23:00:10Z). Register #101 resolved.
