Status: BUILT + PR OPEN 2026-11-14: phext-lattice PR https://github.com/wbic16/phext-lattice/pull/64 (emise/multiversal-tei @ ff262a2, version 1.3.0). Awaiting Will's merge; on merge queue the phext-lattice release task (standing rule).

Notes:
- Will, 2026-11-14 03:33 CST. Multiversal phext = the chain model (libphext Chain API, $1..$N) that teixml-to-mutable 0.1 emits and 0.3 hoists originals from. TEI-XML direct load in edit.phext.io builds on the published teixml-to-mutable conversion. phext-lattice local checkout is stale/not a repo - work from a fresh remote-faithful tree (TOOLS.md).
- What shipped: chain-aware MappedLattice ($N:coord addressing, /api/universes, titlebar universe switcher), chains READ-ONLY in the editor by design (WAL shape cannot be honoured by overlay-rewrite save; refusal names the reason; pre-existing smear-and-flatten hazard closed). TEI load: teixml-to-mutable 0.3.0 as library dep under web feature; picker badges .xml/TEI dirs; /api/open converts beside source with hash-proven reuse; gating untouched. Tests lib 188 + bin 97 green (one pre-existing e2e_local perf failure unchanged). E2E 30/30 route-level checks (no real-browser pass): 276 KB MS converts in 28 ms to a 1,995-universe chain; 58 MB Phextura base opens in 159 ms with full 142,619-entry seed. Limits: search/overview active-universe only; MCP universe navigation not addressed. Record: ~/workspace/teixml-to-mutable-work/LATTICE-TEI.md.
weird# qexo disk gate is a VM instrument: gate on free space and volume fraction, thresholds in state.phext config

Coordinate: 36.6.8/8.6.11/6.6.10
Issued: 2026-11-12 22:51 CST
Request (from session record): Will ran qexo on aletheia-core (2026-11-12) and the disk gate read RED at 809 GB used on a 3.6 TB volume with 2.7 TB free (df: 754 GiB used, 22%). Cause: the gate's absolute thresholds (AMBER 95 GB / RED 102 GB used; files 1.1M/1.3M) were calibrated to the Muse VM's staging quota in the v2 planner and ported value-for-value ever since; the probe measures used bytes on whatever volume the config root sits on, so the VM instrument condemns any real node. Fix: gate on free space + used fraction of the measured volume, report total/free in the plan (units consistent), thresholds read from the config homeland (9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9) in state.phext with sane defaults, and gate copy that names the actual volume instead of 'the VM'. Sequencing: dispatch the moment the v1.0.1 doctor-Windows slice lands — both slices touch Cargo/VERSION/CHANGELOG.
Status: MERGED 2026-11-12 (PR #11, merged_at 2026-11-13T05:34:38Z, after PR #10 had landed on its stacked base; carried to exo by PR #11). exo carries v1.0.2.
reasonsiteRegister numbers are permanent and monotonic — never renumber

A Waiting-on-Will item keeps its register number for life. Never renumber after removals; resolved items keep their number in the register's history; new items take the next unused number. Born of 2026-11-02: item #5 was dropped and the list renumbered; Will reversed both — the citation "#5" must always mean the same item.

— Given 2026-11-02 · Source: AGENTS.md · [distilled]
— Placed at 36.6.13/7.8.14/11.6.11 by holographic merge of the statement above: 8 distinct words — 7 placed from CYOA, 0 at home, 1 at the far corner.
Whenever we change process, run a check to ensure that it sticks

A process change is not done when it is written down; it is done when a later check confirms the change actually took hold — the rule is present where it belongs, the machinery that enacts it exists and runs, and the first real occasion to use it behaved the way the change intended. A change that lives only in the message where it was announced is a defect, the same class as a process that lives only in chat.

— Given 2026-11-08 · Source: main chat, Will's standing rule · [verbatim]
— Placed at 36.6.22/14.13.30/5.5.8 by holographic merge of the statement above: 12 distinct words — 12 placed from CYOA, 0 at home, 0 at the far corner.
